/**
 * Teach Me explanations for english, written in advance so they load
 * instantly, work offline and cost nothing per student. Keyed by
 * passageId|question|options (see teachMeKey in app.js). A question with no
 * entry here falls back to the live AI explanation.
 */
(window.TEACH_ME = window.TEACH_ME || {})["english"] = {
 "|Choose the option nearest in meaning to the underlined word: His FLAMBOYANT lifestyle attracted public attention.|Simple|Extravagant|Modest|Religious": "FLAMBOYANT describes something showy and eye-catching, and \"attracted public attention\" confirms that, so B (extravagant) is right. Simple and modest are opposites, the trap for students who mix up meaning and opposite. Religious has nothing to do with showiness. Memory tip: a flamboyant person \"flames\" with colour; they want to be seen.",
 "|From the options, choose the word that correctly completes the sentence: The judge's verdict was _____ the evidence presented.|at variance with|in variance to|at divergence with|in difference of": "The fixed expression is \"at variance with\", meaning in disagreement with. The verdict did not agree with the evidence, so A is right. \"In variance to\" and \"at divergence with\" sound plausible but use the wrong preposition, and \"in difference of\" is not English. Memory tip: learn it as one block, \"at variance WITH\", like \"in line WITH\".",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the word in capital letters: BOOT|foot|book|pool|could": "BOOT has the long /uː/ sound, the same as POOL, so C is right. Foot, book and could all have the short /ʊ/ sound. That's the trap: they're spelt with \"oo\" or \"ou\" but are said quickly. Memory tip: stretch the word. If you can hold the \"ooo\" (boooot, poooool), it's long /uː/.",
 "|Choose the option that best explains the expression: TO TURN OVER A NEW LEAF|To read a new book|To change one's behaviour for the better|To plant a new crop|To move to a new home": "To turn over a new leaf is to start behaving better. The \"leaf\" is a page in a book, and you start a clean page. So B is right. A, C and D take \"leaf\" and \"new\" literally (a book, a crop, a home), which is the usual trap with idioms. Memory tip: idioms are almost never about the literal objects in them.",
 "|In which of the following sentences is the underlined word used correctly? The thief _____ the house last night.|broken into|broke into|break into|breaking into": "\"Last night\" sets the time in the past, so the simple past \"broke into\" is needed, and B is right. \"Broken into\" needs a helper (\"had broken into\"), \"break into\" is present, and \"breaking into\" needs \"was\". Memory tip: a finished past time marker (yesterday, last night, in 2010) calls for the simple past.",
 "|Choose the option with the correct stress pattern: Photograph|pho-TO-graph|PHO-to-graph|pho-to-GRAPH|pho-to-graph": "PHOtograph takes its main stress on the first syllable, so B is right. The trap is that related words move the stress: phoTOgrapher, photoGRAphic. Option D shows no stress at all. Memory tip: say \"PHOto\" first. The word starts with \"photo\", which is stressed at the front.",
 "|Choose the option nearest in meaning to INDIGENOUS.|Foreign|Imported|Native|Adopted": "Indigenous means native, belonging naturally to a place (indigenous languages, indigenous people), so C is right. Foreign, imported and adopted all mean coming from outside, the opposite. Memory tip: \"indi-GEN-ous\" has the root \"gen\", meaning birth. Born there.",
 "|From the words lettered A to D, choose the word that best fills the gap: The president _____ the bill into law yesterday.|sign|signed|has signed|is signing": "\"Yesterday\" points to a finished past time, so the simple past \"signed\" is correct: B. \"Has signed\" (present perfect) can't be used with a finished time like yesterday, which is a common exam trap. \"Sign\" and \"is signing\" are present. Memory tip: never put \"yesterday\" with has/have + past participle.",
 "|Which of these sentences is grammatically correct?|Neither John nor his brothers is coming.|Neither John nor his brothers are coming.|Neither John nor his brothers was coming.|Neither John nor his brothers comes.": "With \"neither…nor\", the verb agrees with the subject nearer to it. \"Brothers\" is plural, so \"are\" is right: B. \"Is\" and \"was\" agree with John, the farther subject, which is the trap. \"Comes\" is singular. Memory tip: in neither/nor and either/or sentences, look at the noun right before the verb.",
 "|Choose the option opposite in meaning to SYCOPHANT.|Flatterer|Honest critic|Liar|Traitor": "A sycophant is a flatterer who praises people to win favour. The opposite is someone who tells the plain truth even when it's unwelcome: an honest critic, B. A is a synonym (the trap). Liar and traitor are negative words but not opposites. Memory tip: a sycophant \"sucks up\"; a critic speaks up.",
 "|The clause 'which was built by the Romans' in the sentence 'The bridge, which was built by the Romans, is still standing' is:|A defining relative clause|A non-defining relative clause|An adverbial clause|A noun clause": "The clause sits between commas and only adds extra information. Remove it and the sentence still tells us which bridge. That makes it non-defining, so B is right. A defining clause has no commas and identifies the noun (\"the bridge that the Romans built\"). C and D are other clause types. Memory tip: commas around a \"which\" clause mean extra, non-defining information.",
 "|Choose the correctly spelt word:|Accomodate|Accommodate|Acommodate|Accomodate": "Accommodate has a double C and a double M, so B is right. The other options drop one letter, the most common misspelling in English exams. Memory tip: accommodate is big enough to \"accommodate\" two Cs and two Ms.",
 "|In which of the options is the word 'REFUSE' a noun?|Please refuse to sign the document.|The refuse collector came early.|Do not refuse a good offer.|She will refuse to attend.": "In B, \"refuse\" (said REF-yoos) means rubbish, and \"refuse collector\" is a person who collects rubbish, so it's a noun. In A, C and D, \"refuse\" (said ri-FYOOZ) is a verb meaning to say no. Memory tip: noun REFuse (stress first), verb reFUSE (stress second). The same pattern works for REcord/reCORD and OBject/obJECT.",
 "|Choose the expression that best completes the sentence: I am not _____ what he wants.|sure about|sure of|sure on|sure with": "The correct collocation is \"sure of\", so B is right. \"Sure about\" is acceptable in casual speech, but exam English prefers \"sure of\" before a clause like \"what he wants\". \"Sure on\" and \"sure with\" are wrong. Memory tip: be sure OF something, just as you're aware OF it or certain OF it.",
 "|The phrasal verb GIVE UP means:|surrender or quit|offer generously|rise higher|present formally": "\"Give up\" means to stop trying, to quit or surrender, so A is right. B (\"offer generously\") confuses it with \"give away\". C and D take \"up\" literally. Memory tip: \"I give up!\" is what you say when you quit.",
 "|Which option correctly replaces the underlined word? The VERBOSE lecturer lost the attention of his students.|Quiet|Wordy|Brilliant|Strict": "Verbose means using more words than needed. Wordy is a direct synonym, so B is right. \"Lost the attention of his students\" is the clue: long-winded talk bores listeners. Quiet is close to the opposite, and brilliant and strict don't fit the clue. Memory tip: VERBose has \"verb\", a word, so it means too many words.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the analogy: Pisciculture is to fish as apiculture is to ___.|Birds|Bees|Silk worms|Cattle": "Pisciculture is fish farming (Latin piscis, fish), and apiculture is bee-keeping (Latin apis, bee), so B is right. Silkworm farming is sericulture and bird-keeping is aviculture, which are the traps. Memory tip: an APIary is a place where bees are kept.",
 "|He _____ English for five years before he got the certificate.|has studied|studied|had studied|was studying": "The studying (5 years) was finished before another past event (getting the certificate). An action completed before another past action takes the past perfect: had studied, C. \"Has studied\" is present perfect and doesn't fit a past story. \"Studied\" and \"was studying\" lose the \"before\" relationship. Memory tip: \"had + past participle\" means earlier in the past.",
 "|Identify the type of sentence: 'The woman who lives next door has a dog.'|Simple sentence|Compound sentence|Complex sentence|Compound-complex sentence": "The sentence has one main clause (\"The woman has a dog\") and one dependent clause (\"who lives next door\"). One main plus at least one dependent is a complex sentence, so C is right. A compound sentence joins two main clauses with and/but/or, and a simple sentence has just one clause. Memory tip: a \"who/which/that\" clause usually makes the sentence complex.",
 "|Which of these is an example of onomatopoeia?|The brave soldier died.|The brook babbled over the stones.|Time is money.|Life is a journey.": "Onomatopoeia is a word that imitates a sound. \"Babbled\" copies the sound of running water, so B is right. C and D are metaphors (time is money, life is a journey), and A is a plain statement. Memory tip: if you can \"hear\" the word (buzz, sizzle, babble), it's onomatopoeia.",
 "|Choose the option nearest in meaning to AMELIORATE.|Worsen|Destroy|Improve|Ignore": "To ameliorate is to make something better, so C (improve) is right. Worsen is the opposite (the trap), and destroy and ignore don't fit. Memory tip: \"melior\" is Latin for better, so ameliorate means \"make better\".",
 "|In the sentence 'The wounded soldier was carried off the battlefield', the word WOUNDED is:|An adjective|A verb|A noun|An adverb": "\"Wounded\" here describes the soldier, telling us which soldier, so it works as an adjective: A. The trap is that \"wounded\" looks like a past-tense verb. In \"The bullet wounded him\" it is a verb, but here it comes before a noun and describes it. Memory tip: an -ed word placed before a noun is acting as an adjective.",
 "|Choose the word with the odd sound from the others: A. thumb  B. thought  C. those  D. thing|thumb|thought|those|thing": "\"Those\" begins with the voiced /ð/ sound: put your hand on your throat and you feel a buzz. Thumb, thought and thing begin with the voiceless /θ/, just air. So C is the odd one. Memory tip: grammar words (this, that, those, the, they) usually take the voiced /ð/.",
 "|The sentence 'No sooner had he arrived than it started raining' means:|He arrived before the rain|He arrived just as it started raining|He arrived but was not wet|He refused to go out in the rain": "\"No sooner…than\" means the second event happened immediately after the first. He had barely arrived when the rain started, so B is right. A says he arrived before it rained but loses the \"immediately\". C and D add ideas that aren't in the sentence. Memory tip: no sooner = the moment that.",
 "|Choose the option that correctly replaces the underlined expression: She is regarded as the BÊTE NOIRE of the department.|The favourite person|The most feared or disliked person|The most talented person|The most experienced person": "Bête noire (French, literally \"black beast\") means a person or thing that someone especially dislikes or dreads, so B is right. A is the opposite. C and D give positive qualities with no link to the phrase. Memory tip: a bête noire is your \"black beast\", the thing that haunts you.",
 "|SANGUINE most nearly means:|Pessimistic|Optimistic|Doubtful|Angry": "Sanguine means cheerfully optimistic, especially in a difficult situation, so B is right. Pessimistic is the opposite and the main trap. Doubtful leans the same way as pessimistic, and angry is unrelated. Memory tip: think of a \"sanguine smile\", a calm, hopeful one.",
 "|The word 'FLOUT' means:|To follow rules strictly|To openly disregard rules or norms|To praise someone|To pretend ignorance": "To flout a rule is to openly ignore it, so B is right. The classic trap is confusing it with \"flaunt\" (to show off). Option A is the opposite of flout. Memory tip: you flout a law (break it openly) but flaunt your wealth (show it off).",
 "|Choose the appropriate option: The chairman, with all his board members, _____ present at the meeting.|were|are|was|have been": "The real subject is \"the chairman\", which is singular. \"With all his board members\" is just extra information and doesn't make the subject plural, so the verb is \"was\": C. \"Were\" and \"are\" are the trap of agreeing with \"members\". Memory tip: words after \"with\", \"along with\" or \"as well as\" never change the verb.",
 "|Which of the following contains a dangling modifier?|Running fast, the bus was caught by Mary.|Running fast, Mary caught the bus.|Mary ran fast and caught the bus.|The fast-running Mary caught the bus.": "A dangling modifier is a phrase with nothing sensible to describe. In A, \"running fast\" seems to describe the bus, which can't run, so A is right. B fixes it (Mary is running), and C and D have no modifier problem. Memory tip: the person doing the \"-ing\" action must come right after the comma.",
 "|Choose the option with the correct use of the semicolon:|I love music; because it soothes me.|She passed the exam; however, she was not happy.|He is tall; and thin.|They went home; but we stayed.": "A semicolon can join two full sentences, and a linking word like \"however\" goes after the semicolon, followed by a comma. B does this correctly. In A, C and D the semicolon sits before a joining word (because, and, but), which needs a comma or nothing instead. Memory tip: semicolon + however + comma; never semicolon + and/but.",
 "|The word EPHEMERAL means:|Lasting forever|Lasting for a very short time|Very large|Very small": "Ephemeral means lasting a very short time (an ephemeral fashion), so B is right. \"Lasting forever\" is the opposite, and size (C, D) has nothing to do with it. Memory tip: think of a mayfly (\"ephemera\") that lives only a day.",
 "|LACONIC speech is one that is:|Long and detailed|Brief and to the point|Emotional and passionate|Confusing and vague": "Laconic speech uses very few words: brief and to the point. So B is right. A is the opposite (verbose), and C and D describe other styles. Memory tip: the Spartans of Laconia were famous for one-word replies.",
 "|In the sentence 'It was he who stole the money', the word IT is:|A personal pronoun|A dummy/expletive subject|A demonstrative pronoun|An object pronoun": "In \"It was he who stole the money\", \"it\" doesn't refer to anything. It's a placeholder (dummy or expletive subject) used to put emphasis on \"he\", so B is right. A personal pronoun \"it\" would stand for a thing. Demonstrative pronouns are this and that, and it's not an object here. Memory tip: in \"It is…who/that\" sentences, \"it\" is a dummy.",
 "|Choose the option that has the correct plural: The _____ of the economy worry investors.|criterions|criteria|criterias|criterium": "Criterion is singular and criteria is plural, so with \"worry\" (a plural verb) the answer is B. \"Criterions\" and \"criterias\" are common mistakes; criteria is already plural. Memory tip: one criterion, two criteria, just like one phenomenon, two phenomena.",
 "|The tone of a poem refers to:|The rhyme scheme used|The rhythm of the lines|The poet's attitude to the subject|The subject matter of the poem": "Tone is the writer's attitude to the subject, such as mocking, sad or admiring, so C is right. Rhyme scheme and rhythm are about sound, and subject matter is what the poem is about, not how the poet feels. Memory tip: tone = \"tone of voice\", how the poet sounds about the topic.",
 "|Choose the option nearest in meaning: The STOLID manager showed no emotion.|Emotional|Impassive|Energetic|Sensitive": "Stolid means calm and not easily moved, and \"showed no emotion\" is the clue, so impassive (B) is right. Emotional and sensitive are opposites (the trap), and energetic doesn't fit. Memory tip: stolid sounds like \"solid\", someone who doesn't shake.",
 "|Which of the following is an example of a mixed metaphor?|He was a lion in battle.|She lit the fire that would later drown her plans.|Time is a thief.|The world is a stage.": "A mixed metaphor joins two images that clash. Fire can't drown anything, so B is right. A, C and D are each a single, consistent metaphor. Memory tip: picture the image literally. If it's impossible (a fire drowning), it's mixed.",
 "|Choose the option with the correct sentence structure: Hardly _____ when the storm began.|had he arrived|he arrived|did he arrive|he had arrived": "When a sentence starts with \"hardly\", the helping verb comes before the subject: \"Hardly had he arrived when…\". So A is right. B and D keep normal word order, which is wrong after \"hardly\". \"Did he arrive\" is the wrong tense with \"when the storm began\". Memory tip: hardly/scarcely/no sooner at the start means flip the subject and auxiliary.",
 "|JAMB 2022 — Choose the option that best fills the gap: If I _____ you, I would not have done it.|were|was|am|be": "\"If I were you\" is the fixed form for imagined situations: use \"were\" for every subject, even I, he and she. So A is right. \"If I was you\" is common in speech but marked wrong in exams. Am and be are wrong tenses. Memory tip: if I were, if he were, if she were.",
 "|The expression 'to bite more than one can chew' means:|To eat greedily|To take on more responsibility than one can handle|To speak when eating|To waste food": "To bite off more than you can chew is to take on more work or responsibility than you can manage, so B is right. A, C and D take \"bite\" and \"chew\" literally. Memory tip: picture a mouthful too big to chew; that's a task too big to handle.",
 "|Choose the option that correctly interprets the sentence: 'The suspect was given the benefit of the doubt.'|The suspect was acquitted without evidence|The suspect was treated as innocent despite uncertainty|The suspect was punished lightly|The suspect confessed to the crime": "Giving someone the benefit of the doubt means accepting their account as true because the evidence isn't certain, so B is right. A goes too far (nothing says he was acquitted). C and D aren't in the sentence. Memory tip: when in doubt, the doubt works in the person's favour.",
 "|Choose the word nearest in meaning to ALTRUISTIC:|Selfish|Generous and selfless|Ambitious|Arrogant": "Altruistic means selfless and caring about others' good, so B is right. Selfish is the opposite (the trap), and ambitious and arrogant are unrelated. Memory tip: \"alter\" is Latin for other, so altruistic means thinking of others.",
 "|The rhetorical device used in 'Ask not what your country can do for you; ask what you can do for your country' is:|Anaphora|Chiasmus|Alliteration|Hyperbole": "Kennedy reverses the order of the words in the second half (\"what your country can do for you\" becomes \"what you can do for your country\"). That crossed A-B, B-A pattern is chiasmus, so B is right. Anaphora repeats words at the start of lines, alliteration repeats first sounds, and hyperbole is exaggeration. Memory tip: chiasmus comes from the Greek letter χ (chi), an X shape, a cross-over.",
 "|In the sentence 'She has been working since morning', the tense used is:|Simple past|Present perfect|Present perfect continuous|Past perfect": "\"Has been working\" (has/have + been + -ing) is the present perfect continuous, used for an action that started in the past and is still going on, so C is right. Present perfect would be \"has worked\", past perfect \"had worked\", and simple past \"worked\". Memory tip: been + -ing plus \"since/for\" means still going on.",
 "|Choose the option that is OPPOSITE in meaning to VERBOSE:|Talkative|Laconic|Eloquent|Coherent": "Verbose means using too many words; laconic means using very few, so B is the opposite. Talkative is a near-synonym (the trap). Eloquent and coherent describe good speech, not the amount of it. Memory tip: verbose vs laconic is a classic JAMB pair.",
 "|From the options, identify the sentence that contains a misplaced modifier:|Having finished the exam, the students left.|She almost drove her children to school every day.|The children who passed were praised.|He ran quickly to catch the bus.": "In B, \"almost\" sits before \"drove\", which says she nearly drove but didn't. The intended meaning is \"drove her children to school almost every day\", so the modifier is misplaced and B is right. A is correct (the students did finish), and C and D are fine. Memory tip: put almost, only and just right next to the word they modify.",
 "|The JAMB comprehension passage uses the word INVETERATE to describe a gambler. This means:|A first-time gambler|A deeply habitual gambler unlikely to change|A successful gambler|A reformed gambler": "Inveterate means firmly established by long habit and unlikely to change, so an inveterate gambler is a habitual one: B. A and D are the opposite (new or reformed), and C adds success, which the word doesn't say. Memory tip: inveterate = \"veteran\" habit.",
 "|Choose the option with the correct use of the apostrophe:|The dogs bone was stolen.|The dog's bone was stolen.|The dogs' bone was stolen.|The dog'es bone was stolen.": "One dog owns the bone, so the possessive is dog + 's: \"the dog's bone\", B. A has no apostrophe, C (dogs') means several dogs, and D isn't a real form. Memory tip: one owner, 's; plural owner ending in s, just '.",
 "|Which of these best defines the term 'paradox' in literature?|A comparison using 'like' or 'as'|A statement that seems contradictory but reveals a deeper truth|Extreme exaggeration for effect|Giving human qualities to non-human things": "A paradox is a statement that seems to contradict itself but turns out to hold a truth (\"The more you learn, the less you know\"), so B is right. A is a simile, C is hyperbole and D is personification, all common traps. Memory tip: a paradox sounds wrong but is right.",
 "|Choose the option that correctly completes: He paid the debt _____ which he had borrowed the money.|with which|of which|for which|in which": "He borrowed money for the purpose of paying the debt: \"the debt for which he had borrowed the money\". So C is right. \"With which\" would mean he used the debt as a tool, and \"of which\" and \"in which\" don't fit the meaning. Memory tip: borrow money FOR a purpose.",
 "|JAMB 2019 — The word PHLEGMATIC is closest in meaning to:|Enthusiastic|Calm and unemotional|Aggressive|Anxious": "Phlegmatic means calm, unexcitable and slow to show emotion, so B is right. Enthusiastic, aggressive and anxious are all signs of strong feeling, the opposite. Memory tip: in old medicine, too much \"phlegm\" was thought to make people slow and calm.",
 "eng-2001-p1|Members of a language community control at least two dialects because|the convergence of people from disparate regions results in the pressure of communication|there is little demand for the indigenous dialects|they want a lingua franca which is produced by mutual intelligibility|they are made up of both rural and urban dwellers": "The last paragraph says people from different regions converge in towns, and the pressure of communication creates a need for a shared standard dialect alongside each person's own. So A is right. B overstates it (rural areas still use their dialect), C reverses cause and effect, and D is a description, not a reason. Memory tip: for \"because\" questions, find the sentence in the passage that gives the cause.",
 "eng-2001-p1|According to the passage,|a foreign language produces the transference of the correct patterns of dialect features|speech organs are shaped by indigenous languages|a lingua franca is the consequence of a standard dialect|mutual intelligibility is the consequence of the pressure of communication": "The passage ends by saying the standard dialect \"now becomes the lingua franca\", so a lingua franca results from a standard dialect: C. D flips the passage's logic (the pressure of communication creates the need for intelligibility, not the reverse), which is the trap. A and B aren't stated. Memory tip: watch for options that reverse cause and effect.",
 "eng-2001-p1|An appropriate title for this passage is|The Emergence of a Standard Language|The Phenomenon of Accent|The Quest for a Lingua Franca|Members of a Language Community": "The passage traces how a standard dialect emerges, why learners carry an accent into it, and how it becomes the lingua franca, all parts of the emergence of a standard language. So A is right. Accent (B), the lingua franca (C) and language communities (D) are each only one part. Memory tip: the best title covers the whole passage, not one paragraph.",
 "eng-2001-p1|The word 'those', as used in the passage (\"those of form and most of those of orthography and phonology\"), refers to|indigenous languages|forms of orthography and phonology|foreign languages|patterns of an indigenous language": "\"Those\" points back to the previous sentence, \"the patterns of an indigenous language\". The patterns of form, orthography and phonology are those carried over from the learner's own language, so D is right. B repeats words from the phrase but misses what \"those\" stands for, which is the usual reference trap. Memory tip: replace the pronoun with your answer and re-read the sentence.",
 "eng-2001-p1|Phonetic patterns allow for intelligibility tolerance because|transference is both difficult to avoid and easy to tolerate|they are involved and difficult to avoid|they give rise to the phenomenon of accent which is easy to tolerate|they involve muscular patterns which later crystallize": "The passage says transference of phonetic habits is \"both easier to tolerate and more difficult to avoid\" than other transfer, which is why listeners tolerate it. A says exactly this. C and D borrow passage words (accent, muscular patterns) but give a different reason. B is vague. Memory tip: the option that echoes the passage's own reasoning usually wins.",
 "eng-2001-p2|The NDE man appears to be a spectator in the flurry of activities around him because|his new 'body' would not allow him to participate|he is moving rapidly through a long dark tunnel|he can only watch as the events unfold|he is now a dead man": "Out of his body, the man has a \"body\" of a very different nature with different powers, so he can't take part in the resuscitation and can only watch. A gives that reason. C restates the situation without explaining it, B is an earlier stage, and D is false (he lives). Memory tip: \"because\" options must give a reason, not just repeat the event.",
 "eng-2001-p2|According to the passage, scientific evidence has made it possible|to make conjectures about what happens after death|to know a little about what happens in the world of the dead|for one to experience the sensation of dying and living again|for the dead to return and tell their experiences": "The researchers \"think they are finding\" a pattern in near-death reports. That gives grounds for informed guesses about death, not certain knowledge. So A (conjectures) is right. B claims real knowledge of the dead, which is too strong. C and D misread who experiences what. Memory tip: in science passages, prefer the cautious option (suggest, conjecture) over certain claims.",
 "eng-2001-p2|The expression 'as he reaches the point of greatest physical distress', as used in the passage, means when|the man's system finally collapses|the sick man finally stops breathing|his doctor is ready to pronounce him dead|his condition seems to get worse": "\"The point of greatest physical distress\" is the moment the body finally gives way. Right then he hears himself pronounced dead, so A is right. B narrows it to breathing, C shifts focus to the doctor, and D (getting worse) is earlier than the peak. Memory tip: \"greatest\" means the very peak, not a gradual worsening.",
 "eng-2001-p2|A suitable title for the passage is|A Glimpse into the World of the Dead|Visions of an Afterlife|Research into the Lives of the Dead|The Sensation of Death and the Afterlife": "The passage opens with \"It is possible to have a glimpse of life after death\" and describes what the NDE person sees, so A matches it best. B is close but misses the \"glimpse\" idea. C is wrong because they are living people, not the dead. D is too narrow. Memory tip: the opening sentence often gives you the title.",
 "eng-2001-p2|That the man was shown a panoramic instantaneous playback of the major events of his life suggests that|he needs to be entertained to take his mind away from the noise around him|he needs to see the difference between his past life and his new life|there are video machines in the world beyond|he has to assess his deeds in life": "The being of light asks a question \"to make him evaluate his life\", and the playback helps him do it, so he is assessing his deeds: D. A and C treat the playback as entertainment or technology, a literal trap. B is about comparing lives, which the passage doesn't say. Memory tip: look for the purpose clause (\"to make him…\").",
 "eng-2001-p3|Gap 11: …check through the field record books and questionnaires for any _____, inconsistencies and incompleteness.|mistakes|events|records|odds": "The list names faults to look for when checking raw data: mistakes, inconsistencies and incompleteness. So A fits. Events, records and odds aren't faults. Memory tip: in a list, the missing word must belong to the same family as the others.",
 "eng-2001-p3|Gap 12: When it is not possible to carry out these _____, a visit to the field may even be necessary.|plans|corrections|expectations|possibilities": "\"These\" points back to correcting the discovered shortcomings in the sentence before, so \"these corrections\" fits: B. Plans, expectations and possibilities don't link to \"shortcomings\". Memory tip: \"these/this\" in a gap usually refers back to the previous sentence's idea.",
 "eng-2001-p3|Gap 13: In most scientific _____ such revisits are clearly impossible.|questionnaires|data|experiments|conclusions": "Scientific experiments happen under specific conditions and can't simply be revisited, and the next sentence compares them with surveys. So C is right. Questionnaires belong to surveys, and data and conclusions aren't things you \"revisit\" in this sense. Memory tip: \"scientific ___\" plus \"surveys too\" points to experiments.",
 "eng-2001-p3|Gap 14: A road traffic survey _____ to find out the amount and frequency of daily traffic between two towns…|classified|precoded|conducted|experimented": "The verb that goes with \"survey\" is \"conduct\": a survey is conducted to find something out. So C is right. Classified and precoded describe data, not surveys, and \"experimented\" can't take a survey as its subject. Memory tip: conduct a survey, carry out research, run a test.",
 "eng-2001-p3|Gap 15: …cannot be expected to be _____. There is no way of going back to check whether the number of vehicles reported for any particular hour is correct or not.|observed|produced|undertaken|reproducible": "The next sentence says there's no way to go back and check the counts. A one-off traffic count can't be repeated, so it isn't reproducible: D. C (undertaken) makes no sense for a survey already carried out. Memory tip: the sentence after a gap often explains it.",
 "eng-2001-p3|Gap 16: With open-ended questions the _____ have to be classified into relatively small number of groups.|method|responses|errors|conclusions": "Open-ended questions produce free answers (responses), which then have to be grouped, and the next sentence talks about \"classifying answers\". So B is right. Method, errors and conclusions aren't what respondents give. Memory tip: open-ended questions get responses.",
 "eng-2001-p3|Gap 17: The process of classifying answers and of sometimes identifying them by number and letter is called _____.|encoding|recording|coding|recoding": "Assigning answers to categories and labelling them with numbers or letters is called coding, so C is right. Encoding is a more general information term, recording is writing down, and recoding means coding again. Memory tip: survey researchers code answers.",
 "eng-2001-p3|Gap 18: When closed-ended questions are used, it is possible to code all the possible answers before they are actually received. This is called _____.|recoding|coding|precoding|encoding": "Coding the answers before they come in is precoding (\"pre-\" = before), so C is right. Plain \"coding\" (B) misses the \"before\" in the sentence, which is the trap. Memory tip: match prefixes to time words: \"before\" means pre-.",
 "eng-2001-p3|Gap 19: This whole process of checking through questionnaires and notebooks is called _____.|listing|posting|editing|auditing": "Checking completed questionnaires and notebooks for errors and consistency is called editing the data, so C is right. Listing and posting are unrelated, and auditing is a finance term. Memory tip: in research, you edit data before analysing it.",
 "eng-2001-p3|Gap 20: Collected data will eventually have to be used in drawing _____ and writing a report about the population from which it came.|analogies|examples|references|conclusions": "The fixed collocation is \"draw conclusions\", so D is right. You can draw an analogy, but \"analogies about the population\" doesn't fit a report. Examples and references aren't \"drawn\" from data. Memory tip: data leads to conclusions.",
 "eng-2001-p4|The writer seems to suggest that|the volume and speed of traffic on the roads increased with the advent of cars, motor cycles and lorries|there were no roads before the advent of cars and motorcycles and so people had to be mechanically minded|coachmen and grooms were not mechanically minded|the roads that existed were dormant": "Once cars, motorcycles and lorries arrived, the roads \"awoke to feverish activity\" and had to handle \"a volume and speed of traffic for which it had never been intended\". A says this. B and D misread \"the roads had gone to sleep\", which means quiet, not non-existent. C is too general. Memory tip: figurative words (asleep, awoke) aren't literal facts.",
 "eng-2001-p4|The writer uses the expression 'unless mechanically minded' to refer to|town authorities laying down tramlines|coachmen and grooms who chose to become mechanics|coachmen and grooms adaptable to the new technology|those amenable to change and development": "\"Unless mechanically minded\" qualifies the coachmen and grooms: only those who could adapt to the new machines escaped hardship. So C is right. B adds \"became mechanics\", which isn't said. A and D point to other groups. Memory tip: find the noun the phrase sits next to in the passage.",
 "eng-2001-p4|The statement 'By 1910, the motor car was plainly conquering the highway' means that|highway codes for motor cars came into effect by 1910|by 1910 motor cars became a common sight on the highways|the motor car was invented in 1910|by 1910 many people knew how to drive motorcars": "\"Conquering the highway\" means taking over the roads, so by 1910 cars were a common sight there: B. A (highway codes) and C (invention date) take \"highway\" and \"1910\" too literally, and D is about driving skill. Memory tip: \"conquering\" means dominating, being everywhere.",
 "eng-2001-p4|From the passage, it is obvious that|the motorcar and the lorry came to displace the train traffic|motor cars were mere luxuries which many people tried desperately to acquire|the motorcar was invented before the express trains|the train was the fastest means of transport before the motorcar and the lorry": "Cars reached speeds \"which rivaled those of the express trains\". Trains were the benchmark for speed, so D is right. The answer key in circulation gives B, but the passage never says people \"tried desperately\" to buy cars. A and C aren't supported either. Memory tip: \"rivalled X\" tells you X was the standard to beat.",
 "eng-2001-p4|One of the following statements can be deduced from the passage.|Significant improvement occurred in road transport since the advent of motorcars, lorries and motorcycles.|People no longer used trains with the advent of cars and lorries.|Society would be better off without the chaotic volume and speed of motor cars, lorries and motorcycles.|Human society was static without the express speed of cars and motorcycles.": "The passage describes busy roads, speeds rivalling trains, and surfaces improved by tar-spraying, which means road transport improved significantly: A. C is an opinion the writer never gives, though the answer key in circulation chose it. B and D overstate things. Memory tip: \"deduced\" means supported by the passage, not a view you add.",
 "|Identify the word that has a DIFFERENT stress pattern from the others.|administrative|circulation|temperamental|consideration": "adMINistrative is stressed on the 2nd syllable, while circuLAtion, temperaMENtal and consideRAtion are stressed on the 3rd. So A is the odd one. Memory tip: most -ation and -ental words stress the syllable just before the ending; -ative words don't follow that rule.",
 "|Identify the word that has a DIFFERENT stress pattern from the others.|classroom|programme|brother|July": "JuLY is stressed on the 2nd syllable; CLASSroom, PROgramme and BROther are stressed on the 1st. So D is different. Memory tip: July is one of the few months stressed at the end (Ju-LY), unlike JAnuary and FEbruary.",
 "|Choose the word that does NOT have the same vowel sound as the others.|bale|plait|saint|gate": "Plait is said /plæt/ (like \"flat\"), while bale, saint and gate all have the /eɪ/ sound. So B is the odd one. Memory tip: plait is a spelling trap; it rhymes with \"flat\", not \"plate\".",
 "|Choose the word that does NOT have the same vowel sound as the others.|rice|fight|granite|die": "Granite ends in a short /ɪ/ (GRAN-it), while rice, fight and die have the /aɪ/ sound. So C is different. Memory tip: unstressed \"-ite\" at the end of longer words (granite, definite, favourite) is usually short /ɪt/.",
 "|Choose the word that does NOT have the same vowel sound as the others.|wind (moving air)|people|even|key": "Wind (moving air) has a short /ɪ/, while people, even and key all have a long /iː/. So A is the odd one. Memory tip: wind (air) has a short \"i\"; wind (turn a clock) has /aɪ/. The exam specifies which one.",
 "|Choose the word that does NOT have the same vowel sound as the others.|enjoy|log|toil|rejoice": "Log has the short /ɒ/, while enjoy, toil and rejoice have the /ɔɪ/ diphthong. So B is different. Memory tip: \"oi\" and \"oy\" spell /ɔɪ/ (boy, coin); a single \"o\" before a consonant is usually short /ɒ/.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: _____ told of his impending arrival, I worked hard to make his short stay very comfortable.|Have being|Having been|Having being|Have I been": "The sentence needs a perfect passive participle: \"Having been told of his arrival, I…\". So B is right. \"Having being\" and \"have being\" are never correct English, and \"Have I been\" makes a question. Memory tip: after \"having\", always use been, never being.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: The weather is probably _____ appealing for people who live in Jos _____ for foreigners.|no less / than|more / as|as much / as|at least as / but": "The comparison pattern is \"no less + adjective + than\", as in \"no less appealing for people who live in Jos than for foreigners\". So A is right. C is wrong: \"as much appealing as\" isn't English: \"as much\" goes with nouns. Memory tip: with adjectives, say \"as appealing as\", never \"as much appealing as\".",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: Ade tried to _____ all memory of his dead father.|recall|obliterate|eulogize|obstruct": "You obliterate (wipe out) all memory of someone, and \"tried to\" suggests he wanted to forget. So B is right. Recall (A) means remember, which clashes with \"all memory of\". Eulogize means to praise the dead, and obstruct means to block. Memory tip: obliterate = remove completely.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: This imposing edifice _____ a fortune to build.|had costed|costed|cost|have cost": "For prices, the past tense of \"cost\" is \"cost\": \"it cost a fortune\". So C is right. \"Costed\" only means \"worked out the cost of\" (the project was costed by an accountant). \"Have cost\" doesn't agree with the singular \"edifice\". Memory tip: cost, cost, cost for price.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: Three quarters of the hostel _____ been painted and three quarters of the students _____ moved in.|has / has|have / has|have / have|has / have": "With fractions, the verb agrees with the noun after \"of\". \"The hostel\" is singular, so \"has\"; \"the students\" is plural, so \"have\". That gives D. The other options ignore the \"of + noun\" rule. Memory tip: three quarters OF + singular = has; three quarters OF + plural = have.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: They let him go in _____ of his age.|respect|regard|disregard|consideration": "\"In respect of\" means \"with regard to\" or \"because of\", so they released him in respect of his age: A. \"In regard\" needs \"to\" (in regard to), and \"in consideration\" needs \"of\" in a different sense. \"Disregard\" reverses the meaning. Memory tip: learn it as a block, \"in respect of\".",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: _____ you come early to the new house, clean up my flat.|While|By chance|Should|Should in case": "\"Should you come early…\" is an inverted way of saying \"If you come early…\", so C is right. \"Should in case\" doubles up two conditional words, which is the trap. \"While\" and \"by chance\" don't make a proper condition here. Memory tip: should + subject at the start = if.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: These villagers _____ to grow rice.|used|are used|were used|use": "\"Used to + base verb\" describes a past habit: \"These villagers used to grow rice\". So A is right. \"Are used to\" and \"were used to\" mean \"accustomed to\" and need the -ing form (\"used to growing\"). \"Use to grow\" is wrong. Memory tip: used to DO = past habit; be used to DOING = accustomed to.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: When Ajike met her _____ husband at the party, she felt like reconciling with him.|strange|loving|caring|estranged": "\"Reconciling\" means making up after a separation, so the husband must be estranged (no longer close or living with her). So D is right. Loving and caring wouldn't need reconciling, and \"strange\" is a sound-alike trap. Memory tip: estranged = apart.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: He does not seem to know how to solve the problem, _____?|does not he|does he|does he not|doesn't he": "The statement is negative (\"does not seem\"), so the question tag is positive: \"does he?\". So B is right. A, C and D are all negative tags, which is wrong after a negative statement. Memory tip: negative statement, positive tag; positive statement, negative tag.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: Some scientists are trying to _____ human beings in their laboratories.|imitate|implicate|replicate|fabricate": "To replicate is to make an exact copy, the word used for cloning. So C is right. Imitate means to copy behaviour, implicate means to involve in a crime, and fabricate means to make up or manufacture. Memory tip: replicate = replica, an exact copy.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: A wide range of options _____ made available to the political parties during the recently concluded elections.|is|was|were|are": "\"A wide range of options\" refers to many options, so it takes a plural verb, and the past time (\"recently concluded\") needs past tense: \"were\". So C is right. \"Is\" and \"are\" are the wrong tense, and \"was\" treats \"range\" as singular. Memory tip: \"a range/number of + plural noun\" usually takes a plural verb.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: The meeting was called to _____ divergent views on the subject of a national conference.|re-present|reconstitute|reconcile|recommend": "Divergent (differing) views are reconciled, brought into agreement. So C is right. Re-present, reconstitute and recommend don't mean settling differences. Memory tip: you reconcile differences, accounts or people.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: The police came early enough to _____ the bomb planted by the rioters.|detonate|insulate|diffuse|defuse": "Making a bomb safe is to defuse it (remove the fuse), so D is right. Diffuse (spread out) is the classic sound-alike trap, detonate means to explode it, and insulate is about heat or electricity. Memory tip: de-FUSE = take out the fuse.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: The government which _____ recruiting _____ workers suddenly stopped doing so.|is / their|were / their|was / its|are / its": "\"The government\" here acts as one body, and \"stopped\" is past, so a singular past verb and singular pronoun are needed: \"was recruiting its workers\". So C is right. The other options mix singular and plural or use the wrong tense. Memory tip: keep the verb and the pronoun in agreement (was/its or were/their).",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: They had to _____ the generator when the electricity failed.|resort to|switch on|light up|fall back on": "\"Had to\" shows they were forced to turn to a backup. \"Resort to\" and \"fall back on\" both mean turning to an alternative when the usual option fails, so A and D are both accepted. \"Switch on\" only describes pressing the button, and you don't \"light up\" a generator. Memory tip: resort to / fall back on = turn to plan B.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: I do not think any sane person would have acted in such a _____ manner.|rational|cruel|secret|composed": "\"Any sane person would NOT have acted…\" means the manner must be something a sane person avoids, so cruel fits. B is right. Rational and composed describe how sane people do act, which is the opposite trap, and \"secret\" doesn't match \"sane\". Memory tip: read the negative carefully; it flips what fits.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: Neither Agbo nor his parents _____ the meetings now.|has attended|attend|attends|attended": "With neither…nor, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, \"his parents\" (plural), and \"now\" needs the present tense. So \"attend\": B. \"Attends\" agrees with Agbo, the farther subject. \"Has attended\" and \"attended\" are the wrong tense. Memory tip: nearer subject plus the time word decide the verb.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: Modern dancing has become rather scientific and so requires _____.|a rapping voice|bizarre costuming|immense instrumentation|some choreographic skill": "If dancing has become \"scientific\", it needs designed, planned movements, which is choreographic skill. So D is right. Voice, costumes and instruments aren't about the science of movement. Memory tip: choreography = the design of dance steps.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: Of course, we all saw the culprit _____ and hit the man.|approaching|approaches|approached|approach": "After \"see/watch/hear + someone\", English uses the base form of the verb, and it must match \"hit\": \"saw the culprit approach and hit the man\". So D is right. C is wrong: \"saw him approached\" isn't correct English. Memory tip: I saw him come in (not came).",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: The Company Director showed the contractor a _____ of the proposed office complex.|photocopy|photograph|prototype|microfilm": "A building that is only \"proposed\" doesn't exist yet, so it can only be shown as a model or prototype. So C is right. You can't photograph or photocopy something not yet built. A microfilm stores documents. Memory tip: proposed = not built yet = model.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: Had he considered his public image carefully, he _____ for his opponent in the election.|would have stood down|might have stood aside|should have stepped down|would have stepped aside": "\"Had he considered…\" is an inverted third conditional, which needs \"would have + past participle\". And you step aside FOR an opponent. So D is right. C is wrong: \"should have\" breaks the conditional pattern, and you step down FROM a post. Memory tip: had + subject… would have…",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: The editor was not happy that the Nigerian press was hemmed _____.|up|across|in|over": "\"Hemmed in\" means surrounded and restricted, so the press was hemmed in, its freedom limited. So C is right. \"Hemmed up/across/over\" aren't the idiom. Memory tip: hemmed IN = boxed in.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: More _____ to your elbow as you campaign for press freedom!|energy|power|effort|grease": "\"More power to your elbow!\" is a fixed English expression of encouragement, so B is right. C (effort) is wrong: \"more effort to your elbow\" isn't an English idiom. Memory tip: idioms can't be reworded; learn them whole.",
 "|Fill the gap with the most appropriate option: A child that shows mature characteristics at an early age may be described as _____.|precocious|ingenuous|premature|preconceived": "Precocious describes a child who develops abilities or maturity unusually early, so A is right. Premature means too early in general (a premature baby), not mature behaviour. Ingenuous means innocent, and preconceived means formed beforehand. Memory tip: precocious child = \"grown-up\" too soon.",
 "|Identify the word that has the stress on the FIRST syllable.|misread|resign|competent|compel": "COMpetent is stressed on the 1st syllable. MisREAD, reSIGN and comPEL are stressed on the 2nd. So C is right. Memory tip: two-syllable verbs usually stress the 2nd syllable; adjectives like COMpetent often stress the 1st.",
 "|Identify the word that has the stress on the FIRST syllable.|resist|intact|confirm|salon": "SAlon is stressed on the 1st syllable. ReSIST, inTACT and conFIRM are stressed on the 2nd. So D is right. Memory tip: borrowed nouns like salon, ballet and café are tricky; learn them individually.",
 "|Choose the word that has the same consonant sound as the letter 't' in PAST.|pasture|castle|wrestle|preached": "The \"t\" in past is /t/, and the \"-ed\" in preached is also said /t/ (preacht). So D is right. Pasture has /tʃ/, and the \"t\" in castle and wrestle is silent. Memory tip: \"-ed\" after a voiceless sound (ch, k, p, s) is pronounced /t/.",
 "|Choose the word that has the same consonant sound as the letters 'ch' in CHURCH.|feature|chauffeur|ocean|machine": "The \"ch\" in church is /tʃ/, the same sound as the \"t\" in feature (fee-chur). So A is right. Chauffeur, ocean and machine all have /ʃ/ (sh), which is the spelling trap. Memory tip: French-origin \"ch\" (chef, machine) is usually \"sh\".",
 "|Choose the word that has the same consonant sound as the letter 's' in SURE.|charlatan|church|cheer|cheap": "The \"s\" in sure is /ʃ/ (sh), the same as the \"ch\" in charlatan, a French-origin word. So A is right. Church, cheer and cheap all have /tʃ/. Memory tip: sure, sugar and charlatan all start with \"sh\".",
 "|Choose the word that has the same consonant sound as the letters 'th' in THANK.|Thomas|though|Thames|thought": "The \"th\" in thank is the voiceless /θ/, as in thought, so D is right. Though has the voiced /ð/. In Thomas and Thames the \"th\" is just /t/, the trap. Memory tip: names like Thomas and Thames (and thyme) use a plain \"t\" sound.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Amina's performance in the examination SURPASSED MY EXPECTATIONS.|amazed everyone|lacked merit|was extraordinarily good|was disappointing": "To surpass expectations is to do better than expected; the opposite is to fall short, to disappoint. So D is right. A and C are near-synonyms (the trap), and B is negative but vague. Memory tip: in opposite questions, cross out the synonyms first.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: As these boys never act in public, the police are now worried about their COVERT activities.|evil|open|cryptic|ignoble": "Covert means hidden or secret; the opposite is open (overt). So B is right. Cryptic is close to a synonym, and evil and ignoble are about morality, not openness. Memory tip: covert vs overt.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The town was all AGOG at his unexpected return.|unexcited|excited|on fire|surprised": "Agog means very excited and eager, so the opposite is unexcited: A. B (excited) is the synonym trap. Memory tip: everyone is agog at big news.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Some equatorial areas have a SULTRY climate.|a hot|an inclement|a temperate|a stable": "Sultry weather is hot and humid. Inclement weather is cold, wet or stormy, the opposite extreme. So B is right. A is a synonym. Temperate is only mild, and stable doesn't relate to heat. Memory tip: inclement = unpleasant cold, wet weather.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The journalist's write-up contained a PLETHORA of detail.|simplicity|shortage|complexity|spectrum": "A plethora is an excess or overabundance, so the opposite is a shortage: B. Spectrum and complexity don't measure amount, and simplicity is about difficulty. Memory tip: plethora = plenty, too much.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: In the latter part of his life, the famous soldier showed signs of YOUTHFULNESS.|bravery|vitality|energy|senility": "Youthfulness is the vigour of youth; senility is the physical and mental decline of old age. So D is the opposite. Bravery, vitality and energy are positives, and vitality and energy are near-synonyms. Memory tip: senile = senior decline.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: It is INCONCEIVABLE that the rat devoured the cat.|contestable|credible|incomprehensible|unimaginable": "Inconceivable means impossible to believe, so the opposite is credible (believable): B. C and D are synonyms (the trap). Contestable means open to argument. Memory tip: in-conceivable = can't be imagined.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Ali wondered why the principal was AMBIVALENT about the students' future.|inconsiderate|decisive|anxious|ambitious": "Ambivalent means having mixed feelings and unable to decide; the opposite is decisive. So B is right. Anxious and ambitious are sound-alike distractors. Memory tip: ambi- = both ways, so torn between two.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The teacher taught the RUDIMENTS of Chemistry to the first grade.|elements|fundamentals|basics|theories": "Rudiments are the basics of a subject. Elements, fundamentals and basics are all synonyms, so the only option pointing away from the basics is theories (advanced, abstract knowledge). So D is right. Memory tip: rudimentary = elementary.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: In his NAIVETY, he believed all the stories his friend told him.|artlessness|friendliness|incredulity|stupidity": "Naivety is a readiness to believe things too easily; incredulity is an unwillingness to believe. So C is the opposite. Artlessness is a synonym, and stupidity is a harsher near-synonym. Memory tip: incredulous = \"I can't believe it!\"",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Updating the Board's brochure is an ARDUOUS task.|a regular|an annual|a difficult|an easy": "Arduous means difficult and demanding, so the opposite is easy: D. C (difficult) is the synonym trap, and regular and annual are about frequency. Memory tip: arduous = hard labour.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The lecture seemed INTERMINABLE.|brief|interesting|boring|unending": "Interminable means seemingly endless, so the opposite is brief: A. D (unending) is the synonym trap, and boring describes it but isn't an opposite. Memory tip: in-terminable = no terminus, no end.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Had I known about their plan much earlier, I would have NIPPED IT IN THE BUD.|promoted it|squashed it|initiated it|stopped it": "To nip something in the bud is to stop it early. The opposite is to let it grow and encourage it, to promote it. So A is right. C is wrong: it was their plan and already existed, so the speaker couldn't have \"initiated\" it. B and D are synonyms. Memory tip: put each option back into the sentence.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: What a HARMLESS thought he has!|pleasant|perfect|pernicious|pertinent": "Pernicious means harmful, especially in a gradual or hidden way, the opposite of harmless. So C is right. Pleasant, perfect and pertinent are sound-alike distractors that start with \"per-/ple-\". Memory tip: pernicious = poisonous.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: There is much APATHY towards reading among students nowadays.|indecision|enthusiasm|indifference|disinclination": "Apathy is a lack of interest or enthusiasm, so the opposite is enthusiasm: B. Indifference and disinclination are synonyms (the trap), and indecision is unrelated. Memory tip: a-pathy = without feeling.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: YOUR sister should come with us tomorrow.|Should your brother come with us tomorrow?|Should our brother come with us tomorrow?|Should Ado's sister come with us tomorrow?|Should my sister come with us tomorrow?": "Stressing YOUR corrects whose sister is meant. It answers \"Should MY sister come…?\", so D is right. A and B change the person (brother), and C would put the stress on the name. Memory tip: the stressed word is what's being corrected; find the question that gets it wrong.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: They FLEW to Abuja.|Where did they fly to?|Did they go to Abuja by road?|How will they get to Abuja?|Did they fly to Jos?": "Stressing FLEW corrects how they travelled, so it answers \"Did they go to Abuja by road?\": B. D would put the stress on the place, A asks where, and C is in the future. Memory tip: emphatic stress corrects one detail; the question must have that one detail wrong.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: My neighbour BRUISED his thigh while playing football.|Did your neighbour fracture his thigh while playing football?|Did your neighbour break his leg while playing tennis?|Did your neighbour play football yesterday?|Was your neighbour involved in an accident?": "Stressing BRUISED corrects only the type of injury, so it answers \"Did your neighbour fracture his thigh while playing football?\": A. B changes several details at once, and C and D ask something else. Memory tip: the right question differs from the statement in exactly the stressed word.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Adawo is an imp.|Adawo behaves badly.|Adawo behaves decently.|Adawo behaves differently.|Adawo behaves queerly.": "An imp is a mischievous child or person, someone who misbehaves, so A is right. Behaving decently is the opposite. \"Differently\" and \"queerly\" are vague and miss the mischief. Memory tip: impish = naughty.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The solution lies in choosing between various negative alternatives.|The solution lies in choosing between the positive and the negative.|The solutions are many.|The solution can be found in one of the negative options.|The solution is a negative one.": "All the alternatives are negative, and the answer is to pick one of them, so the solution is found in one of the negative options: C. A brings in a positive choice that isn't there. B (many solutions) and D (the solution is negative) misread it. Memory tip: paraphrase questions reward the most literal restatement.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: You must not attend the end-of-year party.|You do not have to decide whether to attend the party or not.|You have to decide whether to attend the party or not.|It is not necessary that you attend the party.|It is necessary that you attend the party.": "\"Must not\" means you are forbidden to go. None of the options says that. C (\"not necessary\") is the answer key's choice and the closest, but it softens a ban into \"you needn't go\". Memory tip: must not = forbidden; need not = not necessary.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The man reasoned that there ought to be a limit to sycophancy.|Sycophants need not talk all the time.|There can be no favour beyond a reasonable point.|Sycophants should know when to grant people's request.|People should know when not to use flattery.": "Sycophancy is flattering people to gain favour, so \"a limit to sycophancy\" means people should know when to stop flattering: D. A, B and C misread the word. Memory tip: sycophant = flatterer.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Oche's chief idiosyncrasy is a passion for pounded yam.|Oche hates pounded yam.|Oche's chief hates pounded yam.|Oche's chief likes pounded yam.|Oche has a penchant for pounded yam.": "An idiosyncrasy is a personal peculiarity, and \"chief\" here means main, not a village chief. So his main peculiarity is a strong liking (penchant) for pounded yam: D. B and C fall for the \"chief\" trap, and A reverses passion. Memory tip: \"chief\" as an adjective means main.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The professor discussed a number of ABSTRUSE topics.|irrelevant|useful|esoteric|relevant": "Abstruse means hard to understand; esoteric means understood only by a few specialists. So C is right. Irrelevant, useful and relevant are about importance, not difficulty. Memory tip: abstruse = obscure.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Tade became TIMOROUS when she was asked to give the valedictory speech.|excited|aggressive|nervous|happy": "Timorous means fearful and nervous, and being asked to give the farewell speech made her nervous. So C is right. Excited, aggressive and happy are not fear words. Memory tip: timorous comes from the same root as timid.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The men were TARDY in offering help.|generous|quick|brave|slow": "Tardy means slow or late, so D is right. Quick is the opposite (the trap). Generous and brave are unrelated. Memory tip: tardy = late for school.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Ugo has often been described as BELLIGERENT.|attractive|combative|innocent|patient": "Belligerent means hostile and eager to fight, so combative is right: B. Patient and innocent point the other way. Memory tip: bellum is Latin for war.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The COALESCENCE of the groups created additional problems.|union|fighting|proscription|disbandment": "Coalescence is the coming together of separate parts into one, a union. So A is right. Fighting, proscription (banning) and disbandment are about conflict or separation, the opposite. Memory tip: coalesce = come together, like a coalition.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Funmi is just being FACETIOUS about her marrying a soldier.|unfaithful|serious|crazy|unserious": "Being facetious means joking about something serious, so she's being unserious: D. B (serious) is the opposite. Unfaithful and crazy aren't the meaning. Memory tip: facetious = flippant.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The village girl wore SUMPTUOUS clothes.|loose-fitting|cheap|faded-looking|expensive": "Sumptuous means splendid and costly-looking, so expensive is right: D. Cheap and faded-looking are opposites, and loose-fitting is about fit. Memory tip: a sumptuous feast is lavish.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Any chief executive of an organization would find RADICAL CHANGES blocked at every turn.|ideas|innovations|developments|suggestions": "Radical changes are new, fundamental changes to how things are done, which is innovation. So B is right. Ideas and suggestions haven't been put into practice, and developments is too general. Memory tip: radical = to the root, so a new way of doing things.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: I cannot understand why Ali should serve in that MORIBUND administration.|crumbling|purposeless|prodigal|oppressive": "Moribund means near death or in terminal decline, so a crumbling administration fits: A. Purposeless, prodigal (wasteful) and oppressive describe other faults. Memory tip: mori- = death (mortal).",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Bose was angry because her friend called her a PILFERER.|liar|hypocrite|criminal|thief": "A pilferer steals small things, a petty thief, so D is right. Criminal is too general, and liar and hypocrite are other faults. Memory tip: pilfer = steal little bits.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: While the hooligans exchanged blows, we looked on COMPLACENTLY.|questioningly|sorrowfully|dejectedly|contentedly": "Complacently means in a self-satisfied, unbothered way, so contentedly is right: D. Questioningly, sorrowfully and dejectedly all show concern or sadness. Memory tip: complacent = comfortably unconcerned.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The player kept on GAMELY to the end of the match.|courageously|skillfully|amateurishly|stubbornly": "Gamely means bravely and willingly, despite difficulty, so courageously is right: A. Stubbornly is close but negative, and skilfully and amateurishly are about ability. Memory tip: \"game\" = willing and up for it.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Art lies in cherishing the initiative and CREATIVE POWER of each person.|gift|strength|potential|mind": "\"Creative power\" here is a person's capacity to create, their potential. So C is right. Gift and strength are narrower, and mind is too general. Memory tip: power to do = potential.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: As the triumphant rebels were returning to their base, they met with a SERIOUS REVERSE.|an enemy|an army|a victory|a defeat": "In war, a reverse is a setback, so a serious reverse for the rebels is a defeat: D. C (victory) is the opposite. Enemy and army are what they might meet but aren't the meaning. Memory tip: a reversal of fortune is a setback.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The Conference Centre caters for TRANSIENTS only.|permanent guests|professionals|novices|temporary guests": "Transients are people who stay only a short time, so temporary guests is right: D. Permanent guests is the opposite. Professionals and novices are about skill. Memory tip: transient = passing through.",
 "eng-2002-p1|The expression 'we may well be asked' as used in the passage means|it is quite likely that we will be asked|we cannot escape being asked|the listener is always justified to ask questions|it is inconceivable that we will be asked": "\"May well\" means \"quite possibly\", so \"we may well be asked to define it\" means it is quite likely someone will ask us: A. B is too strong (\"cannot escape\"), C talks about the listener's rights, and D says the opposite. Memory tip: \"may well\" = very possibly; \"may as well\" = there's no reason not to.",
 "eng-2002-p1|From the passage, which of these is a disadvantage of defining by enumeration?|The words or objects listed may not all share similar characteristics.|Enumeration may not be all-inclusive.|It always leaves doubts in the mind of the speaker.|Many important members of the group may be left out of the enumeration.": "The passage says that when we define \"animal\" only by examples, \"our hearers might be doubtful whether a sea anemone or a slug was to be included\". The examples can't cover every member of the class, so the list is not all-inclusive: B. The key gives C, but C puts the doubt in the speaker's mind, and the passage puts it in the hearers'. D is close, but the passage's worry is about borderline members, not \"important\" ones. A is never mentioned. Memory tip: in \"disadvantage\" questions, find the sentence that names the problem and match it exactly, including who is affected.",
 "eng-2002-p1|One of these summarizes the approaches to definition discussed in the passage.|Specifying clearly what distinguishes a particular word or object from its traditional groups.|Indicating the class of a word and mentioning its general property with examples.|Giving general and specific features followed by examples of what the object or word includes.|Mentioning the class of a word or object with its specific property.": "The passage describes two approaches: naming the class plus the property that sets the thing apart (general and specific features), and listing examples (enumeration). C names both. B mixes them up (\"general property\"), and A and D give only the first approach. Memory tip: a summary question wants the option that covers every part of the passage, not just one paragraph.",
 "eng-2002-p1|The writer uses the expression 'fixing the meaning of the words we use' to mean|getting even with the people who always ask for definitions|using definitions to help build up their vocabulary|repairing the damage done by inadequate definition during communication|using definitions to help people communicate their thoughts and argue logically": "The passage opens: if our thought is to be clear and we want to communicate it, we must fix the meaning of our words. Fixing meaning means settling what words mean through definition so that we communicate and reason clearly: D. A, B and C pick up stray ideas (people who ask, vocabulary, repairing damage) that the passage never links to the phrase. Memory tip: read the sentence the expression sits in; its purpose is usually stated right there.",
 "eng-2002-p1|Which of the following statements can be deduced from the passage?|There are both traditional and modern methods of defining words.|Definitions aid communication when enumeration is accurately handled.|Every speaker or writer has a method of defining words.|Accurate definition is invaluable to communication but difficult to achieve.": "The writer says clear communication depends on fixing meanings, and then shows ways definitions fail (enumeration is \"of limited usefulness\"; a definition can give the wrong distinguishing mark). So accurate definition is invaluable but hard: D. A invents \"modern\" methods, B ties success to enumeration, which the writer calls limited, and C claims every speaker has a method. Memory tip: \"deduced\" means it follows from what the passage says, not from general knowledge.",
 "eng-2002-p2|An appropriate title for this passage is|The Advantages of a Deregulated Economy|Highlighting the Dangers of Deregulation|Making a Case for Deregulation|The problems of the NNPC": "The writer answers the fears about deregulation and ends with \"This appears to be the sense in deregulation\", so the passage argues for it: C. A is narrower (it isn't listing advantages of a whole deregulated economy), B is the opposite view, and the NNPC (D) is only one example. Memory tip: the last sentence of an argumentative passage often reveals the writer's purpose.",
 "eng-2002-p2|Which of the following statements is true according to the passage?|The opposition to the deregulation of the oil industry is not unanimous.|The Nigeria Labour Congress has been able to prevent the Government from deregulating the economy.|The introduction of entrepreneurs to the oil industry will make life easy only for a few Nigerians.|A better life for all Nigerians is conditional upon the deregulation of the economy.": "The passage says \"many Nigerians\" (not all) are resentful, while the \"apostles of deregulation\" support it, so the opposition is not unanimous: A. The NLC only threatened (B is false), C is never claimed, and D overstates the writer's argument. Memory tip: words like \"many\" and \"some\" signal that a view is not shared by everyone.",
 "eng-2002-p2|The writer seems to suggest that|Nigerians do not understand the sense in deregulation|a deregulated economy will cause the Government to be indifferent to the plight of ordinary Nigerians|only a heartless cartel will benefit by the deregulation of the oil industry|the anxiety caused by the deregulation question is groundless": "The writer says nothing from official quarters suggests the Government will give up control, and that deregulation has already made life easier in other sectors. In other words, the fears are unfounded: D. B and C are the fears he rejects, and A is never said. Memory tip: when a writer answers objections one by one, he is suggesting those objections are groundless.",
 "eng-2002-p2|Which of the following conclusions can be reached from the passage?|Nigerians cannot buy fuel at exorbitant prices.|The deregulation of the oil industry does not preclude the Government from exerting its influence.|The deregulation of the economy will solve all the problems of petroleum products distribution.|The Government is hell-bent on leaving Nigerians at the mercy of a heartless cartel.": "The passage says \"the absence of direct control does not mean that it will surrender all its rights\", and the Government will continue to fix maximum prices. So deregulation does not stop the Government exerting influence: B. C overstates (\"all the problems\"), and D contradicts the passage. Memory tip: \"does not preclude\" means \"does not rule out\".",
 "eng-2002-p2|Which of these correctly summarizes the arguments adduced by the advocates of deregulation?|Deregulating the economy will make the NNPC more efficient and less wasteful.|Competition in the oil industry will be beneficial to several Nigerians.|Competition should be allowed in the production and distribution of petroleum products.|The Government should deregulate every aspect of the Nigerian economy.": "The advocates argue that breaking the NNPC monopoly brings competition, which cuts waste and corruption and gives \"reasonable prices\", so competition will benefit Nigerians: B. A is wrong because deregulation means bringing in other firms, not reforming the NNPC. C narrows the argument to a demand, and D (\"every aspect\") goes beyond it. Memory tip: summary options that say \"all\" or \"every\" usually overreach.",
 "eng-2002-p3|One argument given in support of personal writing is that it|has informal features which make it more diverting than impersonal writing|makes writers more focused and less boring|can be more objective than impersonal writing|is the style to use in all situations involving businessmen": "Supporters of personal writing say it is \"more forceful and direct\" and, being \"more like conversation\", \"more interesting\". The key's B (more focused and less boring) matches that, and A (informal, more diverting) says the same thing in other words, so both are accepted. C reverses the passage's point about objectivity, and D (\"all situations\") goes against the writer's conclusion. Memory tip: when two options paraphrase the same sentence, both can be right.",
 "eng-2002-p3|Which of the following statements is true according to the passage?|The impersonal style helps to achieve a measure of objectivity in report writing.|The impersonal style has been widely acclaimed.|Most report writing is characterized by the sparing use of the impersonal style.|Most writers use the impersonal style to achieve objectivity in their reports.": "The early report writers \"reasoned that objectivity is best attained\" by taking the human being out of the writing, so the impersonal style helps achieve a measure of objectivity: A. B and C are never said, and D overstates it (\"most writers ... to achieve\"). Memory tip: \"a measure of\" = some, a hedge that often signals the safe, true option.",
 "eng-2002-p3|According to the passage, most of the writing in newspapers, news magazines and journals is|personal and uninteresting|personal and interesting|impersonal and interesting|impersonal and uninteresting": "The writer says of newspapers, news magazines and journals: \"Most of this writing is impersonal — and usually it is not dull.\" Impersonal and interesting: C. Memory tip: in two-part options, check each half separately against the passage.",
 "eng-2002-p3|From the passage, what determines the appropriateness of a style is the|theme|reader|writer|situation": "The writer ends: \"His decision should be based on the circumstances of each report situation\", so the situation decides: D. The reader's wishes (B) are only one of the things to consider within the situation. Memory tip: look for the \"should be based on\" sentence; it states the deciding factor.",
 "eng-2002-p3|Which of the following best describes the writer of the passage?|He recognizes the need to be critical of report writing.|He cannot be said to be objective.|He is being unnecessarily analytic.|He is completely non-committal.": "The writer weighs both sides, rejects one argument as lacking substance, and then gives clear guidance, so he takes a critical, balanced look at report writing: A. He is not non-committal (D), because he does advise the writer, and nothing suggests he is biased (B). Memory tip: a writer who weighs arguments and then advises is critical and balanced, not neutral.",
 "eng-2002-p4|Gap 16: …to find, if he is able, the common _____ which underlie and account for the facts that he knows.|approaches|instruments|factors|experiments": "The scientist looks for what \"underlie[s] and account[s] for the facts\", meaning their causes or factors: C. A is wrong: approaches (A) and instruments (B) are tools of the scientist, not things beneath the facts, and experiments (D) are how he tests. Memory tip: in cloze, read the words right after the gap; \"which underlie\" needs something that can sit beneath facts.",
 "eng-2002-p4|Gap 17: He chooses, when he can, the method of the controlled _____.|experiment|respondent|data|system": "The rest of the passage describes changing one thing (light) while keeping everything else the same. That is the controlled experiment: A. A respondent answers a survey, data is what you collect, and a system is too vague. Memory tip: \"controlled experiment\" is a fixed scientific phrase.",
 "eng-2002-p4|Gap 18: …all the time keeping all other _____ (temperature, moisture, nourishment) the same.|procedures|conditions|studies|objects": "Temperature, moisture and nourishment are the conditions plants grow in: B. Procedures are steps, studies are pieces of research, and objects are things. Memory tip: the words in brackets after a gap are examples of the answer.",
 "eng-2002-p4|Gap 19: In this way, by keeping other variables _____ and by varying the light only…|dark|constant|good|natural": "The sentence contrasts \"keeping other variables ___\" with \"varying the light only\", so the variables must stay unchanged, or constant: B. Dark (A) was one of the light settings, and good and natural don't contrast with \"varying\". Memory tip: variables are either varied or kept constant.",
 "eng-2002-p4|Gap 20: This _____ of using 'controls' can be applied to a variety of situations.|method|research|tool|rationale": "The passage has already called this \"the method of the controlled experiment\", so \"this method of using controls\": A. Research is the whole activity, a tool is an instrument, and a rationale is a reason. Memory tip: cloze passages often repeat a word used earlier, so look back.",
 "eng-2002-p4|Gap 21: …'must moisture be present _____ is to rust?'|bar|gold|iron|an alloy": "Rust forms on iron, so the question is whether moisture must be present for iron to rust: C. Gold doesn't rust, and \"bar\" and \"an alloy\" are not the metal that rusts. Memory tip: only iron (and steel, which contains iron) rusts; other metals corrode.",
 "eng-2002-p4|Gap 22: …'which variety of beans gives the greatest yield in one _____?'|period|season|climate|weather": "Bean yields are measured per growing season: B. A period is too vague, and climate and weather describe conditions, not a stretch of farming time. Memory tip: farmers speak of a harvest per season.",
 "eng-2002-p4|Gap 23: In the course of his _____, the scientist may find what he thinks is one common explanation for an increasing number of facts.|inquiries|findings|queries|experiment": "The passage is about how a scientist builds knowledge through experiments, and \"in the course of his experiment(s)\" he notices a common explanation: D. Inquiries (A) could also make sense, but findings (B) are results, not something you go through, and queries (C) are single questions. Memory tip: \"in the course of\" needs an activity that takes time.",
 "eng-2002-p4|Gap 24: The explanation, if it seems consistently to fit the various facts, is called _____.|a principle|a thesis|a hypothesis|an antithesis": "An explanation that seems to fit the facts but is still being tested is a hypothesis: C. A principle and a law are established truths, a thesis is an argued claim, and an antithesis is its opposite. Memory tip: hypothesis → tested again and again → law.",
 "eng-2002-p4|Gap 25: If this continues to stand the test of numerous experiments and remains unshaken, it becomes a _____.|notion|deduction|law|thesis": "A hypothesis that \"continues to stand the test of numerous experiments and remains unshaken\" becomes a law: C. A notion is a vague idea, a deduction is a conclusion reasoned out, and a thesis is a claim to be argued. Memory tip: scientific laws (e.g. Newton's laws) are explanations that survived repeated testing.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): _____ any problems, I shall travel to London tomorrow on a business trip.|In spite of|Given|Barring|In case of": "\"Barring any problems\" means \"unless there are problems\": C. \"In spite of any problems\" would mean the problems will happen and he'll travel anyway. \"Given any problems\" makes no sense, and \"in case of any problems\" introduces a plan for if they happen, not a condition for going. Memory tip: barring = except for / unless there is.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Musa has lived here for five years but he does not seem able to get over the enormously _____ and elaborate rules that govern the society.|bizarre|intricate|absurd|fantastic": "Musa can't \"get over\" (get used to) the rules because they are complicated and detailed, so intricate: B. Bizarre, absurd and fantastic describe rules that are strange or unbelievable, which doesn't pair with \"elaborate\". Memory tip: intricate and elaborate both mean having many detailed parts.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The match gave the team a chance to show their _____.|mettle|worth|position|prowess": "\"To show one's mettle\" is an idiom meaning to prove one's courage and ability under pressure: A. Prowess (D) is skill, but the idiom with \"show their\" in a test situation is mettle. Worth and position don't fit. Memory tip: to be \"on one's mettle\" is to be ready to do one's best.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): These _____ must have _____ the World Bank officials.|analysis/attracted|analyses/attract|analyses/attracted|analysis/attract": "\"These\" is plural, so we need \"analyses\" (singular \"analysis\"). After \"must have\" comes the past participle \"attracted\": C. A and D use the singular, and B uses the base form \"attract\". Memory tip: words ending in -sis form their plural with -ses (crisis/crises, thesis/theses).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The highly appreciative audience clapped _____ hands and showered _____ on the lecturer.|its/encomiums|its/invectives|their/encomiums|their/invectives": "Each member of the audience clapped their own hands, so \"their\". An appreciative audience gives praise, which is encomiums: C. Invectives (B, D) are insults, and \"its hands\" (A) treats the audience as one body with one pair of hands. Memory tip: encomium = high praise; invective = abuse.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The members elected Baba _____ of the committee.|to be a chairman|chairman|their chairman|to become chairman": "When a group elects someone to a unique post, the title takes no article: \"They elected Baba chairman\": B. \"A chairman\" and \"to become\" are wrong, and \"their chairman\" changes the structure. Memory tip: elect/appoint/make + person + title with no article (they made him captain).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The officer described the comment as _____ because it was very amusing.|dangerous|sad|ludicrous|libellous": "\"Because it was very amusing\" points to something laughable, so ludicrous: C. Dangerous, sad and libellous don't explain amusement. Memory tip: the reason clause after \"because\" usually tells you the answer.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The new manager was responsible for the _____ of the organization.|uplifting|lifting|uplift|upliftment": "The standard English noun is \"uplift\", meaning improvement: C. \"Upliftment\" (D) is common in Nigerian English but not standard, and \"uplifting\" and \"lifting\" are verb forms. Memory tip: JAMB tests standard British English, so drop the extra \"-ment\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): He is _____ Kaduna _____ an official assignment.|at/in|at/for|in/on|for/in": "We are \"in\" a town or city, and \"on\" a trip, errand, mission or assignment: C. Memory tip: on duty, on assignment, on a journey, on holiday.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The future of the company is _____ though many seem _____ about it.|promising/nostalgic|dicey/unsatisfied|okay/optimistic|rosy/pessimistic": "\"Though\" introduces a contrast: the future is rosy (bright), though many are pessimistic (expect the worst): D. A and C give no contrast, and \"dicey/unsatisfied\" doesn't contrast either. Memory tip: though/although/but need two halves that pull against each other.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The army officer said that more _____ would be needed to prosecute the war.|geurrilas|guerrilas|geurrillas|guerrillas": "The correct spelling is guerrillas: g-u-e-r-r-i-l-l-a-s (gu at the start, double r, double l): D. Memory tip: think \"gue\" as in guest, then \"rr\" and \"ll\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The quality of your questions which _____ always attracted attention _____ never been in doubt.|has/have|have/have|has/has|have/has": "\"Which\" refers back to \"questions\" (plural), so \"which have always attracted attention\". The main subject is \"the quality\" (singular), so \"has never been in doubt\": D. Memory tip: in long sentences, find the real subject of each verb, not the nearest noun.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Despite all preparations, the wedding did not _____.|come on|come up|come along|come off": "\"To come off\" means to take place or succeed as planned, so \"the wedding did not come off\": D. B is wrong: \"come up\" means to arise or be mentioned (\"the matter came up at the meeting\"), which doesn't fit. \"Come on\" and \"come along\" mean to progress. Memory tip: events \"come off\" (happen); topics \"come up\" (are mentioned).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Didn't _____ draw your attention to the entry requirements?|somebody|someone|anyone|everyone": "In negative questions we normally use \"any-\" words: \"Didn't anyone draw your attention...?\": C. \"Somebody\" and \"someone\" are possible when you expect a yes, but the standard, tested form is \"anyone\". \"Everyone\" changes the meaning. Memory tip: negatives and questions take any-; positive statements take some-.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): You are free to solicit _____ support from the Board.|financial|by financial|for financial|a financial": "\"Solicit\" is followed directly by its object: solicit financial support: A. \"By\" and \"for\" add an unwanted preposition, and \"support\" is uncountable here, so no \"a\". Memory tip: you solicit help, solicit votes, solicit support; no preposition.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): As Election Day draws near, the party looks _____ because many of its members have _____ other parties.|cheerless/cross carpeted from|cheerful/moved to|vulnerable/defected to|successful/declared for": "Members leaving makes the party weak or vulnerable, and members \"defect to\" another party: C. A is wrong because \"cross carpeted from other parties\" would mean they joined this party. B and D make the party look strong. Memory tip: defect to = leave and join (the other side).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Ali has been cured of his amnesia; he no longer suffers from _____.|rounds of anxiety|bouts of malaria|pains in his arms|loss of memory": "Amnesia means loss of memory: D. The other options are other illnesses or pains. Memory tip: amnesia → a-mnesia → \"no memory\" (as in amnesty, a \"forgetting\" of offences).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): By this time next year, I _____ twenty years old.|will have been|would have been|could have been|will be": "For a future fact, use \"will be\": \"By this time next year, I will be twenty years old\": D. \"Will have been\" (A) needs something that lasts, as in \"will have been here a year\". \"Would have been\" and \"could have been\" talk about things that did not happen. Memory tip: age in the future = will be.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The operations of the bank _____ not _____ cottage and small-scale industries.|do/cover|did/covered|does/cover|does/covered": "The subject is \"operations\" (plural), so \"do not\". After do/does/did, the main verb takes its base form (\"cover\"): A. B is past and wrongly uses \"covered\", and C and D use singular \"does\". Memory tip: do + not + base verb (do not cover, does not cover, did not cover).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): We did not understand what the magistrate said about _____ and battery.|assault|touching|acid|insult": "\"Assault and battery\" is a fixed legal phrase: assault is the threat of attack, and battery is the attack itself: A. Memory tip: in law, battery = unlawful physical contact.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Whenever we have _____, we feel better.|together a good long talk|a long good talk together|together a long good talk|a good long talk together": "English orders adjectives as opinion, then size/length (good long talk), and an adverb like \"together\" goes after the noun phrase: D. Memory tip: \"a good long talk\", \"a nice big house\": opinion comes before size.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The members of the other team agree _____ all the terms of the contract.|on|by|to|with": "We agree \"to\" terms, proposals and conditions: C. We agree \"with\" a person or an opinion, and \"on\" a decision we reach together. Memory tip: agree TO a plan, WITH a person, ON a point.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): One advantage of the English language in Nigeria is that it puts everyone _____ a common _____.|in/advantage|in/standing|on/footing|at/equality": "The idiom is \"on an equal (common) footing\", meaning on the same level: C. Memory tip: footing = position or basis (on a firm footing, on a war footing).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): If one perseveres, _____ will surely succeed.|she|they|one|he": "Once a sentence begins with the general pronoun \"one\", it continues with \"one\": \"If one perseveres, one will succeed\": C. Switching to he/she/they is a shift in pronoun. Memory tip: one... one... one's.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): _____ a motor can be harmful _____ your health.|To ride/with|To ride/for|Riding/to|Riding/for": "The gerund \"Riding\" is the natural subject of a general statement, and \"harmful to\" is the correct collocation: C. \"To ride... can be harmful\" is stiff, and \"harmful with/for\" is wrong. Memory tip: harmful to, good for, bad for.",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT vowel sound from the others.|mere|weird|hear|tear (verb)": "Tear (verb, to rip) is pronounced /teə/, rhyming with \"air\". Mere, weird and hear all have /ɪə/, rhyming with \"ear\": D. Memory tip: tear (crying) = ear sound; tear (ripping) = air sound.",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT vowel sound from the others.|does|son|flood|world": "World has the long /ɜː/ vowel, as in \"bird\". Does, son and flood all have the short /ʌ/ of \"cup\": D. Memory tip: \"or\" after w often sounds like \"er\" (word, work, world, worm).",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT vowel sound from the others.|cough|rough|rot|dog": "Rough is /rʌf/, rhyming with \"stuff\", so it has /ʌ/. Cough, rot and dog all have the short /ɒ/ of \"hot\": B. D (dog) is wrong: dog has the same vowel as cough and rot. Memory tip: -ough has many sounds (rough /ʌf/, cough /ɒf/, though /əʊ/, through /uː/), so check each word.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: preachED|lotion|castle|question|past": "After a voiceless sound like /tʃ/ in \"preach\", -ed is pronounced /t/: \"preached\" ends in /t/. Past has a clear /t/: D. In castle the t is silent, in lotion \"ti\" is /ʃ/, and in question \"ti\" is /tʃ/. Memory tip: -ed = /t/ after voiceless sounds (p, k, s, sh, ch), /d/ after voiced ones, /ɪd/ after t and d.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: buSiness|eyes|grace|rice|sink": "The s in \"business\" is pronounced /z/ (BIZ-nis). The s in \"eyes\" is also /z/: A. Grace, rice and sink have /s/. Memory tip: plural -s after a vowel sound is /z/ (eyes, days, toys).",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: couGH|thoroughly|of|trophy|though": "The gh in \"cough\" is /f/, just like the ph in \"trophy\": C. Gh is silent in thoroughly and though, and the f in \"of\" is a /v/ sound. Memory tip: gh = /f/ in cough, rough, laugh, enough.",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT stress pattern from the others.|convenient|contribute|embarrass|madam": "MAdam is stressed on the first syllable; conVEnient, conTRIbute and emBARrass are stressed on the second: D. Memory tip: say each word aloud and tap the beat; the odd one out is the one whose strong beat moves.",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT stress pattern from the others.|suffer|blackboard|success|calendar": "SucCESS is stressed on the second syllable; SUFfer, BLACKboard and CAlendar on the first: C. Memory tip: compound nouns like blackboard are stressed on the first part.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: I wonder what will be left of his essay when the EXTRANEOUS material is deleted.|main|relevant|erroneous|superfluous": "Extraneous means not belonging or not relevant, so extra and unnecessary, i.e. superfluous: D. Relevant (B) is the opposite, main (A) is unrelated, and erroneous (C) means wrong. Memory tip: extraneous → \"extra\", outside what is needed.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Tortoises need cool climates and must have places where they can HIBERNATE.|sleep deeply|relax|reproduce|hide at night": "To hibernate is to spend the cold season in a deep sleep: A. Relax, reproduce and hide at night are not the same thing. Memory tip: hibernate is linked to winter (Latin \"hibernus\" = wintry).",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: They are considered to be legal LUMINARIES.|experts|practitioners|directors|powers": "A luminary is a person who is famous and highly respected in a field, a \"light\" in it, so legal luminaries are top legal experts: A. Practitioners (B) are simply people who practise. Directors and powers don't fit. Memory tip: luminary comes from \"lumen\" = light (illuminate).",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Practising medicine is not as lucrative as many people THINK.|know|assume|consider|understand": "\"Not as lucrative as many people think\" means people believe it without proof, i.e. assume: B. \"Know\" (A) would mean it's true, which contradicts \"not as lucrative\". \"Consider\" and \"understand\" fit less well. Memory tip: think = believe or assume when it introduces an opinion.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The manager made DISPARAGING remarks about the retiring officer.|parochial|cynical|derogatory|rude": "Disparaging remarks belittle someone, i.e. they are derogatory: C. Rude (D) is related but general. Parochial means narrow-minded, and cynical means distrustful. Memory tip: dis-parage = to lower someone's worth.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The teacher's apparent nonchalant attitude was MISCONSTRUED by his students.|condoned|misquoted|misinterpreted|misrepresented": "To misconstrue is to understand wrongly, i.e. to misinterpret: C. Misquoted means repeated someone's words wrongly, misrepresented means presented falsely, and condoned means forgave. Memory tip: construe = interpret, so misconstrue = misinterpret.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Let us not indulge in RECRIMINATION.|unfounded allegation|counter-accusation|accusation|indictment": "Recrimination means accusing someone back after they have accused you, i.e. counter-accusation: B. A plain accusation (C) misses the \"in return\" part, and an indictment (D) is a formal criminal charge. Memory tip: re-crimination = accusing again, back.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Teachers of music believe in its THERAPEUTIC effect.|lyrical|healing|sound|rhythmic": "Therapeutic means having a healing effect: B. Lyrical, sound and rhythmic describe music itself, not its effect on people. Memory tip: therapy = treatment, so therapeutic = healing.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The man outran his wife when they heard the EERIE sound.|hissing|harsh|scary|loud": "Eerie means strange in a frightening way, i.e. scary: C. That's why the man ran. Hissing, harsh and loud describe the sound's quality, not the fear it causes. Memory tip: eerie = spooky.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: His success may be described as a PYRRHIC VICTORY.|a deserving victory|costly|indecisive|an easy victory": "A pyrrhic victory is one won at such a terrible cost that it is almost a defeat, i.e. costly: B. It is named after King Pyrrhus, who beat the Romans but lost most of his army. Memory tip: pyrrhic = \"we won, but at what cost?\"",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The Executive Secretary has just ASSUMED OFFICE.|resumed work|been sworn in|started work|returned": "To assume office is to take up a post and begin its duties, i.e. to start work in it: C. Being sworn in (B) is the ceremony that usually comes just before. Resumed and returned would mean coming back. Memory tip: assume = take on (assume responsibility, assume control).",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The warring tribes have been told to PARLEY with each other.|hold talks|observe a truce|suspend hostilities|sign a treaty": "To parley is to hold talks, especially with an enemy, to settle a dispute: A. A truce, suspending hostilities or a treaty may come out of such talks, but they are not the talks themselves. Memory tip: parley is related to French \"parler\" = to speak.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He is A STRINGER FOR a newspaper.|a financier of|a reporter for|an editor of|a freelancer for": "A stringer is a journalist who isn't on a newspaper's staff but is paid for each story they send in, i.e. a freelancer: D. B (reporter) is close but misses the not-on-staff part. Memory tip: newspapers kept a \"string\" of part-time contributors.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Adigun's jokes are always PUERILE.|entertaining|empty|childish|amusing": "Puerile means childishly silly: C. Entertaining and amusing (A, D) are positive, and empty (B) is too vague. Memory tip: puerile comes from Latin \"puer\" = boy.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: I wish the commander were LESS ADAMANT ABOUT his proposed REPRISAL attack on the enemy.|unyielding about/retaliatory|tolerant of/surprise|sentimental about/rehearsed|supportive of/retributive": "Adamant means firmly refusing to change one's mind, i.e. unyielding, and a reprisal is an attack made in revenge, i.e. retaliatory: A. Memory tip: reprisal = paying back an attack.",
 "|Choose the option that has the stress on the FIRST syllable.|comment|dismiss|intact|confuse": "COMment (as a noun) is stressed on the first syllable. DisMISS, inTACT and conFUSE are stressed on the second: A. Memory tip: many two-syllable verbs are stressed on the second syllable.",
 "|Choose the option that has the stress on the FIRST syllable.|commute|import (verb)|intend|export (noun)": "In noun–verb pairs like export/import, the noun is stressed on the first syllable (an EXport) and the verb on the second (to imPORT): D. comMUTE and inTEND are stressed on the second. Memory tip: nouns stress the front, verbs the end (REcord/reCORD, PREsent/preSENT).",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The organization is constantly in a state of flux.|The organization is moribund.|The organization is experiencing good times.|The organization is facing a difficult period.|There are periodic changes in the organization.": "Flux means continuous change, so the organization keeps changing: D. Moribund (A) means dying, and B and C describe how things are going, not change. Memory tip: flux = flow (as in fluid).",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: His meteoric rise to fame surprised everyone.|People were amazed at his rapid success.|He rose to the top quite unexpectedly.|His rise to fame was only temporary.|He became successful very suddenly.": "Meteoric means very rapid, like a meteor, and \"surprised everyone\" means people were amazed. A captures both the speed and the surprise. D only says he succeeded suddenly. Memory tip: in \"best explains\" questions, choose the option that keeps every part of the original sentence.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The politician was pilloried by the press for his inflammatory remarks.|The politician was closely questioned by the press for his remarks.|The politician was criticized by the press for his remarks.|The politician was ridiculed by the press for his remarks.|The politician was snubbed by the press for his remarks.": "To pillory someone is to attack or criticise them severely in public: B. Ridiculed (C) means mocked, which is narrower, and questioned and snubbed are different actions. Memory tip: the pillory was a wooden frame where offenders were locked up for public shaming.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Audu was taken bad in the middle of the night.|He was ill|He was caught.|He was drunk|He was robbed.": "\"Taken bad\" (or \"taken ill\") means suddenly became ill: A. Caught, drunk and robbed are not meanings of this idiom. Memory tip: taken ill = fell sick.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: After the successful operation, he recovered by leaps and bounds.|His well-bound wounds aided his recovery.|He did a lot of keep-fit exercises.|He used to jog regularly.|He got well very rapidly.": "\"By leaps and bounds\" means very quickly: D. A plays on the word \"bound\" (bandaged), and B and C are about exercise, not speed. Memory tip: leaps and bounds = big jumps forward.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: The university has been TEMPORARILY closed.|Has the university been permanently open?|Has the campus been permanently closed?|Has the college been temporarily closed?|Has the university been permanently closed?": "Emphatic stress on TEMPORARILY corrects a mistaken idea about how long: \"Has the university been permanently closed?\" No, TEMPORARILY: D. A changes \"closed\" to \"open\", B and C change \"university\". Memory tip: only the stressed word should differ between the question and the answer.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: The king RAN to the palace.|Where did the king run to?|Did the queen run to the palace?|Did the king walk to the palace?|Who ran to the palace?": "Stressing RAN corrects the way the king moved: \"Did the king walk to the palace?\" No, he RAN: C. B would need stress on KING, and D on KING as well. Memory tip: the stressed word is the one being corrected.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: He travelled ONLY 20 kilometres before his car stopped.|Did he travel only 20 kilometres after his car stopped?|Did he travel exactly 20 kilometres before his car stopped?|Did he travel more than 20 kilometres before his car stopped?|Did you walk only 20 kilometres before the vehicle stopped?": "Stressing ONLY tells us the distance was short. That answers \"Did he travel more than 20 kilometres?\": C. B would stress \"exactly\", A changes \"before\" to \"after\", and D changes the person. Memory tip: ONLY corrects someone who thought there was more.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: The man BOUGHT the newspaper.|What did the man buy?|Is this the newspaper which the man bought?|Who bought the newspaper?|Did the man read the newspaper?": "Stressing BOUGHT corrects the action: \"Did the man read the newspaper?\" No, he BOUGHT it: D. A would need stress on NEWSPAPER, and C on MAN. Memory tip: stressed verb → the question gets the action wrong.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: My UNCLE retired from the police.|Did your brother retire from the police?|Did your brother resign from the police?|Did your uncle retire from the army?|Did your sister resign from the police?": "Stressing UNCLE corrects only who it was, so the question must keep \"retire\" and \"police\" but name someone else: A. B and D also change retire to resign, and C changes police to army. Memory tip: one stressed word = one thing corrected; everything else stays the same.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The Governor's visit is AN UNPRECEDENTED event in the history of the social club.|an unnecessary|a momentous|a perfect|an insignificant": "Unprecedented means never having happened before, so remarkable. Its opposite among the options is insignificant: D. Momentous (B) is close in meaning, not opposite, and unnecessary and perfect are unrelated. Memory tip: if there's no exact antonym, pick the option furthest in meaning.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He gave an unsatisfactory excuse but the boss swallowed it HOOK, LINE AND SINKER.|completely|with a pinch of salt|entirely|without mincing words": "Swallowing a story \"hook, line and sinker\" means believing it completely. Taking it \"with a pinch of salt\" means doubting it: B. A and C are synonyms. Memory tip: a pinch of salt = some doubt.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The principal took exception to the IGNOBLE role the teacher played in the matter.|dishonourable|extraordinary|honourable|embarrassing": "Ignoble means shameful and dishonourable, so its opposite is honourable: C. Dishonourable (A) is its synonym. Memory tip: ig-noble = not noble.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The HARDLINE posture of the labour leader has not helped matters.|imposing|difficult|compromising|uncompromising": "A hardline posture refuses to give way, so its opposite is compromising: C. Uncompromising (D) is a synonym. Memory tip: hardliners won't compromise.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The high cost of living these days calls for a lot of FRUGALITY.|economy|recklessness|prudence|extravagance": "Frugality is careful, sparing use of money, so its opposite is extravagance (wasteful spending): D. Economy and prudence are synonyms, and recklessness is too general. Memory tip: frugal ↔ extravagant.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The evidence the leader gave was INCONTROVERTIBLE.|logical|indubitable|practicable|contestable": "Incontrovertible means impossible to dispute, so its opposite is contestable (open to dispute): D. Indubitable (B) is a synonym. Memory tip: in-contro-vertible = cannot be \"controverted\" (argued against).",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Tunde's reaction UNDERSCORES the point I was making.|contradicts|justifies|summarizes|emphasizes": "To underscore a point is to support and emphasise it, so the opposite is to contradict it: A. Emphasizes (D) is a synonym, and justifies and summarizes are near it. Memory tip: underscore = underline.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Everyone admired the manager's ADROIT handling of the crises in the company.|clever|clumsy|skilful|tactless": "Adroit means skilful, especially with the hands or in handling situations. Its opposite is clumsy: B. D (tactless) is wrong: tactless is the opposite of tactful, a different quality. Clever and skilful are synonyms. Memory tip: adroit ↔ maladroit/clumsy.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The chairman's conduct REDOUNDS TO the image of the company.|contributes to|detracts from|assists in|reflection": "To redound to someone's image is to add to it or reflect well on it, so the opposite is to detract from it: B. \"Contributes to\" and \"assists in\" are synonyms, and \"reflection\" is a noun. Memory tip: redound to one's credit = add to it.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Her PHLEGMATIC temperament endears her to her friends.|cold|stoic|lively|irritable": "Phlegmatic means calm and unexcitable, so the opposite is lively: C. Stoic (B) and cold (A) are near-synonyms. Irritable (D) is a different trait. Memory tip: phlegmatic people don't get worked up.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The President gave another EXTEMPORANEOUS speech last Friday.|fascinating|unfavourable|unprepared|planned": "An extemporaneous speech is given without preparation, so the opposite is planned: D. Unprepared (C) is a synonym. Memory tip: extempore = off the cuff.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The witness AVERRED that she had seen Dosun at the scene of the crime.|confirmed|argued|affirmed|denied": "To aver is to state firmly that something is true. The opposite is to deny it: D. Confirmed and affirmed are synonyms. Memory tip: aver ↔ deny.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The workers suddenly became RESTIVE.|fidgety|calm|disorderly|submissive": "Restive means restless and hard to control, so the opposite is calm: B. Fidgety and disorderly are near-synonyms, and submissive is not the direct opposite. Memory tip: restive ≠ restful; it means the opposite.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The athlete has unexpectedly become INDOMITABLE.|unruly|unconquerable|weak|disoriented": "Indomitable means impossible to defeat, so its opposite is weak: C. Unconquerable (B) is a synonym. Memory tip: in-domitable = cannot be dominated.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He is NOTORIOUS for his drunkenness.|known|renowned|popular|well known": "Notorious means famous for something bad. Its opposite is renowned, famous for something good: B. D (well known) is wrong: \"well known\" is close to notorious's meaning. A notorious person is well known, just for bad reasons. Memory tip: notorious = bad fame; renowned = good fame.",
 "eng-2003-p1|The basic causes of the elimination of certain animals from the earth include|man's decision to live in cities and the development of large farmlands|extensive killing of animals and the fast disappearance of their favourable habitats|man's penchant for meat and the sale of animals for meat and hides|a deliberate battle against Nature and the quest for leopard skin": "The passage says a few animals were reduced \"by extensive killing\", but most are disappearing as fast as \"the particular kind of country they need for existence\" disappears. That is killing plus the loss of habitats: B. A and C are never given as causes, and D twists \"battle against Nature\", which belonged to primitive times. Memory tip: in \"causes\" questions, look for the sentence with \"because\", \"by\" or \"as a result of\".",
 "eng-2003-p1|From the passage, the attitude of the writer can be described as|partial|optimistic|indifferent|pessimistic": "The writer asks, \"Does this mean no room for wild animals? Of course not,\" and says the World Wildlife Fund \"does not believe that all is lost\". That is hopeful, i.e. optimistic: B. He clearly cares (not indifferent), and he takes sides for wildlife (not partial in a negative sense). Memory tip: the writer's attitude usually shows most clearly in the closing paragraph.",
 "eng-2003-p1|The expression 'when man evolved a conscience' means when|man developed an awareness of right and wrong|man's intellect improved tremendously|man acquired new habits|man became a critical creature": "A conscience is the inner sense of right and wrong. The passage links it to \"the awareness of responsibility\" and \"feelings of guilt\": A. Intellect (B), habits (C) and being critical (D) are different things. Memory tip: conscience (right and wrong) ≠ conscious (awake, aware).",
 "eng-2003-p1|Which of the following statements is true according to the passage?|Man kills animals only when he can afford to do so.|Man cannot spare those animals that eat his kind.|Man eats all categories of animals.|Man poses the greatest threat to Nature.": "The whole passage shows animals vanishing \"at the hands of man\": through killing, fashion and destroying habitats. So man is the greatest threat to Nature: D. A, B and C are never stated. Memory tip: \"true according to the passage\" means stated or clearly implied, not just possible.",
 "eng-2003-p1|The sentence 'There will be twice as many of us before most of us are dead' means|some increase in human and animal population growth rates|mankind is fast spreading across the earth|many of us will die as a result of population explosion|the population growth rate will double before our death": "\"There will be twice as many of us before most of us are dead\" means the human population will double within our lifetime: D. B is a different point, C misreads \"dead\", and A adds animals. D says \"growth rate\" where it means population, but it's the only option with the idea of doubling before we die. Memory tip: paraphrase the sentence in your own words first, then pick the closest option.",
 "eng-2003-p2|Which of the following captures the writer's suggestion on how the Eagles could improve their performance in subsequent matches?|Better goalkeeping, a better attack and a stronger midfield.|Replacement of injured players, stronger attackers and a rugged defence.|Good coaching, more strikers and more defenders.|Fair officiating, good goalkeeping and fast players.": "The writer suggests a stronger defence, strikers who perform better, and using reserves \"rather than gamble with unfit players\". B matches all three: replacing unfit or injured players, stronger attackers and a rugged defence. A, C and D add things he never mentions (midfield, coaching, officiating). Memory tip: check every item in a list option against the passage.",
 "eng-2003-p2|A suitable title for this passage is|African Teams in the 2002 World Cup|The FIFA Korea/Japan 2002|The Nigerian and the Senegalese Teams|The Eagles in World Cup 2002": "The passage uses Senegal to set the scene, but it's mainly about the Super Eagles: their match against Argentina, their shortcomings and their chances. So \"The Eagles in World Cup 2002\" fits best: D. A and B are too broad, and C gives Senegal equal weight. Memory tip: the best title covers the main subject of most paragraphs.",
 "eng-2003-p2|From the argument in the last paragraph, it can be concluded that the Eagles were|more experienced than the Senegalese team|not as strong as the Senegalese team|more timid and goal-shy than their opponents|not sure of getting to the next round of the tournament": "The last paragraph argues that if \"World Cup debutants, Senegal\" could hope to reach the knock-out stages, the Eagles should do even better. That implies the Eagles were more experienced: A. B and C contradict the writer's hopeful view, and D is not the point of the argument. Memory tip: \"If X can..., then Y should...\" implies Y is better placed than X.",
 "eng-2003-p2|The word 'debutants', as used in the passage, means|hard-fighters|under-dogs|first-timers|giant killers": "A debutant is someone appearing for the first time (a début). 2002 was Senegal's first World Cup: C. Underdogs (B) are expected to lose, and giant killers (D) beat stronger teams. Senegal was both, but that's not what the word means. Memory tip: début = first appearance.",
 "eng-2003-p2|From the passage, it can be concluded that the writer|was optimistic about the chances of the Eagles|did not fancy the Eagles' chances|was non-committal about the chances of the Eagles|was certain about the Eagles' chances": "The writer says the hope \"was very much alive\" and that the Eagles \"should soar\", while admitting their weaknesses. That is optimistic: A. \"Certain\" (D) is too strong, and B and C go against his tone. Memory tip: hopeful but not sure = optimistic.",
 "eng-2003-p3|The positions maintained by the moralist and the economist can be described as being|at variance|very agreeable|quite indifferent|very passionate": "The moralist condemns smoking and drinking as signs of moral decay, while the economist is interested in them because they create demand and supply. Their positions are different, i.e. at variance: A. Memory tip: at variance = in disagreement.",
 "eng-2003-p3|Which of the following statements is true according to the passage?|People who drink or smoke surely die of cancer.|Everyone ignores the moralist view on drinking and smoking.|Smoking and drinking may have positive effects on the economy.|Total abstinence from drinking and smoking is a religious obligation.": "The passage says smoking and drinking \"generate supply of and demand for tobacco and alcohol\", and that giving them up could \"cripple the breweries, the cigarette factories and some businesses\". So they can benefit the economy: C. A and B are too absolute (\"surely\", \"everyone\"), and D isn't stated. Memory tip: watch out for absolute words like all, always, surely and everyone.",
 "eng-2003-p3|It can be concluded from the passage that morality, religion and economy are|clearly interconnected|certainly different|somewhat interconnected|certainly unrelated": "The passage links all three: \"moral principles associated with religion tend to lead on to economic problems\". So morality, religion and economy are interconnected: A (or C). The answer key gives B (\"certainly different\"), which goes against the passage's main point. Memory tip: if a passage shows one thing leading to another, they're connected.",
 "eng-2003-p3|The view expressed by the writer in the last paragraph is that|the number of alcoholics and smokers is certainly increasing|more people now abstain from drinking and smoking|more people appear to take to drinking and smoking|sales of alcohol and tobacco products have improved tremendously": "The last paragraph says there \"seems to be a growing number of alcohol consumers and cigarette smokers\". \"Seems\" matches \"appear\", so C. The key gives D, but the passage never mentions sales improving \"tremendously\". A is too certain and says \"alcoholics\" instead of consumers. Memory tip: keep the writer's hedges (seems, may, appears) when you match an option.",
 "eng-2003-p3|According to the passage, 'the moralist idea' is that|it is typically African not to smoke cigarettes|people should accept a point of view only when they are convinced|smoking is not good but a little alcohol may be permitted|the smoking of cigarettes is bad and unacceptable": "The moralist \"has always regarded smoking as an indication of moral degradation\", i.e. smoking is bad and unacceptable: D. C wrongly allows some alcohol, which the moralist also condemns. A and B are not the moralist's idea. Memory tip: answer \"according to the passage\" questions from the sentence that defines the term.",
 "eng-2003-p4|Gap 16: A _____ delivery is one in which the speech has been written out word for word and is read.|document|free|manuscript|quantum": "A speech written out word for word and read aloud is called a manuscript speech: C. A document is any written paper, and \"free\" and \"quantum\" don't describe this kind of delivery. Memory tip: the four types of delivery are manuscript, memorized, impromptu and extemporaneous.",
 "eng-2003-p4|Gap 17: …written out word for word and is read to _____.|a gathering|a conference|a congregation|an audience": "The people a speech is read to are its audience: D. A congregation is a church gathering, and a conference is a meeting. Memory tip: speakers address an audience.",
 "eng-2003-p4|Gap 18: This kind of delivery is usually reserved for very _____ occasions.|formal|genuine|impromptu|guaranteed": "The examples given, the State of the Union Address and UN speeches, are formal occasions: A. Impromptu (C) means unprepared, the opposite of a manuscript speech. Memory tip: examples after \"such as\" tell you what kind of thing fills the gap.",
 "eng-2003-p4|Gap 19: …occasions when exact wording is _____.|conclusive|critical|reportive|speculative": "On state occasions every word matters, so exact wording is critical: B. C (reportive) is wrong: \"reportive\" isn't a standard word and makes no sense here. Conclusive means final, and speculative means guessing. Memory tip: critical = extremely important.",
 "eng-2003-p4|Gap 20: …speeches before the United Nations General _____.|Negotiation|Organization|Assembly|Audience": "The UN body where member states give speeches is the General Assembly: C. Memory tip: the UN General Assembly meets in New York every September.",
 "eng-2003-p4|Gap 21: The primary advantage is that the speech may be highly _____ in terms of word choice, turns of phrase, and development of ideas.|polished|advanced|analogous|discreet": "A speech written in advance can be refined until it is polished in its word choice and phrasing: A. Advanced, analogous (similar) and discreet (careful not to reveal) don't describe good writing. Memory tip: polished = refined, smooth.",
 "eng-2003-p4|Gap 22: Reading aloud with meaningful _____ inflection requires the speaker to be very familiar with the text.|anticipatory|profuse|bifocal|vocal": "Inflection is the rise and fall of the voice, so vocal inflection: D. Anticipatory (expecting), profuse (plentiful) and bifocal (two focuses) don't fit. Memory tip: vocal = of the voice.",
 "eng-2003-p4|Gap 23: Such poor delivery could destroy any _____ effects created by the carefully chosen words.|interactive|restrictive|positive|decisive": "Poor delivery ruins the good effects that careful wording would have had, i.e. the positive effects: C. Interactive, restrictive and decisive don't fit \"destroy\". Memory tip: you can only destroy something good.",
 "eng-2003-p4|Gap 24: …effects created by the carefully chosen _____.|language|slang|dialect|rhetoric": "The passage has been talking about word choice and turns of phrase, i.e. carefully chosen language: A. Slang and dialect are informal or regional, which is wrong for formal speeches. Memory tip: in cloze, a gap often repeats an idea mentioned earlier.",
 "eng-2003-p4|Gap 25: Lack of familiarity with the _____ could also prevent the speaker from maintaining eye contact.|exchange|text|note|context": "\"Lack of familiarity with the text\" echoes the earlier line that reading aloud \"requires the speaker to be very familiar with the text\": B. Memory tip: look back in the passage for the same phrase.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: In spite of his humble beginning, Audu now throws his weight around.|Audu is arrogant despite his simple upbringing.|Despite his obvious poverty, Audu is a proud man.|His noble birth notwithstanding, Audu is a corrupt man.|From his poor background, Audu is now a rich man.": "To \"throw one's weight around\" is to act bossy and arrogant. \"In spite of his humble beginning\" means despite his simple upbringing: A. B and D talk about present poverty or wealth, and C changes humble to noble. Memory tip: throw your weight around = push people around.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Ngozi has always considered her father to be an impassioned man.|Her father is a very lively man.|Her father is an emotional man.|Her father is a disciplined man.|Her father is a very strict man.": "Impassioned means full of strong feeling, i.e. emotional: B. Lively (A) is close but means energetic, and disciplined and strict don't fit. Memory tip: im-passion-ed = full of passion.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The elders rebuked Olu for taking issue with his principal.|Olu was cautioned for shouting at his principal.|Olu was scolded for acting in collusion with his principal.|Olu was reprimanded for arguing with his principal.|Olu was blamed for issuing a statement denying his principal.": "To rebuke is to reprimand or scold, and to \"take issue with\" someone is to disagree or argue with them: C. A adds shouting, B changes the meaning to collusion (secret cooperation), and D invents a statement. Memory tip: take issue with = disagree with.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The manager paid us in hard currency.|We were paid in new notes.|We were paid in foreign currency.|We were paid in dollars and pound sterling.|We were paid in a strong and stable currency.": "Hard currency is money from a strong, stable economy that is widely accepted: D. Foreign currency (B) or dollars and pounds (C) are often hard currencies, but not all foreign money is. Memory tip: hard currency = strong, reliable money.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: If he went to London, he would see the Queen.|When he goes to London, he will see the Queen.|He did not go to London and did not see the Queen.|He did not see the Queen when he went to London.|He would like to see the Queen when he goes to London.": "\"If he went..., he would see\" describes an unreal or unlikely situation: he isn't going, so he won't see the Queen. B is the only option that keeps \"he doesn't go and doesn't see her\", even though it puts it in the past. The key also gives B. Memory tip: If + past, would + verb = imaginary now or in the future; If + had + past participle, would have + past participle = didn't happen in the past.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Only those who are GULLIBLE fall victim to his trickery.|saucy|devastated|courteous|astute": "Gullible means easily tricked, so the opposite is astute (shrewd, hard to fool): D. Saucy means cheeky, devastated means shattered, and courteous means polite. Memory tip: astute ↔ gullible.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He is well known for his INORDINATE ambition.|excessive|passionate|moderate|sound": "Inordinate means excessive, beyond normal limits, so the opposite is moderate: C. Excessive (A) is a synonym. Memory tip: inordinate = out of order (too much).",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Students could be TIMID.|friendly|bold|covetous|pugnacious": "Timid means shy and fearful, so the opposite is bold: B. Pugnacious (D) means eager to fight, which goes further than the opposite of timid. Memory tip: timid ↔ bold.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The witness decided to CONCEAL the evidence.|divulge|hide|destroy|pugnacious": "To conceal is to hide, and to divulge is to reveal: A. Hide (B) is a synonym. Memory tip: divulge = disclose secrets.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The members of the congregation were INSPIRED by the sermon.|bewitched|enthralled|disenchanted|disorientated": "Inspired listeners are filled with enthusiasm. Disenchanted listeners feel let down and lose interest: C. Bewitched and enthralled are near-synonyms of inspired. Memory tip: disenchanted = the spell is broken.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Agbenu was ECSTATIC about her result.|dispassionate|sad|pessimistic|mad": "Ecstatic means overjoyed, so the opposite is sad: B. Pessimistic (C) is about expecting the worst, not feeling unhappy now. Memory tip: ecstasy = extreme joy.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The labour leader's RECALCITRANT stance was applauded.|stubborn|flexible|uncompromising|well informed": "Recalcitrant means stubbornly uncooperative, so the opposite is flexible: B. Stubborn and uncompromising are synonyms. Memory tip: recalcitrant = digging in one's heels.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: A cool bath in a hot weather can be truly INVIGORATING.|devastating|unpalatable|debilitating|disgusting": "Invigorating means energizing, so the opposite is debilitating (weakening): C. Unpalatable and disgusting describe taste, and devastating is destruction. Memory tip: vigour = energy; debility = weakness.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: I am LOATH to do the assignment.|willing|unwilling|waiting|dying": "Loath (adjective) means unwilling or reluctant, so the opposite is willing: A. Unwilling (B) is a synonym. Memory tip: loath (reluctant) ≠ loathe (hate).",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Toyin is married to AN IMPATIENT, self-centered man.|a fretful|a tolerant|an edgy|a tolerable": "An impatient person can't wait calmly. A tolerant person puts up with things: B. Tolerable (D) means bearable, which describes situations, not personality. Fretful and edgy are synonyms. Memory tip: tolerant (person) vs tolerable (thing).",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: GREGARIOUS animals can be found in the zoo.|Various|Wild|Lonely|Tame": "Gregarious animals live in groups and like company. The opposite is solitary, and \"lonely\" is the closest option: C. B (wild) is wrong: wild is the opposite of tame, and many wild animals live in herds. Memory tip: gregarious comes from Latin \"grex\" = flock.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The doctor examined the patient PAINSTAKINGLY.|perfunctorily|professionally|painfully|carefully": "Painstakingly means with great care, so the opposite is perfunctorily (carelessly, as a mere routine): A. Carefully (D) is a synonym. Memory tip: perfunctory = done just to get it over with.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The company has continued to MONOPOLIZE the distribution of the products.|centralize|specialize|liberalize|regularize": "To monopolize is to keep sole control, so the opposite is to liberalize (open it up to competition): C. Centralize (A) is close in meaning, not opposite. Memory tip: mono = one controller.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: A CONSERVATIVE estimate put the number of missing persons at forty.|A rough|An accurate|A primitive|An incorrect": "A conservative estimate is a cautious, rough figure, deliberately not overstated. The opposite in this set is an accurate count: B. A rough estimate (A) is close in meaning. Memory tip: conservative estimate = safe, low guess.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The agency has sworn to deal with all the APOSTLES of confederation.|proponents|protagonists|apostates|opponents": "Apostles of a cause are its supporters and promoters, so the opposite is its opponents: D. Proponents and protagonists are synonyms, and apostates are people who abandon a belief. Memory tip: apostle = champion of a cause.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The leader has the UNSTINTING support of his party.|unsparing|laudable|uninspiring|cautious": "Unstinting means given generously, without holding back, i.e. unsparing: A. Laudable means praiseworthy. Memory tip: to stint is to give too little, so unstinting is the opposite.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The party supporters VILIFIED the Chairman for the role he played in the crisis that rocked the party.|elected|challenged|condemned|impeached": "To vilify is to speak abusively about someone and portray them as evil, i.e. to condemn: C. Challenged and impeached are different actions. Memory tip: vilify → villain.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The company is to SHED three thousand staff this year.|demote|lay off|throw up|placate": "When a company sheds staff, it lays them off (dismisses them): B. Demote means to lower in rank. Memory tip: shed = get rid of (as trees shed leaves).",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: There was A GLUT OF oil on the market.|a variety of|an accumulation of|an abundance of|an increase in": "A glut is an oversupply, i.e. an abundance: C. An accumulation (B) is a build-up and an increase (D) is a rise, but neither carries the \"too much\" meaning. Memory tip: glut = flooded market.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: A few years ago, nobody would have believed that the economy would TURN AROUND.|deteriorate|improve|stagnate|change": "When an economy \"turns around\", it goes from doing badly to doing well, so it improves: B. D (change) is wrong: change is too general, since getting worse is also a change. Memory tip: a turnaround = a recovery.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Before announcing his retirement, Ochima resolved to SETTLE ON ACCOUNT WITH the bank.|pay back all he owes|close his account with|retire his loans from|get back at": "To \"settle an account\" is to pay what you owe: A. Closing the account (B) is different. Get back at (D) means take revenge, as in \"settle accounts with an enemy\". Memory tip: settle a bill = pay it.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The boys knew that a storm was IMMINENT.|possible|impending|threatening|encroaching": "Imminent means about to happen, i.e. impending: B. Threatening (C) describes the storm but not its timing. Memory tip: imminent (very soon) ≠ eminent (famous).",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The nurse was in favour of VOLUNTARY EUTHANASIA.|a painless death|a simple operation|a sleeping pill|a major operation": "Euthanasia is deliberately ending a suffering patient's life painlessly, and voluntary means with the patient's consent: A. Memory tip: eu-thanasia = \"good death\" in Greek.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The CYNICS feared that the nation's nascent democracy would fail.|pessimists|delinquents|critics|illusionists": "Cynics believe things will turn out badly and distrust others' motives. Like pessimists, they expect failure: A. Critics (C) point out faults but don't necessarily expect failure. Memory tip: cynics expect the worst.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The ESSENCE of governance is to seek the good and well-being of the majority of the people.|importance|goal|characteristic|secret": "The essence of something is its most important element, what it fundamentally is: A. The key also gives A. Goal (B) is tempting because of \"is to seek\", but essence is about core importance, not aim. Memory tip: essential = most important.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: From what she said, one may INFER that she does not like the course.|suppose|realize|deduce|agree": "To infer is to draw a conclusion from evidence, i.e. to deduce: C. Suppose is guessing, and realize is becoming aware. Memory tip: the speaker implies; the listener infers.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He shared his room with a person whose behaviour was quite NAUSEATING.|disrespectful|disgraceful|discouraging|disgusting": "Nauseating means making you feel sick, i.e. disgusting: D. Disgraceful (B) is about shame, not revulsion. Memory tip: nausea = sickness.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The carpenter built a COMMODIOUS wardrobe.|gigantic|small|spacious|wide": "Commodious means roomy, i.e. spacious: C. Gigantic (A) is about size in general, and wide (D) is one dimension. Memory tip: commodious holds lots of commodities.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Publishing as a business venture has become A HOT POTATO in Nigeria.|unpleasant|profitable|unacceptable|expensive": "A hot potato is a controversial issue that is awkward to deal with. Of the options, unpleasant is the closest: A. Memory tip: you can't hold a hot potato for long.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The man's story sounded PLAUSIBLE to his audience.|fantastic|credulous|credible|entertaining": "Plausible means seemingly reasonable and believable, i.e. credible: C. Credulous (B) means too willing to believe, which describes the listener, not the story. Memory tip: credible story, credulous listener.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): 'I can't stand people prying into my private life', Ladi said. '_____,' agreed Agbenu.|Me neither|Me too|I also|Likewise myself": "To agree with a negative statement (\"I can't stand...\"), use \"Me neither\" or \"Neither can I\": A. \"Me too\" agrees with positive statements, and \"I also\" and \"Likewise myself\" are not standard replies. Memory tip: negative + agree = neither/nor.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The sergeant spoke to me in a _____ manner.|coerce|coarse|course|causal": "Coarse means rough and rude: B. Course is a route or a programme of study, coerce is a verb (to force), and causal relates to cause and effect. Memory tip: coarse (rough) vs course (path or study).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The reason why he was not offered admission was _____ his results could not be found.|because|that|when|owning to": "\"The reason... was that...\" is correct. \"The reason... was because\" repeats the idea of cause, since \"reason\" already means \"because\": B. Memory tip: reason + that, never reason + because.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Adika _____ a message from the club regularly.|receive|receives|has received|had received": "\"Regularly\" signals a habit, which uses the simple present. Adika is singular, so \"receives\": B. Memory tip: habits = simple present (he receives, she goes).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Three quarters of the people in the village _____ killed but only half of their huts _____ affected.|were/was|were/were|was/was|was/were": "After a fraction, the verb agrees with the noun after \"of\": \"three quarters of the people were\" and \"half of their huts were\": B. A (were/was) is wrong: huts is plural. Memory tip: half of + plural noun → were; half of + uncountable noun → was.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): If you saw the photograph of the man, _____ you be able to identify him?|can|will|would|could": "\"If + past simple\" (If you saw) pairs with \"would\" in the main clause: C. \"Can\" and \"will\" belong with \"If you see\". Memory tip: second conditional = If + past, would + verb.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): It is bad to take _____ property without permission.|someone else's|someone's else|someone's else's|someone elses'": "The possessive 's is added to the end of the whole phrase: someone else's: A. Memory tip: someone else's book, nobody else's business.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): As Obande does not know anyone in this city, he hopes that some kind _____ will put him up for the night.|men|individuals|man|inhabitants": "\"Some\" + singular noun means one unknown person, and one person will put him up: \"some kind man\": C. Memory tip: some man (an unknown one) vs some men (several).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Be careful not to _____ this money.|lose|loose|loss|lost": "Lose (verb) means to misplace something: A. Loose (B) means not tight, loss (C) is the noun, and lost (D) is the past form. Memory tip: lose has lost an \"o\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): How is the new editor _____ on with his work?|pushing|going|getting|moving": "\"To get on with\" something means to make progress with it: C. Memory tip: How are you getting on? = How are you progressing?",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Nowadays, many graduates are not well disposed to teaching, _____|do they?|they are?|aren't they?|are they?": "A negative statement (\"are not well disposed\") takes a positive tag with the same verb: \"are they?\": D. Memory tip: negative statement → positive tag; positive statement → negative tag.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The armed robbers went into the house and robbed the three _____.|women's occupants|women occupants|woman occupants|woman's occupants": "Before a plural noun, man and woman also become plural: women occupants (like women doctors): B. Memory tip: men drivers, women teachers.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): It is often _____ that inflation _____ from too much money chasing very few goods.|say/resulted|said/result|said/results|say/result": "\"It is often said\" is passive (said), and \"inflation\" is singular, so \"results\": C. Memory tip: \"it is said that...\" is a fixed passive pattern.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): If you would do me this favour, I _____ be very grateful.|will|shall|would|should": "\"I shall be grateful if you would...\" is a standard formal phrase, so the key's B is right. \"I would be grateful\" and \"I should be grateful\" (C, D) are equally correct, so all three are accepted. Only \"will\" is out. Memory tip: in formal letters, \"I shall/should/would be grateful if you would...\"",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): I have the _____ of meeting the President.|privilege|priviledge|privelege|previlege": "Privilege is spelt p-r-i-v-i-l-e-g-e, with no \"d\": A. Memory tip: there's no \"edge\" in privilege.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): My classmate, _____ I haven't seen for years, wrote to me last week.|that|whose|whom|which": "\"I haven't seen him\" means the pronoun is an object, so \"whom\". \"That\" can't start a clause set off by commas: C. Memory tip: him → whom; he → who.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Four weeks _____ enough for the police to conclude their investigation.|has been|are|were|is": "A period of time treated as one amount takes a singular verb: \"Four weeks is enough\": D. Memory tip: amounts of time, money and distance take singular verbs (ten naira is too much).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The woman is one of the _____ of the society.|elitists|elites|elite|elitist": "\"One of the\" is followed by a plural noun, so \"one of the elites\": B. Elitists (A) are people who believe in rule by an elite. Memory tip: one of the + plural (one of the boys).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The doctor asked the patient what _____.|is the problem|the problem was|the problem is|is your problem": "In reported questions, the word order becomes that of a statement and the tense shifts back: \"what the problem was\": B. Memory tip: no question order after \"asked what/where/why\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): He put _____ in a basket.|a white dozen eggs|dozen white eggs|a dozen white eggs|white dozen eggs": "Numbers come before adjectives: a dozen white eggs: C. Memory tip: quantity, then quality, then noun.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Idakwo ran _____ miss the train.|lest he almost|lest he will|lest he should|lest he may": "\"Lest\" (for fear that) is followed by \"should\" + base verb: \"lest he should miss\": C. Memory tip: lest he should fall = so that he wouldn't fall.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Course _____ writers are to reflect local colour.|material|materials|material's|materials'": "When one noun describes another, there is no apostrophe: \"course materials writers\" (the usual term) or \"course material writers\": B or A. The answer key gives D (materials'), which wrongly adds a possessive. Memory tip: noun + noun (bus driver, materials writer) needs no apostrophe.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): It was a free-for-all and the students were blamed for taking the law _____.|into their hands|in their hands|into their own hands|in their own hands": "The idiom is \"take the law into one's own hands\": to punish someone yourself instead of going to the authorities. That gives C. A is wrong: dropping \"own\" breaks the idiom. Memory tip: idioms are fixed, so don't drop or swap words.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Ali plays _____ violin with remarkable skill.|their|some|a|the": "Musical instruments take \"the\": play the violin, play the drums: D. Memory tip: play the piano, but play football (no article for games).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The candidate's charisma should be a _____ factor in winning the election.|determinable|determining|determinant|determinate": "A determining factor is one that decides the outcome: B. Determinable means able to be found out, determinant is mainly a noun, and determinate means fixed. Memory tip: determining factor = deciding factor.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: cOLOnel|gaol|colony|golden|girl": "Colonel is pronounced like \"kernel\" (/ˈkɜːnəl/), so its \"olo\" has the /ɜː/ sound of girl: D. Memory tip: colonel and kernel sound the same.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: tEnd|jeopardy|turned|earned|caned": "The e in tend is short /e/. Jeopardy is pronounced JEP-ar-dy, so its \"eo\" is also /e/: A. Turned and earned have /ɜː/, and caned has /eɪ/. Memory tip: jeopardy and leopard both say \"ep\".",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: markET|get|mortgage|enter|bachelor": "The unstressed -et in market is pronounced /ɪt/, and the -age in mortgage is /ɪdʒ/, with the same short /ɪ/: B. Get and enter have /e/. Memory tip: unstressed endings like -et, -age and -ive often reduce to /ɪ/.",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT vowel sound from the others.|dear|fair|bear|there": "Dear has /ɪə/ (as in ear), while fair, bear and there all have /eə/ (as in air): A. B is wrong: fair rhymes with bear and there. Memory tip: ear-words (dear, fear, near) vs air-words (fair, bear, there, wear).",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT vowel sound from the others.|hope|cost|coast|won't": "Cost has the short /ɒ/ of hot. Hope, coast and won't have /əʊ/: B. Memory tip: cost vs coast, one vowel letter changes the sound.",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT vowel sound from the others.|naught|north|spot|law": "Spot has the short /ɒ/. Naught, north and law have the long /ɔː/: C. Memory tip: aw, au, augh and or usually give /ɔː/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: coNcrete|attend|anxious|concern|consider": "Before a /k/ or /g/ sound, n becomes /ŋ/, the \"ng\" in sing. Concrete is /ˈkɒŋkriːt/, and anxious is /ˈæŋkʃəs/: B. D is wrong: the n in consider is before /s/, so it's an ordinary /n/. Memory tip: say \"ink\", \"uncle\", \"anxious\": your tongue moves to the back for /ŋ/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: CHalet|college|chemical|chairman|champagne": "Chalet is a French word, so ch = /ʃ/ (sh), as in champagne: D. Chemical has /k/, chairman /tʃ/, and college has no ch. Memory tip: French words keep ch = sh (chef, machine, brochure).",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: teeTH|though|taught|thought|tank": "The th in teeth is voiceless /θ/, as in thought: C. The th in though is voiced /ð/. Memory tip: put your hand on your throat; no buzz means /θ/ (think, teeth), a buzz means /ð/ (this, though).",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT consonant sound from the others.|shoe|ocean|chef|chief": "Chief starts with /tʃ/ (as in church). Shoe, chef and the c in ocean all have /ʃ/: D. Memory tip: chef (French, /ʃ/) vs chief (/tʃ/).",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT consonant sound from the others.|laugh|off|wife|of": "The f in \"of\" is /v/, while laugh, off and wife all end in /f/: D. Memory tip: of /ɒv/ vs off /ɒf/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same stress pattern as the given word (stressed syllable in capitals): ceMENT|employ (noun)|interest|perfect (adjective)|include": "CeMENT and inCLUDE are stressed on the second syllable, so D matches. The noun \"employ\" (A, as in \"in his employ\") is also stressed on the second syllable, so it's accepted too. INterest and PERfect (adjective) are stressed on the first. Memory tip: most noun/verb pairs shift stress, but employ doesn't.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same stress pattern as the given word (stressed syllable in capitals): TYPist|rebel (verb)|superb|refuse (noun)|propose": "TYPist is stressed on the first syllable, and so is the noun REFuse (rubbish): C. The verb reBEL, suPERB and proPOSE are stressed on the second. Memory tip: REFuse (noun, rubbish) vs reFUSE (verb, say no).",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: My MOTHER served rice and fresh fish stew for dinner.|What kind of meal did your mother serve for dinner?|Did your mother serve rice and fresh fish stew for lunch?|What kind of stew did your mother serve for dinner?|Who served rice and fresh fish stew for dinner?": "Stressing MOTHER answers \"who?\": \"Who served rice and fresh fish stew for dinner?\": D. A and C would stress the food, and B would stress the meal time. Memory tip: stressed subject = answer to \"who?\"",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: The President SPOKE to the press.|Who spoke to the press?|Are these the pressmen that the president spoke to?|Did the President speak to the press?|Did the President write to the press?": "Stressing SPOKE corrects how he communicated: \"Did the President write to the press?\" No, he SPOKE: D. A would stress PRESIDENT, and B the press. Memory tip: stressed verb → the question got the action wrong.",
 "eng-2004-p1|The phrase 'near neighbour', as used in the passage, shows that|moonlight generally shares many characteristics with 'earth light'|the earth is nearer to the moon than to the sun|the earth actually shares boundaries with the moon|moonlight shines brighter on the earth's surface than perhaps elsewhere": "Calling the earth the moon's \"near neighbour\" is about distance: the earth is close to the moon, while the sun is described as \"distant\". That gives B. The answer key also allows D, but nothing in the passage says moonlight is brighter on earth than elsewhere. A and C are not suggested by the phrase. Memory tip: a phrase about closeness answers a question about distance.",
 "eng-2004-p1|The passage suggests that 'a blue moon' is a|rare phenomenon|regular event|trick of the moon|life-long opportunity": "\"If, once in a lifetime, you see a blue moon\" tells us it rarely happens: A. It is not regular (B), not a trick (C, the passage says your eyes are not playing tricks), and not an opportunity (D). Memory tip: \"once in a blue moon\" means very rarely.",
 "eng-2004-p1|The sentence 'over the centuries, magical powers have been attributed to the moon' suggests that|people perform magic with the moon|it is a common belief that the moon has magical powers|the moon actually possesses magical powers|the moon reflects magical powers from the sun's rays": "To attribute powers to the moon is to believe or claim it has them. Later the writer calls such ideas \"a common mistake\", so B: it is a common belief. C states the belief as fact, which the writer doesn't accept. Memory tip: attributed to = credited to (believed to come from).",
 "eng-2004-p1|The expression 'either quest' refers to the origin of|the moon or the earth|neither the moon nor the earth|the moon|the earth": "\"Either quest\" means each of the two searches the writer has just mentioned: for the moon's origin and for the earth's origin (\"without determining the earth's origin\"). So A. The key gives B (\"neither\"), but that reverses the meaning. Memory tip: \"either\" = one or the other of two; \"neither\" = not one nor the other.",
 "eng-2004-p1|A suitable title for the passage is|The Magical Powers of the Moon|Facts about the Moon|The Lunar Cycle|Moon-Gazing": "The passage gives many facts about the moon: blue moons, rings, tides, theories of its origin and how it reflects light. \"Facts about the Moon\" covers them all: B. Magic (A) is only one paragraph. Memory tip: the title should fit every paragraph.",
 "eng-2004-p2|Gap 6: The _____ of capitalism claim that its essential characteristic is economic _____ (gap 7).|attorneys|determiners|antagonists|protagonists": "The people who \"claim\" good things for capitalism are its supporters, i.e. its protagonists: D. Antagonists (C) are opponents, attorneys are lawyers, and determiners are a part of speech. Memory tip: pro = for, anti = against.",
 "eng-2004-p2|Gap 7: …its essential characteristic is economic _____.|freedom|manoeuvring|manipulation|exploitation": "The next sentence says producers and consumers are both free, so capitalism's key feature is economic freedom: A. The other options are negative words a supporter wouldn't use. Memory tip: read on past the gap; the next sentence often explains it.",
 "eng-2004-p2|Gap 8: The producer is free to produce whatever goods he _____.|manufactures|buys|sells|fancies": "The producer is free to make whatever he fancies (likes or chooses): D. \"Produce whatever goods he manufactures\" (A) repeats itself, and buys and sells don't fit \"produce\". Memory tip: fancy (verb) = like, want.",
 "eng-2004-p2|Gap 9: …but the _____ is equally free to buy what he wants.|consumer|customer|marketer|controller": "Throughout the passage the producer is set against the consumer (\"brings the producer and consumer together\"): A. Customer (B) is close, but the passage's pair is producer/consumer. Memory tip: producer ↔ consumer.",
 "eng-2004-p2|Gap 10: …equate the supplies of the one to the demands of the other, and _____ the whims and caprice of both.|neutralize|decide|harmonize|settle": "The mechanism brings the two sides together, so it harmonizes their whims (makes them fit together): C. Neutralize means cancel out, and decide and settle don't take \"whims\" as an object. Memory tip: harmony = things working together.",
 "eng-2004-p2|Gap 11: It is this same _____ mechanism which determines what prices the consumers pay.|controlling|production|operational|market": "\"This same ___ mechanism\" points back to \"a market mechanism\" in the previous sentence: D. Memory tip: \"this same\" signals a repeat of an earlier word.",
 "eng-2004-p2|Gap 12: …as well as what share the total _____, in cash or kind, goes to each of the four recognized factors of production.|profit|output|dividends|interest": "What is shared out among land, labour, capital and organization is the total output, paid as rent, wages, interest and profit: B. C (dividends) is wrong: dividends go only to shareholders, not to all four factors. Memory tip: national output is shared among the factors of production.",
 "eng-2004-p2|Gap 13: …goes to each of the four recognized _____ of production – land, labour, capital and organization.|factors|methods|managers|agents": "Land, labour, capital and organization are the four factors of production: A. Memory tip: factor rewards are rent (land), wages (labour), interest (capital) and profit (entrepreneur).",
 "eng-2004-p2|Gap 14: …whatever injustice is done by the _____ of the market mechanism…|pricing|buyers and sellers|managers|operations": "Injustice done \"by the ___ of the market mechanism\" means by its workings, its operations: D. Pricing (A) is only one part, and buyers and sellers are people, not part of the mechanism. Memory tip: the operations of a system = how it works.",
 "eng-2004-p2|Gap 15: …this mechanism tends to bring about a state of _____ between the producers and the consumers.|equilibrium|equidistance|opprobrium|conflict": "Balancing supply and demand creates a state of balance, i.e. equilibrium: A. Equidistance means equal distance, opprobrium means disgrace, and conflict is the opposite. Memory tip: equilibrium = balance (economists talk of equilibrium price).",
 "eng-2004-p3|By describing his father as 'an uncommon man', the writer means that he is|amenable|remarkable|strict|kind": "\"Uncommon\" here means unusual and admirable: in a community where housework was \"beneath male dignity\", he cooked and cleaned. So he was remarkable: B. Amenable means easily persuaded, and strict and kind are qualities, not what \"uncommon\" means. Memory tip: uncommon = out of the ordinary.",
 "eng-2004-p3|Which of the following describes the father's role in the family?|Being too hard on the children.|Usurping his wife's role in the family.|Serving the children.|Leading by example.": "The father \"did everything we did... and commanded us by sheer force of his example\", so he led by example: D. Usurping (B) means taking over unfairly, and he helped rather than replaced his wife. Memory tip: look for the sentence that sums up the person's role.",
 "eng-2004-p3|The word 'atmosphere', as used in the first paragraph of the passage, means|pattern|disposition|preference|unity": "The \"atmosphere\" of the home is its general pattern of life: other homes had quarrels and drunkenness, while this one had strict rules and shared work. That gives A. Disposition (B) describes a person's temperament, not a home. Memory tip: in context, atmosphere = the general mood or way of life of a place.",
 "eng-2004-p3|Which of the following statements captures the family's approach to housework?|No job was reserved for anyone on the basis of gender.|Their mother did the cooking and cleaning willingly.|The boys were not allowed to do girls work.|The water needed was provided by everyone.": "\"The boys did girl's work and my father did it with us\": jobs weren't divided by sex, so A. B and C contradict the passage, and D isn't stated. Memory tip: \"on the basis of gender\" = according to whether you are male or female.",
 "eng-2004-p3|A suitable title for the passage is|A Village Life|The Unusual Parent|The Problem of Water|An Experience in Early life": "The passage centres on the writer's father, \"an uncommon man\" who broke with custom, so \"The Unusual Parent\" fits best: B. D is too general, and C covers only one paragraph. Memory tip: if the passage keeps returning to one person, the title is about them.",
 "eng-2004-p4|The charges levelled against the lamb are|pollution, insolence, obstruction and aggression|exuberance, pollution, wickedness and stubbornness|garrulity, loquacity, pride and arrogance|greed, wandering, insolence and disrespect": "The wolf accuses the lamb of muddying the water (pollution), insulting his father (insolence), taking up the path (obstruction), and the lamb's father seizing him by the throat (aggression): A. Memory tip: match each charge in the story to a word in the option.",
 "eng-2004-p4|From the way the story ended, it can be concluded that the|wolf and the lamb parted amicably|lamb did not survive the encounter|wolf had encountered the lamb's parents|lamb offended the wolf quite seriously": "The wolf \"leapt at the lamb from behind and garroted him\", i.e. strangled him. The lamb did not survive: B. Memory tip: garrote = strangle.",
 "eng-2004-p4|Which of the following aptly describes the moral of the story?|No matter how highly placed he is in society, the unmindful lawbreaker will always meet his nemesis.|The law is weak in defence of the poor against the rich and mighty.|The sins of the forefathers are visited upon the children no matter how long it takes.|If you have made up your mind to hang your dog, any rope will do for the purpose.": "The wolf had already decided to eat the lamb, so each time an excuse failed he invented another. That's the proverb in D: once you have decided to hang your dog, any rope will do. Memory tip: the moral should fit the whole story, not one detail.",
 "eng-2004-p4|The expression 'when this excuse failed him' suggests that the|lamb was prepared for the charges levelled against him|wolf was uncertain of the charges levelled against the lamb|wolf only made up the charges levelled against the lamb|lamb was more intelligent than the wolf": "The wolf's charge was an \"excuse\", a made-up pretext (\"wanted to find a pretext for devouring him\"). When it failed, he simply invented a new one: C. Memory tip: pretext = false reason.",
 "eng-2004-p4|The expression 'angry tone and terrible aspect' refers to the wolf's|towering size and thunderous voice|harsh voice and menacing posture|sonorous voice and overbearing attitude|loud voice and fretting movement": "Tone is the sound of the voice (angry → harsh), and aspect is appearance (terrible → menacing): B. Memory tip: aspect = look or appearance.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: bEAr|rare|fear|beer|steer": "The ea in bear is /eə/, like the vowel in rare: A. Fear, beer and steer have /ɪə/ (as in ear). Memory tip: bear, wear and pear rhyme with air; fear, near and dear rhyme with ear.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: nIne|click|gill|pint|pin": "The i in nine is /aɪ/, as in pint: C. Click, gill and pin have the short /ɪ/. Memory tip: pint is an exception; it doesn't rhyme with mint.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): expostulate|exPOStulate|exposTUlate|expostuLATE|EXpostulate": "Expostulate (to protest or argue) is stressed on the second syllable: ex-POS-tu-late, A. Memory tip: verbs ending in -ate usually stress the syllable two before -ate (con-CEN-trate, ex-POS-tu-late).",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): sufficiency|sufficienCY|suffiCIENcy|sufFIciency|SUFficiency": "Sufficiency is stressed like sufficient, on the second syllable: suf-FI-cien-cy, C. Memory tip: adding -cy usually keeps the stress where the base word has it.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): He isn't coming home, is he? _____, he isn't.|No|Why|Yes|Now": "In English the answer word agrees with the fact. He is not coming, so \"No, he isn't\": A. Memory tip: No + negative verb, Yes + positive verb (Yes, he is / No, he isn't).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The poor widow who could not buy the clothes was seen _____ her wool into _____ yesterday.|making/tailoring|to spin/yards|spinning/yarn|to spill/clothes": "Wool is spun into yarn (thread for weaving and knitting), so \"spinning her wool into yarn\": C. Memory tip: spin wool into yarn, weave yarn into cloth.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): _____ his illness, Muhammad could not come to school.|Referring to|With reference to|Due to|Owing to": "Traditional grammar starts such a sentence with \"Owing to\" (because of) and keeps \"due to\" after \"be\" (\"His absence was due to illness\"). So D is the textbook answer (the paper misprints it as \"Owning to\"). The key's \"Due to\" (C) is widely used today and is also accepted. Memory tip: if you can replace it with \"caused by\", use due to; if you can replace it with \"because of\", use owing to.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): You may not have heard the last word on the matter, _____|have you?|may you have?|haven't you?|mayn't have you?": "After a negative statement the tag is positive, and the key uses \"have\" from \"have heard\": \"have you?\": A. Memory tip: negative statement → positive tag.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Agbo says he is not afraid of _____.|no one|nothing|nobody|anybody": "\"Not\" already makes the sentence negative, so we need \"anybody\": \"not afraid of anybody\": D. \"Not... nobody\" or \"not... nothing\" is a double negative. Memory tip: one negative per clause in standard English.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The city _____ as a federal capital only _____ the last twenty years.|existed/over|has existed/for|was existing/from|is existing/in": "A situation that started in the past and continues now uses the present perfect + \"for\" + a period: \"has existed for the last twenty years\": B. Memory tip: for + period (for 20 years); since + starting point (since 2004).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): He not only looked after the children when their parents died, _____ sponsored their education to university.|also he|also|but also|but he": "\"Not only... but also\" is a fixed pair: C. Memory tip: whenever you see \"not only\", look for \"but also\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): After so many trials, the experiment _____.|paid up|paid off|paid out|paid for": "To pay off means to succeed or bring good results: B. Pay up means pay a debt, pay out means spend, and pay for means buy. Memory tip: hard work pays off.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): His supporters rallied round him in moments of _____.|criticism|disillusionment|acrimony|crisis": "Supporters rally round someone in times of crisis (trouble): D. Criticism, disillusionment and acrimony are things they might face, but \"moments of crisis\" is the natural phrase. Memory tip: rally round = gather to support someone in trouble.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): One needs to exemplify or _____ the aspect of the subject being discussed.|declare|demonstrate|satisfy|supply": "To exemplify is to show by example, i.e. to demonstrate: B. Declare, satisfy and supply don't mean illustrate. Memory tip: \"exemplify or demonstrate\" pairs two near-synonyms.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Actually, he forgot the one to _____ the job was given.|whomever|who|whoever|whom": "After a preposition (to) we need the object form: \"the one to whom the job was given\": D. Memory tip: to whom, for whom, with whom.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The police claim that a number of stolen cars _____ recovered.|is being|has being|have been|has been": "\"A number of\" + plural noun takes a plural verb: \"a number of stolen cars have been recovered\": C. Memory tip: a number of (many) → plural; the number of → singular.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The storm _____ havoc on several buildings in the village.|did|wrecked|made|wreaked": "The fixed phrase is \"wreak havoc\", and its past is wreaked: D. Wrecked (B) is a different verb. Memory tip: wreak havoc, wreak vengeance.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): People dislike Mariam because she is _____.|trickful|tricky|a tricker|trickish": "The adjective is tricky (crafty, deceitful): B. Trickful, tricker and trickish aren't standard words. Memory tip: trick → tricky, like luck → lucky.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The chairman refused to shake _____ with the secretary.|hands|his hand|hand|his hands": "The idiom is \"shake hands with someone\", always plural with no possessive: A. Memory tip: shake hands, make friends.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Ali goes to the stadium regularly, but he _____ to the church for months.|didn't go|hasn't been|haven't been|hadn't been": "\"For months\" up to now takes the present perfect, and \"he\" takes \"has\": \"he hasn't been\": B. Memory tip: present perfect + for/since for ongoing periods.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): No teacher of honour would subject his student to any form of _____.|harrassment|harrasment|harassement|harassment": "The spelling is harassment: one r, double s: D. Memory tip: harass has \"one R, two S's\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The interpreter was wrong because he gave the congregation a _____ translation of the pastor's statement.|unilateral|literary|literal|lateral": "A literal translation goes word for word and can miss the real meaning: C. Literary means to do with literature, and unilateral and lateral are about sides. Memory tip: literal = letter by letter.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Each of the houses _____ a new look.|have got|has|have|were given": "\"Each\" is singular, so it takes \"has\": B. Memory tip: each, every, either and neither + singular verb.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The reporter said that the Honourable Speaker _____ impeached.|is to be|will have been|might have been|may have being": "In reported speech the verb shifts back, so \"might have been impeached\": C. \"Is to be\" and \"will have been\" don't shift, and \"may have being\" is ungrammatical. Memory tip: reporting verb in the past → shift the tense back.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): It is _____ responsibility to look after their parents in old age.|their's|theirs'|theirs|their": "Before a noun we use the possessive determiner \"their\": \"their responsibility\": D. Possessive pronouns (theirs, its, ours) never take an apostrophe. Memory tip: their + noun; theirs stands alone (\"It is theirs\").",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): I shall find time for my _____ when I get _____ with this difficult assignment.|pastime/through|pass-time/over|passtime/through|past-time/over": "Pastime is one word with one s, and \"get through with\" means finish: A. Memory tip: a pastime helps time pass, but it's spelt with one s.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): All God's prophets were given the great _____ to preach salvation to people.|commition|comission|commission|commision": "Commission has double m and double s: C. Memory tip: co-MM-i-SS-ion.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Each producer is able to place a price on his product by considering its _____.|assessment|choice|judgement|worth": "Producers price goods according to their worth (value): D. Memory tip: what's it worth? = what's its value?",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The minister addressed the workers to boost their _____.|mural|morale|morality|moral": "Morale is the confidence and spirit of a group: B. A moral is a lesson, morality is right and wrong, and a mural is a wall painting. Memory tip: morale (spirit) has an extra \"e\" for energy.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: causED|realized|frost|released|chanced": "After a voiced sound like /z/ in \"cause\", -ed is pronounced /d/, so caused ends in /d/. Realized also ends in /d/: A. Released and chanced end in /t/. Memory tip: -ed after voiced sounds = /d/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: misCHief|champagne|Christmas|brochure|ritual": "The ch in mischief is /tʃ/. The t in ritual (RICH-oo-al) is also /tʃ/: D. Champagne and brochure have /ʃ/, and Christmas has /k/. Memory tip: t before \"u\" is often /tʃ/ (nature, ritual, picture).",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: Judge|pleasure|camouflage|spinach|gear": "The j in judge is /dʒ/. In British English the ch in spinach is pronounced /dʒ/ (SPIN-idge): C. Pleasure and camouflage have /ʒ/, and gear has /g/. Memory tip: /dʒ/ is the sound in jam and judge.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: The student BOUGHT the book.|Who bought the book?|What did the student buy?|How will the student get the book?|Did the student steal the book?": "Stressing BOUGHT corrects the action: \"Did the student steal the book?\" No, he BOUGHT it: D. A would stress STUDENT, and B would stress BOOK. Memory tip: stressed verb → corrects the action.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: The teacher wants MY pen.|Does the teacher want your ruler?|Who wants my pen?|Does the teacher want his pen?|What does the teacher want?": "Stressing MY corrects whose pen it is: \"Does the teacher want his pen?\" No, MY pen: C. A would stress PEN, and B would stress TEACHER. Memory tip: stressed possessive → corrects the owner.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The investigators stated clearly that they had reached a dead end in their scrutiny of suspects in the murder case.|The end had come for the suspects in the murder case.|There was no further progress in the investigation of the murder suspects.|The investigators did not know what to do with suspects in the murder.|The investigators had evidence to sentence the suspects to death in the end.": "A dead end is a point where no further progress is possible, so the investigation got nowhere: B. A takes \"end\" literally, and C and D change the meaning. Memory tip: dead end = no way forward.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: In many countries, democracy is ostensibly being practiced.|Many countries have effective democracy.|Democracy is indeed being practiced in many countries.|Democracy is apparently practiced in many countries.|Many countries have democracy in practise.": "Ostensibly means apparently, seemingly, and often hints that the reality is different: C. B (\"indeed\") says the opposite. Memory tip: ostensible = shown on the surface.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Adamu's father is a key figure in that ministry.|Adamu's father's position is essential in the ministry.|The ministry trusts Adamu's father as a central figure.|Adamu's father is a figurehead in the ministry.|Adamu's father keeps the key to the ministry.": "A key figure is someone whose role is essential: A. A figurehead (C) has a title but no real power, and D takes \"key\" literally. Memory tip: key = most important (key point, key player).",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Everyone was ready to play the devil's advocate in the impeachment controversy.|Everyone was willing to fight for the defenceless citizens no matter the consequences.|Everyone was willing to defend an unpopular point of view concerning the impeachment.|Everyone was willing to speak against the impeachment to encourage discussion on it.|Everyone was willing to be an evil genius in the controversy.": "A devil's advocate argues against something, not because they believe it, but to test it and get people discussing it: C. B describes defending an unpopular view out of belief, which is different. Memory tip: play devil's advocate = argue the other side for the sake of argument.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The government warns that drink-driving is punishable under the law.|A drunkard driving can be punished.|Drinking and driving is an offence.|Driving while drinking is an offence.|Driving while drunk is an offence.": "Drink-driving means driving after drinking too much alcohol, i.e. driving while drunk: D. A is too narrow (a drunkard), and B and C miss the point that drunkenness is the offence. Memory tip: drink-driving = drunk driving.",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT stress pattern from the others.|result|ratify|taxi|famous": "ReSULT is stressed on the second syllable; RAtify, TAxi and FAmous are stressed on the first: A. Memory tip: clap the beats to hear the odd one out.",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT stress pattern from the others.|orthopaedic|secondment|photographic|oriental": "SeCONDment is stressed on the second syllable; orthoPAEdic, photoGRAphic and oriENtal are stressed on the third: B. Memory tip: words ending in -ic and -al often stress the syllable just before the ending.",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT vowel sound from the others.|pot|hall|water|altar": "Pot has the short /ɒ/; hall, water and altar have the long /ɔː/: A. Memory tip: \"al\" before a consonant often gives /ɔː/ (all, ball, salt, altar).",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT vowel sound from the others.|tout|foe|foul|owl": "Foe has /əʊ/ (as in go); tout, foul and owl have /aʊ/ (as in cow): B. Memory tip: \"ou\" and \"ow\" are often /aʊ/, but \"oe\" is /əʊ/.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The presence of the captain makes the sailors ILL AT EASE.|impatient|easily ill|uncomfortable|sickly": "Ill at ease means uncomfortable or uneasy: C. \"Easily ill\" and \"sickly\" take \"ill\" literally. Memory tip: at ease = relaxed; ill at ease = uncomfortable.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Ibro shows enough LIBERALITY with his meager income.|insensitivity|prodigality|frugality|generosity": "Liberality means generosity: D. Prodigality is wasteful spending, and frugality is the opposite (careful spending). Memory tip: liberal with money = generous.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: It is a MISNOMER to call three thousand naira a living wage.|a wrong description|an incontrovertible assertion|an appropriate term|a mishmash": "A misnomer is a wrong or misleading name or description: A. Memory tip: mis-nomer = wrong name (nom = name).",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The press described the efforts of the government in PEJORATIVE terms.|superlative|palpable|critical|contemptible": "Pejorative words express contempt or disapproval. The key's \"contemptible\" (D) carries the idea of contempt, and \"critical\" (C) is also a fair synonym, so both are accepted. Memory tip: pejorative ↔ complimentary.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The town was in such a TURMOIL that the dance was called off.|state of darkness|state of confusion|rainy state|mourning state": "Turmoil is a state of great confusion and disturbance: B. Memory tip: turmoil = chaos.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The festivals create in the people a feeling of pride in their cultural HERITAGE.|legacy|possession|history|heirloom": "Heritage is what has been handed down from the past, i.e. a legacy: A. An heirloom (D) is one valued object passed down. Memory tip: cultural heritage = cultural legacy.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Okonkwo manages his household WITH A HEAVY HAND.|using the cane on every occasion|without tolerating weaknesses|like a powerful dictator|like a heavyweight champion": "To manage something \"with a heavy hand\" is to be harsh and oppressive, like a dictator: C. A is too literal, and D plays on \"heavy\". Memory tip: heavy-handed = harsh, using too much force.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: In spite of constant financial support from his father, Udenyi treats his studies with considerable LEVITY.|lassitude|wastefulness|enthusiasm|seriousness": "Levity means not taking serious things seriously (frivolity). No option matches exactly, and seriousness (D) is the opposite. Lassitude (A), a lack of energy and interest, is the closest, and the key gives A. Memory tip: when no option fits exactly, pick the closest in effect.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The school's badge is the INSIGNIA of office for all the prefects in the school.|seal|recognition|power|symbol": "Insignia are badges or emblems that symbolize rank or office: D. A seal is a stamp, and recognition and power are what the badge stands for, not the badge itself. Memory tip: insignia = symbols of office.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: FUNNILY ENOUGH, the priest prayed for the robber who shot him.|timidly|fearlessly|unexpectedly|disappointingly": "\"Funnily enough\" introduces something surprising, i.e. unexpected: C. It doesn't mean amusing here. Memory tip: funnily enough = strangely enough.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The MOTTLED skin of a person with HIV indicates an advanced stage of its development.|pimply|scaly|brown|spotted": "Mottled means marked with irregular spots or blotches of colour, i.e. spotted: D. C (brown) is wrong: mottled is a pattern, not a colour. Memory tip: a mottled leaf is blotchy.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The prosecutor was fully able to SUBSTANTIATE the charge.|expatiate on|weaken|prove|dismiss": "To substantiate a charge is to back it up with evidence, i.e. to prove it: C. Expatiate on means to speak at length about. Memory tip: substantiate = give substance (evidence) to.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The manager's knowledge of the strike is of the UTMOST importance.|basic|genuine|standard|paramount": "Utmost means greatest, so \"of the utmost importance\" means of paramount (supreme) importance: D. Memory tip: utmost = most extreme.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: There has been A DOWNTURN in the affairs of the company.|a turn-around|a massive increase|a decline|little progress": "A downturn is a decline (a fall in business or activity): C. A turnaround is the opposite. Memory tip: down-turn = turning downwards.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: His plans BOOMERANGED on him.|catapulted|backfired|fell|bounced": "A plan that boomerangs comes back and hurts the person who made it, i.e. it backfires: B. Memory tip: a boomerang comes back to the thrower.",
 "|Choose the option that has the stress on the FIRST syllable.|condemn|intact|afternoon|bachelor": "BAchelor is stressed on the first syllable; conDEMN and inTACT on the second; afterNOON on the third: D. Memory tip: afternoon carries its strongest stress on NOON.",
 "|Choose the option that has the stress on the FIRST syllable.|impossible|criticism|eleven|circulation": "CRIticism is stressed on the first syllable; imPOSsible and eLEven on the second; circuLAtion on the third: B. Memory tip: -tion words stress the syllable just before -tion.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The noise of the fans UNNERVED the star player.|calmed|confused|refreshed|helped": "To unnerve someone is to make them nervous, so the opposite is to calm them: A. Memory tip: un-nerve = take away one's nerve (courage).",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: We found a SHADY place for the display.|an enclosed|a stuffy|an open|an unsafe": "A shady place is sheltered from the sun, so the opposite is an open (exposed, sunny) place: C. Memory tip: shady ↔ open/sunny.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The player WRITHED IN PAIN after the fall.|cried out|remained still|walked out|shook violently": "To writhe in pain is to twist and turn, so the opposite is to remain still: B. \"Shook violently\" (D) is close in meaning. Memory tip: writhe = twist about.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The ship was IMPERILLED by high winds.|saved|deceived|destroyed|piloted": "Imperilled means put in danger (peril), so the opposite is saved: A. Memory tip: peril = danger.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The team got an ECSTATIC welcome from the crowd.|a joyous|an unexpected|an expected|a cold": "An ecstatic welcome is wildly joyful, so the opposite is a cold one: D. Joyous (A) is a synonym. Memory tip: warm welcome ↔ cold welcome.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Sailors are unusually DAUNTLESS in their exploits.|excited|ruthless|frightened|selfless": "Dauntless means fearless, so the opposite is frightened: C. Memory tip: daunt = intimidate; dauntless = not intimidated.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Beauty queens sometimes wear OUTLANDISH dresses.|familiar|flashy|beautiful|attractive": "Outlandish means strange and unfamiliar (bizarre), so the opposite is familiar: A. Memory tip: out-land-ish = from another land.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: My father's presentation was rather CASUAL.|divisive|vital|informal|formal": "Casual means relaxed and informal, so the opposite is formal: D. Informal (C) is a synonym. Memory tip: casual dress ↔ formal dress.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The Flying Eagles put up a PLUCKY defence against their opponents.|weak|strong|careless|tactful": "Plucky means brave and spirited, so the opposite is weak: A. Strong (B) is close in meaning. Memory tip: pluck = courage.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The journalist said he was working FREELANCE.|satisfactorily|without a pay|dependently|tirelessly": "A freelance worker works independently for different clients, so the opposite is working dependently (as an employee): C. Memory tip: freelance = self-employed.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The injured man is determined to GET BACK AT his assailant.|visit|forgive|identify|attack": "To \"get back at\" someone is to take revenge, so the opposite is to forgive: B. Attack (D) goes with revenge. Memory tip: get back at = pay back.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The dictator wanted TRACTABLE men in his cabinet.|tough|intelligent|unruly|reliable": "Tractable means easy to control or manage, so the opposite is unruly: C. Memory tip: tractable ↔ intractable (unmanageable).",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: After the war, the victors became increasingly VINDICTIVE.|treacherous|arrogant|vociferous|friendly": "Vindictive means wanting revenge. Of the options, friendly is the furthest from that: D. Treacherous and arrogant are other bad qualities, and vociferous means loud. Memory tip: vindictive ↔ forgiving.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The POTENCY of the drug has been acknowledged.|action|inefficacy|power|loss": "Potency is strength or effectiveness, so the opposite is inefficacy (ineffectiveness): B. Power (C) is a synonym. Memory tip: potent drug = strong drug.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The man who scared the girl was a bit DERANGED.|crazy|amorous|dangerous|sane": "Deranged means mentally unbalanced, so the opposite is sane: D. Crazy (A) is a synonym. Memory tip: deranged = out of order (mentally).",
 "eng-2006-p1|The expression 'as capital is the product of human work' seems to support the argument that|rural people are poor because they lack work opportunities to fight back poverty|urban productivity is affected by mass migration from the rural areas|urban areas will continue to experience overcrowding as long as workplaces are concentrated there|rural dwellers who move to capital cities often overcome poverty": "\"Capital\" here means wealth, and the sentence says wealth comes from work. So people with no work opportunities can't build wealth, which is why rural people stay poor: A. The key gives D, but that misreads \"capital\" as capital cities, and the passage says migrants end up in urban unemployment, not out of poverty. Memory tip: watch out for options that play on a different meaning of a word in the passage.",
 "eng-2006-p1|The expression 'mutual poisoning', as used in the passage, suggests that|migration negates effective urban and rural life|migration can make urban and rural people sick|rural and urban areas complement each other|rural people violate urban hygiene": "\"Mutual poisoning\" means each side harms the other: villages lose their people, and cities are overloaded with unemployment, robbery and slums. So migration ruins effective life in both: A. B takes \"poisoning\" literally, and C says the opposite. Memory tip: mutual = both ways.",
 "eng-2006-p1|The writer describes the features of a 'cottage industry' as|remedying urban migration, available employment, simplicity of operations and affordable products|rural location, simplicity, local sourcing of raw materials and affordability|affordable energy sources, availability of marketing of products|availability of capital, high productivity, low capital demand and simplicity of operations": "The four requirements are: workplaces in rural areas, simple production methods, local materials, and cheap enough to set up in large numbers. That's B. A, C and D bring in things never listed (energy sources, high productivity, capital). Memory tip: match each word in the option to one requirement in the passage.",
 "eng-2006-p1|Which of the following statements is true according to the passage?|Urban workplaces are responsible for rural poverty and misery.|Providing work opportunities in the rural areas will lead to low productivity, illiteracy and poverty.|Rural work opportunities could reduce urban unemployment.|Urban workplaces absorb rural/urban migrants.": "Rural unemployment becomes urban unemployment through migration, so creating work in rural areas would also ease urban unemployment: C. B reverses the cause, and D contradicts \"cities that cannot absorb them\". Memory tip: follow the chain of cause and effect in the passage.",
 "eng-2006-p1|In the first sentence of the passage, the word 'work', used differently, can be explained as|industrial and earn|job and struggle|employment and insure|career and prepare": "In the first sentence, \"work opportunities\" means jobs, and \"work their way out of poverty\" means struggle out of it by effort: B. Memory tip: the same word can carry two meanings in one sentence, so read each in context.",
 "eng-2006-p2|Which of the following best summarizes the argument in the passage?|There are three laudable though unequal ways of news presentation by a daily paper.|Press freedom is an important feature of a daily newspaper.|There are three obvious functions of a daily newspaper.|A good newspaper is that which is simple, logical and balanced in its news coverage.": "After comparing three types of paper, the writer concludes that the ideal one presents news clearly and simply, in good English, with views from different outlooks. That is simple, logical and balanced: D. A misreads the opening, which says the three ways are \"not equally laudable\". Memory tip: a summary question is usually answered by the conclusion.",
 "eng-2006-p2|The writer seems to suggest that|there can be no unbiased newspaper presentation|objectivity, simplicity and clarity are the hallmark of good journalism|the important function of a daily is to further develop the news headlines which the radio usually produces|sensational reporting is a feature of press freedom": "The ideal paper presents news \"clearly and simply\" and shows \"the points of views of various competent people of different outlooks\", i.e. objectivity, simplicity and clarity: B. A contradicts the first type's \"unbiased view\". Memory tip: suggestion questions are answered by the writer's ideal, not by the examples.",
 "eng-2006-p2|The phrase 'political hysteria', as used in the passage, means|tension and ill feeling|hatred and rivalries|anxiety and misunderstanding|crisis and confusion": "Hysteria is uncontrolled emotion. Propaganda papers appeal \"to emotion, not reason\", stirring up tension and ill feeling: A. B (hatred and rivalries) goes too far, and C and D miss the emotional heat. Memory tip: hysteria = wild, uncontrolled emotion.",
 "eng-2006-p2|According to the passage, the desirable newspaper is the one which|is eclectic and modest|is popular and sensational and has a slight political favour|does not create political hysteria|is intellectual and unbiased": "The desirable paper offers sound articles in good English and the views of people of different outlooks, so it is intellectual and unbiased: D. B describes the second type, and C names only one feature. Memory tip: choose the option that sums up all the qualities listed.",
 "eng-2006-p2|An appropriate title for this passage is|The Logic of Press Freedom|The Political Hysteria of a Daily Newspaper|Three Ideal Functions of a Daily Newspaper|The Ideal Daily Newspaper": "The passage reviews three kinds of daily paper to arrive at what the ideal one should be: D. C is wrong because the three types aren't all ideal. Memory tip: the title is often hinted at in the final paragraph.",
 "eng-2006-p3|One argument in support of women's liberation is that it|compels men to take over the tasks of cooking, cleaning and other household chores|encourages Nigerian women to eke out a living independently|enables more women to attain higher positions|brings improvement at home, at work and in politics": "The passage shows the movement changing life \"at home, at work and in politics\" for the better, so D. A overstates (men share chores, they don't take them over), and C is only one part. Memory tip: choose the option that covers the whole argument.",
 "eng-2006-p3|Which of the following captures the vision of the writer on the future of Nigerian women?|It is possible for Nigerian women to attain equal status with men.|Nigerians will never accept a woman president for the country.|Nigerian higher institutions can produce more women doctors, lawyers and accountants than men.|If the present trend is not checked, Nigerian women will take over the roles of men.": "The writer charts women's steady progress (ministers, managers, senators, deputy governors), things that \"would have been unthinkable\" a few years ago, pointing towards equality: A. The key gives D, but the passage says role reversal \"is the exception, not the rule\", and the writer welcomes the change rather than calling for it to be checked. Memory tip: a writer's vision matches their tone, which here is positive and measured.",
 "eng-2006-p3|The expression 'causing sweeping changes', as used in the passage, means|producing far-reaching effects|cleaning up the Nigerian society|improving husband-wife relationship|resulting in unpalatable consequences": "Sweeping changes are wide-ranging, far-reaching ones: A. B takes \"sweeping\" literally, C is too narrow, and D adds a negative judgement. Memory tip: sweeping = broad, extensive.",
 "eng-2006-p3|From the last paragraph, it can be concluded that|the liberation movement is women's reaction to men's actions|Nigerian men are opposed to the women liberation movement|the effects of women's liberation are felt only at home and at work|the women liberation movement has brought untold hardship on many Nigerian men": "Women point out that \"it was they, the men who created the conditions leading to the reaction of the women\", so the movement is a reaction to men's actions: A. B overgeneralizes \"some men\", and C contradicts \"politics\". Memory tip: \"reaction\" in the passage matches \"reaction\" in the option.",
 "eng-2006-p3|Which of the following statements is true according to the passage?|The quest for women's liberation appears to be making progress in Nigeria.|The Nigerian society is now free of all forms of discrimination against women.|Women in Nigeria have now established a ministry called Ministry for Women Affairs.|The majority of Nigerian women have now taken over men's traditional roles.": "Changes are \"already being felt\" at home, at work and in politics, so the quest seems to be progressing: A. B and D overstate, and C is wrong because the government, not the women, set up the ministry. Memory tip: hedged options (\"appears to be\") often beat absolute ones.",
 "eng-2006-p4|Gap 16: There is often _____ between the pronunciation of a word and its spelling.|a similarity|concord|discrepancy|discord": "A discrepancy is a mismatch between two things that should agree, here pronunciation and spelling: C. Concord and similarity are the opposite, and discord is a quarrel. Memory tip: discrepancy = difference that shouldn't be there.",
 "eng-2006-p4|Gap 17: One cannot always tell how to spell a word _____ its pronunciation.|as from|throughout|as to|by": "To tell something \"by\" a sign is to judge it from that sign (\"you can tell by his face\"), so you can't always tell the spelling by the sound: D. C (\"as to\") is wrong: \"as to\" means \"concerning\" and doesn't fit. Memory tip: tell by = judge from.",
 "eng-2006-p4|Gap 18: In order to understand the peculiarity of the English spelling _____, it is good to know something about the history of the language.|system|confusion|difficulty|code": "The passage opened with \"its spelling system\", and \"the English spelling system\" continues that: A. Memory tip: cloze passages often reuse an earlier phrase.",
 "eng-2006-p4|Gap 19: While the _____ people spoke English, the literate upper classes spoke French and wrote in Latin.|middle-class|less enlightened|uneducated|educated": "English was spoken by \"people who could neither read nor write\", in contrast to the \"literate upper classes\". They were the uneducated: C. Memory tip: look for a contrast word (while) and pick the opposite of \"literate\".",
 "eng-2006-p4|Gap 20: Later, when English became a _____ language, there was no system for spelling its words.|literary|coded|romantic|written": "Spelling only becomes an issue when a language is written: D. Literary means relating to literature. Memory tip: no writing, no spelling.",
 "eng-2006-p4|Gap 21: …the first writers of English were French-speaking _____ who knew English only slightly.|scribes|newscasters|interpreters|orators": "Scribes wrote documents by hand before printing, so they were the first writers: A. Newscasters, interpreters and orators speak rather than write. Memory tip: scribe → script, write.",
 "eng-2006-p4|Gap 22: …therefore, they carried many French spelling _____ into English.|idiosyncrasies|habits|attitudes|mannerisms": "Writers carry their spelling habits from one language into another: B. Idiosyncrasies (A, peculiarities) also fit, so both are accepted. The key gives D (mannerisms), but mannerisms are personal gestures, not spelling patterns. Memory tip: spelling habits, speech mannerisms.",
 "eng-2006-p4|Gap 23: …who were used to writing in Latin, often _____ letters into words even when they were not pronounced.|juxtaposed|interjected|inserted|interpolated": "They put (inserted) silent letters into words, for example the b in debt from Latin debitum: C. Interpolated is used of words added to a text, and juxtaposed means placed side by side. Memory tip: insert = put in.",
 "eng-2006-p4|Gap 24: …because the _____ words in Latin was spelled that way.|antecedent|corresponding|synonymous|opposing": "The matching, or corresponding, Latin word was spelled with that letter: B. Antecedent means earlier, and synonymous means same in meaning. Memory tip: corresponding = matching.",
 "eng-2006-p4|Gap 25: Finally, the confusion when the _____ of certain words changed while the spelling remained the same.|diction|pronunciation|morphology|orthography": "When pronunciation changes but spelling stays the same, the two drift apart: B. Orthography (D) is spelling itself, which did not change. Memory tip: this is why \"knight\" has a silent k; the sound changed but the spelling did not.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The usually hostile crowd was captivated by the player's WINSOME attitude.|colourful|obnoxious|drunken|friendly": "Winsome means charming and likeable, so its opposite is obnoxious (very unpleasant): B. Friendly (D) is close in meaning. Memory tip: winsome = winning, appealing.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He gave himself up by his PLEBEIAN tastes.|robust|laughable|voracious|patrician": "Plebeian means of the common people, so its opposite is patrician (aristocratic): D. Memory tip: in ancient Rome, plebeians were the common people and patricians the nobles.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The reporter made a MASTERLY analysis of the situation.|a proper|an unskilful|an in-depth|a mistaken": "Masterly means very skilful, so its opposite is unskilful: B. Memory tip: masterly = done like a master.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Akpan was able to DOUSE the flames with the new piece of equipment.|reduce|reinforce|extinguish|ignite": "To douse flames is to put them out, so the opposite is to ignite them: D. Extinguish (C) is a synonym. Memory tip: douse with water.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: A TENTATIVE solution was provided.|A definitive|A provisional|An amicable|A convincing": "Tentative means provisional, not final, so its opposite is definitive: A. Provisional (B) is a synonym. Memory tip: tentative ↔ definitive.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The notice reads, 'No CASH transaction in this hall'.|gainful|cheque|money|business": "Payment in cash is contrasted with payment by cheque: B. Money (C) includes cash. Memory tip: in \"opposite\" questions, look for the contrasting item in the same category.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: In our last play, Segun acted the HERO.|assassin|villain|devil|criminal": "In a play, the hero is opposed by the villain: B. Memory tip: hero ↔ villain.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The man drew a sword as people CONGREGATED round him.|fled from|gathered round|praised|mobbed": "To congregate is to gather together, so the opposite is to flee from: A. Gathered round (B) is a synonym. Memory tip: congregation = a gathering.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: NO SOONER had he arrived than it started to rain.|simultaneously|seldom|just before|long before": "\"No sooner... than\" means one thing happened immediately after another, so the opposite is long before: D. Memory tip: no sooner = immediately.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The police found a CONCLUSIVE proof of the boy's guilt.|a corroborative|a consolatory|an incriminating|a doubtful": "Conclusive proof settles the question, so the opposite is doubtful proof: D. Corroborative and incriminating proof both support guilt. Memory tip: conclusive = final, decisive.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He has no reason to HAVE HIS HEART IN HIS MOUTH.|be afraid|feel guilty|be confident|think negatively": "To have your heart in your mouth is to be very frightened, so the opposite is to be confident: C. Memory tip: heart in mouth = terrified.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: That little boy has become quite CHUBBY.|intelligent|tall|thin|huge": "Chubby means plump, so its opposite is thin: C. Memory tip: chubby cheeks = round and fat.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The point you have made is quite APT.|helpful|irrelevant|illogical|insensitive": "Apt means fitting and relevant, so its opposite is irrelevant: B. Memory tip: an apt remark fits the situation.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The chief said he would use the power INHERENT IN his office to stop the celebration.|impervious to|allotted to|unrelated to": "Inherent in means naturally part of, so its opposite is unrelated to: C. The paper gives only three options here. Memory tip: inherent = built in.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The action was PREMEDITATED.|unplanned|unnecessary|catastrophic|uncoordinated": "Premeditated means planned in advance, so its opposite is unplanned: A. Memory tip: pre-meditate = think about beforehand.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: buSHes|shoes|rapes|shapes|rakes": "The underlined sh in bushes is /ʃ/. Shoes and shapes both begin with /ʃ/, so A and C are both accepted. Memory tip: sh is almost always /ʃ/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: CHaracter|church|arch|unique|charade": "The ch in character is /k/. The \"que\" in unique is also /k/: C. Church and arch have /tʃ/, and charade has /ʃ/. Memory tip: Greek words keep ch = k (character, chemistry, chorus).",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: garaGE|gear|sure|pressure|measure": "In British English the -ge of garage is /ʒ/, the sound in measure: D. Sure and pressure have /ʃ/, and gear has /g/. Memory tip: /ʒ/ is the buzzing version of /ʃ/ (measure, vision, garage).",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: cOck|cup|cope|cork|cop": "The o in cock is the short /ɒ/, as in cop: D. Cup has /ʌ/, cope /əʊ/, cork /ɔː/. Memory tip: cock/cop/hot/dog all share /ɒ/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: sAYs|fierce|wet|rain|dam": "Says is pronounced /sez/, with the short /e/ of wet: B. Its \"ay\" is not like say. Memory tip: says and said both have /e/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: hUrt|hate|hut|girl|hot": "The ur in hurt is /ɜː/, as in girl: C. Memory tip: ur, ir and er before a consonant usually say /ɜː/ (hurt, girl, her).",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Adamu is rather MEDDLESOME in dealing with his friends.|intimidating|quarrelsome|impertinent|uncaring": "A meddlesome person interferes in others' business, which is impertinent (rudely intrusive): C. Quarrelsome means always arguing. Memory tip: meddle = interfere.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: His discovery of the vaccine became a QUANTUM LEAP in the search for a scientific cure for the illness.|slow but steady step|victorious battle|fast but sure step|great improvement": "A quantum leap is a sudden, huge advance, i.e. a great improvement: D. A and C wrongly suggest gradual steps. Memory tip: quantum leap = giant step forward.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The party men REVEALED all night when the election results were announced.|mourned|fought|celebrated|discussed": "The paper prints \"revealed\", but it means \"revelled\": to revel is to celebrate noisily, so C. Memory tip: revel = party; reveal = show.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The judge emphasized his MORBID desires in his judgement.|uncritical|unpleasant|inordinate|ravenous": "Morbid means unhealthily interested in death or gruesome things, so unpleasant is the closest: B. Memory tip: morbid curiosity = unhealthy interest in dark things.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The commander said that the battle would be sustained, total and RELENTLESS.|brutal|fierce|innocuous|continuous": "Relentless means never easing up, i.e. continuous: D. Fierce and brutal describe intensity, not the lack of a break. Memory tip: relentless = without relenting (letting up).",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: His loss suddenly became REDEEMABLE.|repulsive|incurable|exclusive|recoverable": "Redeemable means able to be recovered or regained: D. Incurable (B) is the opposite. Memory tip: redeem = buy back, recover.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The move to expel the chairman was SANCTIONED by members of the party.|opposed|supported|unleased|initiated": "To sanction a move is to approve or support it: B. (It can also mean penalize, as in \"sanctions\", but here members approved the move.) Memory tip: sanction has two opposite meanings; the context decides which.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: After the meeting, I read a LEER on his face as he looked at the woman.|bad look|satisfying meaning|poor smile|pleasant disposition": "A leer is a nasty, sly or suggestive look: A. Memory tip: leer = unpleasant stare.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The plan has gone AWRY.|wrong|wild|well|wide": "To go awry is to go wrong: A. Memory tip: awry = off course.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Because Jamiu is so easily OFFENDED, he was not considered for the post of headboy.|troublesome|stubborn|docile|irritable": "Someone easily offended is touchy and quick-tempered, i.e. irritable: D. Docile (C) is the opposite. Memory tip: thin-skinned = easily offended.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The witness provided a MONSTROUS ACCOUNT of the event.|shocking|supportive|verbatim|tragic": "A monstrous account is outrageous and shocking: A. Verbatim means word for word. Memory tip: monstrous = appalling.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He is a PROLIFIC writer.|an influential|a very productive|a clever|a well-known": "A prolific writer produces a lot of work, i.e. is very productive: B. Influential and well-known describe fame, not output. Memory tip: prolific = producing much.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The chairman advised the participants to follow CONVENTION in stating their points.|eloquence|prudence|tradition|advice": "Convention is the accepted, traditional way of doing things: C. Memory tip: conventional = traditional.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The recommended novel has a CONVOLUTED theme.|an attractive|a simple|a complicated|a disgusting": "Convoluted means complicated and hard to follow: C. Memory tip: convoluted = twisted.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The government has announced its PREPAREDNESS for the scheme.|determination|pleasure|readiness|regret": "Preparedness is readiness: C. Determination is firmness of purpose. Memory tip: prepared = ready.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same stress pattern as the given word (stressed syllable in capitals): DOVEtail|proviso|jargon|simplicity|psychiatry": "DOVEtail is stressed on the first syllable, like JARgon: B. ProVIso, simPLIcity and psyCHIatry are stressed on the second. Memory tip: compound nouns are usually stressed on the first part.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same stress pattern as the given word (stressed syllable in capitals): apPArent|paragraph|appetite|arrested|telephone": "ApPArent is stressed on the second syllable, like arRESted: C. PAragraph, APpetite and TElephone are stressed on the first. The answer key in circulation gives no answer here. Memory tip: apparent follows appear (ap-PEAR).",
 "|Choose the option that has the same stress pattern as the given word (stressed syllable in capitals): unFAIR|first-class|instant|insight|towards": "UnFAIR is stressed on the second syllable, like toWARDS: D. \"First-class\" (A), used as an adjective, is also stressed on its second part, so it is accepted too. INstant and INsight are stressed on the first. The answer key gives no answer for this one. Memory tip: un- adjectives keep the stress of the base word (FAIR → unFAIR).",
 "|Choose the option that has the stress on the FIRST syllable.|mature|madam|frequent (verb)|convict (verb)": "MAdam is stressed on the first syllable. MaTURE, and the verbs freQUENT and conVICT, are stressed on the second: B. Memory tip: FREquent (adjective) vs freQUENT (verb).",
 "|Choose the option that has the stress on the FIRST syllable.|pronounce|prepare|provoke|insult (noun)": "INsult (noun) is stressed on the first syllable; pronounce, prepare and provoke are stressed on the second: D. Memory tip: an INsult, to inSULT.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): Catholicism|catholiCIsm|CathoLIcism|CAtholicism|caTHOlicism": "Catholicism is stressed on the second syllable: ca-THOL-i-cism, D. C is wrong: adding -ism moves the stress away from the first syllable of Catholic. Memory tip: CATHolic → caTHOLicism.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): Genealogy|geneAlogy|Genealogy|geNEAlogy|geneALOGY": "Genealogy is stressed on the \"al\" syllable: ge-ne-AL-o-gy. A marks exactly that. The key's D (geneALOGY) starts with the stressed AL but capitalizes three syllables, so it is accepted too. Memory tip: words ending in -logy stress the syllable just before it (bi-OL-o-gy, ge-ne-AL-o-gy).",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT stress pattern from the others.|syllabus|competent|quality|represent": "RepreSENT is stressed on the third syllable; SYLlabus, COMpetent and QUAlity are stressed on the first: D. Memory tip: represent works like re-pre-SENT, with the stress at the end.",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT stress pattern from the others.|registration|quadruplicate|ability|revivalist": "RegisTRAtion is stressed on the third syllable; quaDRUplicate, aBIlity and reVIvalist are stressed on the second: A. B is wrong: quadruplicate follows the same pattern as the others. Memory tip: -tion words stress the syllable just before -tion.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The class has fallen in with the teacher's plan.|The class has accepted the plans|The class has ignored the plans|The class has modified the plans|The class has rejected the plans": "To \"fall in with\" a plan is to agree to it: A. Memory tip: fall in with = go along with.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: I owe you far less than you owe me.|I owe you something, but you owe me much more|Your debt is not much greater than mine|What we owe each other is approximately the same|My debt to you is greater than yours to me": "Both people owe something, but the other person's debt is much larger: A. B and C weaken \"far less\", and D reverses it. Memory tip: \"far less\" = a big difference.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Ogiri takes after his father, he fawns upon anyone with influence.|Ogiri, who looks like his father, follows rich and influential people about|Ogiri, who always follows his father, tries to act like an influential man|Like his father, Ogiri hates influential people|Like his father, Ogiri likes to flatter people with influence in society": "\"Takes after his father\" means he resembles him in character, and \"fawns upon\" means flatters. So like his father, he flatters influential people: D. A takes \"takes after\" as looks, B as following, and C reverses the meaning. Memory tip: take after = resemble (a parent).",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: He had hardly spoken when the bell rang.|He found it difficult to speak, and then the bell rang|The bell rang very soon after he spoke|When the bell rang, he was still speaking|He spoke in a harsh manner and the bell rang": "\"Had hardly... when\" means the second thing happened almost immediately after the first: B. A misreads \"hardly\" as \"with difficulty\". Memory tip: hardly... when = no sooner... than.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: After the war, the victors became increasingly vindictive.|Repressive measures were taken against those who lost the war|Those who won the war became treacherous|Vengeful attacks were incessantly carried out on those who lost the war|Friendly measures were taken to heal the wounds": "Vindictive means wanting revenge, so the victors carried out vengeful attacks on the losers: C. A (repressive) and B (treacherous) are other bad behaviours. Memory tip: vindictive = vengeful.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: I think she takes her guardian's support for granted.|She wants her guardian to grant her more support|Her guardian has been helping her for a long time, but she does not show enough gratitude|She thinks her guardian will no longer support her as he has been doing|Her guardian has been helping her for a long time, and she is very grateful to him": "To take something for granted is to fail to value it because you've come to expect it: B. D says the opposite, and A misreads \"granted\". Memory tip: take for granted = not appreciate.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: You are to write a report and give it to the security office.|After writing your report, give it to the security office|You are writing a report, as the security office directed|You have been ordered to write a report, which you will give to the security office|The report you are writing should be given to the security office": "\"You are to...\" expresses an instruction or order: you've been told to write a report and hand it in. That's C. A and D assume the report is already written or being written. Memory tip: \"be to + verb\" = an instruction or arrangement.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The dispute over land acquisition has now come to a head.|The problem has now been referred to the headquarters|Because of the dispute, land acquisition has now been halted|The problem has now been solved|The problem has reached crisis proportions": "To come to a head is to reach a crisis point: D. A takes \"head\" as headquarters. Memory tip: things come to a head when they can't be ignored any longer.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: In our college, the regulations concerning resits are a grey area.|The regulations have often been ignored|The regulations are not very clear|The regulations have just been revised|The regulations are perfectly clear": "A grey area is something unclear or not clearly defined: B. D is the opposite. Memory tip: grey area = neither black nor white.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The prefect reeled off the names of all the students who broke the library law.|The prefect gave an accurate list of the students who disobeyed library regulations|The prefect omitted the names of the students who broke into the law library|The prefect submitted a complete list of the students who stole library books|The prefect gave a disjointed list of the students in the law library": "To reel off names is to recite them quickly and easily, and \"broke the library law\" means disobeyed library rules. So A. The key gives C, but that changes \"broke the library law\" to \"stole books\" and \"reeled off\" to \"submitted\". Memory tip: reel off = rattle off from memory.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The brave hunter killed the _____ in the forest.|big spitting black cobra|big black spitting cobra|black big spitting cobra|big spitting cobra black": "Adjective order is size (big), then colour (black), then type (spitting) next to the noun: B. Memory tip: opinion, size, age, shape, colour, origin, material, purpose + noun.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The _____ robber showed no mercy during the attack.|benevolent|loquacious|senseless|ruthless": "Showing no mercy means being ruthless: D. Benevolent means kind, loquacious means talkative, and senseless means foolish. Memory tip: ruth (old word) = pity, so ruthless = pitiless.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Despite all preparations, the wedding did not _____.|come by|come off|come along|come on": "To \"come off\" is to take place as planned: B. Come by means obtain, come along means progress, and come on means hurry or start. Memory tip: events come off.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Olu brought his clothes and those of his _____.|elder brothers|senior brother's|senior brother|elder brother's": "\"Those of\" already shows possession, so no 's is needed: \"those of his elder brothers\": A. D (\"elder brother's\") is wrong: it marks possession twice. \"Senior brother\" is Nigerian usage; standard English says \"elder brother\". Memory tip: those of + noun, without 's.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): His father hardly misses the news because he goes about with his _____ radio.|potable|pointable|portable|pocketable": "A portable radio can be carried about: C. Potable means drinkable, and pointable and pocketable don't fit. Memory tip: port = carry (porter, transport).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Did you have _____ when you went to visit the medical doctors?|diarrhoea|diarrhae|diarhoea|diarhea": "The British spelling is diarrhoea: double r, and \"oea\": A. Memory tip: dia-rr-hoea.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): If he _____ for admission, I am sure he would succeed.|had applied|will apply|has applied|applied": "\"Would succeed\" in the main clause pairs with the past simple in the if-clause: \"If he applied\": D. Memory tip: second conditional = If + past, would + verb.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The investigation Commission has treated all the _____ submitted to it by the warring factions.|protests|applications|petitions|enquiries": "Warring factions submit petitions (formal complaints or requests) to a commission: C. Memory tip: petition = formal written request to an authority.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The police claim that a number of stolen cars _____ recovered.|has been|have been|have being|has being": "\"A number of\" + plural noun takes a plural verb: \"have been recovered\": B. Memory tip: a number of → plural; the number of → singular.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Everybody is allowed to _____ his views on state matters.|show|air|hear|share": "To air one's views is to express them publicly: B. Show, hear and share don't form this idiom. Memory tip: air an opinion, air a grievance.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): For beating _____ his coursemate, Agbaji was _____ from the university.|away/sent out|on/expelled|at/removed|up/rusticated": "To beat someone up is to attack them, and to rusticate a student is to suspend them from university: D. Memory tip: rusticate = send to the countryside (rus = country), i.e. away from campus.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The old man was rushed to hospital when his blood pressure dropped to a _____ level.|dangerous low|dangerous lowly|dangerously low|dangerously lowly": "An adverb (dangerously) modifies the adjective (low): \"a dangerously low level\": C. \"Lowly\" means humble, not low in amount. Memory tip: adverb + adjective + noun (extremely high price).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Until he lost the election, Agbo _____ to be the Students' Union president.|would have hoped|had been hoping|has been hoping|has hoped": "An action continuing up to a past event takes the past perfect continuous: \"had been hoping\" until he lost: B. Memory tip: had been + -ing = ongoing up to a point in the past.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): _____ been there to look for her several times without success.|He'll|He is|He's|Is he": "\"He's\" here means \"He has\": \"He has been there several times\": C. He'll and He is don't fit \"been\". Memory tip: he's = he is or he has; \"been\" tells you it's has.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): He wrote to you, _____?|hadn't he|wouldn't he|hasn't he|didn't he": "A positive past simple statement takes a negative tag with \"did\": \"He wrote to you, didn't he?\": D. Memory tip: past simple → did/didn't in the tag.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Our neighbour was attracted by the _____ from my mother's cooking.|scent|flavour|aroma|stench": "A pleasant smell of cooking is an aroma: C. Stench is a bad smell, scent is usually used for flowers or perfume, and flavour is taste. Memory tip: the aroma of coffee or cooking.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The clubs were not _____ on a solid ethical base.|founded|funded|created|found": "To found an organisation on a principle is to base it on that principle. The past participle is \"founded\": A. Found (D) is the past of find, and funded means given money. Memory tip: found (establish) → founded; find → found.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): We have been living peacefully here but _____ hear rumours of wars.|again|occasionally|eventually|slowly": "Living peacefully but now and then hearing rumours of war = occasionally: B. Memory tip: occasionally = from time to time.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): I watched him as he _____ the hole.|is digging|dugged|dug|digs": "The past tense of dig is dug, matching \"watched\": C. Dugged isn't a word. Memory tip: dig, dug, dug.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Your performance will improve if you _____ hard.|practise|would practice|practiced|are practicing": "A first conditional uses \"will\" + \"if\" + present simple: \"if you practise hard\": A. In British English the verb is spelt practise (the noun is practice). Memory tip: practiCe = noun, practiSe = verb (like advice/advise).",
 "eng-2007-p1|A suitable title for this passage is|Livestock and Human Casualties of the Bird Flu Pandemic in Nigeria|HIV/AIDS and the Bird Flu Pandemic in Nigeria|The Ravaging Effects of Epidemics in Nigeria|Controlling the Bird Flu Pandemic in Nigeria": "Most of the passage is about the measures taken, and further measures suggested, to stop bird flu spreading in Nigeria, so \"Controlling the Bird Flu Pandemic in Nigeria\" fits best: D. A picks out casualties, but there were only \"a few human casualties\". B and C go wider than the passage. Memory tip: choose the title that covers what most paragraphs are about.",
 "eng-2007-p1|From the passage, it can be inferred that|the Kaduna poultry farmer who lost many birds was not adequately compensated by government|bird flu had already resulted in a high human and livestock death toll|Nigerians no longer consume poultry products as a result of bird flu|bird flu in Nigeria was first discovered by the Food and Agriculture Organisation (FAO) Diagnostic Centre": "The passage says many Nigerians \"had already excluded its meat from their menu\", so poultry farmers were counting losses. C is the closest inference, even though it overstates \"many\" as all Nigerians. B is wrong (few human casualties), and D is wrong because the virus was diagnosed at Vom first and only confirmed by the FAO. A is never stated. Memory tip: an inference must rest on a sentence in the passage.",
 "eng-2007-p1|The expression 'to return to the status quo ante' refers to the|state of affairs following the bird flu pandemic in Nigeria|condition of things prior to the incidence of bird flu|bird flu pandemic that had ravaged many countries in Europe, Asia and the Middle East|killing of birds as a result of bird flu and its effects on poultry farmers": "\"Status quo ante\" is Latin for \"the way things were before\", so it means returning to how things were before bird flu: B. A reverses the time. C and D are events, not a state of affairs. Memory tip: ante = before (as in antenatal).",
 "eng-2007-p1|From the third paragraph, the steps taken to check the spread of bird flu in Nigeria were|restricting movement, paying compensation and caging free-range birds|public enlightenment, controlling migratory birds and killing infected birds|monitoring all poultry farms, quarantining affected farms and killing and disposing of affected birds|isolating ravaged farms, destroying and disposing of affected birds and restricting movements": "The third paragraph lists quarantining affected farms, killing and disposing of birds, and restricting movement, which matches D. A, B and C slip in measures from the last paragraph (caging, compensation, monitoring) that were only suggested. Memory tip: in paragraph-specific questions, check the exact paragraph, not the whole passage.",
 "eng-2007-p1|Which of the following statements is true according to the passage?|The death of many poultry birds led to the discovery of bird flu in Nigeria|If it had not been quickly controlled, bird flu might have proved more deadly than HIV/AIDS|Nigeria's handling of the bird flu pandemic justified African countries' inability to cope with the disaster|Poultry farms in Nigeria had been quarantined": "The announcement \"was a sequel to the death of a large number of birds\" in a Kaduna farm, so the deaths led to the discovery: A. D overstates it (only affected farms were quarantined). B and C aren't in the passage. Memory tip: \"true according to the passage\" means stated, not guessed.",
 "eng-2007-p2|One conclusion that can be drawn from the opening paragraph is that|lazy people work only to satisfy their own immediate needs|lazy people are thorough-bred loafers who cannot even eat their porridge|though lazy people have ugly faces, they like to see themselves in the mirror|it is a waste of time to advise a lazy man because he will always use a sieve to sift the advice": "The loafer \"would never hold up a bigger pot than he wanted filled for himself\", so lazy people do only what meets their own immediate needs: A. C contradicts the passage, which says they \"will never bear to look at themselves\", though the answer key in circulation chose it. D twists the sieve image. Memory tip: never choose an option the passage directly contradicts.",
 "eng-2007-p2|From the argument in the last paragraph, it can be concluded that the sluggards are|phlegmatic when advised|weary of sound advice|intolerant of good advice|impervious to advice": "Like seed that takes \"many days\" of threshing to get out of the straw, laziness stays \"no matter what you do with them\", so nothing gets through to them: impervious, D. A, B and C describe reactions to advice, but the point is that it has no effect. Memory tip: impervious = nothing gets through.",
 "eng-2007-p2|The expression 'if the shower should turn to beer, he might wake himself up a bit' suggests that lazy people|are pleasure-loving|have beer showers|are gourmets|are inconsiderate": "The loafer won't stir even if it rains porridge, but beer, a pleasure, might wake him a bit, so lazy people are moved only by pleasure: A. B takes the image literally. Gourmets love fine food, which is too specific. Memory tip: ask what the image implies, not what it literally says.",
 "eng-2007-p2|Which of the following explains the point being made in the second paragraph?|The drone and the slothful often look themselves up in the mirror|The drone and the slothful hover around beehives because of their overdependence on honey|The drone and the slothful are idle so they depend on others for their needs|The drone and the sluggards are lazy people who sleep a lot": "Bees drive the drone out \"for inactivity and overdependence\", and the slothful man is compared to it, so both are idle and depend on others: C. A and B invent details, and D drops the dependence idea. Memory tip: the comparison's reason is usually the answer.",
 "eng-2007-p2|Which of the following represents the author's view in the passage?|The slothful will not get out of bed unless someone wakes them up|Idle and unproductive people should have no place in society|Loafers carry their basins about looking for who to fill them with food|Laziness leads to poverty so both should be rejected with equal vehemence": "The writer says lazy men \"ought to be treated like the drone which the bees drive out\", meaning the idle have no place in society: B. D contradicts him, though the answer key in circulation chose it. He pities poverty but would whip laziness, so he treats them differently. A and C are details, not his view. Memory tip: the author's view is what he argues for throughout.",
 "eng-2007-p3|Modern secretaries are different from the old ones because they|are more educated and better exposed to professional training|may become teachers in polytechnics and universities|are able to become graduates of various universities|can use modern gadgets such as computers and other machines": "Modern secretaries train in polytechnics and universities and take courses in administration, law and more, so they are more educated and better trained: A. B and C are things the passage doesn't say, and D (gadgets) is never mentioned. Memory tip: compare the \"old\" and \"new\" paragraphs directly.",
 "eng-2007-p3|Which of the following statements can be deduced from the passage concerning the old perspective of secretaries?|The training they received from retired professionals was sufficient|They were highly respected by other professionals|One could see that they were well educated|They did not deserve to be addressed as secretaries": "The passage calls it \"unrealistic\" to call an unqualified person a secretary. Many old secretaries were only typists with \"doubtful certificates\", so they didn't deserve the title: D. B and C are the opposite of what's described, and A contradicts \"doubtful certificates\". Memory tip: \"deduce\" means follow the writer's judgement.",
 "eng-2007-p3|The expression 'unguarded ones', as used in the passage, refers to|two people who may be attracted to each other|husbands of female secretaries|the female secretaries and their bosses|the devil and all his cohorts": "The paragraph is about secretaries being constantly close to their bosses. The \"unguarded ones\" are those who don't guard against that temptation, the secretaries and bosses: C. A echoes the sentence before but is less specific. B and D are other people mentioned. Memory tip: read the whole paragraph to see whom an expression refers to.",
 "eng-2007-p3|The expression 'utter disdain', as used in the passage, means|undue criticism|complete contempt|deserved respect|professional misconduct": "\"Utter\" means complete, and \"disdain\" means contempt, so the phrase means complete contempt: B. C is the opposite (the trap). A and D are other negatives. Memory tip: utter = total (utter nonsense, utter disdain).",
 "eng-2007-p3|The writer seems to suggest that|there was nothing wrong in having males as secretaries|secretaries were best trained by retired chief typists|only graduates who studied abroad were good secretaries|the Ordinary National Diploma was sufficient for secretaries": "Male secretaries were humiliated in the past, but the writer ends by saying secretaries, \"including the male ones, are more important and well respected today\", so he sees nothing wrong with men in the job: A. B, C and D contradict the passage. Memory tip: the conclusion often reveals the writer's attitude.",
 "eng-2007-p4|Gap 16: …many of whom have probably not _____ eyes on him for a long time.|lied|laid|lay|lain": "The idiom is \"to lay eyes on\" someone, and after \"have\", the past participle of lay is laid: \"have not laid eyes on him\". So B is right. Lain comes from lie (recline), and lied means told a lie, which are the classic lay/lie traps. Memory tip: lay (put) – laid – laid; lie (rest) – lay – lain.",
 "eng-2007-p4|Gap 17: The morning of the ofala sees large crowds _____ in the palace arena.|gather|gathers|were gathered|to be gathering": "After \"see + object\", English uses the base form of the verb: \"sees large crowds gather\". So A is right. \"Gathers\" doesn't agree with \"crowds\", and C and D are the wrong forms after \"sees\". Memory tip: see/watch/hear + someone + base verb.",
 "eng-2007-p4|Gap 18: Suddenly a cannon _____ repeatedly.|bangs|knows|shoots|booms": "The deep, loud sound a cannon makes is a boom, so D is right. A door or gun bangs, \"shoots\" needs an object, and \"knows\" is a sound-alike distractor. Memory tip: cannons boom, thunder rumbles, bells ring.",
 "eng-2007-p4|Gap 19: Rich _____ from the royal musicians fills the air.|vibration|gyration|percussion|explosion": "Musicians play drums and gongs, which is percussion, and \"rich percussion\" is a natural pairing. So C is right. Gyration is spinning, vibration is shaking, and explosion doesn't fit music. Memory tip: percussion = instruments you strike.",
 "eng-2007-p4|Gap 20: The Igwe emerges like the rising sun, _____ in the white-bordered gold and black robes.|surrounded|dazzled|royally|resplendent": "\"Resplendent\" means splendidly dressed and dazzling, which fits \"like the rising sun\" and the fine robes. So D is right. \"Dazzled\" would mean he was blinded, and \"royally\" is an adverb in the wrong place. Memory tip: resplendent = shining in glory.",
 "eng-2007-p4|Gap 21: The Igwe then begins the _____ dance with his wife.|cultural|social|traditional|royal": "The ofala is a traditional festival, and the king opens it with the traditional dance, so C is right. Royal and cultural are close, but the dance itself is the traditional dance, the usual collocation. Memory tip: festivals have traditional dances.",
 "eng-2007-p4|Gap 22: …he dances round the arena _____ and praises of the crowd.|at the cheer|to the cheers|with the cheers|from the cheers": "You dance \"to\" music or \"to the cheers\" of a crowd, meaning accompanied by them, so B is right. \"At\", \"with\" and \"from\" are the wrong prepositions. Memory tip: dance TO the music, TO the cheers.",
 "eng-2007-p4|Gap 23: Finally the Igwe takes his seat and his people begin to _____.|pay their tolls|spray him with gifts|pay their respects|observe his steps": "Bowing and hailing the king is paying one's respects, so C is right. A toll is a fee, such as a road toll. \"Spray him with gifts\" and \"observe his steps\" don't fit what the people then do. Memory tip: pay respects / pay homage.",
 "eng-2007-p4|Gap 24: 'Igweee! You will live long _____,' they declare.|on the throne|at the throne|in the throne|by the throne": "A king reigns \"on the throne\", so the wish is to live long on the throne: A. \"At\", \"in\" and \"by\" aren't the idiom. Memory tip: ON the throne, like on a chair.",
 "eng-2007-p4|Gap 25: The Igwe _____ their homage by touching their backs with the royal fly whisk.|replies|acknowledges|replies|responds": "You acknowledge homage, meaning you recognise it, so B is right. \"Replies\" and \"responds\" would need \"to\" (respond to their homage). The paper also prints \"replies\" twice. Memory tip: acknowledge a greeting, acknowledge applause.",
 "|Choose the option that has the stress on the FIRST syllable.|integral|intact|negotiate|about": "INtegral is stressed on the 1st syllable (the usual British pattern). InTACT, neGOtiate and aBOUT are stressed on the 2nd. So A is right. Memory tip: integral, like INteger, starts strong.",
 "|Choose the option that has the stress on the FIRST syllable.|understand|afternoon|taxi|address": "TAXi is stressed on the 1st syllable. UnderSTAND and afterNOON are stressed on the last, and adDRESS on the 2nd. So C is right. Memory tip: most two-syllable nouns (TAXi, TAble) stress the first syllable.",
 "|Choose the option that has the stress on the FIRST syllable.|reproduce|conduce|renewal|calendar": "CALendar is stressed on the 1st syllable. ReproDUCE, conDUCE and reNEWal are not. So D is right. Memory tip: prefixes like re- and con- are rarely stressed.",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT stress pattern from the others.|superman|slaughter|surcharge|superior": "SuPErior is stressed on the 2nd syllable; SUPerman, SLAUGHter and SURcharge are stressed on the 1st. So D is the odd one. Memory tip: -ior words (suPErior, inTErior) stress the syllable just before.",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT stress pattern from the others.|evacuee|expedite|European|cigarette": "EXpedite is the only one stressed on the 1st syllable. EvacuEE, EuroPEan and cigaRETTE are stressed near the end. So B is the odd one. Memory tip: -ee, -ean and -ette endings usually take the stress.",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT stress pattern from the others.|education|individual|Cinderella|agriculture": "AGriculture is stressed on the 1st syllable. EduCAtion, indiVIDual and CindeRELla are stressed later. So D is different. Memory tip: -ation stresses the syllable before it, while -culture words (AGriculture, HORticulture) start strong.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for ADVERTISEMENT (syllables in capitals are stressed).|advertiseMENT|adVERtisement|ADvertisement|adverTISEment": "In British English, which JAMB follows, advertisement is stressed on the 2nd syllable: adVERtisement. So B is right. American English often says ADvertisement, which is the trap. Memory tip: think British for JAMB stress: adVERtisement.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for ASSOCIATION (syllables in capitals are stressed).|associAtion|AssoCIAtion|associaTION|aSSOciation": "Words ending in -tion are stressed on the syllable just before -tion: associA-tion. So A is right. The other options put the stress too early or on -tion itself. Memory tip: -tion rule: stress the syllable right before.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for CAPITALISM (syllables in capitals are stressed).|caPItalism|CApitalism|capiTAlism|capitaLIsm": "Capitalism is stressed on the 1st syllable: CApitalism. So B is right. Adding -ism doesn't move the stress from CApital. Memory tip: -ism keeps the stress of the base word (CApital → CApitalism).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): If he arrives late, everyone _____ him.|would have blamed|will blame|must blame|would blame": "\"If + present\" (arrives) is a first conditional, which takes \"will + verb\" in the main clause: \"everyone will blame him\". So B is right. \"Would\" and \"would have\" belong to other conditionals, and \"must\" changes the meaning. Memory tip: If + present → will.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Because of his _____ nature, Ayo was tricked out of his wristwatch.|pliable|kind|uncompromising|uncalculating": "Pliable means easily persuaded or influenced, the sort of person who gets tricked: A. An uncompromising person wouldn't be talked into anything (the opposite). \"Kind\" is too general, and \"uncalculating\" is weaker. Memory tip: pliable = bendable, easily bent.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Yesterday in the hall, Ola said that _____ his watch.|he had forgot|he had mislaid|he has misplaced|he have lost": "Reported speech after a past reporting verb (\"said\", yesterday) moves the tense back: \"I have mislaid\" becomes \"he had mislaid\". So B is right. C is wrong: \"he has misplaced\" doesn't shift back. A uses \"forgot\" wrongly, and D is ungrammatical. Memory tip: said + have → had.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): When they examined his body, they found that he _____.|was dead|had been dead|died|had been dying": "They found him already in a state of death: \"he was dead\". So A is right. \"Died\" names an event, not a state. \"Had been dead\" and \"had been dying\" don't fit \"found that\". Memory tip: found that he was + state.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The politician _____ the family of the deceased.|wept over|condoled|commiserated with|greeted": "To commiserate with someone is to express sympathy, so C is right. \"Condole\" needs \"with\" (condoled with), so B is incomplete, which is the trap. \"Greeted\" and \"wept over\" don't mean expressing sympathy. Memory tip: commiserate WITH, condole WITH.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Our soldiers are starting to zero _____ the enemy.|at|on at|unto|in on": "The phrasal verb is \"zero in on\", meaning to aim at or focus on, so D is right. \"Zero at/on at/unto\" aren't English. Memory tip: zero IN ON a target.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): _____ went to the stadium to watch a football match.|Me and Amadi|Amadi and myself|Amadi and I|Amadi and me": "The subject of \"went\" needs the subject pronoun \"I\", and politeness puts the other person first: \"Amadi and I\". So C is right. \"Me\" is an object pronoun, and \"myself\" needs an \"I\" elsewhere in the sentence. Memory tip: drop \"Amadi and\": \"I went\", not \"me went\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): I look forward to _____ you next week.|seen|be seeing|see|seeing": "In \"look forward to\", \"to\" is a preposition, so it's followed by the -ing form: \"look forward to seeing you\". So D is right. \"See\" is the trap of treating \"to\" as part of an infinitive. Memory tip: look forward to / object to / used to (= accustomed) + -ing.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): My father has been away on a journey but my mother says she _____ he _____ this Friday.|expected / would arrive|expects / will arrive|expected / will arrive|expects / would arrive": "\"Says\" is present, so the reported verbs stay present and future: \"she expects he will arrive this Friday\". So B is right. Mixing past and present tenses (A, C, D) is the error. Memory tip: present reporting verb, no tense shift.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): I _____ to pursue my education.|did not have the opportunity|did not chance|was not opportuned|was not chanced": "Standard English says \"did not have the opportunity\", so A is right. \"Opportune\" is an adjective with no verb form, so \"was not opportuned\" is a Nigerianism. \"Chance\" as a verb means to risk. Memory tip: opportunity is a noun, so you have it; you can't \"be opportuned\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): There was _____ any traffic as he drove home.|no|usually|normally|hardly": "\"Hardly any\" means almost none: \"There was hardly any traffic\". So D is right. \"No any\" is ungrammatical, and \"usually any\" and \"normally any\" don't work. Memory tip: hardly any / scarcely any = almost none.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The rain fell for days _____.|on end|at end|at ending|on ending": "\"For days on end\" is a fixed expression meaning continuously for days, so A is right. \"At end\", \"at ending\" and \"on ending\" aren't English. Memory tip: for hours/days/weeks ON END.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Didn't _____ draw your attention to the entry requirement?|nobody|everyone|anyone|everybody": "Negative questions use \"anyone\": \"Didn't anyone draw your attention…?\". So C is right. \"Didn't nobody\" is a double negative, and \"everyone/everybody\" changes the meaning. Memory tip: not + anyone, never not + nobody.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The idle steward could not see _____ on the wall and he lost his job.|what will be written|what is written|the hand writing|the writing": "The idiom \"the writing on the wall\" means a clear sign of coming trouble, here losing his job. \"The handwriting on the wall\" is a variant of it, so the app accepts C and D. A and B take the wall literally. Memory tip: see the writing on the wall = see trouble coming.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Making a phone call instead of paying a visit represents a _____ trend.|sudden social new major|new sudden major social|sudden new major social|new social sudden major": "The adjective that says what kind of thing the noun is goes right next to it (\"social trend\"), which rules out A and D. B and C both follow that rule, so the app accepts both. Memory tip: in an adjective list, the type word sits closest to the noun.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Kola was fined; _____ thieves were given a jail sentence.|rest|rest of the|the rest|the rest of the": "The complete phrase is \"the rest of the thieves\": definite article, then rest, then \"of the\" plus the noun. So D is right. The other options leave out a necessary word. Memory tip: THE rest OF THE + noun.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The team has benefitted from the coach's _____ of experience.|reservoir|wealth|minefield|world": "\"A wealth of experience\" is the fixed collocation for a great deal of experience, so B is right. Reservoir and world sound plausible but aren't the idiom, and minefield means a dangerous area. Memory tip: a wealth of knowledge, a wealth of experience.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The girl cannot speak with a clear voice; she is always _____.|blabbing|gesticulating|vociferating|stuttering": "\"Cannot speak with a clear voice\" points to stuttering, speaking with involuntary repetitions, so D is right. Blabbing is talking too much, gesticulating is using hand gestures, and vociferating is shouting. Memory tip: st-st-stutter.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Government should invest more in _____ training.|Teacher's|Teachers'|Teacher|Teachers": "\"Teachers' training\" (training of teachers, plural possessive) is correct, and so is the standard compound \"teacher training\", so the app accepts B and C. \"Teacher's\" means one teacher, and \"Teachers\" lacks the apostrophe. Memory tip: many teachers = teachers'.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): He says he _____ find me a job, but will accommodate me.|cannot be able to|is not able to|was not able to|could not": "\"Says\" is present, so the reported ability is present: \"he is not able to find me a job\". So B is right. A is wrong: \"cannot be able to\" stacks two words for ability and isn't grammatical. C and D are past. Memory tip: can OR be able to, never both.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The next village is at least 30 kilometres away.|The next village is 30 kilometres away, or possibly more.|The next village is only 30 kilometres away.|The next village is approximately 30 kilometres away.|The next village is 30 kilometres away, possibly less.": "\"At least 30\" means 30 is the minimum, so it may be more but not less: A. B (only), C (approximately) and D (possibly less) all break \"at least\". Memory tip: at least = no fewer than.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The country is holding its first free elections for five years.|There were elections in the country five years ago.|The country held elections five years ago but they were not free.|The last time the country held free elections was five years ago.|The first free elections in the country have now taken five years to conduct.": "\"The first free elections for (= in) five years\" means the elections five years ago were free and none since have been. So the last free elections were five years ago: C. B gets it backwards. Memory tip: first X in N years = the last X was N years ago.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: There's a door at either end of the building.|The building has two ends but only one door.|The building has two ends and two doors.|The building has two ends, but I do not know which of the two has a door.|The building has many ends each with a door.": "\"At either end\" means at each of the two ends, so there are two doors: B. A, C and D misread \"either\" as \"one\" or \"many\". Memory tip: either end = both ends.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: My advice to Tolu was 'Look before you leap'.|Tolu was advised to consider the possible consequences before taking action.|Tolu was advised to consider her opinion before reaching any decision.|Tolu was advised to weigh her decisions by looking and leaping.|Tolu was advised to leap only after looking.": "\"Look before you leap\" is a proverb: think about the consequences before you act. So A is right. D takes it literally, the classic proverb trap. Memory tip: proverbs give advice, not instructions to jump.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: There should be a level playing field for women entering politics.|Women should not be allowed to enter politics.|Men and women should be able to compete in the political arena on an equal basis.|Sport facilities should be provided for women entering politics.|Special privileges should be given to women who want to enter politics.": "A \"level playing field\" means equal conditions for everyone, with no advantage either way, so B is right. D gives special privileges, which is the opposite of level. C takes \"playing field\" literally. Memory tip: level = equal for all.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The woman wouldn't have lived throughout the night.|The woman might not have lived if she hadn't got the right support.|It was likely that the woman died before morning.|From all indications, the woman was taking much worse, though she overcame her ordeal.": "The sentence suggests she wasn't expected to survive the night but did. C is the answer key's choice and the closest, though the paper prints only three options and the sentence is ambiguous. B reads it as \"she probably died\". Memory tip: when no option is perfect, choose the closest.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The official had allegedly been taking bribes.|The official accused some people of taking bribes.|The official had been taking bribes without feeling any guilt.|It was proved that the official had been taking bribes.|Some people accused the official of taking bribes.": "\"Allegedly\" means someone has claimed it without proof, so some people accused him: D. C says it was proved, the opposite of alleged. A reverses who accused whom. Memory tip: alleged = said but not proven.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Emeka hardly ever holds with my view on good governance.|Emeka never really supports my opinion of good governance.|My view on what good governance is conforms with that of Emeka.|Emeka sometimes opposes my idea of good governance.|Emeka may never hold a view which conflicts with mine on good governance.": "\"Hardly ever holds with\" means almost never agrees with, so A is closest. C (\"sometimes opposes\") is far too weak, and B and D say the opposite. Memory tip: hardly ever = almost never.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: He could not have rewarded them more handsomely.|He spoke to them in a very generous way.|He rewarded them well, and he was also a good-looking man.|He did not reward them well.|He rewarded them very well.": "\"Could not have rewarded more handsomely\" means the reward was as generous as possible, so D is right. \"Handsomely\" here means generously, not good-looking (B's trap). C reverses it. Memory tip: could not have … more = the most possible.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: We have to iron out our differences at once.|We must ensure that our problems are eliminated at all cost.|We must clear up our misunderstanding without delay.|We have to solve some of our problems for now.|We have to settle our disagreement once and for all.": "\"Iron out\" means to resolve, and \"at once\" means immediately, so B is right. D confuses \"at once\" with \"once and for all\". A and C change the scope. Memory tip: at once = right now.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The vice-chancellor RELINQUISHED power at the end of his term.|clung on to|abandoned|gave up|wielded": "To relinquish is to give up, so the opposite is to cling on to it: A. \"Abandoned\" and \"gave up\" are synonyms (the trap). \"Wielded\" means used power, which is close but not the opposite. Memory tip: relinquish = let go.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Mr. Adeyemo is quite a CONCEITED young man.|a practical|an unassuming|an experienced|a resourceful": "Conceited means proud and full of oneself; unassuming means modest. So B is right. Practical, experienced and resourceful are unrelated positives. Memory tip: conceit = self-importance.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: For anything to do with academic work, he has a great AVERSION.|conversion|attention|predilection|contempt": "An aversion is a strong dislike; a predilection is a strong liking. So C is the opposite. \"Contempt\" is close to a synonym (the trap). Memory tip: predilection = preference.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The man paid a large sum to REDEEM his mortgaged property.|ransom|pawn|acquire|recover": "To redeem a mortgaged property is to get it back by paying; to pawn is to hand something over as security in the first place. So B is the opposite. \"Recover\" and \"ransom\" are near-synonyms. Memory tip: pawn it, then redeem it.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The police demanded a FACTUAL account of the accident.|an undistinguished|an admissible|a supportive|an uncorroborated": "A factual account rests on facts; an uncorroborated one is unconfirmed by evidence, so D is the opposite. \"Admissible\" and \"supportive\" lean positive. Memory tip: corroborate = confirm.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: 'Is it necessary for us to go?' she asked in her SHRILL voice.|indistinct|angry|quiet|aggressive": "A shrill voice is high-pitched and piercing; the opposite is quiet: C. The paper misprints it as \"quite\". \"Angry\" and \"aggressive\" describe mood, not loudness. Memory tip: shrill = loud and sharp.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The students were UNDETERRED by the noisy lecture environment.|encouraged|discouraged|frustrated|challenged": "Undeterred means not put off; the opposite is discouraged: B. \"Encouraged\" is close to a synonym (the trap). Memory tip: deter = discourage, so undeterred = not discouraged.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The stadium was SEETHING with people when we entered.|empty|noisy|filled|crowded": "\"Seething with people\" means packed and swarming; the opposite is empty: A. \"Filled\", \"crowded\" and \"noisy\" are synonyms. Memory tip: seething = swarming.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Do not be DISCOURAGED by failure in life.|dissuaded|overjoyed|disorientated|actuated": "Discouraged means having lost the will to act; actuated means moved or driven to act, so D is the opposite. \"Dissuaded\" is a synonym, and \"overjoyed\" is about mood. Memory tip: actuate = set in action.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The suggestions he made were very UNOBTRUSIVE.|sincere|reasonable|forceful|helpful": "Unobtrusive means low-key and not drawing attention; the opposite is forceful. So C is right. \"Reasonable\" and \"helpful\" are positives, not opposites. Memory tip: obtrude = push forward; unobtrusive = keeps back.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Lami and her husband are simply MUNIFICENT.|niggardly|angry|happy|wealthy": "Munificent means very generous; niggardly means stingy. So A is the opposite. \"Wealthy\" is a trap: rich people aren't always generous. Memory tip: munificent = magnificent giving.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The man advised his wife to steer a MIDDLE COURSE in the argument between her and her employees.|be unrelenting|be unrepentant|be recalcitrant|be compromising": "Steering a middle course means compromising. Refusing to compromise is being recalcitrant (C) or unrelenting (A), so the app accepts both. \"Be compromising\" (D) is a synonym. Memory tip: middle course = meet halfway.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The refugees were received at the TRANSIT camp.|permanent|passage|temporary|nearby": "A transit camp is a temporary stopping place; the opposite is permanent: A. \"Temporary\" is the synonym trap. Memory tip: transit = passing through.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Otokpa took after his late father who was a TEETOTALLER.|sober|a disciplinarian|careless|a drunk": "A teetotaller never drinks alcohol; the opposite is a drunk: D. \"Sober\" is a synonym. Memory tip: tee-total = totally no alcohol.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The study he carried out of the problem was quite COMPREHENSIVE.|detailed|inscrutable|sketchy|complete": "Comprehensive means thorough and complete; sketchy means rough and lacking detail. So C is the opposite. \"Detailed\" and \"complete\" are synonyms. Memory tip: comprehensive = covers everything.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letters 'ea' in PEAR.|dear|there|near|year": "\"Pear\" is pronounced /eə/, like \"there\", so B is right. Dear, near and year have /ɪə/, the spelling trap. Memory tip: pear, bear and wear rhyme with there; dear, near and year rhyme with ear.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter 'o' in POTABLE.|pot|post|port|paw": "Potable (drinkable) is pronounced POH-table, with /əʊ/ as in post: B. Pot has a short /ɒ/, and port and paw have /ɔː/. Memory tip: potable water = POH-table water.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letters 'ue' in BLUE.|root|look|book|foot": "\"Blue\" has a long /uː/, as in root: A. Look, book and foot have the short /ʊ/. Memory tip: if you can hold the \"ooo\", it's long /uː/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the final 's' in SHOWS.|house|reason|loss|horse": "The final \"s\" in shows is pronounced /z/ (showz), the same as the \"s\" in reason: B. House, loss and horse end in /s/. Memory tip: -s after a vowel sound is usually /z/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letters 'th' in CLOTHE.|they|three|mouth|Thomas": "The \"th\" in clothe is the voiced /ð/, as in they: A. Three and mouth have the voiceless /θ/, and Thomas has /t/. Memory tip: verbs ending in -the (clothe, breathe) use the voiced /ð/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter 'n' in ENGLISH.|edge|thin|end|think": "In \"English\", the \"n\" before \"g\" is /ŋ/ (as in sing), and so is the \"n\" before \"k\" in \"think\": D. Thin and end have a plain /n/, and edge has none. Memory tip: n + g/k often makes the /ŋ/ \"ng\" sound.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The tax rebate was a CONCESSION given to the civil servants.|an incentive|an allowance|work|intelligence": "A concession is something granted or allowed, so an allowance is closest: B. An incentive is an encouragement, which isn't quite the same. The last two options are misprints on the paper. Memory tip: concede = allow, so a concession is an allowance.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The rest of the boys admire Olu's VERVE.|energy|handsomeness|work|intelligence": "Verve means energy and enthusiasm, so A is right. Handsomeness and intelligence are other qualities to admire, which is the trap. Memory tip: verve = vigour.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The noise across the road DROWNED the sound of the keyboard in my room.|overshadowed|neutralized|balanced|equalized": "To drown out a sound is to make it inaudible by being louder, to overshadow it: A. Neutralized, balanced and equalized suggest evening out, not overpowering. Memory tip: drowned out = overpowered.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The astute businessman neatly FENDED OFF questions about his private life.|asked|welcomed|sidestepped|explained": "To fend off questions is to avoid or deflect them, to sidestep: C. \"Welcomed\" and \"explained\" are the opposite. Memory tip: fend off = keep away.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He holds on firmly to his HACKNEYED view of life.|principled|wayward|positive|obsolete": "Hackneyed means worn out and overused (a hackneyed phrase), so obsolete (out of date) is the closest: D. \"Principled\" and \"positive\" are flattering, and \"wayward\" means unruly. Memory tip: hackneyed = tired and stale.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He DEPRECATED the attempts made to unseat him.|resisted|condemned|despised|challenged": "To deprecate something is to express disapproval of it, to condemn it: B. C (despised) is wrong: despising is a feeling, not expressed disapproval. \"Resisted\" and \"challenged\" are actions. Memory tip: deprecate = speak against.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: BEADS of sweat ran down Ngozi's armpit.|ponds|springs|rivulets|pools": "Beads of sweat are small drops; running down, they form thin trickles, rivulets: C. Ponds and pools are standing water, and springs come from the ground. Memory tip: rivulet = a tiny river.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The last criticism at the rally was made IN IGNORANCE.|unimpeachable|objective|impeccable|uninformed": "Criticism made in ignorance is uninformed, made without the facts: D. The paper misprints it as \"uniformed\". Unimpeachable, objective and impeccable are all positive, the opposite. Memory tip: in ignorance = uninformed.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The debt the company incurred became rather INTOLERABLE.|dangerous|unprecedented|inevitable|insupportable": "Intolerable means too much to bear, so insupportable is closest: D. Dangerous, unprecedented and inevitable describe other qualities. Memory tip: in-tolerable = can't be tolerated or supported.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: We were warned not to indulge in RECRIMINATIONS.|accusations|indiscretions|counter-charges|frivolous allegations": "A recrimination is an accusation made in return for one received, a counter-charge: C. A is too general (any accusation), and B and D are other things. Memory tip: re-crimination = accusing back.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The shortage of petrol was a PERENNIAL problem.|a seasonal|a recurring|an incurable|an annual": "Perennial means recurring again and again, year after year, so B is right. \"Annual\" and \"seasonal\" are too specific, and \"incurable\" is a different idea. Memory tip: perennial plants come back every year.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The police are not happy with the growing number of DEPRAVED people around the politician.|indolent|violent|immoral|loquacious": "Depraved means morally corrupt, so immoral is right: C. Indolent (lazy), violent and loquacious (talkative) are other faults. Memory tip: depraved = deeply wicked.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: I visited Amina UMPTEEN TIMES in her college, but she never came to me.|seldom|often|occasionally|twice": "Umpteen means very many, so umpteen times means often: B. Seldom, occasionally and twice are all few times, the opposite. Memory tip: umpteen = countless.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Things may change soon, but at the moment our economic situation is PRECARIOUS.|uncertain|unattractive|redeemable|buoyant": "Precarious means insecure and uncertain, so A is right. \"Buoyant\" is the opposite (strong), and the others are unrelated. Memory tip: precarious = could fall at any moment.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The striking workers have vowed not to return to work until the decision is REVERSED.|implemented|rescinded|dismissed|reverted": "To reverse a decision is to cancel it, to rescind it: B. \"Implemented\" is the opposite. \"Reverted\" means went back, which is close but not the word for cancelling a decision. Memory tip: rescind = cancel officially.",
 "eng-2008-p1|From the passage, it can be inferred that|the writer is not dogmatic about any of the definitions of success|possessions inherited are fraudulent wealth|true success will be known to all men in due time|the writer believes that success is symbolic of good living": "The writer lays out several views of success (possessions, health, culture, the spiritual) and never insists that one is the only right one; even the ending says true success \"may be\" contentment. So the writer is not dogmatic: A. B twists the point about heirs squandering wealth, and D is only one of the views reported. Memory tip: \"may\", \"perhaps\" and a list of views signal a writer who is not dogmatic.",
 "eng-2008-p1|The expression 'success is a relative term' suggests that it|has one meaning|has different meanings|is not comprehensible|can be defined materially": "The passage explains \"success is a relative term\" at once: it \"varies not only from person to person, but also from culture to culture.\" So it has different meanings: B. A says the opposite, and D picks out only the materialist view. Memory tip: relative = it depends on who is judging.",
 "eng-2008-p1|From the passage, a successful person is described as one who|believes in divine intervention|enjoys life after retirement|has a good family|is contented with life": "The final line gives the writer's own conclusion: \"true success may be one's ability to be satisfied with one's position in life.\" Satisfied means contented: D. C (a good family) is only part of one definition the writer reports. Memory tip: in \"which is the writer's view\" questions, check the concluding sentence first.",
 "eng-2008-p2|According to the passage, which of the following describes the perception of speech sound?|Acoustic|Auditory|Articulatory|Forensic": "Paragraph three says \"Auditory or perceptual phonetics studies the perception of speech sounds via the ears.\" So the answer is B. Acoustic studies sound waves in the air, articulatory how sounds are made, and forensic recorded speech in legal cases. Memory tip: auditory comes from the Latin for hearing, as in audio and audience.",
 "eng-2008-p2|From the passage, the purpose of describing a language as a system is to show that|all human languages are the same in every sense|human languages are different at the phonological level|every human language has its own separate patterns of structure at all levels|human languages are too complex to understand at any level": "The passage says a language is a system at each of four levels, with sub-systems making up its structure: \"a system of systems\". That means each language has its own patterns of structure at all levels: C. B mentions only one level, and A and D are never claimed. Memory tip: choose the option that covers the whole idea, not just one part of it.",
 "eng-2008-p2|What distinguishes a language from another, according to the passage, is|its finite number of sounds|the indefinite nature of its patterns|its communicative and other purposes|its linguistic description at all levels": "Each language combines its sounds \"in a number of indefinite patterns according to its own phonological rules. This is what makes each language unique.\" So the distinguishing feature is the indefinite nature of its patterns: B. A is tempting, but every language picks a finite number of sounds, so that alone is shared by all. Memory tip: look for the sentence starting \"This is what makes…\": it names the cause.",
 "eng-2008-p2|The style of the passage could best be described as|expository|descriptive|narrative|argumentative": "The passage explains facts about linguistics (levels, approaches, definitions) without telling a story or arguing a case. That is expository writing: A. Descriptive writing paints a picture of a person or place; argumentative writing tries to persuade. Memory tip: expository = explains, like a textbook.",
 "eng-2008-p2|The expression 'systematic patterns of structure', as used in the passage, means that language|is an interconnecting network of structure|is made up of organized phonetics and phonology|is an interconnecting and semantics|comprises similar phonetics, phonology, morphology and semantics": "\"Systematic patterns of structure\" refers back to language as \"a system of systems\", sub-systems linked together at every level. So it means an interconnecting network of structure: A. B names only phonetics and phonology, and D wrongly says all languages are similar. Memory tip: system = parts connected to make a whole.",
 "eng-2008-p3|One conclusion that can be drawn from the first paragraph is that|only children suffer Schizophrenia disorder|the time of occurrence in men and women differs|women suffer the disorder in their early twenties|men suffer the disorder in their early thirties": "Paragraph one says the disorder \"often appears earlier in men, usually in the late teens or early twenties, than in women… in the twenties to early thirties.\" So the time of onset differs: B. C and D swap the ages, and A contradicts \"very rare before adolescence\". Memory tip: when ages or dates are compared, check which group goes with which.",
 "eng-2008-p3|Which of the following is true according to the passage?|Schizophrenia disorder affects only the menfolk|Schizophrenia disorder develops only in the womenfolk|patients with Schizophrenia like to keep to themselves than stay with others|children develop the Schizophrenia disorder after adolescence": "Children over five can develop the disease, \"but it is very rare before adolescence\", so it usually develops after adolescence: D. A and B contradict \"men and women with equal frequency\", and C is never stated. Memory tip: \"only\" in an option is a red flag; check if the passage really limits it.",
 "eng-2008-p3|Which of the following statements is true of the concept of delusions in the second paragraph?|The belief in distorted claims|The expressions of personal beliefs|The evidence of reasoning and contradictions|The belief in unsubstantiated claims": "\"Delusions are false personal beliefs that are not subject to reason or contradictory evidence\", that is, beliefs in claims nobody can prove: D. B leaves out that they are false, and C says the opposite. (\"Believe\" in the paper is a misprint for \"belief\".) Memory tip: unsubstantiated = without evidence (substance).",
 "eng-2008-p3|From the passage, it can be inferred that|family members and friends avoid Schizophrenia patients|cocaine and marijuana are good stimulants for patients|substance abuse may assist a patient's healing process|some drugs slow down a patient's healing process": "Paragraph three says substance abuse \"can reduce the effectiveness of treatment\" and stimulants like cocaine worsen symptoms. So some drugs slow down healing: D. B and C state the opposite, and A is never said: family and friends are worried, not avoiding the patients. Memory tip: an inference must follow from what is written, not from what you imagine.",
 "eng-2008-p3|The expression 'living in a world distorted by hallucinations' means|the world of sound and intellectual thought|the expression of ideas that do not have bases|a patient's expression of connected ideas|the world is very unfair to Schizophrenic patients": "Hallucinations are \"perceptions that occur without connection to an appropriate source\", so the patient lives with ideas that have no basis in reality: B. C says \"connected\" ideas, the opposite; D adds a moral judgement the passage never makes. Memory tip: distorted = twisted away from the truth.",
 "eng-2008-p4|Gap 16: There was basically two schools of _____.|reasoning|ideas|practice|thought": "\"School of thought\" is a fixed expression for a group of people who share a view: D. \"Schools of ideas\" or \"of reasoning\" are not idiomatic. Memory tip: learn fixed collocations as whole units: school of thought, train of thought.",
 "eng-2008-p4|Gap 17: ...and so to repeat such miracles or something near it, do not leave the _____ to the invisible hands of the free market.|economy|structure|miracle|analyses": "The free-market idea is that the \"invisible hand\" runs the economy. The first group says don't leave the economy to it: A. \"Miracle\" repeats the word but makes no sense here, since a miracle is not left to the market. Memory tip: in a cloze, ask what the surrounding sentence is about (here, the economy).",
 "eng-2008-p4|Gap 18: There was a place for, and indeed a great virtue in, state _____.|market|intervention|persuasion|enforcement": "The group that rejects pure free-market forces naturally sees \"a great virtue in state intervention\", government stepping into the economy: B. \"State market\" is not a phrase, and persuasion and enforcement don't fit economic policy. Memory tip: free market vs state intervention is the classic pair in economics.",
 "eng-2008-p4|Gap 19: The other group _____ that it was not so much a miracle...|synthesized|analysed|believed|recommended": "The two schools hold opinions, and \"that it was not so much a miracle\" is an opinion, so \"the other group believed\": C. \"Recommended\" needs advice, not a judgement, and \"synthesized/analysed that\" are awkward. Memory tip: a that-clause expressing a view goes with believe, think, argue.",
 "eng-2008-p4|Gap 20: ...induced by the equally unpredictable, even if visible, hands of politicians and policy _____.|deciders|masters|executors|markers": "The collocation is \"politicians and policy makers\". The paper's \"markers\" is a misprint for \"makers\", which is why the key gives D. Deciders, masters and executors don't combine with \"policy\". Memory tip: policy makers make policy, decision makers make decisions.",
 "eng-2008-p4|Gap 21: The Asian _____ was rather one of a cultural phenomenon...|experience|practice|logic|method": "The writer is describing what happened in Asia as a whole: \"The Asian experience was rather one of a cultural phenomenon\": A. Practice, logic and method suggest a deliberate technique, not a whole cultural event. Memory tip: \"the X experience\" is a common way to describe a nation's history of development.",
 "eng-2008-p4|Gap 22: ...to produce a delightfully desirable spectacle of progress and _____.|purpose|affluence|prospect|prosperity": "\"Progress and prosperity\" go together naturally to describe economic success: D. Affluence is close in meaning, but \"prosperity\" is the set pairing; purpose and prospect don't fit \"spectacle\". Memory tip: listen for common word-pairs (progress and prosperity, peace and stability).",
 "eng-2008-p4|Gap 23: The concern of the economists was how to safely _____ and then apply the Asian prescription...|prescribe|guage|stimulate|re-orientate": "The economists wanted to adapt the Asian prescription to other countries' conditions and then apply it: re-orientate means to adjust to a new direction or situation: D. \"Prescribe the prescription\" repeats itself, and \"guage\" (gauge) is misspelt. The key's re-orientate (D) and the misprinted 'guage', i.e. gauge (B), both fit, so both are accepted. Memory tip: in a cloze, read past the gap (\"and then apply\") to see what logically comes first.",
 "eng-2008-p4|Gap 24: ...in many other countries of the world enduring the _____ of unrelenting and ...|anger|risk|throes|punishment": "To be \"in the throes of\" something is to be in the middle of a painful struggle with it, which fits \"enduring the throes of poverty\": C. B is wrong: a risk is something that might happen, and these countries are already enduring poverty, so \"enduring the risk\" makes no sense. Anger and punishment don't fit either. Memory tip: throes = violent pains; \"throes\" not \"throws\".",
 "eng-2008-p4|Gap 25: ...of unrelenting and _____ poverty.|momentous|compelling|scorching|aggravating": "\"Scorching\" (burning, severe) pairs with \"unrelenting\" to describe harsh poverty: C. Momentous means historically important, compelling means persuasive, and aggravating means annoying, none of which fits poverty. Memory tip: intensifying adjectives for hardship: grinding, scorching, abject.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The court brought out the fact that the elections were not free and fair.|The court said that the elections were not rigged|The court explained that the elections were marred|The court made it clear that the elections were not flawless|The court saw that the elections were properly conducted": "\"Brought out the fact\" means made it clear, and elections that were \"not free and fair\" had faults, so they were not flawless: C. A and D say the opposite (no rigging, properly conducted). B is close, but \"explained\" is not what \"brought out\" means here. Memory tip: in these questions, match both the verb and the idea, not just the general mood.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The computer operator was sacked for incompetence.|The computer operator was sacked for his foolishness|The computer operator was sacked for his laziness|The computer operator was sacked for lack of skill|The computer operator was sacked for lateness": "Incompetence means not having the skill or ability to do a job well: C. Foolishness, laziness and lateness are different faults; a lazy worker may be very skilled. Memory tip: competent = able; in- turns it into \"not able\".",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: He was a member of the think tank of the previous administration.|As an expert, he criticised government|He was an expert who gave advice to government|He was an expert that government did not listen to|As an expert, government must have taken to his advice": "A think tank is a body of experts who study problems and give ideas and advice to a government: B. A and C add details (criticising, being ignored) that the sentence never mentions, and D is ungrammatical. Memory tip: think tank = experts who think for policy makers.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: In spite of various criticisms, Bala sticks to his guns.|Bala always goes out with his guns, ready to kill|people do not like Bala because he is violent|Bala is criticized because of his bad behaviour towards people|Bala adheres to his beliefs even when others do not agree with him": "To \"stick to one's guns\" is to refuse to change your opinion even when others criticise it: D. A, B and C take \"guns\" literally or add bad behaviour. Memory tip: idioms with weapons (stick to your guns, bury the hatchet) are almost never about real weapons.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Chike plays to the gallery whenever he meets a group of people.|Chike plays well with people at the gallery|Chike seeks cheap popularity|Chike shows distrust and hatred to people|Chike shows distrust and hatred to people": "To \"play to the gallery\" is to act in a showy way to win cheap popularity with onlookers, as actors once did for the cheap seats: B. A takes \"gallery\" literally. (C and D are printed identically in the paper.) Memory tip: the gallery = the crowd you want applause from.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: He kept a stiff upper lip over the accident.|He sustained injury on his upper lip|He endured the misfortune with courage|He could not talk because of the accident|His friends deserted him because of the accident": "To \"keep a stiff upper lip\" is to stay calm and brave in trouble without showing your feelings: B. A takes the lip literally, and C and D add things the idiom doesn't say. Memory tip: a stiff upper lip does not tremble, so the person doesn't cry.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: He had travelled the length and breadth of Africa.|He must have travelled to some big cities of Africa|He had travelled to many parts of Africa|He had travelled to all parts of Africa|He had travelled to all the villages and towns of Africa": "\"The length and breadth\" of a place means all over it, every part: C. B (\"many parts\") is too weak, and D (\"all the villages and towns\") is too literal. Memory tip: length + breadth = the whole area.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: She wriggled out of her wedding gown.|She could not take off her gown herself so she was assisted|Her dress got torn because of the snake-like pattern|No one could have imagined that her dress was so light|It took her some time to remove her close-fitting dress": "To \"wriggle out of\" a tight dress is to twist your body to and fro to get it off slowly, which happens when the dress is close-fitting: D. B misreads \"wriggle\" as a snake pattern, and A adds help that isn't mentioned. Memory tip: wriggle = small twisting movements, like a worm.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Visitors who are observant do not outstay their welcome.|All visitors must be alert so as to know when to leave a place|Some visitors know when to leave a place so as to make their hosts happy|Some visitors are unable to know when their hosts do not want them around|All visitors, except a few, know when to leave a place": "Being observant (alert, watchful) is what makes a visitor know when to leave. So the sentence teaches that visitors must be alert to know when to go: A. B adds a reason (\"to make hosts happy\") that isn't stated, and C and D speak about how many visitors do so, which the sentence doesn't count. B, which keeps 'some visitors', is also accepted. Memory tip: \"who are observant\" names the condition, so pick the option built on it.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The group had been made a scape goat during the event.|Members of the group had been punished for a sin that they did not commit|Members of the group had been blamed for a sin that they committed|The group had been punished so that no one would commit the sin again|The group had been blamed merely to serve as a deterrent to others": "A scapegoat is someone made to take the blame or punishment for wrongs others committed: A. B says they did commit it, and C and D bring in deterrence, which is a different idea. Memory tip: in old Hebrew ritual the scapegoat carried away the sins of the people, sins that were not its own.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Sola is APATHETIC about his work.|indifferent|enthusiastic|half-hearted|bored": "Apathetic means showing no interest or concern. The opposite is enthusiastic, full of eager interest: B. Indifferent, half-hearted and bored are near-synonyms of apathetic, the trap in this question. Memory tip: a- (without) + pathos (feeling) = without feeling.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The members of the family had a HASTY breakfast before starting out on their journey.|hurried|leisurely|tasty|nasty": "The paper prints \"nasty\", but option D is also \"nasty\" and A is \"hurried\", so the italic word must be a misprint for \"hasty\". A hasty breakfast is a hurried one; the opposite is leisurely: B. Tasty (C) would be the opposite of nasty, but nasty can't be its own answer. Memory tip: hasty = done quickly; leisurely = done without hurry.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The POVERTY level among the masses is unbearable.|arrogance|saturation|affluence|influence": "Poverty is the state of being poor; affluence is wealth: C. Influence (D) is a look-alike trap meaning power to affect others, and saturation means being completely full. Memory tip: affluent comes from Latin for \"flowing towards\", as money flows to the rich.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The chairman was accused of implementing policies that favoured the ELITE.|oppressed|executives|jobless|citizens": "The elite are the small privileged, powerful group at the top of society. Of the options, the oppressed (those kept down by power) are their opposite: A. Executives are part of the elite, and \"citizens\" includes everyone. Memory tip: elite = top; oppressed = pressed down.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The manager was impressed by the man's EAGERNESS.|impatience|indifference|negligence|calmness": "Eagerness is keen desire or interest; indifference is lack of interest: B. Impatience is close to eagerness, not its opposite; negligence means carelessness. Memory tip: eager is to indifferent as hot is to cold.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He is known for making THREADBARE arguments as a scholar.|justifiable|baseless|empty|militant": "A threadbare argument is worn-out and weak, like old cloth with the threads showing. The opposite is a sound, justifiable argument: A. Baseless and empty are near-synonyms, the trap. Memory tip: threadbare clothes are thin; threadbare arguments are thin too.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The lecturer's OBSCURE publications counted against him during the promotion exercise.|widespread|closed|circulated|restricted": "Obscure publications are little known, hardly seen by anyone. Widespread means known or found everywhere: A. B and D (closed, restricted) are near-synonyms; \"circulated\" is close but doesn't describe how well known something is. Memory tip: obscure = in the shadows.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The man found his wife physically REPELLENT.|attractive|fatuous|unpleasant|flamboyant": "Repellent means so unpleasant that it pushes you away. The opposite is attractive, which pulls you towards it: A. Unpleasant is a synonym, and fatuous (silly) and flamboyant (showy) are unrelated. Memory tip: repel pushes away; attract pulls in, like magnets.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: As an ABSOLUTE RULER, Adayi brooks no opposition from his subordinates.|a despotic dictator|a shameless king|a benevolent leader|a magnanimous democrat": "An absolute ruler holds all power and \"brooks no opposition\". The closest opposite is a ruler who shares power and accepts opposition, a democrat: D. The key's C (a benevolent leader) is the opposite of a despotic ruler, which is also defensible, so both C and D are accepted. A is a synonym. Memory tip: for the opposite of a type of ruler, think about how power is held.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The response to the advertisement has been PHENOMENAL.|spiritual|frightening|unimpressive|catastrophic": "Phenomenal means remarkably great or impressive. The opposite is unimpressive: C. Catastrophic and frightening are bad in a different way (disaster, fear), not the opposite of \"remarkable\". Memory tip: phenomenon = something remarkable to see.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: My father lived a SPARTAN life before his death.|extravagant|peaceful|quiet|luxurious": "A Spartan life is simple and plain, without comfort or luxury, like the ancient Spartans. The opposite is luxurious: D. Extravagant is about spending wastefully, which is close but less exact; peaceful and quiet are unrelated. Memory tip: Spartan vs luxurious = plain vs rich living.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Bola's parents went through an ACRIMONIOUS divorce.|a bitter|an acrid|a sour|a peaceful": "Acrimonious means bitter and angry. The opposite is peaceful: D. A bitter, acrid and sour are all synonyms of acrimonious, the trap. Memory tip: if three options mean the same as the word, the odd one out is the opposite.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Lliya's account of the incident is APPALLING.|tasking|fascinating|horrifying|ridiculous": "Appalling means shocking and horrifying. Of the options, the pleasant opposite is fascinating, very interesting: B. Horrifying is a synonym, and ridiculous and tasking don't oppose \"shocking\". Memory tip: appal = make pale with shock.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: She ENCAPSULATED her view of the nature of life in a poem.|summed up|explained|expatiated on|reflected": "To encapsulate is to express something briefly, in a short form. To expatiate on is to speak or write about it at great length: C. Summed up is a synonym, the trap. Memory tip: a capsule is small, so encapsulate = make it short; expatiate = expand.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: We were held SPELLBOUND by his eloquence.|unimpressed|freed|deceived|unenlightened": "Spellbound means held fascinated, as if by magic. The opposite is unimpressed: A. \"Freed\" plays on the idea of being bound and is a literal trap. Memory tip: spellbound audiences are deeply impressed.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: We were at a loss to understand why Oli should RETRACT his statement before the judge.|assert|repeat|withdraw|reiterate": "To retract a statement is to take it back, to withdraw it: C. Assert, repeat and reiterate all mean to state it again or firmly, the opposite. Memory tip: re- (back) + tract (draw), as in tractor: draw back.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The workers hope to appear before the ARBITRATION panel to resolve the lingering dispute.|trial|legislative|investigative|reconciliatory": "Arbitration is a hearing in which a neutral person or panel listens to both sides and gives a binding decision, much like a trial: A. Reconciliatory (D) is tempting, but bringing parties to agree by themselves is mediation, not arbitration. Memory tip: an arbitrator decides, a mediator helps the parties decide.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He went to the polls in defiance of sound advice and recorded an ABYSMAL performance.|surprisingly good|disappointingly bad|mediocre|landslide": "Abysmal means extremely bad, as deep as an abyss: B. Mediocre (C) means only average, not as bad as abysmal, and A and D (landslide) mean the opposite. Memory tip: abyss = bottomless pit, so abysmal = rock bottom.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He was not able to WITHSTAND the attack from his immediate family.|survive|overcome|match|resist": "To withstand an attack is to stand against it, to resist it: D. Survive (A) is close, but surviving means coming through it alive, not opposing it; overcome means to defeat it. Memory tip: with- here means \"against\", as in withhold and withdraw.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Your effort to bring peace between us is futile because you are BIASED.|convinced|clever|prejudiced|worried": "Biased means unfairly favouring one side, that is, prejudiced: C. Convinced and clever don't involve unfairness, which is the core of bias. Memory tip: biased = judged before (pre-judged) the facts.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Our front door is always kept AJAR.|locked|sealed|half-closed|permanently open": "A door that is ajar is slightly open, not fully shut: half-closed is nearest: C. \"Permanently open\" overstates it, and locked and sealed are the opposite. Memory tip: ajar = a little gap.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: His embroidery was an EXACTING task for the tailor.|an unwarranted|an exciting|a demanding|a disturbing": "The paper prints \"exciting\", but option B is also \"an exciting\", so the word must be a misprint for \"exacting\". Exacting means making great demands on skill and care: C, a demanding task. Embroidery is fine, careful work, which fits. Memory tip: exacting = needs exactness.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The student's REMONSTRANCES were rebuffed by the authority.|protest|suggestions|compliants|regrets": "Remonstrances are strong protests or complaints against something: A. \"Suggestions\" are much milder, and \"compliants\" is a misspelling, not a real word. Memory tip: remonstrate = protest loudly.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: I had to make my way through the THRONG of people in the church.|group|crowd|gathering|regrets": "A throng is a large, dense crowd of people: B. Group and gathering are close, but they don't carry the idea of a packed mass you \"make your way through\". Memory tip: throng = thronged, crowded, pushing.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The man's wife has her MISGIVINGS about him.|fears for|distrust of|hatred for|love for": "Misgivings are feelings of doubt or distrust about someone or something: B. \"Fears for\" means worry for his safety, which is different, and hatred is far too strong. Memory tip: misgiving = a feeling that something is mis- (wrong).",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Violent crime is only one of the MALADIES afflicting modern society.|damages|illnesses|wounds|crises": "A malady is an illness or disease, here used of society's problems: B. Crises and damages are general troubles, and wounds are injuries, not illnesses. Memory tip: mal- (bad), as in malaria and malnutrition.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Tade has an AQUILINE nose.|a large and curved nose|a wide and straight nose|a big and pointed noses|a small and attractive nose": "Aquiline describes a nose that is large and hooked like an eagle's beak: A. The others give the wrong shape (straight, pointed, small). Memory tip: aquila is Latin for eagle.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Everyone was talking about the CAMARADERIES in the club.|companionship|quarrelsomeness|commissioning|disunity": "Camaraderie is friendly fellowship and trust among people who spend time together: companionship: A. B and D are the opposite. Memory tip: camaraderie comes from comrade, a close companion.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: 'You're IMPOSSIBLE', she said to me.|unreasonable|unforgiving|daft|strong": "Calling someone \"impossible\" means they are very difficult and unreasonable to deal with: A. Daft means silly, which is a different fault. Memory tip: an impossible person makes agreement impossible.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: I was being directed by my SIXTH SENSE all through the ordeal.|intelligence|judgement|intuition|imagination": "The sixth sense is the power to know things without using the five senses or reasoning, that is, intuition: C. Judgement and intelligence rely on reasoning, which the sixth sense does not. Memory tip: five senses + one \"gut feeling\" = sixth sense.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): They _____ wet if they had gone out in the rain.|would get|would have got|will have got|had got": "\"If they had gone out\" is a past unreal (third) conditional, so the main clause needs \"would have + past participle\": would have got: B. A is for the second conditional, and C and D mix tenses. Memory tip: if + had done, would have done.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The students are still _____ in the dark regarding the principal's decision.|very so|much too|very much|much so": "\"Very much\" is the standard intensifier in \"very much in the dark\" (completely uninformed): C. \"Much too\" needs an adjective (much too dark), and \"very so\" and \"much so\" are not used before a phrase. Memory tip: very much + prepositional phrase (very much in love, very much alive).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): When I came in, I _____.|laid on the floor|lie on the floor|lain on the floor|lay on the floor": "The speaker was resting, which is the verb \"lie\" (lie, lay, lain). Its past tense is \"lay\": D. A is wrong: \"laid\" is the past of \"lay\" (lay, laid, laid), which means to put something down and needs an object. B and C are the wrong forms. Memory tip: hens lay eggs (object); people lie down (no object).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Either the teacher or the students _____ to blame.|is|are|has being|are being": "With \"either…or\", the verb agrees with the nearer subject. \"Students\" is plural, so \"are to blame\": B. \"Has being\" and \"are being\" are wrong forms. Memory tip: proximity rule: either/neither…or/nor, look at the noun next to the verb.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): I will pay my debt _____.|instalmentally|by instalmentally|in instalments|in instalmentally": "The standard expression is \"pay in instalments\", in parts over time: C. A is wrong: \"instalmentally\" is not a standard English word, and B and D combine a preposition with it. Memory tip: we pay in cash, in full, in instalments.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): You should anticipate and prepare for changes _____ in your office.|that has taken place|which takes place|taking place|that would take place": "\"Anticipate\" and \"prepare for\" look ahead, so the changes are still to come, and \"changes\" is plural: \"that would take place\": D. A and B use singular verbs (has, takes) with a plural noun, and C is less clearly about the future. Memory tip: check both time and number in relative clauses.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The scholar examined _____ of the subject.|three a part analysis|a part-three analysis|a three-part analysis|a three part analysis": "A number + noun used before another noun is a compound adjective: it stays singular and is hyphenated, \"a three-part analysis\". D (the key) and C give this phrase; C has the standard hyphen and D leaves it out, so both are accepted. A and B have the words in the wrong order. Memory tip: a ten-year-old boy, a three-part series: no plural -s, use hyphens.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The litigants finally opted _____ settlement.|by and out of court|in an out-of-court|to an out of court|for an out-of-court": "One opts for something, and \"out-of-court\" is hyphenated as a compound adjective before \"settlement\": D. A and C use the wrong preposition, and B \"opted in\" is a different phrasal verb. Memory tip: opt for = choose; opt out of = decline.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): She refrained _____ smoking.|at|off|from|in": "Refrain always takes \"from\": refrain from smoking: C. \"Off\" is a common error (\"keep off\"), but not with refrain. Memory tip: refrain from, abstain from, desist from: all take \"from\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Birds whistle while bears _____.|gibber|purr|growl|bellow": "The sound a bear makes is a growl: C. D is wrong: \"bellow\" is the deep roar of a bull. Cats purr and monkeys gibber. Memory tip: learn animal sounds in groups: lions roar, bears growl, bulls bellow, cats purr.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): I am not surprised that Zali had a road accident, he was always driving too fast, _____?|isn't he|wasn't he|didn't he|did he": "The main clause \"he was always driving\" is positive with \"was\", so the tag is negative with the same verb: wasn't he: B. \"Isn't\" is the wrong tense, and \"didn't\" uses the wrong auxiliary. Memory tip: positive statement, negative tag; copy the helping verb.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Oduma had _____ enough money to pay the fines so he went to jail for distributing libellous _____.|no/brochures|not/circulars|no/publications|not/pamphlets": "Before \"enough\", English uses \"not\" (not enough money), never \"no\", which rules out A and C (the key's answer). Libellous pamphlets (D) or circulars (B) are printed sheets attacking someone, so both are accepted. Memory tip: not + enough; no + noun (no money).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The principal said he _____ so many visitors that day that he _____ exhausted.|had been having/felt|has been having/feels|was having/feels|is having/felt": "The reporting verb \"said\" is past, so the reported action moves back: \"had been having so many visitors\", and \"felt\" stays in the past: A. B, C and D mix in present forms. Memory tip: in reported speech, keep the whole sentence in the past.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): His mother always reprimands him for being a _____ reader of cheap novels.|vicious|veracious|vivacious|voracious": "A voracious reader reads eagerly and a lot, as if hungry for books: D. Vicious (cruel), veracious (truthful) and vivacious (lively) are sound-alike traps. Memory tip: voracious = vora- (devour).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Femi has been _____ behind in classwork.|lacking|lagging|larking|lurking": "To lag behind is to fall behind others: B. Lacking needs an object, larking means playing about, and lurking means hiding. Memory tip: jet lag puts you behind time.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Judging _____ his face, he is not guilty.|at|through|with|by": "The fixed expression is \"judging by\" (or \"judging from\") his face: D. \"At\", \"through\" and \"with\" are not used with judging in this sense; the phrase means \"forming an opinion from\". Memory tip: think of the proverb \"don't judge a book by its cover\": judge by, judging by.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): What were the _____ served in the restaurant?|spices|desserts|diets|condiments": "Desserts are dishes (sweet courses) served at a meal: B. Spices and condiments are added to food, not \"served\" as dishes, and diets are eating plans. Watch the spelling: dessert (pudding) vs desert (sand). Memory tip: dessert has two s's because you always want seconds.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): She _____ her hair _____ in the room before we left.|smoothened/off|Smoothened/on|smoothened/up|smoothened/down": "Hair is smoothed down, pressed flat and neat: D. C is wrong: we don't \"smooth up\" hair; we put hair up or do it up. Off and on don't fit either. Memory tip: smooth down your hair, your skirt or your feathers.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): This is a village where _____ knows _____.|everyone/everyone else|anyone/anyone|every person/themselves|someone/someone else": "In a close village \"everyone knows everyone else\": A. \"Anyone knows anyone\" isn't natural English, \"every person…themselves\" changes the meaning, and D says only one person knows another. Memory tip: everyone + everyone else = all know all.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): 'Did you say that you saw him _____ the road yesterday?|crossed|cross|to be crossing|crosses": "After \"see + object\" we use the bare infinitive (or the -ing form): \"saw him cross the road\": B. \"Crossed\" and \"crosses\" are finite forms, and \"to be crossing\" is wrong after see. Memory tip: see/hear/watch someone do something (no \"to\").",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: kEY|sit|beat|seat|tread": "The \"ey\" in key is the long vowel /iː/. Beat (B) and seat (C) both have /iː/, so both are accepted; the key gives C. Sit has short /ɪ/, and tread has /e/. Memory tip: ee and ea usually give /iː/, but watch exceptions like tread and bread.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: tAUght|law|aunt|count|plateau": "The \"au\" in taught is the long vowel /ɔː/, as the \"aw\" in law: A. Aunt has /ɑː/, count has /aʊ/, and plateau ends in /əʊ/. Memory tip: aw and au often give /ɔː/: law, saw, caught, taught.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: dUtch|lottery|love|loud|lurch": "The \"u\" in Dutch is /ʌ/, the same as the \"o\" in love: B. Lottery has /ɒ/, loud /aʊ/, and lurch /ɜː/. Memory tip: o before v often gives /ʌ/: love, dove, glove, above.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: boTH|bother|thaw|the|there": "The \"th\" in both is voiceless /θ/, the same as in thaw: B. Bother, the and there all have voiced /ð/. Memory tip: put your hand on your throat; /ð/ buzzes, /θ/ does not.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: bouQUet|bucket|banquet|liquid|squeeze": "Bouquet is a French word and its \"qu\" is pronounced /k/ (boo-KAY), like the \"ck\" in bucket: A. Banquet, liquid and squeeze have /kw/. Memory tip: French borrowings often have qu = /k/: bouquet, unique, technique.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: houSes|rice|rise|curse|course": "The \"s\" in the middle of houses is voiced /z/ (HOW-ziz), as in rise: B. Rice, curse and course end in /s/. Memory tip: house has /s/, but houses has /z/ in the middle.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: Say|treason|weigh|fright|saw": "Say and weigh both end in the diphthong /eɪ/, so they rhyme: B. Treason has /iː/, fright /aɪ/, and saw /ɔː/. Memory tip: eigh often sounds /eɪ/: weigh, eight, neighbour.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: Knew|tool|dawn|night|queue": "Knew is /njuː/ and queue is /kjuː/, both ending in /juː/: D. Tool has /uː/ but ends in /l/, so it doesn't rhyme; dawn and night are unrelated. Memory tip: to rhyme, everything from the vowel to the end must match.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: Toy|torn|take|foil|tour": "Foil has the same diphthong /ɔɪ/ as toy: C. Torn has /ɔː/, take /eɪ/, and tour /ʊə/ or /ɔː/. This is the closest match the paper offers. Memory tip: oy and oi both give /ɔɪ/: toy, boil, coin.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): Comfortable|COMfortable|comFORtable|comfortaBLE|comforTAble": "Comfortable is stressed on the first syllable: COM-for-ta-ble: A. Many speakers wrongly stress \"FOR\", option B. Memory tip: comfort is stressed on COM, and -able doesn't move the stress.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): Incapacitate|inCApacitate|incaPAcitate|INcapacitate|incapaciTATE": "Incapacitate is stressed on the third syllable: in-ca-PA-ci-tate: B. It follows capacity (ca-PA-ci-ty). Memory tip: words ending in -ate with 4+ syllables often stress the syllable before -i-, as in in-ca-PA-ci-tate and as-SAS-si-nate.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): Encouragement|Encouragement|enCOUragement|encouRAgement|encourageMENT": "Encouragement keeps the stress of encourage, on the second syllable: en-COU-rage-ment: B. A is wrong: A shows no stressed syllable in capitals. Suffixes like -ment don't move the stress. Memory tip: find the stress of the root word first (en-COU-rage), then add the suffix.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: THEIR children speak better French than English.|Do their husbands speak better French than English?|What language do their children speak better than English?|Do their children speak better French than English?|Whose children speak better French than English?": "Stress on THEIR contrasts these people's children with someone else's, so the sentence answers \"Whose children…?\": D. A puts the contrast on husbands, B on the language, and C on nothing in particular. Memory tip: the stressed word answers the question word (who, whose, what, where).",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: They RAN to the playground.|Where did they run to?|Did they walk to the playground?|Who ran to the playground?|Did they run from the playground?": "Stress on RAN corrects the way they moved, so it answers \"Did they walk to the playground?\": B. A would need stress on PLAYGROUND, C on THEY, and D on TO. Memory tip: emphatic stress on a verb corrects the action.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: Timi BOUGHT a new car.|Did Timi hire a new car?|Does Timi deal in cars?|Has Timi acquired a new car?|Does Timi have a new car?": "Stress on BOUGHT contrasts buying with another way of getting the car, so it answers \"Did Timi hire a new car?\": A. C and D just ask if he has one, which needs no contrast. Memory tip: ask what the stressed word is correcting.",
 "eng-2009-p1|The passage suggests that the greatest problems of mass education in Nigeria are|the absence of educational broadcasts and underutilization of urban teachers|the reluctance of most teachers to work in rural areas and the fact that Nigeria is an orate society|the absence of immediate feedback in the teaching process and lack of teaching facilities|widespread penury and ever-increasing demand for formal education": "The first paragraph names the \"two major problems\": population explosion, which makes demand for schooling \"far outstrip\" provision, and \"debilitating mass poverty\". Penury means poverty, so that is D. The key gives B, but teachers rejecting rural postings is a side effect, and being an orate society is used as a point in favour of broadcasting. Memory tip: when a question asks for \"the greatest problems\", look for where the writer names them outright.",
 "eng-2009-p1|Which of the following can be concluded from the passage?|The Nigerian government does not support the use of radio and television for teaching|Telephone and postal services are inimical to effective school broadcasting|The formal education system is more expensive than the non-school type|Nigerians reject teaching and learning through radio and television because of lack of immediate feedback": "The passage says broadcasting \"can cut the cost of education as boarding and tuition will become unnecessary\", so the formal school system is the costlier one: C. The key gives B, but telephone and postal services are said to help (\"may be used to clarify difficulties\"), not to be inimical (harmful). Memory tip: a conclusion must follow from what the passage says; check each option for words that reverse it.",
 "eng-2009-p1|According to the passage, the major advantages of educational broadcasts may be summarised as|mass outreach, reduced costs and equal educational opportunities|mass education, reduced poverty level and qualitative education|mass access to qualitative education and reduction of the crippling economic crisis|increased number of schools, reduced cost of schooling and enhanced mass education": "The writer praises broadcasting for laying the foundation for mass education (mass outreach), cutting costs, and making quality \"even throughout the country\" (equal opportunities): A. B and C claim it reduces poverty or the economic crisis, which the passage never says. Memory tip: in a summary option, every part must be backed by the passage.",
 "eng-2009-p1|From the last sentence of the passage, it can be deduced that education broadcasting is|unknown in Nigeria|rarely used in Nigeria|often used in Nigeria|randomly used in Nigeria": "\"A beginning must therefore be made to promote aggressive school broadcast\" shows it has hardly started, so it is rarely used: B. A goes too far (\"unknown\"), and C contradicts the call for a beginning. Memory tip: \"a beginning must be made\" means it is not yet being done much.",
 "eng-2009-p1|A suitable title for this passage is|The role of Radio and Television in Nigeria Education|Why Nigerian Teachers should Use Radio and Television for Teaching|The Need to promote Teaching and Learning Through Radio and Television in Nigeria|Enhancing Schools Broadcasting for Effective Teaching and Learning in Nigeria": "The passage builds a case for radio and television teaching and ends by calling for it to be promoted, so C, \"The Need to promote Teaching and Learning Through Radio and Television\", fits best. B narrows it to teachers, and A is too neutral for an argument. Memory tip: the best title matches both the topic and the writer's purpose.",
 "eng-2009-p2|The reference to the Nigerian Policy on Education in the passage implies that|the government recognizes Nigerian pidgin|pidgin will continue to enjoy acceptance by many Nigerians|it is simple to understand|Nigerian pidgin is used at all levels": "The policy allows \"the major languages in the child's environment\" at the elementary level, which makes teaching in pidgin justifiable, so pidgin's acceptance can continue: B. A is tempting, but the policy never names pidgin. Memory tip: \"implies\" questions want what follows from the reference, not what is stated outright.",
 "eng-2009-p2|From the passage, which of the following is the most important reason why Nigerian pidgin is popular?|it is used in social occasions|it has no ethnic affiliation|it is extensively used along the coast|it is used by many creative writers": "Pidgin is \"the first choice of most Nigerians\" when they are \"among those from different ethnic and linguistic backgrounds\": it belongs to no single ethnic group, so B. The key gives D; writers using pidgin is a sign of its popularity, so both are accepted. A is evidence of spread, not its cause. Memory tip: separate the cause of something from the signs of it.",
 "eng-2009-p2|The style of this passage could best be described as|narrative only|narrative and expository|persuasive and argumentative|descriptive only": "The writer traces how pidgin developed over time (narrative) and explains its uses and status (expository): B. It is not mainly persuasive (C), and it does more than describe (D). Memory tip: narrative tells what happened; expository explains; argumentative tries to win you over.",
 "eng-2009-p2|According to the passage, the phrase lingua franca means|Nigeria pidgin|common language|second language|mother tongue": "A lingua franca is a common language used between speakers of different languages; pidgin is called one because it is \"the most widely spoken\" across groups: B. A is just the example, and D is the language you learn first at home. Memory tip: lingua franca = common go-between language.",
 "eng-2009-p2|The fact that Nigerian pidgin is used in primary education in places like Warri, Sapele and Port Harcourt suggests that it is|universally accepted|a lingua franca|a language of instruction|Nigeria's second lingua franca": "Teachers \"taught their pupils in Nigerian pidgin\", so there it serves as a language of instruction: C. The key gives D, but the passage never calls pidgin a \"second lingua franca\", and the question is about classrooms. A overstates. Memory tip: answer from the exact sentence the question points to.",
 "eng-2009-p3|Which of the following is true according to the passage?|Lack of sleep is caused by either a person's lifestyle or involuntary sleep and not by both|Voluntary sleep deprivation can be attributed to the way in which individual lives|some illnesses cause sleep disorders, which can be both voluntary and involuntary|Healthful sleep is needed only for one's well-being": "The passage says deprivation can be \"voluntary... resulting from a person's lifestyle\", that is, from the way the individual lives: B. The key gives D, but \"only\" is not in the passage, which shows lack of sleep also wrecks cars, careers and marriages. C is wrong because illness causes involuntary deprivation. Memory tip: watch for \"only\", \"always\" and \"never\" in options.",
 "eng-2009-p3|The passage suggests that|medical doctors have found 17 distinct sleep disorders|inability to sleep was once considered to be chronic|insomnia and fever are medical conditions that are indicative of other problems|the US Congress has recognised that insomnia is a symptom of other problems": "Insomnia \"is often considered to be a symptom of other problems, much as fever suggests some sort of infection\", so both point to other problems: C. A and D credit the wrong body (it was a commission of the US Congress, and it recognized 17 disorders). Memory tip: check who did what in the passage before choosing.",
 "eng-2009-p3|The expression '.... mocking the clock' as used in the passage, captures the|phenomenon of retailers making a lot of profit because they operate at night|fact that retail business is a waste of time|total disregard of time by retailers and services|fact the time available to retailers and services is not enough to do good business": "Round-the-clock (24/7) retailers ignore the normal day-and-night limits, as if laughing at the clock: total disregard of time, C. A brings in profit at night only, which is not the point of the image. Memory tip: to \"mock\" something is to treat it as if it doesn't matter.",
 "eng-2009-p3|In the first sentence of the passage, the word 'debt' is quoted because|it has been used for a specific effect|it has been used in the first sentence|of the seriousness of the matter it addresses|it is capable of destroying people": "Quotation marks around one word show it is used in a special sense; here \"debt\" means a shortage of sleep, not money owed: A. The key gives C, but seriousness is not why a word is put in quotes. Memory tip: quotes around a single word = \"I'm using this word in a special way\".",
 "eng-2009-p3|From the passage, doctors find it hard to convince their patients of the gravity of lack of sleep because|it is caused by a variety of reasons|the patients are often tired and cannot get enough sleep|people believe that it enhances one's personality|its impact on the patient is slow": "The last paragraph gives the reasons doctors struggle: some people treat chronic fatigue as \"a status symbol\" (C), and the condition \"often worsens very gradually\", so victims don't notice it (D). Both are accepted. The key gives A, but the variety of causes is mentioned about insomnia, not as the reason patients ignore it. Memory tip: look for \"because\" in the paragraph the question points to.",
 "eng-2009-p4|Gap 16: Business executives ... have come to the _____ that there should be a better approach to buyer-seller ...|realization|conclusion|level|point": "\"Come to the conclusion that...\" is the fixed expression for reaching a judgement: B. \"Come to the realization\" exists but is rarer, and \"come to the level/point that\" is not the idiom here. Memory tip: learn \"come to a conclusion\", \"arrive at a decision\", \"reach an agreement\" as set pairs.",
 "eng-2009-p4|Gap 17: ... a better approach to buyer-seller _____.|relationship|existence|agreement|friendship": "The text is about how buyer and seller deal with each other, and later says \"the beginning of a relationship\": A. Agreement and friendship are too narrow, and existence makes no sense. Memory tip: look ahead in a cloze passage for the same idea repeated.",
 "eng-2009-p4|Gap 18: Today, such _____ as consultative selling, relationship marketing and value-added selling have become common catchphrases ...|concepts|words|clauses|definitions": "Consultative selling and relationship marketing are ideas or approaches, so concepts: A. They are not mere words, clauses or definitions. Memory tip: use \"concept\" for an idea or approach, \"term\" for a word.",
 "eng-2009-p4|Gap 19: Salespeople are now _____ from pushy, hard selling to consultations ...|emerging|reversing|dangling|shifting": "Moving from pushy selling \"to\" consultation is shifting: D. \"Reversing from... to\" is not idiomatic, and emerging and dangling don't fit. The paper prints \"not\" here, a misprint for \"now\". Memory tip: \"shift from X to Y\" describes a change of approach.",
 "eng-2009-p4|Gap 20: Customer _____ has assumed a higher dimension.|interest|awareness|view|service": "Customer service is the standard collocation, and the passage stresses \"excellent service\" and attention to customers' needs: D. Interest and awareness don't take on \"a higher dimension\" in this context. Memory tip: in business texts, \"customer service\" and \"customer loyalty\" are fixed pairs.",
 "eng-2009-p4|Gap 21: ... the only way to stay in business and possibly be ahead of _____ is to give adequate attention to the needs of customers.|distribution|competition|consumption|production": "To stay in business and \"be ahead of\" others means beating rival firms, the competition: B. Distribution, consumption and production are parts of trade, not rivals. Memory tip: \"ahead of the competition\" is a set expression.",
 "eng-2009-p4|Gap 22: ... customer _____ is what ensures a robust bottom line.|respect|dignity|loyalty|obedience": "Customers who keep coming back (loyalty) keep profits healthy, the \"robust bottom line\": C. Respect, dignity and obedience don't produce repeat sales. Memory tip: bottom line = profit; customer loyalty = repeat business.",
 "eng-2009-p4|Gap 23: We now have a very _____ marketplace in which people feel a need for personal intimacy and excellent service.|tangible|rowdy|sensitive|desirable": "A market where people want \"personal intimacy and excellent service\" responds to small things: sensitive, C. Tangible and rowdy don't fit, and desirable doesn't explain the need. Memory tip: read the relative clause after the gap; it describes the word you need.",
 "eng-2009-p4|Gap 24: They want a salesperson who comes to them with a _____ knowledge of their needs ...|prior|full|biased|simple": "A salesperson with \"ready-made solutions\" already knows the customer's needs beforehand: prior knowledge, A. Full knowledge is possible but doesn't match the \"ready-made\" idea, and biased is negative. Memory tip: prior = before.",
 "eng-2009-p4|Gap 25: The ability to _____ the needs of the customer and provide adequate information ...|analyse|present|organize|discuss": "Before you can provide information that helps a customer, you must analyse (study closely) their needs: A. Present and discuss don't go naturally with \"the needs of the customer\" here. Memory tip: we analyse needs, problems and data.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: It was our collective idea but Ado was doing everything to steal the show.|Ado was not part of the planning but he tried to rob us of the benefits of it|We planned the show together but Ado was trying to abort it|It was a joint project but Ado is trying to attract the most attention|We collated the idea for the show but Ado was trying to steal the programme": "\"Collective\" means joint, and to \"steal the show\" is to attract the most attention and praise: C. B and D take \"steal\" literally or as stopping the show, and A says Ado wasn't part of the planning. Memory tip: steal the show = outshine everyone else.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: As we watched, he just popped along to the shop for some bread.|He has walked slowly to the shop|He stole some bread from the shop but we could not stop him|He broke into the bread shop before our very eyes|He went quickly to the shop for some bread": "To \"pop along\" somewhere is to go there quickly or for a short time: D. A says slowly, the opposite, and B and C misread \"pop\" as stealing or breaking in. Memory tip: pop in, pop out, pop along all mean a quick, short trip.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: In all ramifications, Chief Adeyemi was a successful principal of the school.|He was partly a failure and partly a success|Everyone admitted that he was largely successful during his tenure|He was not totally successful in the school|His tenure witnessed a huge success": "\"In all ramifications\" means in every respect, so he was completely, hugely successful: D. A and C suggest partial failure, and B weakens it to \"largely\". Memory tip: \"in all ramifications\" = in every way, totally.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: I couldn't buy the biscuit because of the hold-up in the supermarket.|The shop had not opened|The attendant was slow|Armed robbers were there|There was nobody to attend to me": "A hold-up is usually a delay, so the slow attendant (B), as the key says. But \"hold-up\" also means an armed robbery, and nothing in the sentence rules that out, so C is accepted too. Memory tip: hold-up = delay or robbery; hold up = delay or rob.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: You could have heard the sound, if you weren't asleep.|You were not asleep so you heard the sound|You were asleep so you did not hear the sound|You heard the sound though you were asleep|You did not hear the sound though you were not asleep": "\"Could have heard... if you weren't asleep\" is an unreal condition: in fact you were asleep and did not hear it, B. A and D reverse the facts, and C contradicts the sentence. Memory tip: in unreal conditionals, the real situation is the opposite of what is said.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Musa will not do the job well because he has an itching palm.|He has rashes in his palm|He is always looking for something to touch|He is always ready to accept bribe|He is always tapping people on the back": "Someone with an \"itching palm\" is greedy for money, especially bribes: C. A, B and D take \"palm\" literally. Memory tip: itching palm = itching for money.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Emeka was always conspicuous by his absence when it came to attending lectures.|That Emeka always attended lectures made him conspicuous at school|Emeka's absence from lectures was all too glaring|It was obvious that Emeka was not present at lectures|What made Emeka conspicuous was his occasional absence from school": "To be \"conspicuous by your absence\" is to be noticed because you are missing; his absence was glaring, B. C says the same thing plainly, so both are accepted. A reverses it, and D says \"occasional\", but he was always absent. Memory tip: conspicuous by absence = noticeably missing.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: He was injured by friendly fire.|He was wounded in a war by a friend of his who fired a shot mistakenly|He was injured by a gunshot that was fired by a friend|He was hit in a war by a weapon fired by his own side|It was a war and he was hit by a bomb fired by an unknown soldier": "\"Friendly fire\" is fire from your own side in a battle that hits you by mistake: C. A and B take \"friendly\" to mean a personal friend, and D brings in an unknown soldier. Memory tip: friendly fire = shot by your own side.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Kola visits us on occasions, and I don't like that.|I do not like Kola because he visits us only when an event is being celebrated|I hate the fact that Kola only visits us when something is being celebrated|That Kola does not visit us often is something I object to|Kola seldom visits us and that is because he is fair-weather friend": "\"On occasion(s)\" means now and then, not often, and the speaker dislikes that: C. A and B misread \"occasions\" as celebrations, and D adds \"fair-weather friend\". Memory tip: on occasion = occasionally, from time to time.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: At the time I arrived, Olu was lying in third place.|Olu's third position coincided with my arrival|When I arrived Olu was sleeping in the space reserved for him|It was a competition and Olu was in third position when I arrived|On my arrival at the competition, Olu went to sleep": "\"Lying in third place\" is said of a race or competition: he was in third position when I arrived, C. B and D take \"lying\" to mean sleeping, and A misses the competition sense. Memory tip: lie first/second/third = be in that position in a contest.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: We had difficulty eating the meat because it was LEATHERY.|bitter|stringy|tough|tender": "Leathery meat is tough and hard to chew; the opposite is tender: D. Tough and stringy mean the same as leathery, and bitter is about taste. Memory tip: for opposites, first find the synonym in the options, then look away from it.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The boy is too INDOLENT to do well at school.|intelligent|industrious|inactive|slothful": "Indolent means lazy; industrious means hard-working: B. Inactive and slothful are synonyms (traps), and intelligent is about brains, not effort. Memory tip: indolent = idle.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Salasi's TREMENDOUS effort has yielded results.|absolute|insignificant|unimaginable|prohibitive": "Tremendous means very great; insignificant means very small and unimportant: B. Absolute and unimaginable lean towards \"great\", and prohibitive means too costly. Memory tip: tremendous effort vs insignificant effort.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The COMPUTERIZED service of the new banks is heart-warming.|manual|mechanical|electrical|condensed": "Computerized work is done by computer; manual work is done by hand: A. Mechanical and electrical are still machine-made. Memory tip: manual comes from Latin manus, hand.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Life has become a MISERY for many owing to the harsh economic condition in the country.|drudgery|ridicule|ritual|comfort": "Misery is great suffering; comfort is ease and well-being: D. Drudgery is hard, dull work, close to misery. Memory tip: miserable vs comfortable.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The company director has a VIVACIOUS personality.|a positive|an unmanageable|an unimpressive|a gregarious": "Vivacious means lively and spirited; of the options, an unimpressive (dull) personality is the opposite: C. Gregarious (sociable) and positive are close in meaning to vivacious. Memory tip: vivacious, like vivid, is full of life.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Young people have a PENCHANT for fast cars.|likeness|passion|madness|dislike": "A penchant is a strong liking; the opposite is dislike: D. Passion is a synonym, and likeness means resemblance, not liking. Memory tip: penchant = fondness.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Most of the latest actions are MILITANT in nature.|commanding|civil|zealous|passionate": "Militant means aggressive and ready to fight; civil means polite and peaceful: B. Zealous and passionate are close to militant. Memory tip: militant comes from military.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: His ideas sounded quite palatable yet they were JETTISONED by the group.|accepted|dumped|denied|criticized": "To jettison is to throw away or reject; the opposite is to accept: A. Dumped is a synonym, and denied and criticized are other negatives. Memory tip: ships jettison cargo by throwing it overboard.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The compulsory leave made her feel LISTLESS.|restful|great|fat|energetic": "Listless means lacking energy and interest; the opposite is energetic: D. Restful is a trap: rest is not the same as energy. Memory tip: listless = lifeless.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: 'A novel is an EMBELLISHED falsehood', said the teacher.|enriched|exaggerated|adorned|obliterated": "To embellish is to add decoration or details; to obliterate is to wipe out completely: D. Enriched, exaggerated and adorned are all close to embellished. (The paper doesn't italicize the word.) Memory tip: embellish = add; obliterate = erase.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: I have had enough of your IMPUDENCE.|politeness|incivility|arrogance|boldness": "Impudence is rude disrespect; the opposite is politeness: A. Incivility, arrogance and boldness are close to impudence. Memory tip: an impudent child \"talks back\".",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: We were COLLECTIVELY responsible for keeping the machine in good shape.|jointly|severally|dutifully|socially": "Collectively means together as a group; severally means separately, each on his own: B. Jointly is a synonym. Memory tip: in law, \"jointly and severally\" = together and each one alone.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: His comment was not PRINTABLE.|punishable|suitable|offensive|unfair": "Only \"printable\" is italicized. Printable means fit to be published, so its opposite is offensive (too rude to print): C. The key gives B, but \"suitable\" means nearly the same as printable, so it's a synonym, not an opposite. Memory tip: find the opposite of the italic word itself, not of the whole sentence.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: CURIOUSLY, he escaped unhurt.|interestingly|unsurprisingly|annoyingly|unusually": "\"Curiously\" here means strangely, surprisingly; the opposite is unsurprisingly: B. Interestingly and unusually are close to curiously. Memory tip: curiously = oddly; unsurprisingly = as expected.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Agwube's explanation of her poor performance at the examination only BEGGED THE QUESTION.|delayed her punishment|sounded quite convincing|did not address the issue|overflogged the matter": "To \"beg the question\" is to dodge the real issue, or to take for granted the very thing that needs explaining. So her explanation did not address the issue: C. D is wrong: \"overflogged the matter\" means discussed it too much, which is a different idea. Memory tip: an answer that begs the question leaves the real question unanswered.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: We have every need to preserve some of our national monuments for POSTERITY.|prosperity|future|national pride|collective gain": "Posterity means future generations, the future: B. Prosperity (wealth) is the sound-alike trap, and national pride and collective gain are reasons, not meanings. Memory tip: posterity = posted to the future.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He COMMANDS the confidence and respect of all his subordinates.|requests|enjoys|enforces|conjures": "To \"command respect\" is to receive it because you deserve it, so he enjoys their confidence and respect: B. Enforces and requests suggest forcing or asking for it, and conjures means calling up by magic. Memory tip: you command respect by earning it, not demanding it.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The president has mapped out so many LAUDABLE projects to embark upon.|laughable|good|praiseworthy|valuable": "Laudable means deserving praise: praiseworthy, C. Laughable is the sound-alike trap, and good and valuable are too general. Memory tip: laud = praise (as in applaud).",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Adamu's father is a STERN man.|wicked|strict|playful|conservative": "A stern man is serious and strict: B. Wicked is too strong, playful is the opposite, and conservative is about opinions. (The paper labels the last two options d and d.) Memory tip: a stern look says \"no nonsense\".",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Nobody knew the source of the ALTERCATION between the couple.|alter ego|quarrel|deep love|wealth": "An altercation is a noisy argument, a quarrel: B. Alter ego is a sound-alike trap meaning another self, and deep love is the opposite. Memory tip: altercation = alteration of tempers, a row.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: His poetry is said to be INSCRUTABLE.|ludicrous|unlearned|inseparable|mysterious": "Inscrutable means impossible to understand or interpret: mysterious, D. Ludicrous means ridiculous, and inseparable is a sound-alike. Memory tip: inscrutable = can't be scrutinized (examined) and understood.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Hers was a SPECIOUS argument about the government of the day.|misleading|precious|spacious|true": "A specious argument looks right on the surface but is false: misleading, A. Spacious and precious are sound-alike traps, and true is the opposite. Memory tip: specious = only seems correct.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The Lagos bus conductor wore his professional LIVERY.|badge|shoe|uniform|cap": "Livery is the special uniform worn by people in a particular job, such as drivers or servants: C. Badge and cap are only parts of one. (The paper labels the last two options c and c.) Memory tip: livery = work uniform.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: If Joy hadn't been TRIPPED halfway through the race, she would have come first.|discouraged|disqualified|interrupted|exhausted": "Being tripped (made to stumble) broke her run in the race, so she was interrupted: C. Disqualified and exhausted are possible events but not what \"tripped\" means. Memory tip: in nearest-meaning questions, choose what the word does in that sentence.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: There must be something SINISTER about her late arrival.|reasonable|joyful|hopeful|ominous": "Sinister means suggesting evil or danger: ominous, D. Reasonable, joyful and hopeful are all positive, the opposite direction. Memory tip: sinister and ominous both mean \"something bad is coming\".",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The UNIFICATION of the country has brought about great technological advancement.|restoration|agreement|cohesion|stability": "Unification is bringing parts together into one, which gives cohesion (unity): C. Agreement and stability may follow, but they are not what the word means. Memory tip: unify = make one; cohesion = sticking together.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: His AMNESIA has affected his career.|loss of focus|loss of sight|loss of memory|loss of direction": "Amnesia is loss of memory: C. The other options lose sight, focus or direction, not memory. Memory tip: amnesia and amnesty both involve forgetting (a crime is \"forgotten\" in an amnesty).",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Idris' FORBEARANCE endeared him to us.|hardwork|patience|wisdom|good manners": "Forbearance is patient self-control and tolerance: B. Hard work, wisdom and good manners are good qualities, but not the meaning. Memory tip: forbear = bear with someone patiently.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: They hated the police and BY IMPLICATION, me.|extension|inference|examination|application": "\"By implication\" means as something implied, i.e. something one can infer: B. If they hated the police, one can infer that they hated me too. Extension, examination and application don't mean that. Memory tip: a speaker implies; a listener infers.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): He is really very mean. He _____ not lend me the money I wanted.|should|could|would|won't": "\"He would not lend me the money\" shows unwillingness, which suits a mean man: C. \"Won't not\" is a double negative, and should and could miss the idea of refusal. Memory tip: would not / won't = refuses to.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): When the cashier told him to join the queue, he considered it _____ his dignity.|a drawback to|an affront on|withdrawal from|a strain on": "An affront is an insult to someone's pride or dignity: B. A drawback, a withdrawal and a strain don't fit \"considered it ___ his dignity\". Standard British English usually says \"an affront to\". Memory tip: an affront hits your pride.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): If anyone greets you, it is only polite to return _____ greeting.|your|the one's|anyone's|one's": "In a general statement about good manners the impersonal \"one/one's\" is used: \"it is only polite to return one's greeting\", D, as the key gives. \"The one's\" and \"anyone's\" are not idiomatic here. Memory tip: in general rules, keep to \"one... one's\" (e.g. \"One must do one's duty\").",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Amina lives _____ campus.|in the|on|at the|inside": "We say a student lives \"on campus\", with no article: B. \"In the campus\" and \"at the campus\" are common errors. Memory tip: on campus, on site, on board.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): An acceptance of the offer would be a departure from the several rejections that _____.|had taken place|have taken place|will take place|took place": "The rejections came before the moment being considered, so the past perfect \"had taken place\" is used: A. \"Will take place\" is future, and the simple past doesn't show the earlier time. Memory tip: past perfect = the earlier of two past times.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The _____ part of a new book is often written by a more experienced writer than the author.|foreward|foreword|forward|forword": "A foreword (fore + word) is the short introduction at the front of a book, often written by someone else: B. Forward is a direction, and foreward/forword are misspellings. Memory tip: the foreword comes before the words of the book.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The officer acted in _____ the instructions.|accordance by|accordance to|accordance of|accordance with": "The fixed phrase is \"in accordance with\": D. \"Accordance to/by/of\" are wrong. Memory tip: in accordance with, in line with, in keeping with.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The bomb was _____.|detonated|dismantled|deactivated|depleted": "A bomb can be detonated (exploded), the key's A. With no other context, dismantled (taken apart) and deactivated (made harmless) are equally correct, so B and C are accepted too. Depleted means used up, which doesn't fit. Memory tip: detonate = explode; defuse/deactivate = make safe.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): If you want to succeed in life, don't be envious _____ other people's achievement.|of|to|with|on": "Envious takes \"of\": envious of other people's achievement, A. \"Envious to/with/on\" are wrong. Memory tip: envious of, jealous of, proud of.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): They are taught to value honour _____ their lives.|from|in|than|above": "\"Value honour in their lives\" is correct, the key's B. \"Value honour above their lives\" (prize honour more than life) is also standard English, so D is accepted too. \"From\" and \"than\" don't follow value. Memory tip: value X above Y = think X more important than Y.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Our plan for the trip fell _____ when the rich man did not give us a _____.|off/boat|through/line|down/jacket|in/help": "A plan that fails \"falls through\", and only B has \"through\". Off, down and in don't make the right phrasal verb with a plan. (The paper prints \"life\" where the second gap is.) Memory tip: plans and deals fall through.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): I missed the match though it was shown on television on two _____ nights.|concurrent|consistent|concrete|consecutive": "Consecutive means one after another: two nights in a row, D. Concurrent means at the same time, and consistent and concrete don't fit. Memory tip: consecutive = in sequence.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The company paid some money to Abu to _____ him for the losses he suffered during the accident.|indemnify|condole with|settle|recompense": "To indemnify someone is to pay them for a loss, A, as the key gives. \"Recompense him for the losses\" means the same, so D is accepted too. Condole with is to sympathise, and settle needs a claim or debt as object. Memory tip: indemnify / recompense / compensate someone for a loss.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): You are just putting the hat on, _____?|don't you|isn't it|aren't you|not so": "A positive statement with \"are\" takes a negative tag with \"aren't\": \"You are..., aren't you?\" C. \"Isn't it\" and \"not so\" are common Nigerian-English errors. Memory tip: copy the helping verb into the tag and flip positive/negative.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Government has frowned upon politicians who are out to _____ wealth.|gain|amass|steal|waste": "To amass wealth is to pile up a great deal of it, the usual phrase for greedy accumulation: B. Gain is weak, and steal and waste change the meaning. Memory tip: amass = gather a mass of.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): I tried to discourage him, but he persisted _____ revealing the secret to his son.|for|in|on|to": "Persist takes \"in\": persist in doing something, B. \"For\", \"on\" and \"to\" are wrong after persist. Memory tip: persist in, insist on.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): He was determined to _____ all opposition into submission.|push|cow|box|pound": "To cow someone into submission is to frighten them into obeying: B. Push, box and pound are physical actions that don't form this idiom. Memory tip: cowed = frightened into giving in.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): I am told that the market is _____ in to the interests of people from all _____ of life.|tuned/walks|constructed/places|designed/places|set/works": "\"Tuned in to\" means alert and responsive to, and \"all walks of life\" means every kind of person: A. The other pairs don't make either idiom. Memory tip: people from all walks of life = people of every background.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Uche has been in _____ with his pen pal for years.|exchange|correspondence|favour|cord": "Pen pals write to each other, so they are \"in correspondence\": B. In exchange, in favour and in cord don't fit. Memory tip: correspond = exchange letters.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Abba became _____ about his inability to pass the examination.|despondent|correspondence|favour|joyful": "Failing an examination makes him sad and hopeless: despondent, A. Joyful is the opposite, and correspondence and favour are nouns that don't fit after \"became\". Memory tip: despondent = in low spirits.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: cAUGHt|hot|water|sup|wrong": "The \"augh\" in caught is the long /ɔː/, the same as the \"a\" in water: B. Hot and wrong have the short /ɒ/, and sup has /ʌ/. Memory tip: caught, taught, water, saw all share /ɔː/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: cOUntry|worry|courteous|bought|poultry": "The \"ou\" in country is /ʌ/, like the \"o\" in worry: A. Courteous has /ɜː/, bought /ɔː/ and poultry /əʊ/. Memory tip: country, couple, double, worry, son all have /ʌ/ (as in cup).",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: trAp|wonder|because|plait|quantity": "The \"a\" in trap is /æ/; plait is pronounced /plæt/, rhyming with flat: C. Quantity and because have /ɒ/, and wonder has /ʌ/. Memory tip: plait is spelt with \"ai\" but sounds like \"plat\".",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: reSt|result|show|unasked|wise": "The \"s\" in rest is /s/, as in unasked: C. The \"s\" in result and wise is voiced /z/, and show has /ʃ/. Memory tip: s between vowels is often /z/ (result, rose); before a consonant it stays /s/ (rest, asked).",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: Joint|dagger|advantage|guardian|mightier": "The \"j\" in joint is /dʒ/, the same as the \"g\" in advantage: B. Dagger and guardian have hard /g/, and mightier has silent gh. Memory tip: g before e, i or y is often /dʒ/ (age, giant); before a, o, u it is /g/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: THought|taught|weather|theatre|fate": "The \"th\" in thought is the voiceless /θ/, as in theatre: C. Weather has the voiced /ð/, and taught and fate have /t/ and /f/. Memory tip: put your hand on your throat; /θ/ (think) has no buzz, /ð/ (this) buzzes.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: Sheer|dear|shirt|spare|shade": "Sheer /ʃɪə/ rhymes with dear /dɪə/: A. Shirt has /ɜː/, spare /eə/ and shade /eɪ/. Memory tip: rhyme = same vowel and final sound, whatever the spelling.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: Great|creek|crate|knit|treat": "Great is pronounced /greɪt/, so it rhymes with crate: B, not with treat or creek, which have /iː/. Memory tip: great, break and steak are the \"ea = /eɪ/\" exceptions.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: Own|brown|frown|gown|phone": "Own /əʊn/ rhymes with phone /fəʊn/: D. Brown, frown and gown have /aʊ/, though they look similar. Memory tip: own, shown, grown, blown (/əʊ/) vs down, town, gown (/aʊ/).",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): Ceremoniously|CEremoniously|ceREmoniously|cereMOniously|ceremoNIously": "Ceremoniously is stressed on the third syllable: ce-re-MO-ni-ous-ly, C. The base word CEremony stresses the first, but -ious pulls the stress to the syllable just before it. Memory tip: in words ending -ious, the stress falls on the syllable just before it (harMOnious, inDUStrious, cereMOnious).",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): Understandable|unDERstandable|understandABLE|UNderstandable|underSTANDable": "Understandable keeps the stress of the verb understand: un-der-STAND-a-ble, D. The suffix -able doesn't take stress. Memory tip: -able usually leaves the stress where the base word had it (underSTAND → underSTANDable).",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): Management|MAnagement|maNAgement|manaGEment|manageMENT": "Management keeps the stress of manage: MAN-age-ment, A. The suffix -ment never takes the stress. Memory tip: -ment, -ness and -ful don't move stress (MANage → MANagement).",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: LAM called Uche a pilot.|What did Lam call Uche?|Did Okoro call Uche a pilot?|Did Lam call Uche a driver?|Did Lam wish Uche to be a pilot?": "Stress on LAM corrects who did the calling, so the sentence answers a question that names someone else: \"Did Okoro call Uche a pilot?\" B. C would need stress on PILOT, and D on CALLED. Memory tip: the stressed word corrects the wrong part of the question.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: The girl DANCED in the village square.|Who danced in the village square?|Is this the village square where the girl danced?|Did the girl dance in the village square?|Did the girl sing in the village square?": "Stress on DANCED corrects the action, so it answers \"Did the girl sing in the village square?\" D. A would need stress on GIRL, and C is a plain yes/no question with no contrast. Memory tip: find the option that gets the stressed word wrong.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: My father READ the newspaper.|Did my father steal the newspaper?|Who read the newspaper?|What did my father read?|Whose father read the newspaper?": "Stress on READ corrects the action, so it answers \"Did my father steal the newspaper?\" A. B would need stress on FATHER, and C on NEWSPAPER. Memory tip: emphatic stress answers a question that got that one word wrong.",
 "eng-2010-p1|According to the writer, the exciting thing about Spaceship Earth is that it|has no instruction manual|is not difficult to maintain|is peculiar to other automobiles|is relatively easy to operate": "The writer calls it \"one outstanding important fact\" that \"no instruction book came with it\", and the whole passage builds on that: A. B and D are never claimed, and C makes no sense, since Spaceship Earth is compared with a car, not said to be peculiar to automobiles. Memory tip: the writer's main point is usually the fact he keeps coming back to.",
 "eng-2010-p1|The absence of an instruction manual in Spaceship Earth has|made the operation of the Spaceship Earth difficult|forced man to devise other means of travelling in spacecraft|challenged man's inquisitiveness|made man helpless": "Without a manual, man had to find out for himself which mangoes kill and which nourish, devise experiments and discover principles. That challenged his curiosity, his inquisitiveness: C. D is a trap: man is helpless at birth, not because of the missing manual. B invents travel in spacecraft. Memory tip: ask what the missing thing forced people to do.",
 "eng-2010-p1|From the passage, it can be deduced that man|learns by experiment and deduction|learns by chance and accidentally|is incapable of solving all his problems|by his nature is in constant search of knowledge": "The passage says we were forced \"to devise scientific experimental procedures and to interpret effectively the significance of the experimental findings\". Experimenting, then drawing conclusions, is learning by experiment and deduction: A. B (by chance) is the opposite of deliberate experiment, and C is never said. Memory tip: \"deduced\" answers must rest on words actually in the passage.",
 "eng-2010-p1|The writer's mood in the passage is that of|non-committal|pessimism|optimism|frustration": "The writer treats the missing manual as a good thing: because of it \"we are learning how we can safely survive on the planet\", and man discovered \"his most important capabilities\". That hopeful tone is optimism: C. B (pessimism) and D (frustration) are the opposite mood. Memory tip: mood = the writer's feeling; look at how he judges the main problem.",
 "eng-2010-p2|The expression '... stress is an issue everybody can relate to experimentally' means that|it is better understood when experienced|its problem can be solved by everybody|everybody avoids it|everybody encounters it": "\"Experimentally\" here means through experience, so stress is something everyone understands from having gone through it: A. B and C are not in the sentence, and D, though true in a sense, leaves out the idea of understanding through experience. D (everybody encounters it) is also accepted: the paragraph says every doctor shares the problem with patients. Memory tip: break the expression into its parts (\"relate to\" = understand, \"experimentally\" = by experience).",
 "eng-2010-p2|Which of the following is true according to the passage?|Stress is mostly caused by internal factors.|Stress can only be avoided during relaxation.|Stress is better handled by the individual.|The issues of stress can be solved with no effort.": "The passage says \"most of the stress people experience is self-generated\", i.e. from internal factors: A. The key gives C, which is also supported, since we cause our own stress and \"can do something about it\", so both are accepted. B and D are never stated. Memory tip: \"self-generated\" = internal; external stressors are things done to you.",
 "eng-2010-p2|According to the passage, the major step in controlling stress is|changing one's attitude to stressors|understanding the history of disease|visiting family doctors for check-up and treatment|constant exercise to dissipate every energy": "The passage says \"nothing beats aerobic exercise\" for draining off stress energy and calls exercise \"the most logical way to dissipate the excess energy\": D. B and C are never mentioned as steps, and A is weaker than the passage's own strong words for exercise. A (changing one's attitude) is also accepted: the passage's 'basic premise' is to change what you do and think. Memory tip: superlatives like \"nothing beats\" and \"the most logical\" point to the writer's main step.",
 "eng-2010-p2|The experts feel one can control one's stress because|its causes are understandable|it is not difficult to control|external factors contribute less stress|it is individually initiated": "\"Because we cause most of our own stress, we can do something about it\": control comes from the fact that stress is individually initiated: D. A and B are not the reason given, and C twists the point about external factors. Memory tip: look for \"because\" in the passage to answer a \"because\" question.",
 "eng-2010-p2|From the passage, it can be deduced that stress is|hormonal disorder in the body system|individually induced problems|bodily reaction to internal factors|bodily reaction to external factors": "Most stress is \"self-generated\" and \"we create the majority of our upset\", so stress is largely a problem the individual brings on: B. D is tempting, since stress is \"the body reaction\" to triggers, but the passage stresses that most of it comes from within. A (hormonal disorder) is wrong: hormones only carry the response. D is also accepted: the passage defines stress as the body's reaction to triggers like work pressure. Memory tip: \"deduce\" questions follow the writer's main emphasis.",
 "eng-2010-p3|According to the writer, corruption is triggered off by|unnecessary affluence|selfish interests|private officials|public officers": "\"Corruption occurs when a public official expects to be induced to perform an act\": D, public officers. C is wrong: private officials are never mentioned. A and B are not given as triggers. Memory tip: check that every word in an option (here \"private\") actually appears in the passage.",
 "eng-2010-p3|From the passage, one of the consequences of corruption is that|large projects are executed|it is beneficial to wealthy people who stole public wealth|it impedes the progress of a nation|people get what they want with so much money to spend": "The passage says \"corruption can slow down development\" and \"impoverishes countries\", so it impedes the progress of a nation: C. A is wrong because money goes to white elephant projects, not useful ones, and B and D praise what the passage condemns. Memory tip: impede = hinder, slow down.",
 "eng-2010-p3|Who, according to the writer, should prevent corruption?|Lawyers and Police|Corrupt public officers|The government|The people": "The writer says corruption needs \"broad-based preventive measures, and the involvement of the society at large\": the people, D. B is absurd, and A and C name only one group, while the passage calls for society as a whole. Memory tip: \"society at large\" = all the people.",
 "eng-2010-p3|Which of the following is an indication of ethical standard of governance?|Fundamental human right|Socio-economic instability|Democratic governance|Bloated expenditure": "The passage links an ethical standard of governance with good economic, political and social development. Of the options, only democratic governance shows good, ethical government: C. B and D (instability, bloated spending) are signs of corruption, and A is a right, not a standard of governance. Memory tip: eliminate options that describe the problem instead of the solution.",
 "eng-2010-p3|The essence of fighting corruption, according to the passage, is to|send the corrupt to jail|promote good governance|punish corrupt politicians|make people richer": "\"Fighting corruption and promoting governance is therefore crucial\", and corruption \"deprives their citizens of good governance\": the essence of the fight is good governance, B. A (printed \"yell\", meaning jail) and C are only punishments, and D is not the stated aim. Memory tip: \"essence\" = the main purpose, not one method.",
 "eng-2010-p4|Gap 16|ideology|phenomenon|idea|component": "Drought is a natural event that happens and brings other developments with it: a phenomenon, B. An ideology is a set of beliefs, and a component is a part of something. Memory tip: phenomenon = an observable event or occurrence (plural: phenomena).",
 "eng-2010-p4|Gap 17|feedback|Results|Implications|outcomes": "Developments that affect citizens \"have implications for\" them: C. \"Have results for\" and \"have outcomes for\" are not natural English, and feedback is a response to something. Memory tip: learn the collocation \"have implications for\".",
 "eng-2010-p4|Gap 18|managed|examined|manipulated|studied": "A crisis that is \"not successfully managed\" could cause unrest: A. Examined and studied mean looked at, not handled, and manipulated means controlled unfairly. Memory tip: we manage a crisis, an emergency or a situation.",
 "eng-2010-p4|Gap 19|objects|subjects|issues|topics": "The rains have become \"issues of considerable anxiety\": C, matters of concern. Subjects and topics are used for things discussed or studied, and objects is wrong here. Memory tip: an issue is a problem people worry about.",
 "eng-2010-p4|Gap 20|amount|supply|size|flood": "\"A periodic reduction in the normal amount of rainfall\": A. Rainfall is measured in amounts; supply is for goods, size for objects, and flood is the opposite of drought. Memory tip: \"amount\" is used with uncountable nouns like rainfall, water, money.",
 "eng-2010-p4|Gap 21|effective|disastrous|evident|inevitable": "In drought-prone areas the consequences are disastrous, as \"dry spells could be very severe\" confirms: B. Effective is a positive word, and evident and inevitable miss the idea of severity. Memory tip: let the next sentence confirm your choice.",
 "eng-2010-p4|Gap 22|routine|organized|urgent|delayed": "The two aspects of coping are emergency measures and organized (planned) measures: B. Urgent repeats \"emergency\", and delayed would not help. The key's choice contrasts hasty measures with planned ones. Routine (A) is also accepted, as the opposite of emergency measures. Memory tip: \"one ... the other\" usually introduces a contrast.",
 "eng-2010-p4|Gap 23|longitudinal|objective|simultaneous|parallel": "Distress and the sale of cattle happen alongside famine: parallel consequences, D. Longitudinal refers to length or long-term study, and objective means unbiased. Simultaneous (at the same moment) is close, but \"lasting and parallel\" describes effects that run alongside famine over time. Memory tip: parallel = happening side by side.",
 "eng-2010-p4|Gap 24|action|process|scheme|plan": "Government help given under a programme is a relief scheme: C. Action and process are too vague, and a plan is only an idea until it becomes a scheme that gives out inputs. Memory tip: relief scheme, pension scheme, loan scheme.",
 "eng-2010-p4|Gap 25|normal|correct|idealistic|effective": "With preparedness, emergency measures work better, i.e. they are more effective: D. Normal, correct and idealistic do not fit \"if a mechanism is established to ensure preparedness\". Memory tip: effective = producing the desired result.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Though Mr. Iro is a new chairman, he views other members with jaundiced eye.|He takes a rather forceful position on dealing with his members|He takes an unfavourable position concerning his members|He takes a sickly view of his members|He takes a rather hazy view of his members": "To view someone with a jaundiced eye is to judge them with prejudice or disfavour: B. C takes \"jaundiced\" literally as sickness, a common trap, and A and D miss the negative attitude. Memory tip: jaundiced view = bitter, unfavourable view.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: People are not interested in who rules.|People are not ruled by the leaders they want|People are not concerned about who rules them|The rulers are not concerned about the people|People who rule are not interested in the ruled": "\"People are not interested in who rules\" means they do not care who rules them: B. A, C and D change who is doing the caring or bring in new ideas (leaders they want, rulers' attitude). Memory tip: in \"explain the sentence\" questions, keep the same subject and the same meaning.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: It was good to steer a middle course in whatever one does.|It is always good to get midway in anything one does|It is always good to act with moderation|It is always good to move away from the forefront|It is always good to work very hard": "To steer a middle course is to avoid extremes, i.e. to act with moderation: B. A takes \"middle\" literally (getting midway), and C and D are unrelated. Memory tip: middle course = moderation, the middle way.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The witness said he had no axe to grind with his brothers.|He had no hatred for the brothers|He had no axe and therefore stole the machete|He had no axe and therefore borrowed their machete|He had no vested interest in the brothers": "To have an axe to grind with someone is to have a grudge or grievance against them, so having none means he had no hatred for them: A. B and C take \"axe\" literally. D is close to another meaning of the idiom (a selfish motive), but \"with his brothers\" points to a grudge. D is also accepted: 'an axe to grind' can also mean a selfish private motive. Memory tip: idioms are almost never about the literal objects.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The footballers moved with their tails between their legs.|they moved happily because they won the match|they were unhappy because they had been despised by their opponents|they were ashamed because they had been defeated|they moved with their tails between their legs": "To go off with one's tail between one's legs is to leave ashamed after a defeat, like a beaten dog: C. A is the opposite, B adds \"despised\", and D just repeats the idiom literally. Memory tip: picture the beaten dog.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The headmaster managed to talk his way out of having to give a speech.|he delivered a speech despite the difficulty|he managed to give a speech out of a difficult situation|he managed to get himself out of a difficult situation|he managed to talk on his way": "To talk one's way out of something is to escape a duty or trouble by clever talk; he avoided giving the speech, so he got out of a difficult situation: C. A and B say he did give the speech, the opposite. Memory tip: \"out of having to\" = avoided doing it.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: As regards the matter, we have crossed the rubicon.|we are completely at a loss|we are irrevocably committed|we are already qualified|we are perfectly committed": "Julius Caesar crossed the river Rubicon and so started a war he could not turn back from. To cross the Rubicon is to take a step that cannot be undone: irrevocably committed, B. D's \"perfectly\" misses the no-turning-back idea. Memory tip: Rubicon = point of no return.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Uche is full of himself.|He is very conceited|He is complete|He is a rich man|He is careful": "Someone full of himself has too high an opinion of himself: conceited, A. B takes \"full\" literally, and C and D are unrelated. Memory tip: full of oneself = proud, self-important.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: As debutants in that tournament, the Super Eagles were up against their first opponents by three goals to nil.|The Super Eagles were playing in the tournament for the first time, but they won their match by three goals to nothing|Though the Super Eagles were rated as the weakest side in the tournament, they won their first match by three goals to nil|Even though the Super Eagles were playing without some of their regulars, they won their match by three goals to nil|As the best attackers in the match, the Super Eagles easily defeated their opponents by three goals to nothing": "Debutants are those appearing for the first time, and being \"up ... by three goals to nil\" means winning 3-0: A. B, C and D add ideas not in the sentence (weakest side, missing regulars, best attackers). Memory tip: debut = first appearance.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The woman was mournful as her husband was found dead drunk.|She was sad because her husband was absolutely drunk|She was apprehensive that her husband would drink again as soon he recovered from the drunken stupor|She was sad because her husband was drunk and always as helpless as a dead man|She was mourning because her husband drank and died": "Dead drunk is an intensifier meaning extremely drunk, not dead: A. D is the trap, reading \"dead\" literally. B and C add ideas not in the sentence. Memory tip: dead drunk, dead tired, dead right: \"dead\" here means completely.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: I am optimistic about the interview though it was a MIND-BENDING exercise.|An enervating|A debilitating|A difficult|An easy": "A mind-bending exercise is extremely difficult and taxing to the mind; its opposite is an easy one: D. A, B and C (enervating, debilitating, difficult) all mean the same as the word, not the opposite. Memory tip: in antonym questions, cross out the synonyms first.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The trader was amused by the CUT-THROAT rush for the goods.|Worrisome|Strange|Lacklustre|Mad": "A cut-throat rush is fierce, intense and ruthless; the opposite is a lacklustre one, without energy or eagerness: C. Mad is close to the original meaning, and worrisome and strange do not oppose it. Memory tip: lacklustre = dull, lacking energy.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The teacher said that Ali's essay was full of many REDUNDANT details.|Unexplained|Strange|Necessary|Useful": "Redundant details are unnecessary ones; the opposite is necessary: C. Useful (D) is tempting but weaker: something can be useful without being needed. Memory tip: redundant = not needed, so its opposite is needed.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: His father surmounted the MYRIAD of obstacles on his way.|Most|Few|All|Many": "A myriad is a countless, very large number; the opposite is few: B. Many (D) is a synonym, and most and all do not contrast with it. Memory tip: myriad = countless.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Her INGENUOUS smile drew our attention.|Witty|Naïve|Clever|Arrogant": "Ingenuous means innocent, artless and trusting. Of the options, clever (knowing, worldly-wise) is the opposite: C. Naïve (B) is a synonym, the trap. Do not confuse ingenuous with ingenious (clever). Memory tip: ingenuous = genuine and innocent; ingenious = inventive.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Ndeni gave a FLAWLESS speech at the party.|A wonderful|A careless|An interesting|An imperfect": "Flawless means without any fault; the opposite is imperfect: D. Wonderful and interesting are near-synonyms of a good speech, and careless describes the speaker, not the result. Memory tip: flaw = fault, so flawless = perfect.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Beneath Ado's GRUFF exterior, he's really very kind-hearted.|Nice|Harsh|Rough|Gentle": "Gruff (printed \"guff\" on the paper) means rough and unfriendly in manner. Its opposite can be nice, the key's A, or gentle, D, so both are accepted. Harsh and rough (B, C) are synonyms. Memory tip: the \"but\" in \"gruff exterior ... kind-hearted\" signals a contrast, which points you to the antonym.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The captain says sports is being DEBASED by commercial sponsorship.|Localized|Perverted|Elevated|Overvalued": "To debase is to lower the quality or value of something; the opposite is to elevate, raise up: C. Perverted (B) is close to a synonym, and overvalued is about price, not quality. Memory tip: de- = down, so debase = bring down.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Governing a country is not always as STRAIGHTFORWARD as people sometimes imagine.|Complicated|Troublesome|Untoward|Irksome": "Straightforward means simple and easy to do; the opposite is complicated: A. Troublesome and irksome mean annoying, and untoward means unexpected and unpleasant. Memory tip: straightforward is the opposite of complicated or complex.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The crowd was very RECEPTIVE to the speaker's suggestion.|Disobedient|Repellent|Alert|Hostile": "A receptive crowd is willing to accept ideas; the opposite is hostile: D. Disobedient is about orders, and repellent describes something that drives others away. Memory tip: receptive = open to; hostile = against.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: There was a general ACQUIESCENCE on the new drug law.|Resistance|Discrepancy|Compromise|Agreement": "Acquiescence is quiet acceptance; the opposite is resistance: A. Agreement (D) is the synonym trap, and compromise is a meeting halfway. Memory tip: acquiesce = accept without protest.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Aisha seems to feel AMBIVALENT about her future.|Decisive|Anxious|Ambitious|Inconsiderate": "Ambivalent means having mixed feelings, unable to decide; the opposite is decisive: A. Anxious is a feeling that can go with ambivalence, not its opposite. Memory tip: ambi- = both (ambidextrous), so ambivalent = pulled both ways.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The report of the committee contained a PLETHORA of details.|Shortage|Simplicity|Multitude|Spectrum": "A plethora is an excess, far too many; the opposite is a shortage: A. Multitude (C) is a synonym trap. Memory tip: plethora = plenty; shortage = too few.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The weather was still very HEAVY AND SULTRY.|Wintry and shadowy|Cold and friendly|Cloudy and thundery|Hot and uncomfortable": "Heavy and sultry weather is hot, humid and oppressive. Cold is the opposite of hot, and friendly (pleasant) is the opposite of oppressive: B. D is a synonym, and C describes stormy weather. Memory tip: oppose each word in the phrase one by one.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Ada gave her husband a look that made words SUPERFLUOUS.|Redundant|Spurious|Unnecessary|Scanty": "Superfluous means more than is needed; scanty means too little: D. Redundant and unnecessary (A, C) are synonyms, the trap. Memory tip: super- = over, so superfluous = overflowing; its opposite is scanty.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: A political IMPASSE does not offer the best opportunity for merrymaking.|manifesto|party|gridlock|rally": "An impasse is a deadlock in which no progress can be made: gridlock, C. Manifesto, party and rally are political words that only fit the setting, not the meaning. Memory tip: impasse = no way to pass.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: We were all ENTHUSIASTIC as we awaited the result of the election.|bemused|agitated|elated|nervous": "Enthusiastic means eager and excited, and elated (very happy and excited) is closest: C. Agitated and nervous suggest worry, and bemused means puzzled. Memory tip: pick the option with the same feeling (positive or negative) as the word.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The uniform makes the guards look ABSURD.|dirty|smart|sensible|ridiculous": "Absurd means ridiculous, foolish-looking: D. Smart and sensible are near-opposites, and dirty is unrelated. Memory tip: absurd = laughable, ridiculous.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The law is often TARDY in reacting to changing attitude.|quick|slow|exclusive|generous": "Tardy means slow or late: B. Quick (A) is the opposite, the trap in a synonym question. Memory tip: a tardy student comes late.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Isa and Ola ate SUMPTUOUS meal on their brother's wedding day.|expensive|foreign|insipid|cheap": "A sumptuous meal is lavish, rich and costly: expensive, A. Cheap (D) is the opposite, and insipid means tasteless. Memory tip: sumptuous = splendid and expensive.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Kaltume CROUCHED over the paper on her desk.|wrote on|stood on|walked over|bent over": "To crouch over something is to bend low over it: D. Stood on and walked over describe movements that are not crouching, and wrote on misses the posture. Memory tip: crouch = bend the knees and lower the body.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The PANACEA for a country's economic mess lies in systematic planning and hardwork.|cure|hope|foresight|trouble": "A panacea is a remedy for all problems: a cure, A. Hope and foresight are good qualities but not remedies, and trouble is the opposite. Memory tip: panacea = cure-all (pan- = all).",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Thousands of workers have been victims of RETRENCHMENT since the military came back to power.|Unemployment|Trench mentality|Suffering|Increase in penury": "Retrenchment is the cutting of staff to save costs, which leaves workers out of jobs: unemployment, A. B plays on the sound of \"trench\", and C and D are possible results, not the meaning. Memory tip: retrench = cut down, especially jobs.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The principal gave his speech OFFHAND at the sports meeting.|calmly|beautifully|unconcerned|unprepared": "To speak offhand is to speak without preparation: unprepared, D. Unconcerned (C) fits \"offhand\" meaning casual in manner, but a speech given offhand is one given off the cuff. Memory tip: offhand speech = impromptu, off the cuff.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Jankoli was dressed in an old ASSORTMENT of clothes.|Avalanche|Homogeneity|Sameness|Melange": "An assortment is a mixture of different kinds: a melange, D. Homogeneity and sameness (B, C) are opposites, and avalanche is a great mass falling suddenly. Memory tip: melange is French for mixture.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The girl's father was ASTOUNDED to see her appear from the shrine.|collected|Overwhelmed|embarrassed|Astonished": "Astounded means greatly surprised: astonished, D. Overwhelmed (B) is close but means overpowered by emotion, not surprise; collected means calm. Memory tip: astound, astonish, amaze all mean surprise greatly.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The director's remark was extremely APPOSITE to the issue being discussed.|Appropriate|Inconsequential|Emphatic|Adequate": "Apposite means apt, very appropriate: A. Adequate (D) only means good enough, and inconsequential is the opposite. Memory tip: apposite = appropriate (both start with \"app\").",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Her reputation is without a BLEMISH.|Struggle|Problem|Fault|Blessing": "A blemish is a mark or flaw that spoils something: a fault, C. Problem (B) is too general, and blessing is the opposite idea. Memory tip: without a blemish = spotless, faultless.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Ugo is ELIGIBLE for the post of secretary.|Nominated|Invited|Qualified|Intelligent": "Eligible means qualified or entitled to be chosen: C. Nominated and invited mean already put forward, which is a step beyond eligible. Memory tip: eligible = meets the requirements.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: This is an ABRIDGED version of No Longer at Ease.|An outdated|An enlarged|An illustrated|A shortened": "An abridged version is a shortened one: D. Enlarged (B) is the opposite. Memory tip: abridge = shorten; an abridged novel is a cut-down edition.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Lemoti _____ as a painter, but also as a sculptor.|is a gifted only not|is only not gifted|is gifted not only|is only gifted": "\"Not only ... but also\" must link matching parts, and \"not only\" goes just before the first item: gifted not only as a painter, but also as a sculptor: C. A and B scramble the words, and D drops \"not\". Memory tip: put \"not only\" directly before the first thing you compare.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): He can recall the important dates in the nation's history, it is interesting to listen as he rattles _____.|off|over|up|out": "To rattle off is to recite a list quickly and easily from memory: A. Rattle over, up and out are not used this way. Memory tip: rattle off names, dates or figures.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The boy told his mother _____.|that was the girl he told her about|that was the girl I told you about|that was the girl I told her about|that is the girl he told her about": "In reported speech, \"I\" becomes \"he\", \"you\" becomes \"her\", and \"is\" moves back to \"was\": A. B keeps the direct-speech pronouns, C keeps \"I\", and D keeps \"is\". Memory tip: change the pronouns to fit who is reporting.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Last Monday his father asked me _____.|if I had come some days before|if I had come the day before|did you come yesterday|had I come yesterday": "A reported question uses \"if\" with statement word order, the past perfect, and \"yesterday\" becomes \"the day before\": B. C and D keep question word order, and A changes \"yesterday\" to \"some days before\". Memory tip: yesterday becomes the day before; tomorrow becomes the next day.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): His wife was badly injured in the fracas, but I think she will pull _____.|up|over|through|back": "To pull through is to recover from a serious illness or injury: C. Pull up (stop), pull over (move to the roadside) and pull back (withdraw) do not mean recover. Memory tip: you pull through an illness.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): A wide range of options _____ made available to students in the final year last year.|is|were|are|was": "The head of \"a wide range of options\" is \"range\", which is singular, and \"last year\" needs the past: was, D. Is and are are present tense. B (were) is common in everyday speech, but JAMB follows the head-noun rule. The key gives no answer. Memory tip: find the head noun before \"of\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): One of the women who _____ in the premises _____ been ordered to quit.|sells/have|sell/has|sell/have|sells/has": "\"Who\" refers to \"the women\", so \"who sell\" is plural; the main subject is \"one\", so \"has been ordered\": B. D is wrong: it makes \"who\" agree with \"one\", which standard grammar treats as an error. Memory tip: \"one of those who\" + plural verb, then a singular main verb.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The new trade agreement should facilitate _____.|more economic rapid growth|economic more rapid growth|rapid economic more growth|more rapid economic growth": "\"More\" modifies \"rapid\", and the classifying adjective \"economic\" stays next to the noun: more rapid economic growth, D. A, B and C break up \"more rapid\" or move \"economic\". Memory tip: the classifying adjective (economic, political) goes closest to the noun.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The principal said that he was pleased _____ my effort.|on|of|with|about": "We say \"pleased with\" someone or something: C. \"Pleased about\" is used with situations, not someone's effort, and \"of\" and \"on\" are wrong. Memory tip: pleased with you, pleased with your work.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Paper is made _____ wood pulp.|on|of|from|with": "\"Made from\" is used when the raw material is changed in the process, as wood pulp becomes paper: C. \"Made of\" (B) is for materials you can still see, like a wooden table. Memory tip: changed material, \"from\"; visible material, \"of\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Long after everyone _____ the hall, Obi still sat inside.|left|is leaving|has left|had left": "Everyone's leaving happened first, and Obi was still sitting later, so the earlier past action takes the past perfect: had left, D. A is possible in speech, but \"long after\" points clearly to the earlier action. Memory tip: the earlier of two past actions = had + past participle.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): They are the _____ dresses.|babys'|baby|babies|babies'": "The plural of baby is babies, and a plural ending in -s takes the apostrophe after the s: babies' dresses, D. A (babys') is a misspelling, and C has no apostrophe. Memory tip: make it plural first, then add the apostrophe.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The politician was sent _____ exile.|onto|into|on|to": "The standard expression is \"sent into exile\" (or \"went into exile\"): B. C is wrong: \"sent on exile\" is not standard English. Onto and to do not fit either. Memory tip: into exile, in exile (living there), out of exile.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): When we looked up, we _____ the plane some miles away.|site|cited|sited|sighted": "To sight something is to see it, especially far away: sighted, D. Site and sited are about location, and cited means quoted or mentioned. Memory tip: sight = see, site = place, cite = quote.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Vital _____ is still spread _____ word of mouth in most villages in Africa.|information/from|information/with|information/by|information/through": "The fixed expression is \"by word of mouth\", meaning by people telling one another: C. \"Through\" (D) is tempting but not the idiom. Memory tip: learn \"by word of mouth\" as one unit.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Western education is one of the _____ of colonial rule.|legacies|evidence|remnants|inheritance": "A legacy is something handed down from the past, here colonial rule: legacies, A. Evidence is uncountable and has no plural \"evidences\" here, remnants suggests leftover scraps, and inheritance usually means property passed to an heir. Memory tip: legacies of colonial rule, of a leader, of history.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The federal government has _____ child trafficking.|postulated|projected|prescribed|proscribed": "To proscribe is to forbid by law: D. Prescribe (C) is the trap: it means to recommend or order, as a doctor prescribes medicine. Memory tip: proscribe = prohibit (both start with \"pro\").",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The man was happy that his son confessed his guilt and so the others were _____.|Implicated|accused|punished|exonerated": "When the son confessed, the others were cleared of blame: exonerated, D. Implicated, accused and punished all mean the opposite, being blamed. Memory tip: exonerate = free from blame.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Based on the facts before me, I have no alternative _____ to hold you responsible.|only|as|than|but": "The fixed expression is \"no alternative but to\": D. \"No alternative than\" is a common error. Memory tip: no choice but, no option but, no alternative but.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Many people would always find reasons to _____ the law.|arrogate|debase|circumvent|circumspect": "To circumvent the law is to find a clever way round it: C. Arrogate means to claim without right, debase means to lower in value, and circumspect is an adjective meaning cautious, so it cannot follow \"to\". Memory tip: circum- = around, so circumvent = get around.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: cOUp|whup|hoot|couple|scout": "The ou in coup is the long /uː/ (the p is silent), as in hoot: B. Whup has a short vowel, couple has /ʌ/, and scout has /aʊ/. Memory tip: coup comes from French, so ou = /uː/, as in group and soup.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: indIct|bright|fish|pick|brick": "Indict is pronounced in-DITE, so the underlined i is /aɪ/, as in bright: A. Fish, pick and brick all have short /ɪ/, the trap for anyone who reads the spelling. Memory tip: in indict and indictment the c is silent and the i says /aɪ/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: rOAred|towered|coast|brought|rod": "The oa in roared is /ɔː/, the same vowel as the ou in brought: C. Towered has /aʊə/, coast /əʊ/, and rod the short /ɒ/. Memory tip: roared, poured, bought, caught all share /ɔː/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: sheaTH|bathe|length|months|paths": "Sheath ends in the voiceless /θ/, as in length: B. Months, /mʌnθs/, also has /θ/, so C is accepted too. Bathe has the voiced /ð/, and paths is usually /pɑːðz/ in British English. Memory tip: put your hand on your throat; /θ/ has no buzz, /ð/ does.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: High (the paper underlines 'gh'; this is a misprint for the h at the start)|what|honest|who|vehicle": "The paper underlines 'gh' in 'high', but those letters are silent, so the underline is a printing error; it was meant for the h at the start, which is /h/. The wh in who is pronounced /h/ too: C. Honest and vehicle have a silent h, and what begins with /w/. Memory tip: wh is /h/ before o (who, whom, whose, whole), but /w/ in most other words (what, when, where).",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: oF course|plough|dough|over|orphan": "The f in \"of\" is pronounced /v/, as in over: C. Orphan has /f/, the trap for spelling readers, and the gh in plough and dough is silent. Memory tip: \"of\" is the one common word where f says /v/.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: Boys|stays|moist|noise|elbows": "Boys /bɔɪz/ rhymes with noise /nɔɪz/: same vowel and same /z/ ending. Moist has /ɔɪ/ but ends in /st/, and stays has /eɪ/. So C. Memory tip: a rhyme needs the same vowel and the same final sounds.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: Shine|clean|fine|machine|lain": "Shine /ʃaɪn/ rhymes with fine /faɪn/: B. Machine is the trap: it is spelt with \"ine\" but said /iːn/, like clean. Lain has /eɪ/. Memory tip: rhyme by sound, not by spelling.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: Seer|snare|spare|spear|square": "Seer /sɪə/ rhymes with spear /spɪə/: C. Snare, spare and square all have /eə/. Memory tip: seer, spear, near and fear share /ɪə/.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): political|poliTIcal|poLItical|POlitical|political": "Political is stressed on the second syllable: po-LI-ti-cal, B. POlitics is stressed on the first, but adding -al moves the stress to the syllable before it. Memory tip: words ending in -ical stress the syllable just before -ical (poLItical, hiSTORical).",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): satisfactory|saTISfactory|satisFACtory|SATisfactory|satisfactory": "Satisfactory is stressed on the third syllable: sa-tis-FAC-to-ry, B. C is a trap from SATisfy, but the stress moves in the longer word. Memory tip: think of satisFACtion; satisfactory keeps the stress on the same FAC syllable.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): captivity|captiVIty|CAPtivity|capTIvity|CAPtiviTY": "Captivity is stressed on the second syllable: cap-TI-vi-ty, C. B is a trap from CAPtive, but -ity pulls the stress forward. Memory tip: -ity words stress the syllable just before -ity (capTIvity, aBIlity).",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: EMEKA finished his home work yesterday.|Was Emeka helped to do his homework?|Did Emeka do his homework?|When did Emeka finish his homework?|Who finished his home work yesterday?": "Stress on EMEKA says who did it, so the sentence answers \"Who finished his homework yesterday?\": D. B would stress \"finished\", and C would stress \"yesterday\". Memory tip: the stressed word answers the question or corrects the wrong idea.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: Taiwo SAILED to London.|Did Taiwo fly to London?|Did Taiwo sail to Brazil?|Did Taiwo sail to London?|Where did Taiwo sail to?": "Stress on SAILED corrects the way he travelled, so the sentence answers \"Did Taiwo fly to London?\": A. B would need stress on London, and D asks a where-question. Memory tip: emphatic stress usually corrects a wrong guess in a yes/no question.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: My bag is made of LEATHER.|Whose bag is made of leather?|Is my bag made of polythene?|Is Abu's bag made of leather?|Is my bag made of leather?": "Stress on LEATHER corrects the material, so it answers \"Is my bag made of polythene?\": B. A would stress \"my\", C would stress \"My\" against Abu's, and D needs no correction at all. Memory tip: find the option that is wrong about exactly the stressed word.",
 "eng-2011-p1|The writer's posture, as conveyed in the statement 'Electricity from living cells is no new idea', can be described as|ineffectual|contentious|logical|unguarded": "Right after \"Electricity from living cells is no new idea\", the writer lines up evidence in order: shocks from fish, Franklin's lightning, Galvani's muscles, the catfish and eel, our own hearts and brains. A claim backed step by step with evidence is logical: C. It is not contentious (he isn't picking a quarrel) or unguarded (careless). Memory tip: to judge a writer's posture, look at what follows the statement.",
 "eng-2011-p1|Which of the following is true according to the passage?|Scientists felt that the bio cell would produce very costly energy.|Bio cells, at the beginning, derived their energy from sugar.|Sugar and fuel were initially used as source of energy for bio cells.|Bio cells were forms of power used by the scientists.": "\"Early bio cells were powered with sugar\" is restated almost word for word in B. The key gives C, which can also be defended (the cell ran on a liquid fuel, and early ones used sugar), so both are accepted. A is false: scientists expected the bio cell to produce power cheaply. Memory tip: the option that closely restates one sentence of the passage is usually safest.",
 "eng-2011-p1|The inventor of the bio cell justified the need for it by saying that it would|develop ways for changing bio cells into fuel for use|yield a source of energy without much spending|produce electricity for all types of machines|produce signals to guide all ships and other vessels": "Scientists felt the bio cell \"will one day produce power cheaply\" and would use materials that would otherwise be waste: energy without much spending, B. C and D stretch \"for radio, for signal to guide ship\" into \"all machines\" and \"all ships\". Memory tip: beware of options that turn the passage's \"some\" into \"all\".",
 "eng-2011-p1|According to the passage, electricity was first discovered in|heart and brains|muscles of animals|lightning|fish": "Man felt \"the strange shock produce by some fish even before electricity was really discovered\", so fish came first: D. Franklin's lightning, Galvani's muscles and the heart and brain came later. Memory tip: \"even before\" signals the earliest event in a sequence.",
 "eng-2011-p2|Which of the following represents the writer's view in the passage?|Evil thoughts may come but there is virtue in keeping them out.|Evil thought will continue to sting us like vipers as long as there are enemies who cause offence.|Like the pendulum, evil thought will always come to our mind no matter what we do.|Like most birds, evil thoughts fly swiftly in our minds without perching.": "The writer says we cannot stop birds flying over our heads, but we can stop them nesting in our hair; vain thoughts may knock, but we must not open the door. So evil thoughts may come, but there is virtue in keeping them out: A. C contradicts this by saying we can do nothing. Memory tip: the writer's overall view usually runs through every paragraph, not just one image.",
 "eng-2011-p2|Which of the following statements represents the view expressed by the writer in the first paragraph?|Evil thought will eventually ruin the evil man.|If we do not stop the pendulum of thought from swinging, our thoughts will soon become our enemies.|Too many evil thoughts leave fatal consequences.|It is possible to decide what controls our thoughts.": "Paragraph 1 warns that if our thoughts become our enemies \"they will be too many for us and will drag us down to ruin\". Too many evil thoughts lead to ruin, i.e. fatal consequences: C. B misuses the pendulum image, which only describes how fast the mind moves. Memory tip: when a question names a paragraph, answer from that paragraph alone.",
 "eng-2011-p2|From the argument in the second paragraph, it can be concluded that evil thoughts control the lives of people who|are helpless because they fly out of their minds|cherish idle and slothful ways|are thieves with evil instincts|treasure and ruminate on them": "Paragraph 2 says the trouble starts when we \"receive them joyfully\", turn evil over like a morsel in the mouth and \"meditate upon evil\". Evil thoughts control people who treasure and ruminate on them: D. A reverses the point, and C takes the thieves image literally. Memory tip: ruminate = chew over, think about again and again.",
 "eng-2011-p2|The expression 'Think of the devil, and he will appear', as used in the passage, suggests that|like the devil, evil thoughts must not reign in our hearts|evil thoughts are fantasies which exist only in people's minds|uncontrolled evil thoughts may lead to evil deeds|the devil gives evil thoughts only to those who invite him in": "The writer explains the saying at once: \"turn your thought toward evil and your hands will soon follow\". Thoughts left uncontrolled lead to evil deeds: C. B says the thoughts are only fantasies, which misses the point, and D takes the devil literally. Memory tip: proverbs in a passage are explained by the sentence right next to them.",
 "eng-2011-p2|Which of the following statements summarizes the argument of the fourth paragraph?|Heavy traffic on a miry and dirty road may lead to evil thoughts.|The more evil we think, the more vile we are likely to become.|Evil people should not be welcomed as guests in our homes the same way we welcome good people.|Evil thoughts control the key to the human heart and no one can keep them out.": "The paragraph on the \"traffic of bad thinking\" (the paper calls it the fourth) says every wave of wicked thought \"adds something to the corruption\". The more evil we think, the viler we become: B. A takes \"traffic\" and \"mire\" literally. Memory tip: in figurative passages, the literal option (roads, mud) is usually the trap.",
 "eng-2011-p3|According to the writer, people lead and motivate others because they want to|project individual contribution|encourage selfless service|make the world a home|prevent empty search": "\"We lead, inspire and motivate people to strive and succeed\": the aim is to get each person to put in their own effort and contribution, A. D (\"prevent empty search\") echoes \"discourage vain pursuits\", but that is the reason for painting a true picture of life, not for leading. Memory tip: match the question to the exact sentence it paraphrases, not a neighbouring one.",
 "eng-2011-p3|According to the passage, balance must be enthroned because it is|a critical interdependent function|an amazing help for conscience|a critical part of fidelity|a serious way of ensuring success": "The passage says \"Balance must be enthroned as a critical component of truth\". Fidelity means faithfulness or exactness to the truth, so \"a critical part of fidelity\" says the same thing: C. The key gives B, but nothing links balance to conscience. Memory tip: fidelity = faithfulness (to a partner, or to the facts).",
 "eng-2011-p3|The word 'inclinations', as used in the passage, means|creeds|tendencies|inhibitions|power": "Inclinations are natural leanings, the things you tend to like or do: tendencies, B. Creeds are beliefs, and inhibitions are the opposite (things that hold you back). Memory tip: to be inclined to do something = to have a tendency to do it.",
 "eng-2011-p3|Which of the following statements is true according to the passage?|Greatness in life emerges when square pegs are put in round holes.|People do certain things in life because they know the repercussions.|People agree on all issues and behave the same way for the same reason.|Understanding life at different levels gives no account of visible acquisition.": "\"We eat because we all understand the consequences of not eating. We all wear clothes because each of us comprehends lunacy.\" People act because they know the repercussions: B. The key gives D, but the passage says different levels of understanding explain why some become wealthy and others don't. C contradicts \"we all behave differently\". Memory tip: examples in a passage often carry the answer to \"which is true\".",
 "eng-2011-p3|From the passage, it can be inferred that|people insincerely discuss facts that govern their behaviour|all managerial decisions are based on assumptions|people make conscious effort to acquire hidden knowledge|all things in life exist on some beliefs": "\"Everything in life operate under certain assumptions\", and assumptions are beliefs taken for granted, so all things rest on some beliefs: D. B (management decisions) is also stated, so it is accepted. The key gives C, but the passage never mentions hidden knowledge, and only \"some of us\" act consciously. Memory tip: an inference must rest on what the passage actually says.",
 "eng-2011-p4|Gap 16: We live in an era of amazing _____ change spawned by advancing technology and industrialization.|well-define|fast-paced|favorable|social": "Change \"spawned by advancing technology\" is amazing because it is rapid: fast-paced, B. Favourable doesn't fit a passage about change harming the climate, and \"well-define\" is not even a correct form. Memory tip: read the words after the gap; they explain the adjective.",
 "eng-2011-p4|Gap 17: However, man's _____ promoting and defending change ... is proving unfavourable to the climate.|knowledge of|attitude to|commitment to|opinion of": "\"Commitment to\" is followed by the -ing form: commitment to promoting change, C. \"Attitude to promoting\" is possible in form, but the sentence describes a deliberate effort, which is commitment. Knowledge of and opinion of don't fit \"in a deliberate effort\". Memory tip: committed to / commitment to + -ing (to is a preposition here).",
 "eng-2011-p4|Gap 18: ... in a deliberate effort to establish _____ that stimulate advancement for man's concern.|customs|companies|trade-zone|variations": "The passage is all about change, and man promotes change to establish variations (new ways of doing things) that stimulate advancement: D. Customs are old, fixed habits, the opposite of change. Memory tip: keep the passage's main idea in mind when choosing a cloze word.",
 "eng-2011-p4|Gap 19: ... is proving unfavourable to the climate with threatening _____.|repercussions|clouds|pressure|implications": "Threatening repercussions are dangerous after-effects, here of man's actions on the climate: A. Implications are things that are suggested or may follow, a weaker word than the damaging results meant here, and clouds and pressure are literal traps. Memory tip: repercussions = consequences that come back at you.",
 "eng-2011-p4|Gap 20: As a matter of fact, climate change is now _____ globe issue.|an acceptable|a foremost|the only|the last": "Climate change has \"awakened widespread concern across the globe\", so it is now a foremost (leading) issue: B. \"The only\" and \"the last\" overstate it, and \"acceptable\" contradicts the concern. Memory tip: foremost = most important, at the front.",
 "eng-2011-p4|Gap 21: It is a major test of Africa's _____.|popularity|energy|ingenuity|incapability": "A great problem tests a people's ingenuity, their cleverness at finding solutions: C. Incapability is negative, and you can't be tested on your inability. Memory tip: ingenuity, from ingenious = clever and inventive.",
 "eng-2011-p4|Gap 22: ... human action are changing the earth's climate and creating major disturbance in human _____ and ecosystems.|geography|society|systems|life": "Climate change disturbs human life and the natural ecosystems: D. Geography and society are less natural partners for \"ecosystems\" here. Systems (C) is also accepted: it is the IPCC's own phrase, 'human systems and ecosystems'. Memory tip: in a cloze, pick the noun that pairs most naturally with the word joined to it by \"and\".",
 "eng-2011-p4|Gap 23: The rising global _____ for energy ...|command|demand|warming|supply": "We speak of demand for energy, and it is rising demand that drives emissions: B. Supply is the opposite side, and \"warming for energy\" makes no sense. Memory tip: demand for, supply of.",
 "eng-2011-p4|Gap 24: ... commensurate with the level of greenhouse _____ it spews out ...|structure|paints|emulsion|emissions": "Greenhouse emissions are the gases a country \"spews out\": D. Structure, paints and emulsion take \"greenhouse\" literally as a building. Memory tip: greenhouse gases, greenhouse emissions, greenhouse effect.",
 "eng-2011-p4|Gap 25: ... a strategy that cannot, thus far, be termed _____.|notable|liable|credible|flexible": "The writer is doubtful about leaving Africa's problem to others, so the strategy cannot be called credible (believable): C. B is wrong: \"liable\" means legally responsible or likely to, and a strategy cannot be \"termed liable\". Memory tip: credible = can be believed (credit, incredible).",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: If he were here, it could be more fun.|He was expected but did not show up to make the occasion lively|There was no fun because he was not present|He did not show up and so the occasion lacked much fun|He was being expected to supply more fun": "\"If he were here\" is an unreal condition: he is not here, so the occasion has less fun than it could: C. A adds that he was expected, which the sentence doesn't say, and B goes too far (\"no fun\"). Memory tip: if + were = he is not actually here.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The secretary said that the postponement of the meeting was due to unforeseen circumstances.|The date of the meeting was shifted as a result of unexpected reasons|The meeting's date was put off for strange reasons|The meeting was called off as a result of obstacles hitherto unknown|The meeting broke off as a result of unusual difficulties": "To postpone is to move to a later date, and unforeseen means unexpected: A. C (\"called off\") means cancelled, and D (\"broke off\") means stopped in the middle. Memory tip: postponed = put off to later; called off = cancelled.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The hunter has a bird's-eye view of the animals.|He views the animals from a high position|He views the bird's eye|He views the birds on the tree with one eye|He watches animals and birds closely": "A bird's-eye view is a view from high above, as a flying bird sees things: A. B and C take \"bird\" and \"eye\" literally. Memory tip: idioms are never about the literal objects they mention.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Even though Susan was the last in the examination, her result wasn't too different from what had been expected.|Her result was poor|Her result was a disappointment|Her result was as expected|She had not been serious with her studies": "\"Not too different from what had been expected\" means it was roughly as expected: C. A and D may be true of someone who came last, but the sentence only compares the result with the expectation. Memory tip: answer what the sentence says, not what you might guess about it.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Mrs Adasu does all her work with more haste, less speed.|She accepts whatever she does with more haste and speed|She approaches whatever she does hurriedly|She addresses everything she does very quickly to avoid mistakes|She does everything carefully to avoid mistakes": "The proverb \"more haste, less speed\" means rushing causes mistakes and makes you slower. Someone who works by it takes care to avoid mistakes: D. B and C describe hurrying, the very thing the proverb warns against. Memory tip: more haste, less speed = slow and careful wins.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: She stopped her education as her uncle left her in the lurch.|Her uncle deceived her|Her uncle disinherited her|Her uncle refused to help her|Her uncle disrespected her": "To leave someone in the lurch is to abandon them when they most need help: her uncle refused to help her, C. Deceived and disinherited are traps. Memory tip: left in the lurch = left stranded without support.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The plan to upgrade the dispensary to a general hospital did not materialize.|The plan did not meet the required specifications|The arrangement did not work out as wished|It was difficult to obtain the materials|The materials purchased were not the right ones": "A plan that does not materialize does not happen as hoped: B. C and D play on \"materials\". Memory tip: materialize = become real; it has nothing to do with building materials.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Okon's company took a hit last year.|His company improved last year|His company made a huge success last year|His company was badly damaged last year|His company was established last year": "To take a hit is to suffer a loss or damage: C. A and B are the opposite. Memory tip: a business \"takes a hit\" the way a boxer does.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: My eldest son, who is in Lagos, is studying English.|Only my son is in Lagos studying English|My only son is in Lagos studying English|One of my sons is in Lagos studying English|My sons are in Lagos but only one is studying English": "\"Eldest\" is the superlative, used when there are three or more sons. So he has several sons, and one of them is in Lagos studying English: C. B (\"only son\") contradicts \"eldest\". Memory tip: elder of two, eldest of three or more.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: If I went to the village, I would visit the king.|If I go to the village I will visit the king|I did not go to the village and I did not visit the king|All the times I went to the village I also visited the king|I will visit the king when I go to the village": "\"If I went ... I would visit\" is an unreal condition: the speaker is not actually going, so there is no visit. Strictly, B puts this in the past (\"did not go\"), which would need \"If I had gone ... I would have visited\", so no option is perfect; B, the key's answer, is the closest. A and D turn it into a real plan, and C reads \"would\" as a past habit. Memory tip: past tense + would = imagined now or in the future; had + past participle + would have = didn't happen in the past.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Since its INCEPTION in 1983, the newspaper has attracted thousands of readers.|renaissance|coming|commencement|publication": "Inception means the beginning or start of something: commencement, C. Publication is a trap (it's a newspaper), but the word is about when it began. Memory tip: inception, like \"incipient\", means at the start.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Mrs Asio wanted her sister to stop being so DETACHED.|friendly|careless|indifferent|passionate": "Detached means emotionally distant and unconcerned: indifferent, C. Passionate and friendly are opposites. Memory tip: detached = not attached = not involved.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Lantana DWELT in a ruined cottage on the hillside.|sat|worked|slept|lived": "Dwelt is the past tense of dwell, to live in a place: D. Memory tip: a dwelling is a home.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The mistake brought the show to an IGNOMINIOUS end.|a good|a palatable|a disgraceful|a satisfactory": "Ignominious means shameful and humiliating: disgraceful, C. The other options are all positive. Memory tip: ignominy = public shame.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He COMPLIMENTS me on my way of doing things.|complements|imitates|disgusts|praises": "To compliment someone is to praise them: D. A is the trap: complement (with an e) means to complete or go well with. Memory tip: compLIment = I like what you did; compLEment = compLEtes.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The girl is angry with her friend who had ENSNARED her into this relationship.|tricked|encouraged|encouraged|forced": "To ensnare is to trap someone, as an animal in a snare, by trickery: A. Forced misses the cunning in it. (The paper prints \"encouraged\" twice, as B and C.) Memory tip: a snare is a trap.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Their new house was roofed with CORRUGATED sheets.|folded|iron|aluminium|corrupted": "Corrugated describes the shape: sheets pressed into ridges or folds: A. B and C name metals, but corrugated sheets can be iron or aluminium. D is a sound-alike trap. Memory tip: corrugated = wavy ridges.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The stockbroker said it was an ASTUTE move to sell the shares then.|a bad|a shrewd|an unprofitable|an insincere": "Astute means sharp and clever in judging a situation: shrewd, B. Memory tip: an astute businessman is a shrewd one.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The principal described Oche as the most TACTFUL person he had ever worked with.|passionate|discrete|hard-working|innovate": "A tactful person is careful not to offend, which is being discreet. The paper prints \"discrete\" (meaning separate), clearly a misprint for discreet: B. Memory tip: discrEEt = careful; discrETE = separate.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The old woman is suffering from DEMENTIA.|lucidity|senility|insanity|sagacity": "Dementia is the loss of memory and reasoning that comes with old age: senility, B. Insanity is mental illness in general, not age-related. Lucidity and sagacity are opposites. Memory tip: senile, senility, senior = old age.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Some drugs have DELETERIOUS effect on a child's development.|debilitating|helpful|harmful|healing": "Deleterious means harmful: C. Debilitating (weakening) is a trap, since it's narrower. Memory tip: deleterious = damaging to health or well-being.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Fila has always been described as BELLIGERENT.|beautiful|attractive|combative|innocent": "Belligerent means aggressive and ready to fight: combative, C. Memory tip: bellum is Latin for war, as in \"bellicose\".",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Laraba saw a FORLORN little figure sitting outside the class.|wise and intelligent|lonely and unhappy|smart and healthy|short and ugly": "Forlorn means sad, lonely and abandoned: B. Memory tip: forlorn = forsaken and lonely.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The circular SUPERSEDES all previous correspondence on the matter.|supports|displaces|eliminates|circumvent": "To supersede is to take the place of something older: displaces, B. \"Eliminates\" is close but supersede means replacing, not just removing. Memory tip: the new law supersedes (replaces) the old one. Note the spelling: -sede.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Her problem was EXACERBATED by the loss of her father.|exaggerated|solved|aggravated|infuriated": "To exacerbate a problem is to make it worse: aggravated, C. Exaggerated means overstated, a sound-alike trap. Memory tip: exacerbate = make more acute (worse).",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The warring communities were COERCED into negotiating a settlement.|driven|compelled|persuaded|pressured": "To coerce is to force someone. Driven, compelled and pressured all mean forced, so the opposite is persuaded (won over without force): C. Memory tip: coercion = force; persuasion = reasoning.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: His father served as a MERCENARY in the army.|preacher|regular|recruit|officer": "A mercenary is a hired soldier fighting for pay. The opposite is a regular, a professional soldier of the country's own army: B. Recruit and officer are ranks. Memory tip: mercenary = for money; regular = regular army.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Jummai is CRUEL to her husband.|harsh|brutal|passionate|ferocious": "Harsh, brutal and ferocious all mean much the same as cruel. Only \"passionate\" points the other way; it is probably a misprint for \"compassionate\": C. Memory tip: when three options are synonyms, the odd one out is the opposite.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The teacher who beat the student was treated with MERCY.|disrespect|contempt|vengeance|kindness": "Mercy is forgiveness instead of punishment. The opposite is vengeance, punishment in revenge: C. Kindness is a synonym, the trap. Memory tip: show mercy vs. take vengeance.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: His wife hated his GARRULOUS attitude.|outspoken|unfriendly|reticent|thoughtful": "Garrulous means talking too much. Reticent means saying little and keeping things to yourself: C. Outspoken is closer to a synonym. Memory tip: garrulous = gabby; reticent = reserved.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: 'Agoshito is a CALLOW youth,' said the teacher.|An ignorant|An experienced|An idle|An organized": "Callow means young and inexperienced. The opposite is experienced: B. Ignorant is a trap, closer in meaning. Memory tip: a callow youth is green, raw and untested.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: What you are asking me to do is a HERCULEAN task.|a strenuous|a demanding|a lovely|an easy": "A herculean task needs huge strength or effort, like Hercules' labours. The opposite is an easy task: D. Strenuous and demanding are synonyms. Memory tip: Hercules = giant effort.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Nkechi was a NOVICE when she was first employed.|manager|clerk|supervisor|professional": "A novice is a beginner. The opposite is a professional, someone skilled and experienced: D. Clerk, manager and supervisor are job titles, not levels of experience. Memory tip: novice = new.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: 'I do not trust him,' he said, in a rare moment of CANDOUR.|reproach|dishonesty|frankness|fairness": "Candour is honest frankness. The opposite is dishonesty: B. Frankness is a synonym, the trap. Memory tip: a candid answer is an honest one.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Mrs Akunilo looks ANAEMIC today.|strange|sick|weak|strong": "Anaemic means pale and weak from lack of blood. The opposite is strong: D. Sick and weak are synonyms. Memory tip: anaemia = too little blood = weakness.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: It is INCONCEIVABLE that the sun shone in the night.|credible|unthinkable|impossible|contestable": "Inconceivable means impossible to imagine or believe. The opposite is credible: A. Unthinkable and impossible are synonyms. Memory tip: in- = not; conceivable = imaginable.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: She only gives a SUPERFICIAL impression of warmth and friendliness.|a strong|a fake|a deep|an unrealistic": "Superficial means on the surface only. The opposite is deep: C. A fake impression is close to a superficial one, the trap. Memory tip: super- (above) + facies (face) = only skin-deep.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: As a PRUDENT businessman, Adayi does not leave anything to chance.|A frugal|Shrewd|careless|unsuccessful": "Prudent means careful and wise. The opposite is careless: C. Frugal and shrewd are near-synonyms. Memory tip: a prudent person leaves nothing to chance.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: His ANTIPATHY affected the growth of his business.|hatred|receptiveness|loyalty|hostility": "Antipathy is a strong dislike, close to hostility and hatred (A and D). The opposite is a welcoming openness, receptiveness: B. Loyalty is a trap. Memory tip: anti- (against) + pathy (feeling) = feeling against.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Okonkwo's LETHAL right foot did the magic in the football match.|Weak|wicked|fat|harmless": "Lethal means deadly. The opposite is harmless: D. Weak is a trap, since a weak foot is not quite the opposite of a deadly one. Memory tip: lethal = can kill.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): When his car tyre _____ on the way, he did not know what to do.|has burst|had burst|bursted|burst": "The sentence is in the simple past (\"did not know\"), and burst stays the same in every form: burst, burst, burst. So \"when his tyre burst\": D. \"Bursted\" does not exist, and \"has burst\" clashes with the past. Memory tip: burst, cost, cut, hit, put: one form for all.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Lami's father _____ as a gardener when he was young, but now he is a driver.|had been working|used to work|has worked|use to work": "\"Used to\" + verb describes a past habit or state that is no longer true. He was a gardener then; now he is a driver: B. D drops the -d: the correct form is \"used to\". Memory tip: used to = then, not now.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): _____ he switches on the light, the shadow disappears.|Whenever|Except|Since|Until": "Whenever means every time: each time he switches on the light, the shadow disappears: A. Until and except don't fit, and since would make it a reason, not a time. Memory tip: whenever + present simple = something that always happens.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): It is important that you clear the refuse in front of your house every _____.|fourtnight|fortnight|fourthnight|forthnight": "Fortnight (two weeks, from \"fourteen nights\") drops the u: f-o-r-t-n-i-g-h-t, B. Memory tip: fourteen has a u, fortnight does not, just like forty.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The policemen became suspicious as the hoodlums _____ in their office.|ferreted|ferreted|ferreted about|ferreted about": "To ferret about is to search busily, poking into everything; \"ferret\" alone needs an object (ferret out the truth). So \"ferreted about\": D. The paper prints C and D the same, so both are accepted. Memory tip: a ferret is an animal that hunts in burrows; to ferret about is to rummage.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Suara needn't come with us, _____?|does she|will she|can she|need she": "In \"needn't come\" (no \"to\"), need is a modal auxiliary like can't or mustn't, so the tag repeats it in the positive: need she? D. A is wrong: \"does she\" only matches \"Suara doesn't need to come\". Memory tip: the tag uses the same auxiliary as the statement.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Unoka _____ the whole house to find his missing wristwatch.|scourged|scoured|scored|scouted": "To scour a place is to search it thoroughly, which fits looking for a lost watch: B. D is wrong: to scout is to explore an area for information, as soldiers do. Scourged means whipped, and scored means got points. Memory tip: scour = search every corner (or scrub hard).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Ife asked me _____.|what time it was|what is it by my time|what time is it|what time it is": "In a reported question, the word order becomes that of a statement (it was), and the tense moves back after \"asked\": A. C keeps the question order, and D keeps the present tense. Memory tip: She asked what time it was, not what time is it.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): There are many ways to kill a rat, so we should be _____ in our approach to the task ahead of us.|ecletic|eclectic|ecleKtic|eclectiK": "Eclectic (drawing on many methods, choosing the best of each) is spelt e-c-l-e-c-t-i-c: B. A drops the second c, and C and D replace c with k. Memory tip: ec-lec-tic: c in both middle syllables.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Audu took these actions purely _____ his own career.|on furtherance of|in furtherance of|to furtherance in|in furtherance with": "The fixed phrase is \"in furtherance of\", meaning to help advance something: B. The prepositions \"on\", \"to\" and \"with\" are wrong here. Memory tip: in furtherance of = to further (advance).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Here is Mr Odumusu who teaches English _____ in our school.|pronuntiation|pronounciation|pronunciation|pronountiation": "Pronounce keeps its o-u, but the noun drops the o: pronunciation, C. B (\"pronounciation\") is the classic error. Memory tip: proNUNciation, like a nun.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Instead of _____, she lied.|pleading|her to plead|her pleading|plead": "A preposition such as \"instead of\" is followed by the -ing form: instead of pleading, she lied, A. C adds an unnecessary \"her\", and D is a bare verb. Memory tip: preposition + -ing (instead of going, before eating).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Of the three girls, Uka is the _____.|so much notorious|notorious|naught|naughtiest": "Comparing one girl with the others in a group of three needs the superlative: the naughtiest, D. B (\"the notorious\") lacks a superlative, and \"so much notorious\" is wrong. Memory tip: two = -er (naughtier), three or more = -est (naughtiest).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): I wonder how he will _____ being absent from school for a long time.|make in|make up|make off|make out": "To make up for something is to compensate for it, here for lost school time: B. Make off means run away, and make out means understand. Memory tip: make up for = compensate.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Please sit on the _____.|carier|career|carrier|carrear": "A carrier is the seat or rack at the back of a bicycle or motorcycle: C. A career is a profession. Memory tip: a carrier carries, so it keeps the double r of carry.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): I want to _____ his chance to acquaint you with the latest development.|size|seize|sieze|cease": "To seize a chance is to grab it: B. Size and cease are sound-alikes, and \"sieze\" is misspelt. Memory tip: seize breaks the \"i before e\" rule, like weird.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Getting a well-paid job nowadays is an _____ task.|utmost|upbeat|uphill|upfield": "An uphill task is very difficult, like climbing a hill: C. Upbeat means cheerful, and utmost means greatest. (The paper prints \"on\" for \"an\".) Memory tip: uphill = hard work.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The secretary has no right to _____ my affairs.|spy from|meddle in|toy at|complain into": "To meddle in someone's affairs is to interfere in them: B. Spy on (not from) and complain about (not into) are the right prepositions. Memory tip: meddle in, interfere in.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Bola studiously avoided _____ the question.|parrying|answering|projecting|destroying": "Avoiding answering a question means deliberately not answering it: B. To parry a question is already to dodge it, so \"avoided parrying\" would mean he answered it. Memory tip: avoid + -ing (avoided answering).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The school authority dismissed him for _____ but I won't tell you about it yet.|certain reason|a reason|more reason|a certain reason": "\"A certain reason\" names a definite reason the speaker is keeping back, matching \"I won't tell you about it yet\": D. The key's \"a reason\" is also grammatical, so it is accepted too. A needs an article, and C makes no sense. Memory tip: \"a certain\" = a particular one I'm not naming.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: bUbble|guy|bull|bumper|gurgle": "The u in bubble is the short /ʌ/, as in bumper: C. Bull has /ʊ/, gurgle /ɜː/, and guy /aɪ/. Memory tip: the u in cup, bus, bubble, bumper is /ʌ/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: wEIght|whale|while|wheat|writhe": "The ei in weight is /eɪ/, the a in whale: A. While and writhe have /aɪ/, and wheat /iː/. Memory tip: weight rhymes with wait.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: lEAch|gear|cedar|cheer|death": "The ea in leach is the long /iː/, the e in cedar: B. Gear and cheer have /ɪə/, and death the short /e/. Memory tip: a following r changes the vowel, so gear is not /iː/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: menTion|that|machine|church|test": "The t in -tion is /ʃ/, the ch in machine: B. Church has /tʃ/, and that /ð/. Memory tip: -tion is always \"shun\".",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: prestiGe|bag|badge|reggae|leisure": "In British English (RP) prestige ends in /ʒ/, pres-TEEZH, the sound of s in leisure and measure: D. Many speakers, especially Americans, say it with /dʒ/ as in badge, which is the key's B, so both are accepted. Bag has /g/ and reggae has /g/. Memory tip: French loanwords often keep /ʒ/ (prestige, garage, beige).",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: KNot|cot|keep|norm|king": "The k in kn- is silent, so kn in knot sounds /n/, like norm: C. Cot, keep and king all have /k/. Memory tip: silent k in knife, knee, know, knot.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: fuel|cruel|fool|rule|field": "Fuel /ˈfjuːəl/ and cruel /ˈkruːəl/ both end in /uːəl/: A. Fool and rule have one syllable, /uːl/. Memory tip: fuel and cruel each have two vowel sounds at the end.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: match|harsh|batch|such|watch": "Match and batch share /ætʃ/: B. Watch has /ɒtʃ/ in spite of its spelling. Memory tip: rhyme follows sound, not spelling.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: sheer|Sheila|care|ear|sherry": "Sheer and ear both end in /ɪə/: C. Sheila is /ˈʃiːlə/, care /eə/. Memory tip: the starting consonant doesn't matter for a rhyme.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): termination|terminaTION|TERmination|termiNAtion|terMInation": "Termination is stressed on the syllable before -tion: ter-mi-NA-tion, C. Memory tip: -tion words always stress the syllable just before -tion.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): meditative|meDItative|mediTAtive|MEditative|meditaTIVE": "In British English meditative is stressed on the first syllable, /ˈmedɪtətɪv/, like MEditate: C. B is wrong: the \"ta\" syllable is weak. Memory tip: -ative words often keep the stress of their verb (MEDitate, MEDitative).",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): suggestible|suggeSTIble|Suggestible|suGGEstible|suggestible": "Suggestible keeps the stress of sugGEST: su-GGES-ti-ble, C. Memory tip: -ible and -able usually don't move the stress (sugGEST, sugGESTible).",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: Uche LOVES Toyota cars.|Who loves Toyota cars?|What brand of car does Uche love?|Does Uche hate Toyota cars?|Does Uche love bicycles?": "Stressing LOVES corrects a wrong verb: he loves Toyota cars, he doesn't hate them: C. A would need UCHE stressed, B TOYOTA. Memory tip: the stressed word is the one that answers or corrects the question.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: The POLICE arrested the suspect.|Did the police placate the suspect?|Who arrested the suspect?|Who did the police arrest?|Did the police arrest the suspect?": "Stressing POLICE tells you who did the arresting: B. C would need SUSPECT stressed, and D ARRESTED. Memory tip: stressed subject = answer to \"who?\".",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: Maiduguri is the CAPITAL of Borno State.|Is Maiduguri the capital of Plateau State?|Which state is Maiduguri the capital of?|Is Maiduguri a town in Borno State?|What is the capital of Borno State?": "Stressing CAPITAL corrects \"a town\": Maiduguri is not just a town in Borno, it is the capital: C. A would need BORNO stressed. Memory tip: emphatic stress points to the word being corrected.",
 "eng-2012-p1|From the passage, one can say that all ethnic groups have|different traditional religions with some elements of similarities|completely different religious practices|the same traditional religion|the same religious manifestations with common deities": "The second paragraph says each ethnic group \"has its own religious traditions\", yet \"the various traditional religions have much in common\": a high god, lesser gods, spirits and ancestors. Different, but with shared elements: A. B ignores the shared features, while C and D ignore the differences. Memory tip: \"yet\" in a passage often joins the two halves of the right answer.",
 "eng-2012-p1|According to the first paragraph, Nigerians believe that the|supernatural and natural world co-exist|natural and supernatural worlds are antagonistic|supernatural world controls the natural world|supernatural world exploits the natural world": "The unseen beings \"watch and judge us\", and if offended \"they may have to be appeased with prayers and sacrifices\". That is power over people in the natural world: C. The key gives C, but A is accepted too, because the paragraph also describes the unseen world existing \"apart from\" the natural world we see, i.e. side by side. B and D (enmity, exploitation) are never mentioned. Memory tip: test each option against the exact sentence the question points to.",
 "eng-2012-p1|Traditional religion has waned in Nigeria owing to the|influence of Islam over Bori spirits|influence of Christianity over local oracles|decline of the interest in traditional religions|influence of non-traditional religions": "Two causes are given: in Hausa land traditional religion went underground \"owing to the influence of Islam\", and the oracles waned \"with the coming of Christianity\". Both are non-traditional religions: D. A and B each name only one part of the story, and C gives no cause at all. Memory tip: when the passage gives several examples, choose the option that covers them all.",
 "eng-2012-p1|Which factor is common to all traditional religions as mentioned in the passage?|Prayer only|Divination|Sacrifice only|Rituals": "The final paragraph calls divination \"an important element of traditional religion\", in general, not of one group: B. A and C add \"only\", which the passage never says, and rituals are mentioned in the first paragraph for religion in general. Memory tip: be wary of options with \"only\"; they are usually too narrow.",
 "eng-2012-p2|It can be inferred from the passage that|Nigerians have access to foreign books only|Nigerian undergraduates do not read textbooks|Nigerians read foreign and indigenous books alike|Nigerians read mostly foreign books": "The passage says the few Nigerians who read concentrate \"on foreign books than indigenous productions\", and bookshops stock foreign books because demand for them is higher: D. A and C go against this, and B is about handouts, not reading habits. Memory tip: an inference must follow from what is stated, not go beyond it.",
 "eng-2012-p2|The reason for lack of indigenous books in most renowned bookshops, according to the passage, is that|Nigerians prefer reading foreign books|foreign books attract more buyers|indigenous books are sometimes not available|the low quality of indigenous books": "The manager said bookshops prefer foreign books \"because of higher demands for them\", meaning foreign books sell better: B. A is close, but the passage gives buyer demand as the shops' reason. D (low quality) is mentioned later in connection with publishers. Memory tip: answer with the reason the passage itself gives, from the person it quotes.",
 "eng-2012-p2|The expression '... that earned her the award is profoundly gripping', as used in the passage, means that the book|is highly interesting and captures attention|is of high quality to the writer|attracts many indigenous and foreign readers|is widely acknowledged by many authors": "Something gripping holds your attention completely, and \"profoundly\" means deeply. So the book is highly interesting and captures attention: A. B, C and D talk about quality, sales or other authors, which \"gripping\" does not mean. Memory tip: gripping = it grips (holds) the reader.",
 "eng-2012-p2|The university science lecturer gives his reason for issuing handouts as|lack of teaching aids among students|low purchasing power|low quality of books|lack of sufficient time": "The lecturer dictated notes because the library \"stopped buying new books when the local currency was devalued\". A weaker naira means less money to buy books, i.e. low purchasing power: B. D (time) is not mentioned, and C (quality) is a complaint about publishers, not his reason. Memory tip: devaluation means your money buys less.",
 "eng-2012-p2|A suitable title for this passage is|Nigerian Literary Writers|Nigerian Publishers and International Awards|Poor Reading Culture in Nigeria|Why Nigerian Lecturers Sell Handouts": "The passage opens with \"the reading culture in Nigeria is now at a low ebb\", then looks at why: unrewarded authors, poor publishing and handouts instead of textbooks. The title that covers it all is Poor Reading Culture in Nigeria: C. A and B fit only the middle paragraph, and D fits only the last. Memory tip: a good title covers the whole passage, often taken from its first sentence.",
 "eng-2012-p3|The attitude of the writer of the passage can best be described as|objective|critical|non-committal|emotional": "The writer finds fault with the old saying \"experience is the best teacher\" and ends by saying it \"may not be justifiable after all\". That is a critical attitude: B. The writer clearly takes sides, so the tone is not objective or non-committal. Memory tip: a writer who argues against a common belief is being critical.",
 "eng-2012-p3|It can be deduced from the passage that|all experiences are best teachers|learning through pains is better|experience is superior to wisdom|wise thoughts are more desirable than experience": "The whole passage argues that learning by wisdom is \"easier and cheaper\" than learning by experience, so wise thoughts are more desirable: D. A and C say the opposite, and B praises learning \"through pains\", which the writer calls \"tough, costly and inconvenient\". Memory tip: a \"deduce\" question usually restates the writer's main argument.",
 "eng-2012-p3|Which of the following statements is true according to the passage?|Man must make mistake in order to survive|The totality of our actions should be decided by our judgement|If we do not make mistakes, experience must be our teacher|Our future would be judged by our past mistakes": "\"Our life now is the sum total of our decisions and our future will be determined by our decision of today.\" In other words, what we do should be decided by our judgement: B. C reverses the writer's point, and A and D are never said. Memory tip: look for the option that paraphrases one sentence in the passage.",
 "eng-2012-p3|According to the passage, we must be disciplined to|make experience our best teacher in reality|learn from experience in future|choose the learning opportunity that comes our way|decide against making experience our teacher": "\"We must therefore be disciplined to remain with our resolve to make a clean break with experience as our best teacher\": D. A says the exact opposite of the writer's argument. C is tempting because the passage says discipline \"will demand taking advantage of every learning opportunity\", but the question repeats the wording of the clean-break sentence. Memory tip: match the key phrase of the question (\"disciplined to\") to the sentence that uses it.",
 "eng-2012-p3|The phrase 'a complete and wholehearted devotion', as used in the passage, means|acting without doubts|learning with tension|learning without pains|teaching with ease": "Wholehearted devotion means full commitment, holding nothing back and having no second thoughts. That is acting without doubts: A. B (tension), C (no pains) and D (teaching) are not what \"devotion\" means. Memory tip: wholehearted = your whole heart is in it, with no doubts.",
 "eng-2012-p4|Gap 16: Most of these feature _____ twelve weeks of pregnancy.|In the penultimate|In the first|Around|For": "Most miscarriages happen early, so they feature \"in the first twelve weeks\": B. \"Penultimate\" means second to last, which makes no sense here, and \"for twelve weeks\" changes the meaning. Memory tip: \"in the first/last ... weeks\" is the usual way to mark a period of pregnancy.",
 "eng-2012-p4|Gap 17: The most common _____ is vaginal bleeding.|type|Cause|Period|Symptom": "Bleeding is a sign the woman notices, so it is a symptom: D. The rest of the passage lists more symptoms (cramping, breast tenderness). A \"cause\" would be what makes the miscarriage happen, which comes later in the passage. Memory tip: symptom = what you notice; cause = why it happens.",
 "eng-2012-p4|Gap 18: You may see blood clots, brown discharge or other tissues that are not _____ identifiable.|clearly|naturally|directly|medically": "Tissues \"not clearly identifiable\" are hard to recognise: A. \"Naturally\", \"directly\" and \"medically identifiable\" don't fit what someone would see. Memory tip: \"clearly identifiable / clearly visible\" is a common collocation.",
 "eng-2012-p4|Gap 19: …having to pass urine more _____ than usual stop unexpectedly.|painfully|frequently|gradually|Commonly": "Needing to urinate more often is a familiar early sign of pregnancy, and \"more often\" is \"more frequently\": B. \"More painfully\" or \"more gradually\" don't describe this symptom. Memory tip: frequently = often, which answers \"how many times?\"",
 "eng-2012-p4|Gap 20: …and the miscarriage is only _____ in a routine scan.|prevented|managed|discovered|Stopped": "When there are no signs at all, the miscarriage is only found, or discovered, during a routine scan: C. A scan detects things; it doesn't prevent, manage or stop them. Memory tip: ask what a scan actually does: it shows (discovers).",
 "eng-2012-p4|Gap 21: …the way the genetic material from the egg and sperm have combined during _____.|pregnancy|incubation|mating|Fertilization": "The genetic material of egg and sperm combines at fertilisation: D. Mating comes before this and is not where the genetic problem arises, and \"pregnancy\" is too general. Incubation is for eggs kept warm, as with birds. Memory tip: fertilisation = egg + sperm joining.",
 "eng-2012-p4|Gap 22: It can be difficult to find out why this has _____.|occurred|enlarged|continued|emerged": "To find out why a miscarriage has happened is to find out why it has occurred: A. \"Enlarged\", \"continued\" and \"emerged\" don't describe an event happening. The answer key in circulation gives no answer for this question. Memory tip: occur = happen.",
 "eng-2012-p4|Gap 23: Imbalances in pregnancy hormones, problems in the immune _____…|syndrome|process|response|system": "The body's defence against disease is \"the immune system\": D. \"Immune response\" exists, but the passage is listing problems in a body system, alongside hormones. Syndrome and process don't collocate here. Memory tip: learn fixed terms: immune system, nervous system, digestive system.",
 "eng-2012-p4|Gap 24: The risk of miscarriage _____ with age because the quality of eggs deteriorates.|Increases|starts|reduces|appears": "Egg quality \"deteriorates\" (gets worse) with age, so the risk goes up: A. \"Reduces\" says the opposite, while \"starts\" and \"appears\" don't fit, because the risk is always there. Memory tip: follow the logic of \"because\": worse eggs mean a higher risk.",
 "eng-2012-p4|Gap 25: It is also increased with _____ pregnancies such as twins.|complicated|advance|multiple|confirmed": "Twins are an example of a multiple pregnancy, i.e. more than one baby: C. \"Such as twins\" points straight to this. Memory tip: an example after \"such as\" tells you what the gap word must cover.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Hardworking students must not have a finger in every pie at school.|Hardworking students must have a role to play in most activities in the school|Only hardworking students must participate in all activities in the school|Hardworking students do not participate in all activities in the school|Hardworking students must ask others to participate in school activities": "To have a finger in every pie is to be involved in everything. So hardworking students should not take part in every school activity: C. A and B say the opposite, and D brings in other people. The paper prints \"very pie\"; the idiom is \"every pie\". Memory tip: a finger in every pie = involved in everything.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The vice chancellor is riding the crest of the last quarter of his administration.|The vice chancellor enjoys the acknowledgment of the success of his administration|The vice chancellor does not enjoy the people's criticism of his administration|The vice chancellor hopes to overcome soon, the poor comments on his administration|The vice chancellor does not talk of his successes in office": "To ride the crest of a wave is to enjoy great success, so the vice chancellor is enjoying recognition of his administration's success: A. B, C and D all suggest criticism or silence, which the idiom doesn't mean. Memory tip: the crest is the top of the wave, the high point.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: She was absolved by the court from the charge.|She was convicted for the charge|She was blamed and charged to court|Her case was resolved by the court|She was declared free from the charge": "To absolve someone is to set them free from blame or guilt, so she was declared free from the charge: D. A (convicted) is the opposite, and C (resolved) doesn't say which way the case went. Memory tip: absolve = set free; acquit is a close cousin.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The landlord is fond of throwing his weight about.|The landlord likes healthy exercise|The landlord is overweight|The landlord gives orders to people|The landlord is respected by his tenants": "To throw one's weight about is to use one's power to boss people around, so the landlord gives orders to people: C. A and B take \"weight\" literally. Memory tip: throwing your weight about = being bossy.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The company ought to have issued warrants for one billion shares.|The company has issued one billion shares|The management expected the company to issue more than one billion shares|Members of the company bought less than one billion shares|The company did not issue one billion shares": "\"Ought to have done\" talks about something that should have happened but didn't. So the company did not issue the shares: D. A says the opposite, and B and C add details not in the sentence. Memory tip: ought to have / should have + past participle = it did not happen.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: He needed not to have played in the position of quarterback in the volley ball.|He participated in the game in his unusual position|Nobody expected him to have participated in the game|He wanted to play in a position other than the one he was offered|Someone did not want him to play in the position that he played": "\"Needed not to have played\" (better: \"need not have played\") means he did play, though it wasn't necessary, here in a position that wasn't his normal one: A. B and D bring in other people's expectations. Memory tip: need not have done = it was done, but it was unnecessary.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: I wouldn't have responded to his rude talk, if I were you.|The advice was taken by the respondent, so he did not respond to the talk|The adviser put himself in the respondent's position, so he did not respond to the talk|The respondent replied to the speaker's talk, although he ought not have done so|What was advisable was that the respondent gave it back to the speaker": "\"I wouldn't have responded ... if I were you\" is advice given after the event: the person did reply, but should not have: C. A and B say he didn't respond, which misses the point. Memory tip: wouldn't have + past participle, used as advice, criticises something already done.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: He could not speak out because he had a feet of clay.|His feet was muddy|He was weak and cowardly|He was clumsy and lazy|He was shy and timid": "Feet of clay means a hidden weakness in someone who seems strong. Here it explains why he could not speak out, so he was weak and cowardly: B. A takes clay literally, and C and D change the meaning. Memory tip: feet of clay = a weakness behind a strong image.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The player wasted a golden opportunity during the penalty shoot-out.|The player first the bar|The player did not score the shot|The player scored the shot that made them win the gold cup|Instead of a silver cup, they received the golden one": "A golden opportunity is an excellent chance, and wasting it in a shoot-out means he missed his kick: B. C says the opposite, and D takes \"golden\" literally. A is printed in a garbled form in the paper. Memory tip: waste an opportunity = fail to use it.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: As far as Abu is concerned, Mero should be given fifty naira at the most.|All Abu is saying is that Mero probably deserves more than fifty naira and not less|All Abu is concerned with is that Mero should be given nothing more than fifty naira|In Abu's estimation, Mero merits not more than fifty naira|In Abu's opinion, Mero deserves fifty naira or probably more": "\"As far as Abu is concerned\" introduces Abu's opinion, and \"at the most\" sets fifty naira as the upper limit. So in Abu's estimation, Mero merits not more than fifty naira: C. A and D reverse \"at the most\", and B drops the idea of Abu's opinion. Memory tip: at the most = no more than; at least = no less than.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: As an IDIOT, the boy is weak in class.|a deviant|a dunce|an expert|a genius": "An idiot is a very foolish person; the opposite is a genius: D. \"A dunce\" means the same as idiot. \"An expert\" knows one field well, but the sentence is about general cleverness in class. Memory tip: idiot vs genius are the two ends of the intelligence scale.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: We were SHOCKED by the news that he had lost the money.|astonished|disconcerted|unconcerned|surprised": "To be shocked is to be greatly upset by news. Astonished, disconcerted and surprised are all near-synonyms, so the opposite is unconcerned: C. Memory tip: when three options mean the same thing, the odd one out is usually the opposite.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The principal was advised to be FLEXIBLE on critical issues.|livid|cautious|evasive|rigid": "Flexible means able to bend and willing to change; rigid means stiff and unwilling to change: D. \"Cautious\" and \"evasive\" describe other attitudes. Memory tip: flexible vs rigid, as with a rubber band vs an iron rod.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Bola always looks SOBER.|excited|serious|worried|helpless": "Sober here means serious and calm in manner; the opposite is excited: A. \"Serious\" is a synonym, and worried or helpless don't contrast with calmness. Memory tip: sober can mean \"not drunk\" or \"serious and calm\"; read the context.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Dupe was promoted for her EFFICIENCY.|ability|incompetence|inconsistency|rudeness": "Efficiency is doing a job well, without waste; incompetence is lacking the ability to do it: B. \"Ability\" is close to a synonym, and inconsistency or rudeness are other faults. Memory tip: efficient vs incompetent.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The management wants to consider her RETICENT behaviour in due course.|disapproving|disciplinarian|contemplative|loquacious": "Reticent means reserved, unwilling to say much; loquacious means very talkative: D. \"Contemplative\" (thoughtful) is close to reticent, not opposite. Memory tip: loquacious comes from Latin loqui, \"to speak\", as in eloquent.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Election process often become VOLATILE.|calm|strange|sudden|latent": "Volatile means unstable and likely to erupt suddenly; the opposite is calm: A. \"Sudden\" is linked to volatile, not opposite, and \"latent\" means hidden. Memory tip: volatile liquids evaporate quickly, volatile situations explode quickly.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Oche entered the principal's office in a rather ABRASIVE manner.|gentle|rude|lackadaisical|indifferent": "Abrasive means harsh, rough and rude in manner; the opposite is gentle: A. \"Rude\" is a synonym, and \"lackadaisical\" and \"indifferent\" mean careless or uninterested. Memory tip: an abrasive scrubs like sandpaper; gentle is the opposite of rough.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Otokpa is a member of the AD HOC committee on stock acquisition.|improvised|formal|temporary|fact-finding": "An ad hoc committee is set up informally for one particular purpose; a formal (standing) committee is the opposite: B. \"Temporary\" and \"improvised\" are near-synonyms of ad hoc. Memory tip: ad hoc = \"for this\" one purpose only.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: His gift to the poor was always INFINITESIMAL.|large|small|supportive|shameful": "Infinitesimal means extremely small; the opposite is large: A. \"Small\" is a synonym. Memory tip: infinitesimal = so small it is close to zero.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The economist concluded that several factors have been ADDUCED to explain the fall in the birth rate.|affirmed|diffused|mentioned|refuted": "To adduce reasons is to put them forward as evidence; to refute is to prove them wrong: D. \"Affirmed\" and \"mentioned\" are close to adduced, not opposite. Memory tip: adduce = add as proof; refute = knock down.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The presidential system is an ANTIDOTE to some political ailments.|an answer|a reply|an injury|an obstacle": "An antidote is a remedy that removes a poison or problem, and an obstacle blocks a solution: D. The key gives D, but C (an injury, which causes harm, as a poison does) is just as much an opposite, so both are accepted. A and B (an answer, a reply) are close to the meaning of antidote, not opposite. Memory tip: antidote = cure; its opposites are poison and harm.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Ola thought that her father was very CALLOUS.|parlous|compassionate|wicked|cheerful": "Callous means cruel and unfeeling; compassionate means feeling for others' suffering: B. \"Wicked\" is close to callous, and \"parlous\" means dangerous, a common trap word. Memory tip: callous skin is hard, and a callous heart is hard too.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He was very much respected, though he had no TEMPORAL power.|spiritual|mundane|permanent|ephemeral": "Temporal power is worldly, secular power; its opposite is spiritual power: A. \"Mundane\" means worldly (a synonym). \"Permanent\" and \"ephemeral\" are traps based on \"temporary\". Memory tip: temporal (of this world) vs spiritual (of the soul).",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The way the worship was organized was rather HIT-AND-MISS.|systematic|hasty|slow|funny": "Hit-and-miss means careless and unplanned, sometimes right and sometimes wrong; the opposite is systematic: A. \"Hasty\" and \"slow\" are about speed, not method. Memory tip: hit-and-miss = left to chance; systematic = done by a plan.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Some men will continue to cause offences until they are given a taste of their own MEDICINE.|placated|revenged on|recompensed for|cured": "To give someone a taste of their own medicine is to treat them as badly as they have treated others, i.e. to take revenge on them: B. \"Cured\" takes medicine literally, and \"placated\" and \"recompensed\" mean pleasing or rewarding them. Memory tip: own medicine = paid back in the same coin.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Okibe was rusticated for his DEROGATORY remark about the principal.|complimentary|unsavoury|unwarranted|lacklustre": "A derogatory remark is insulting and shows no respect, i.e. unsavoury (unpleasant, offensive): B. \"Complimentary\" is the opposite, and \"unwarranted\" (unjustified) misses the insulting sense. Memory tip: derogatory = putting someone down.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Justice is difficult to enforce because people are unwilling to accept any loss of SOVEREIGNTY.|autonomy|position|leadership|kingdom": "Sovereignty is supreme power and independence: being in charge of oneself, i.e. autonomy: A. \"Kingdom\" is a place ruled, and \"position\" or \"leadership\" are too narrow. Memory tip: a sovereign nation is autonomous; it governs itself.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: There are still VIRTUOUS women in our society today.|clever|upright|devilish|intelligent": "Virtuous means having high moral standards, i.e. upright: B. \"Clever\" and \"intelligent\" are about the mind, not morals, and \"devilish\" is the opposite. Memory tip: virtue = goodness, not cleverness.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The type of response is TYPICAL of a lazy teacher.|symptomatic|characteristic|universal|incontestable": "\"Typical of\" means having the usual qualities of, i.e. characteristic of: B. \"Symptomatic\" means being a sign of a problem, and \"universal\" means true everywhere. Memory tip: typical = characteristic, a mark of the type.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Akin is an INVETERATE gambler.|a selfish and self-centred|an extremely unlucky but popular|an incurable but fearful|a long time and incorrigible": "An inveterate gambler has had the habit for a long time and cannot change it: D. A and B describe character or luck, and C adds \"fearful\", which has nothing to do with inveterate. Memory tip: inveterate = long-established and hard to change (from Latin vetus, \"old\", as in veteran).",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He was too PETRIFIED to give the closing remarks at the conference.|frightened|delighted|agitated|happy": "Petrified means so frightened one cannot move: A. \"Agitated\" is a related state but not the same, and \"delighted\" and \"happy\" are opposites. Memory tip: petrified = turned to stone (Greek petra, rock) with fear.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: During a particular time of the day, the road SHIMMERS in the heat.|darkens|lightens|shines|beams": "To shimmer is to shine with a soft, wavering light, as a road does in the heat: C. \"Beams\" suggests a strong ray, and \"darkens\" is the opposite. Memory tip: shimmer = shine with a tremble.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Every human being is VULNERABLE to communicable diseases.|liable|lifted|immuned|closed": "Vulnerable to means open to harm, i.e. liable to (likely to get): A. \"Immuned\" (properly \"immune\") is the opposite, and \"lifted\" and \"closed\" don't fit. Memory tip: vulnerable to = liable to = exposed to.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Mariam looks rather FURTIVE to Shehu.|intoxicated|unfriendly|sad|sly": "Furtive means secretive, trying not to be noticed, i.e. sly: D. \"Unfriendly\" and \"sad\" describe mood, not secretiveness. Memory tip: a furtive glance is a sly, sneaky glance.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The student's union leader delivered his speech EXTEMPORE.|out-of-hand|off the cuff|accurately|courageously": "Extempore means spoken without preparation, i.e. off the cuff: B. \"Out of hand\" means out of control, a common trap. \"Accurately\" and \"courageously\" describe how well or bravely, not preparation. Memory tip: off the cuff = notes on your shirt cuff, made up on the spot.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: His story gave us an INKLING of what he passed through during the strike.|a possible idea|a taste|a summary|the right view": "An inkling is a slight idea or hint: A. \"A summary\" gives the whole thing, and \"the right view\" is too certain. \"A taste\" can mean a brief experience, but inkling is about knowledge. Memory tip: an inkling is a little idea.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: These policies have been ESPOUSED by the ruling party.|condemned|rejected|supported|outlined": "To espouse a policy is to adopt and support it: C. \"Condemned\" and \"rejected\" are opposites, and \"outlined\" only means described. Memory tip: espouse comes from spouse; you take the idea as your own.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: We must not FORECLOSE reconciliation as the purpose of his trip.|exclude|consider|underestimate|forgo": "To foreclose a possibility is to rule it out in advance, i.e. exclude it: A. \"Consider\" is the opposite, and \"forgo\" means to go without something you could have had. Memory tip: foreclose = close off before it starts.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Her finding EXPLODED widely held beliefs about learning.|challenged|debunked|projected|confirmed": "To explode a belief is to show it is false, i.e. to debunk it: B. \"Challenged\" is weaker (it only questions), and \"confirmed\" is the opposite. Memory tip: an exploded myth has been blown apart.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): He was both a writer and a politician, but he was better _____ a singer.|as if|like|as|to be": "\"As\" introduces a role or capacity: he was better in the role of a singer, so \"better as a singer\": C. \"Like a singer\" would compare him to a singer without saying he is one, and \"as if\" needs a clause. Memory tip: as = in the role of; like = similar to.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Vacancies in the company will be notified by _____.|bulletin|publication|publicity|advertisement": "Jobs are made known to the public by advertisement: D. \"Publicity\" is general promotion, \"publication\" is the act of publishing, and \"bulletin\" would need an article (\"a bulletin\"). Memory tip: vacancies are advertised.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The driver was short of petrol, so he _____ down the hills with the engine switched off.|glided|coasted|wheeled|taxied": "A car coasts when it rolls downhill without engine power: B. A plane taxis on the runway, a glider glides through the air, and \"wheeled\" means pushed on wheels. Memory tip: coast = move along without power.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): He started his career as an _____ teacher.|auxillary|auxilliary|auxilary|auxiliary": "The correct spelling is auxiliary: one x, one l, and -iary: D. A, B and C each drop or double letters. Memory tip: aux-IL-IARY has an i before and after the single l.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): His many years of success in legal practice, _____ didn't come without challenges.|indeed|but|in spite of it all|however": "The second part (\"didn't come without challenges\") contrasts with \"many years of success\", and \"however\" marks that contrast: D. \"But\" cannot follow the subject this way, and \"indeed\" would add emphasis, not contrast. Memory tip: however = contrast, and it can sit after the subject inside commas.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): One should be careful how _____ behaves in the public, shouldn't _____?|one/one|he/he|she/one|one/he": "In formal British English, a sentence that begins with \"one\" keeps \"one\" throughout, so: how one behaves ..., shouldn't one?: A. Mixing \"one\" with he or she breaks this rule. Memory tip: once you start with \"one\", stay with \"one\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): _____, a good leader must have two characteristics.|First and formost|First and formust|First and farmost|First and foremost": "The expression is \"first and foremost\", spelt f-o-r-e-most: D. The others misspell foremost. Memory tip: foremost = \"fore\" (front) + most, as in forehead and forefront.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): We visited his house _____ three times.|like|for like|about|for about": "\"About three times\" means approximately three times: C. \"Like\" is informal and not standard in exams, and \"for\" doesn't go before a number of times (we say \"for three days\" but \"three times\"). Memory tip: about = roughly, with numbers.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): She was _____ the verge of tears.|at|on|by|with": "The fixed expression is \"on the verge of\" (very close to): B. Memory tip: on the verge of tears / on the brink of collapse; both take \"on\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Everyone makes mistakes occasionally; nobody is _____.|incorrigible|imperfect|infallible|indestructible": "Since everyone makes mistakes, nobody is infallible (incapable of error): C. \"Imperfect\" would give the opposite meaning, \"incorrigible\" means unable to be reformed, and \"indestructible\" means unable to be destroyed. Memory tip: infallible = can't fail.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The woman would not part with her _____ pot.|discarded earthen black|discarded black earthen|earthen discarded black|black earthen discarded": "Adjective order is opinion or description, then colour, then material, then the noun: discarded (description) + black (colour) + earthen (material) + pot: B. Material always sits closest to the noun. Memory tip: OSASCOMP: Opinion, Size, Age, Shape, Colour, Origin, Material, Purpose.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): We stood up when the principal came in, _____?|isn't it|didn't we|not so|did us": "The main clause is positive and past simple (\"We stood up\"), so the tag is negative past with the same pronoun: didn't we?: B. \"Isn't it\" and \"not so\" are non-standard tags, and \"did us\" uses the wrong pronoun case. Memory tip: positive statement, negative tag; keep the same tense and subject.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The professor of _____ medicine has _____ the mystery of flu.|vetinary / unraveled|vertrinary / unravelled|veterinary / unraveled|veterinary / unravelled": "The correct spellings are veterinary and, in British English, unravelled (with ll): D. C has \"unraveled\", the American spelling, while A and B misspell veterinary. Memory tip: vet-ER-inary has \"er\" after vet; British English doubles the l before -ed (travelled, unravelled).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Her mother brought her some _____.|clothes|yards|cloth|clothing": "Clothes are garments to wear, which is what a mother would bring her daughter: A. Cloth means fabric, and \"yards\" needs a noun after it (yards of cloth). Memory tip: clothes (to wear) vs cloth (material).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Many workers were _____ as a result of the textile closure.|laid down|laid off|laid out|laid up": "To be laid off is to lose one's job because there is no work, as when a factory closes: B. \"Laid up\" means ill in bed, \"laid out\" means arranged, and \"laid down\" means set down (as in laying down rules). Memory tip: laid off = sent home from work.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The driver died in the _____ road accident.|fatal|brutal|serious|pathetic": "A fatal accident is one that causes death, which fits \"died in the\": A. \"Serious\" doesn't necessarily mean death, and \"brutal\" and \"pathetic\" don't collocate with accident. Memory tip: fatal = causing death.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): _____ your parents frown _____ our friendship, we shouldn't see each other anymore.|Because / over|Since / at|Although / at|As / upon": "\"Since\" gives a reason, and \"frown at\" means to show disapproval: B. The key gives B, but D (As ... upon) is equally correct, since \"as\" also gives a reason and \"frown upon\" is the standard idiom for disapproving, so both are accepted. C (Although) makes no sense: a contrast doesn't fit here. Memory tip: frown on/upon something = disapprove of it.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): For more productivity, the company is focusing attention on the possible _____ of available resources.|synergy|tapping|alignment|arrangement": "To tap resources is to draw on and use them, which is what improves productivity: B. \"Synergy\" means combined effect and doesn't fit \"the possible ... of resources\". \"Alignment\" and \"arrangement\" are weaker. Memory tip: tap resources, like tapping a tree for sap or rubber.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): _____ she didn't trust him, she married him.|After|Much as|Since|Though": "\"Though\" introduces a contrast: she married him in spite of not trusting him: D. The key gives D, and B is accepted too, because \"much as\" also means \"although\" (Much as I like him, I can't agree). \"After\" and \"Since\" don't express contrast. Memory tip: though, although, even though and much as all introduce a contrast.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): I wanted to know his political beliefs, so I asked him what _____.|this was|these are|this is|these were": "After past \"asked\", reported speech moves the tense back: \"What are these?\" becomes \"what these were\". Plural \"beliefs\" needs \"these\": D. B keeps the present \"are\", and A and C use singular \"this\". Memory tip: in reported speech after a past verb, present becomes past.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: bOOk|cool|cook|fool|tool": "The oo in book is the short /ʊ/, as in cook: B. Cool, fool and tool have the long /uː/. Memory tip: book, cook, look, good, foot have the short sound; food, cool, fool, tool have the long sound.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: villAge|page|pig|made|came": "In village, the -age ending is unstressed and weak: /ˈvɪlɪdʒ/, the same /ɪ/ as in pig: B. A is wrong: page, made and came all have /eɪ/, which is not the sound of -age in village. Memory tip: unstressed -age (village, cottage, message) sounds /ɪdʒ/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: pAtch|starch|fare|mad|brave": "The a in patch is the short /æ/, as in mad: C. Starch has the long /ɑː/, fare has /eə/, and brave has /eɪ/. Memory tip: a short a between consonants (cat, mad, patch) is usually /æ/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: tanGErine|gear|danger|girl|ignore": "The g in tangerine is soft /dʒ/, as in danger: B. Gear, girl and ignore have a hard /g/. Memory tip: g before e or i is often soft (gentle, giant), but there are exceptions (gear, girl).",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: Hair|heir|hour|honest|house": "The h in hair is pronounced /h/, as in house: D. A is wrong: the h in heir is silent (heir sounds like \"air\"), as it is in hour and honest. Memory tip: learn the silent-h words: heir, hour, honest, honour.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: ediTIon|bash|catch|bastion|rating": "The ti in edition is pronounced /ʃ/ (/ɪˈdɪʃn/), like the sh in bash: A. In bastion the ti is /tʃ/ or /ti/, catch has /tʃ/, and rating has /t/. Memory tip: -tion is almost always /ʃn/.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): demarcation|demarCAtion|DEmarcation|deMARcation|demarcaTION": "Words ending in -tion are stressed on the syllable just before -tion: de-mar-CA-tion: A. Memory tip: -tion pulls the stress to the syllable before it (eduCAtion, demarCAtion).",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): impossible|imPOSsible|IMpossible|imposSIble|impossiBLE": "Impossible is stressed on the second syllable, im-POS-si-ble, like possible itself: A. The negative prefix im- is not stressed. The paper misprints the word as \"imposible\". Memory tip: un-, in-, im- prefixes usually leave the stress where it was.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): imperialism|IMperialism|imPErialism|impeRIalism|imperialiSM": "Imperialism keeps the stress of imperial: im-PE-ri-al-ism: B. C puts the stress on the \"ri\", and A and D stress the prefix and the suffix. Memory tip: -ism doesn't move the stress (imPErial, imPErialism).",
 "|Choose the option that has the stress on the FIRST syllable.|madam|eighteen|invent|command": "MAdam is stressed on the first syllable: A. eighTEEN, inVENT and comMAND are stressed on the second. Memory tip: many two-syllable verbs (invent, command) are stressed on the second syllable.",
 "|Choose the option that has the stress on the FIRST syllable.|nineteen|mother|estate|announce": "MOther is stressed on the first syllable: B. esTATE and anNOUNCE are stressed on the second, and nineTEEN is too when said on its own. Memory tip: -teen numbers are stressed on -teen when said alone.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: The traditional chief NARRATED the story to the children.|The children heard the story from the traditional chief|Who narrated the story to the children?|The children could not listen to the story by the traditional chief|Did the chief hide the story from the children?": "Emphatic stress on NARRATED corrects or contrasts the action. It answers \"Did the chief hide the story from the children?\": no, he NARRATED it: D. B would put the stress on CHIEF, and A and C are statements, not the question being answered. Memory tip: the stressed word is the one that replaces the wrong word in the question.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: The ACCOUNTANT paid the workers' July salary in September.|When were the workers paid?|Did the cashier pay the workers' salary in September?|Workers received their July salary in September?|The September salary was paid in July?": "Stress on ACCOUNTANT corrects who paid: \"Did the cashier pay ...?\" No, the ACCOUNTANT did: B. A would need stress on SEPTEMBER. Memory tip: stress on a person = a correction about who did it.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: The cat DEVOURED the rat.|Did the rat devoured the cat?|What devoured the rat?|Did the cat pat the rat?|Is this the rat the cat devoured?": "Stress on DEVOURED corrects the action: \"Did the cat pat the rat?\" No, it DEVOURED it: C. B would need stress on CAT, and A would need stress on RAT. Memory tip: stress the verb when the question gets the action wrong.",
 "eng-2013-p1|Which of the following is true according to the passage?|Anyone, with almost no training, can run pots on a wheel.|Pots can be made quickly and correctly.|A pot thrown on a wheel is less likely to break.|The potter does not have to work hard if he uses the wheel.": "The last sentence says that in this way \"a small and medium-sized pot can be made more quickly and accurately\", so pots can be made quickly and correctly: B. A and D are never said (the potter needs skill and works hard), and C, about breaking, isn't mentioned at all. Memory tip: \"true according to the passage\" means you can point to the sentence that says it.",
 "eng-2013-p1|From the passage, how does a potter make several pots of almost identical size?|By having the knowledge of different pots.|By weighing the lumps of clay.|By having the right tools.|By knowing what to do from experience.": "The lumps of clay \"are weighed out so that each pot will be roughly the same size\", e.g. one and a half kilogrammes for a pint-sized jug: B. Tools (C) and experience (D) help with shaping, but the passage links equal size directly to weighing. Memory tip: look for the \"so that\" clause, which tells you the purpose of an action.",
 "eng-2013-p1|The phrase 'trims off any waste clay', as used in the passage, means to|cut away unnecessary parts|force the clay to the centre|divide the clay into two|wash away different colours": "To trim something is to cut bits off to make it neat, and \"waste clay\" is the clay the pot does not need: A, cut away unnecessary parts. B describes an earlier step (forcing the lump to the centre), and C and D are not in the passage. Memory tip: trim = cut off the extra, as when you trim hair or a hedge.",
 "eng-2013-p1|The word 'congenial', as used in the passage, means|congested|precise|similar|nice": "Abuja is described as a town where learners \"can find a congenial temporary home\", i.e. a pleasant, friendly place to live: D, nice. A (congested) is a sound-alike trap, and C (similar) comes from the sense \"like-minded\", used of people, not of a home. Memory tip: a congenial place or person is agreeable and easy to be with.",
 "eng-2013-p2|Which of the following is true according to the passage?|Music can enhance evaluation performance|All listeners are music makers|All artistes are objective in their feelings|Music influences feelings at different levels": "The key gives A: the passage says \"alert and repeated listening will enhance our ability to compare performances and judge music\", and A is a loose summary of that. D is equally supported, since our response is \"rooted in deep feelings\" and music can be anything from a barely noticed background to a totally absorbing experience, so both are accepted. B and C are false: critics differ and responses are subjective. Memory tip: cross out options that clash with the passage first (\"objective\" vs \"subjective\").",
 "eng-2013-p2|The expression '...stage and hall', as used in the passage, means the|artiste and his music|artiste and the audience|producer and the director|director and the audience": "In a live show, \"feelings are exchanged between stage and hall\". The stage is where the artiste performs and the hall is where the audience sits, so the phrase means the artiste and the audience: B. Producers and directors (C, D) are never mentioned. Memory tip: a place can stand for the people in it, as \"the whole hall cheered\" means the audience cheered.",
 "eng-2013-p2|From the passage, it can be deduced that music is|appreciated as the environment dictates|better appreciated in a crowd|better appreciated when we are happy|better appreciated by professional critics": "The passage says people listen in different ways: music can be \"a barely perceived background as in a film\" or \"a totally absorbing experience as in a concert\". So how we appreciate music depends on the setting: A. B, C and D each add a claim (crowds, happiness, critics) the passage doesn't make; in fact critics disagree. Memory tip: a deduction must follow from the passage, not from general opinion.",
 "eng-2013-p2|According to the writer, live performances provide a special excitement because they are|stage-managed|interactive|error-free and original|educative": "Live performance is exciting because the listeners are \"actively involved\" and \"feelings are exchanged between stage and hall\": the artiste and audience respond to each other, so it is interactive: B. C is wrong because the artiste works to avoid problems, not because shows are error-free. Memory tip: interactive = both sides act on each other.",
 "eng-2013-p2|According to the passage, music plays a vital role in human society because|music provides enjoyment and relief|it is easy to appreciate music|stage performance is the most popular music opportunity|everybody can listen to music through the CD, MP3 and DVD": "The opening sentences say music plays a vital role because \"good music provides entertainment and emotional release\": enjoyment and relief, A. D mentions CDs and MP3s, but those explain how music reaches more places, not why it matters. Memory tip: answer \"why\" questions from the sentence that gives the reason, not from nearby details.",
 "eng-2013-p3|Gap 11: It should therefore be classified with certain rare natural _____, such as volcanic…|programmes|occurrences|resources|laws": "A volcanic eruption is a natural event, and the bomb is to be grouped \"with certain rare natural occurrences\": B. Resources (C) are things we use, and laws (D) are rules, not events. Memory tip: \"natural occurrences\" or \"natural phenomena\" is how scientists describe events like eruptions and earthquakes.",
 "eng-2013-p3|Gap 12: …such as volcanic _____.|insurrection|exhaustion|eruption|expulsion": "Volcanoes erupt, so the fixed pair is \"volcanic eruption\": C. Insurrection is a rebellion, exhaustion is tiredness, and expulsion is throwing someone out. Memory tip: learn collocations in pairs: volcanic eruption, torrential rain, heavy traffic.",
 "eng-2013-p3|Gap 13: As with all events on this _____, it is impossible to describe what happens in details.|scanner|skate|snow|scale": "\"Events on this scale\" means events of this size; a hydrogen bomb is so huge that it is impossible to describe everything in detail: D. Scanner, skate and snow are look-alike distractors that make no sense. Memory tip: \"on this scale / on a large scale\" refers to size or extent.",
 "eng-2013-p3|Gap 14: …and the most impressive of these arises from _____.|pressure waves|pressure volume|pressure air|pressure gauge": "The rest of the passage describes the waves the explosion produces, shock waves or blast wave, and gravity waves, so the most impressive effect arises from pressure waves: A. B is wrong: \"pressure volume\" is not a meaningful phrase here, and nothing \"arises from pressure volume\", so A is marked correct. Memory tip: in a cloze, check your choice against the words that come later in the passage.",
 "eng-2013-p3|Gap 15: The immediate result of the _____ is that the air surrounding the bomb is raised…|reduction|commotion|detonation|distortion": "The immediate result of the detonation (the bomb going off) is that the air gets extremely hot: C. A commotion (B) is a noisy disturbance among people, and distortion (D) is a change of shape. Memory tip: bombs are detonated, so detonation means explosion.",
 "eng-2013-p3|Gap 16: …the air surrounding the bomb is raised very rapidly to an enormously high _____.|way|temperature|class|profile": "The next sentence talks about \"the hot gases\", so the surrounding air is raised to an enormously high temperature: B. You cannot raise air to a high \"way\", \"class\" or \"profile\". Memory tip: \"raised to a high ...\" is followed by something that can be measured, such as temperature or pressure.",
 "eng-2013-p3|Gap 17: The hot gases expand violently as great _____, compressing the air around them…|firearms|fireballs|fireworks|firesmokes": "The hot gases of a nuclear blast form huge glowing balls of fire, called fireballs: B. Firearms are guns, fireworks are for celebrations, and \"firesmokes\" is not a word. Memory tip: fireball = a ball of flame from an explosion.",
 "eng-2013-p3|Gap 18: …compressing the air around them into what is called _____, or blast wave…|shock jocks|shock therapy|shock waves|shock troops": "Air compressed by an explosion travels outward as \"shock waves, or blast wave\": C. Shock jocks are radio presenters, shock therapy is a medical treatment, and shock troops are soldiers. Memory tip: the phrase \"or blast wave\" just after the gap is a synonym clue for shock waves.",
 "eng-2013-p3|Gap 19: The force of the explosion lifts the _____ around the bomb.|form|atmosphere|space|height": "This kind of wave arises \"because of the weight of the air\", and the explosion lifts the air around the bomb, the atmosphere: B. C is wrong: space is emptiness with no weight to lift, while the paragraph is all about the air, so B is marked correct. Memory tip: atmosphere = the layer of air around the earth.",
 "eng-2013-p3|Gap 20: The gravity waves can also resemble ordinary _____ waves.|stream|lake|ocean|river": "Gravity waves in the air are compared to the familiar rolling waves of the sea: ocean waves, C. Streams, lakes and rivers have ripples, not the big waves the comparison needs. Memory tip: \"ordinary ... waves\" points to the most familiar kind, ocean waves.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The team's poor performance at the tournament plumb the depths of horror.|The team's performance took them to the next round.|The team's performance was enjoyed by all.|The team's performance was full of disappointment.|The team performed well.": "To plumb the depths is to sink to the lowest point, so a performance that \"plumb the depths of horror\" was dreadful, full of disappointment: C. A, B and D all describe a good performance, the opposite of \"poor\". Memory tip: plumb the depths (of despair, of horror) = reach the very worst.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Tolu and Chinedu live in each other's pockets.|They are long-term business partners.|They steal from each other.|They blackmail each other.|They are very close to each other.": "People who \"live in each other's pockets\" are together all the time; they are very close: D. Don't take it literally: it says nothing about stealing (B), blackmail (C) or business (A). Memory tip: idioms about pockets and closeness (live in each other's pockets, hand in glove) describe very close relationships.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: As the drama unfolded, Olatinuke was advised to keep her shirt on.|She was advised to wear her shirt.|She was advised to commit herself.|She was advised to stay calm.|She was advised to join the club.": "\"Keep your shirt on\" is an informal way of saying \"calm down, don't lose your temper\": C. A is the literal trap; idioms rarely mean exactly what the words say. Memory tip: keep your shirt on = keep your cool = stay calm.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: He is a clinging child.|He is a handsome young man.|He is possessive.|He likes to cling with his sister.|He is a bully.": "A clinging child holds on to a parent or friend and won't let them out of sight, emotionally dependent and possessive: B. C takes \"cling\" literally (and \"cling with\" is not idiomatic; we cling to), and A and D describe other things. Memory tip: clinging/clingy = too dependent, wanting someone all to yourself.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Zinana's examination result was not unfavourable.|She failed her examination.|Her examination did not meet her expectation.|She was successful in her examination.|Her result could not earn her admission.": "\"Not unfavourable\" is a double negative: two negatives cancel out, so the result was favourable and she did well: C. A, B and D all treat it as a bad result. Memory tip: not + un- = positive, often said modestly (\"not bad\" means good).",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: You need to brush up on your Spanish.|You need to study the history of Spain.|You need to improve your skills.|You need a brush from Spain.|You need to learn to play with a Spaniard.": "To \"brush up on\" a subject is to improve or refresh your knowledge of it, here your Spanish: B. A (history of Spain) and C (a brush) are traps built on the words Spanish and brush. Memory tip: brush up = polish an old skill again.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Amaka would pass for a beauty queen.|She would pass the drink to the queen who is sitting next to her.|She would be accepted by all as a beauty queen.|She walked past the beauty queen.|She was acting as a beauty queen.": "To \"pass for\" something is to be accepted or taken as it: Amaka is so beautiful she would be taken for a beauty queen: B. A and C play on the other meanings of \"pass\" (hand over, go past). Memory tip: pass for = be mistaken for, be accepted as.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: 'I can't wait to become a mother,' the new bride declared.|She sees motherhood as a burden.|She is excited about motherhood.|She is not keen on becoming a mother.|She will be patient as a mother.": "\"I can't wait\" means I am very eager: the bride is excited about becoming a mother: B. It does not mean she is impatient (D), and A and C say the opposite. Memory tip: \"I can't wait to ...\" = I'm really looking forward to it.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Usman needs to get his acts together if he wants to pass the examination.|He needs to put all points down in the examination.|He needs to organize himself.|He needs to be fast when writing the examination.|He needs to put on his stage costume.": "To \"get your act together\" (printed \"acts\") means to organize yourself and start working effectively: B. D is the literal stage trap (\"act\" as in a play), and A and C are only small parts of being organized. Memory tip: get your act together = sort yourself out.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Ramatu expressed her feelings in no uncertain terms.|She expressed it clearly and strongly.|She expressed it secretly and courageously.|She expressed it quietly and cautiously.|She expressed it feebly and sickly.": "\"In no uncertain terms\" means very clearly and forcefully, with no room for doubt: A. B, C and D add ideas (secret, quiet, feeble) that clash with \"no uncertain\". (The paper prints \"Ramatu's expressed\", a misprint for \"Ramatu expressed\".) Memory tip: no uncertain = certain, i.e. plain and direct.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Chibuzor gave a CURT nod and walked away.|gentle|rude|polite|shocking": "A curt nod is short and abrupt, almost rude, so the opposite is polite: C. B (rude) is close to a synonym, and gentle (A) is softer but not the direct opposite of rudeness. Memory tip: curt = rudely brief; opposite = polite, courteous.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The girl took a CURSORY glance at the letter and hid it.|sententious|concise|brief|lasting": "A cursory glance is quick and careless. None of the options says \"thorough\", but lasting (long) is the opposite of quick: D. Brief (C) and concise (B) are near-synonyms, and sententious means preachy. Memory tip: cursory = hasty, superficial; think \"cursor\" racing across a screen.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The relationship between the couple has been FROSTY.|fraudulent|cordial|amenable|frugal": "A frosty relationship is cold and unfriendly, so the opposite is warm and friendly, cordial: B. Frugal (D) means thrifty, a sound-alike trap. Memory tip: frosty/icy/cool = unfriendly; cordial/warm = friendly.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The Nobel laureate's activity in the field of science is HEINOUS.|indelible|laudable|deplorable|forgettable": "Heinous means utterly wicked; the opposite is laudable, deserving praise: B. Deplorable (C) is a synonym, a common trap in opposite questions. Memory tip: in \"opposite\" questions, first cross out the synonym, it is often there to catch you.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The accused was EVENTUALLY convicted.|initially|consequently|subsequently|finally": "Eventually means in the end, after a delay; the opposite is initially, at the beginning: A. Finally (D) and subsequently (C) are synonyms. Memory tip: initially vs eventually = at first vs at last.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The PLEBS can be found in every society of the world.|masses|middle class|elite|politicians": "The plebs are the ordinary common people; the opposite is the elite, the privileged few at the top: C. Masses (A) is a synonym. Memory tip: plebeian (common) vs patrician/elite (upper class).",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Everyone's condition was APPALLING.|simple|cloudy|pleasant|complex": "Appalling means shockingly bad, so the opposite is pleasant: C. Simple/complex (A, D) and cloudy (B) describe other qualities, not good versus bad. Memory tip: to be appalled is to be shocked, so appalling = shockingly bad.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The man's MORDANT wit is apparent to the entire village.|kind|scathing|caustic|withering": "Mordant wit is sharp and biting. Scathing, caustic and withering all mean the same, so the only opposite is kind: A. Memory tip: when three options are synonyms of each other, the odd one out is usually the answer in an opposite question.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The war against malaria keeps WAXING.|happening|decreasing|increasing|wavering": "To wax is to grow or increase (as the moon waxes); its opposite is to wane, i.e. decrease: B. Increasing (C) is the synonym trap, and wavering (D) is a sound-alike. Memory tip: wax and wane = grow and shrink.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The soldiers tried in their DOGGED defence of the city.|indifferent|strong|miserable|classical": "A dogged defence is stubborn and determined; the opposite is a defence with no real interest or effort, indifferent: A. Strong (B) is close to a synonym. Memory tip: dogged = holding on like a dog to a bone.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Ayodeji is an ARDENT supporter of education for the girl child.|an optimistic|a cogent|a passionate|an ignorant": "An ardent supporter is a passionate, enthusiastic one: C. Optimistic (A) is hopeful, not the same thing, and cogent (B) means convincing, used of arguments. Memory tip: ardent comes from Latin for \"burning\", a burning (passionate) support.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The scholars' EPITAPH was demolished.|monument|embodiment|farmland|book": "An epitaph is the inscription on a tomb or memorial. Since this one \"was demolished\", the nearest option is monument, the structure it stands on: A. Embodiment, farmland and book have no link to graves. Memory tip: epitaph = words on a tombstone (epi = upon, taphos = tomb).",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Mohammed does his work with so much ARDOUR.|enthusiasm|discouragement|knowledge|indifference": "Ardour is great enthusiasm or passion: A. Discouragement and indifference (B, D) are opposites, there to trap careless readers. Memory tip: ardour and ardent belong together: burning passion.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The athlete is proud to be in the VANGUARD of sports development.|unforgettable position|leading position|destructive position|emerging position": "The vanguard is the front part of an army or movement, the leading position: B. Emerging (D) means just starting, not leading. Memory tip: vanguard = advance guard, out in front.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Nwankwo was on the VERGE of signing a two-year contract with the club.|shore|brink|summit|height": "On the verge of = on the brink of: just about to do something: B. Shore (A) is a literal \"edge\" trap, and summit (C) is the top, not the edge. Memory tip: verge and brink both mean edge, and both are used for being about to act.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: I am tired of your ETERNAL argument.|open|constant|strong|useless": "An \"eternal argument\" is one that never ends, so constant: B. It is exaggeration for an argument that goes on and on; open, strong and useless (A, C, D) miss the time meaning. Memory tip: eternal = endless, so it pairs with constant, perpetual, ceaseless.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The lamb is a FEEBLE little animal.|fat|quiet|loving|weak": "Feeble means weak: D. A lamb may also be quiet or loving, but those are not what feeble means. Memory tip: feeble = frail = weak.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The actress SCREAMED when she noticed an object behind her.|wailed|protested|waded in|stormed out": "To scream is to cry out loudly in fear; wailed (cried out loudly) is the nearest: A. Protested (B) is objecting, and \"waded in\" and \"stormed out\" (C, D) describe actions, not sounds. Memory tip: in \"nearest meaning\", match the kind of action first (here a sound).",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The EXHIBITION was an eye opener to all.|dispatch|display|style|examination": "An exhibition is a public display of things to see: B. Examination (D) is a sound-alike, and dispatch means sending off. Memory tip: to exhibit = to show, so an exhibition = a display.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: As a journalist, Bala has always had a NOSE for stories.|soft comment|cynical statement|an instinct|a command": "To have \"a nose for\" something is to have a natural instinct for finding it, like a dog sniffing out a trail: C. A comment, a statement and a command (A, B, D) are kinds of speech, which have nothing to do with sniffing things out. Memory tip: a nose for news = an instinct for stories.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The girl says she is averse _____ what others admire.|for|from|to|with": "The adjective averse always takes \"to\": she is averse to (opposed to) what others admire: C. \"Averse from\" is old-fashioned, and \"for\" and \"with\" are wrong. Memory tip: averse to, opposed to, allergic to, all with \"to\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Our teacher defined _____ in his introductory.|onomatopiea|onomatopoeia|onomatopoeia|onomatopea": "The correct spelling is o-no-ma-to-POE-ia: B. The paper prints B and C exactly the same, so C is as right as the key's B, and both are accepted. A (-piea) and D (-pea) misspell the ending. Memory tip: onomatopoeia ends in \"poeia\", the same root as \"poet\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The philanthropist devoted himself _____ the poor.|to helping|in helping|by helping|to be helping": "We devote ourselves to something, and when a verb follows the preposition \"to\" it takes -ing: devoted himself to helping: A. D (\"to be helping\") treats \"to\" as part of an infinitive. Memory tip: devoted to, committed to, look forward to + -ing.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Tinu likes apples _____ she does not like oranges.|or|for|so|but": "The two halves contrast (likes apples, doesn't like oranges), so we need but: D. Or gives a choice, for gives a reason, and so gives a result. Memory tip: contrast = but; result = so; reason = for/because.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The students had a _____ on Independence Day.|march past|match pass|march pass|match past": "A march past is a parade where marchers pass a saluting point, as on Independence Day: A. \"Match pass/past\" confuses march with match, and \"pass\" (C) is the wrong word, since they march past something. Memory tip: you march past the dais, so it's a march past.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Do you mind _____ another hour or two?|to wait|to have waited|wait|waiting": "\"Mind\" is always followed by the -ing form: Do you mind waiting? D. \"Mind to wait\" (A) is a common error. Memory tip: mind, enjoy, avoid, finish and suggest all take -ing.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The continuous rain has really _____ the soil.|melted up|mopped up|satiated|saturated": "Soil soaked with as much water as it can hold is saturated: D. Satiated (C) also means \"filled to the full\" but is used of appetite (food or desire), not soil. Mopped up and melted up don't fit. Memory tip: saturated = soaked with liquid; satiated = full of food.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The police described the boy as being _____ hand.|on by|up to|over at|out of": "\"Out of hand\" means out of control, as a boy the police describe as unruly: D. \"Up to\" (B) needs a following word (\"up to no good\"), and the other two are not idioms. Memory tip: out of hand = out of control; at hand = near; on hand = available.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): It was very easy for the two political parties to form a _____ government.|co-operative|colonial|collusion|coalition": "A government formed by two or more parties working together is a coalition government: D. Collusion (C) is secret plotting to cheat, a look-alike trap, and co-operative (A) is not the usual term. Memory tip: coalition = parties coming together to rule.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): All farmers were encouraged _____ carry out fumigation on their farms.|to|from|in|with": "The pattern is \"encourage someone to do something\": encouraged to carry out: A. \"From\" is used with discourage (discouraged from doing). Memory tip: encourage to, discourage from.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): There are lots of _____ in the park.|luxury buses moving fast|luxury buses fast moving|moving fast luxury buses|fast-moving luxury buses": "Adjectives come before the noun, and a two-word adjective before a noun is hyphenated: fast-moving luxury buses: D. B and C break the normal order. Memory tip: a fast-moving bus, a well-known writer, a ten-year-old boy: hyphenate the compound before the noun.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Yours is to command; _____ is to obey.|their|theirs|theirs'|their's": "Like yours, the possessive pronoun theirs stands alone and never takes an apostrophe: B. Their (A) needs a noun after it (\"their duty\"), and theirs' and their's (C, D) don't exist. Memory tip: hers, ours, yours, theirs, its: no apostrophes.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Local governments are authorized to pass _____.|bye's-law|bye-law|bye-laws|byes'-laws": "Local rules made by a council are bye-laws (also spelt by-laws), and authorities pass many of them, so the plural bye-laws: C. A and D add a wrong possessive apostrophe, and B is singular with no article. Memory tip: a by-law (or bye-law) is a local rule; the plural simply adds -s: bye-laws.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Umar: I have never visited the dentist. Aliyu: _____.|neither have I|I also never|neither myself|I myself haven't": "To agree with a negative statement, use \"Neither + auxiliary + subject\": Umar says \"I have never...\" so Aliyu says \"Neither have I\": A. B and C are ungrammatical, and D changes the response. Memory tip: positive agreement = \"So have I\"; negative agreement = \"Neither have I\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Usman would have won the race _____.|if he had run faster faster|although he ran faster|only if he could run fast|if he had run faster": "\"Would have won\" is the third conditional, which takes \"if + had + past participle\": if he had run faster: D. A says the same but prints \"faster\" twice, so it is not correct as printed; B and C break the pattern. Memory tip: third conditional = if + had done, would have done.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): My father told me to take the money from _____ it.|ever who offers|whoever offers|whomever offers|whomsoever offer": "\"Whoever\" is the subject of \"offers\" in the clause \"whoever offers it\", so it takes the subject form even after the preposition \"from\": B. Whomever (C) is the object form, and D wrongly uses \"offer\". Memory tip: decide who/whom by the job inside its own clause, not by the word before it.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Our teacher defined _____ as the killing of one's mother.|patriach|matricide|matriarch|patricide": "Matricide is the killing of one's mother (matri = mother, -cide = killing): B. Patricide (D) is killing one's father, and matriarch (C) is a woman who heads a family. Memory tip: matri = mother, patri = father, -cide = killing (as in insecticide).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): If you are confused _____ anything, phone my office.|about|for|of|with": "We are confused about something: A. \"Confused for\" means mistaken for (\"I was confused for my brother\"), not the sense needed here. Memory tip: confused about a matter, confused with/for another person or thing.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): We have a family mutiny _____ our hands.|from|of|on|for": "To have a problem \"on your hands\" is to have to deal with it: we have a family mutiny on our hands: C. Memory tip: on your hands = your problem to handle; off your hands = no longer your responsibility.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): We should try to help _____.|the less fortunate|this less fortunate|the less fortunate|less fortunate": "\"The\" + adjective names a whole group of people: the poor, the rich, the less fortunate: A. The paper prints A and C identically, so C is as right as the key's A and both are accepted. B (this) and D (no article) are wrong. Memory tip: the + adjective = all the people of that kind.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: glAsier|gleam|flat|feign|glass": "The a in glazier (printed \"glasier\") is pronounced /eɪ/, as in glaze, and feign /feɪn/ has the same vowel: C. D is wrong: glass has the long /ɑː/, a different sound, so C is marked correct. Gleam has /iː/ and flat /æ/. Memory tip: glaze, glazier and feign all rhyme with \"day\".",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: lAUd|lavatory|loud|lathe|core": "The au in laud is the long /ɔː/ sound, the same as core /kɔː/: D. Loud (B) has /aʊ/, a spelling trap, and lavatory and lathe have other a sounds. Memory tip: laud, law, cause and core share /ɔː/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: cOma|colonel|cogent|come|comma": "The o in coma is /əʊ/ (KOH-ma), the same as the o in cogent /ˈkəʊdʒənt/: B. Comma (D) has the short /ɒ/, come (C) has /ʌ/, and colonel is /ˈkɜːnl/. Memory tip: coma and comma differ by one m and one vowel: /əʊ/ vs /ɒ/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: loSe|mouse|nurse|noise|horse": "The s in lose is pronounced /z/ (looz), the same as the s in noise: C. Mouse, nurse and horse all end in /s/. Memory tip: lose /z/ vs loose /s/, the extra o is the clue to the /s/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: Guitar|jam|strange|judge|rogue": "The g in guitar is the hard /ɡ/ sound, the same as in rogue: D. Jam, strange and judge all have the soft /dʒ/ sound. Memory tip: g before u (guitar, guard, rogue) keeps its hard /ɡ/ sound.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: looSe|sell|fuse|close|rouse": "The s in loose is /s/, the same as the s in sell: A. Fuse and rouse have /z/, and so does close as a verb (to close a door). Memory tip: loose /luːs/ rhymes with goose; lose /luːz/ rhymes with choose.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: rite|list|wit|wright|rim": "Rite and wright are both pronounced /raɪt/ (like write and right), so they rhyme: C. Wit and list have the short /ɪ/, and rim doesn't end in /t/. Memory tip: in rhyme questions say the words aloud and ignore the spelling: rite, right, write and wright sound the same.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: joys|elbow|pots|boys|stays": "Joys /dʒɔɪz/ and boys /bɔɪz/ share the same vowel and ending /ɔɪz/, so they rhyme: C. Stays has /eɪz/ and pots /ɒts/. Memory tip: a rhyme needs the same vowel and the same final sound.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: call|wall|quail|dull|slate": "Call /kɔːl/ rhymes with wall /wɔːl/: A. Quail and slate have /eɪ/, and dull has /ʌ/. Memory tip: all, ball, call, fall, wall: the \"-all\" family rhymes.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): dedication|dedicaTION|deDIcation|dediCAtion|Dedication": "Words ending in -tion are stressed on the syllable just before it: de-di-CA-tion: C. -tion itself (A) is never stressed. Memory tip: -tion, -sion and -ic pull the stress onto the syllable just before them.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): international|interNAtional|internaTIONal|International|inTERnational": "International follows the -tion rule: in-ter-NA-tion-al, stress on the syllable before -tion: A. B stresses the -tion part, and C and D stress syllables that are weak. Memory tip: adding -al after -tion doesn't move the stress: naTION → interNAtional.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): information|inFORmation|Information|inforMAtion|InformatION": "Information is stressed on the syllable before -tion: in-for-MA-tion: C. A (inFORmation) copies the stress of inFORM, but adding -ation moves it. Memory tip: inFORM → inforMAtion; exPLAIN → explaNAtion.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: Adamu is leaving a CAR behind.|What is Adamu leaving behind?|Is Adamu driving the car in front?|Who is leaving a car behind?|Where is Adamu leaving a car?": "Stressing CAR means that's the new information: Adamu is leaving a car (not something else) behind. That answers \"What is Adamu leaving behind?\": A. C would need stress on ADAMU, and D on the place. Memory tip: the stressed word answers the question word (what, who, where).",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: Lambusa TOOK OFF the wig.|Who took off the wig?|What did Lambusa do?|Did Lambusa take off a wig?|Did Lambusa take off the ring?": "Stressing TOOK OFF highlights the action: he took off the wig, rather than doing something else with it. That answers \"What did Lambusa do?\": B. A would need stress on LAMBUSA, and D on WIG. Memory tip: stress on the verb = the question is about the action.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: The bed is IN the room.|Is the bed in the parlour?|Was the bed in the room?|What is in the room?|Where is the bed?": "Stressing IN highlights the bed's exact position: in the room, not outside or beside it. Of the options, that best answers \"Where is the bed?\": D. A (Is the bed in the parlour?) would need stress on ROOM, and C (What is in the room?) on BED. Memory tip: stress on a preposition (IN, ON, UNDER) is about position.",
 "eng-2014-p1|Which of the following is true according to the passage?|Change is inimitable|Change is inestimable|Change is invaluable|Change is inevitable": "The passage shows political and social change sweeping \"all over the globe\", pushed by people's desire for freedom and a better life. Something that keeps happening everywhere and cannot be avoided is inevitable: D. A is wrong: \"inimitable\" means impossible to copy, which has nothing to do with the passage. B and C (inestimable, invaluable) mean \"very precious\". Memory tip: inevitable = cannot be avoided; inimitable = cannot be imitated.",
 "eng-2014-p1|It can be deduced from the passage that political and social changes are|intertwined|antithetical|independent|repulsive": "The writer says political and social changes \"may appear to be distinct\" but \"are analytically inseparable\". Things that cannot be separated are intertwined: A. The key gives B, but \"antithetical\" means directly opposed, the reverse of what the passage says. C (independent) is the \"appearance\" the writer rejects. Memory tip: look for the word \"but\" or \"while\", where the writer turns from appearance to fact.",
 "eng-2014-p1|A suitable title for this passage is|Reasons for political change|The struggle for political power|Elements of politics|Social change and political empowerment": "The passage explains basic ideas of politics: what political and social change are, why they happen, and a definition of politics (\"who gets what, when and how\"). \"Elements of politics\" covers all of this: C. A and B describe only part of the last paragraph, and D brings in \"empowerment\", which is not discussed. Memory tip: a good title covers the whole passage, not one paragraph.",
 "eng-2014-p1|The word 'alteration', as used in the passage, means|multiplication|recognition|modification|complication": "\"Alteration or transformations\" are used together, so alteration means change, i.e. modification: C. Recognition (B) appears elsewhere in the paragraph, which is the trap; multiplication and complication have nothing to do with change. Memory tip: to alter = to change; an alteration = a modification.",
 "eng-2014-p2|It can be inferred from the passage that snakes are|heterogeneous creatures|voracious cow eaters|great insect eaters|homogeneous reptiles": "Snakes come in \"over two thousand five hundred species belonging to more than ten families\", so they are a very varied group: heterogeneous, A. D (homogeneous) means all alike, the opposite. B is wrong because eating a cow is \"uncommon\", and insects are never mentioned. Memory tip: hetero- = different; homo- = same.",
 "eng-2014-p2|The most notable thing about snakes, according to the passage, is that they|abound in Gombe and Plateau state|are versatile in reproduction|eat big but seldomly|exist in families": "The first paragraph dwells on how snakes eat: they survive on \"an extremely meager diet\", wait \"months between successive meals\" and swallow large prey whole. That is eating big but seldom: C. B twists \"versatility\", which is about habitat, not reproduction, and A is only a detail. Memory tip: the \"most notable\" point is the one the writer spends most words on.",
 "eng-2014-p2|Which of the following is true according to the passage?|Snakes are not in the polar regions|Snakes are endemic on every continent|There are countless number of snakes in the republic of Ireland|Snakes are seldom seen at the Antarctica": "The passage says \"The only places without snakes are parts of the polar regions and isolated islands\": A. B contradicts \"except Antarctica\", C contradicts Ireland being snake-free, and D is wrong because snakes are not just seldom seen in Antarctica, they are absent. Memory tip: in \"true according to the passage\" questions, check each option against one exact sentence.",
 "eng-2014-p2|It can be deduced from the passage that snakes have|no external auditory organ|visible internal locomotive organs|no visual sense of measurement|large appetite for antelopes": "The key gives B, but no option can really be deduced from this passage, which says nothing about snakes' ears, organs of movement or eyesight, and calls eating antelopes \"uncommon\" (so D is wrong too). A is true in real life (snakes have no external ears) but is not in the passage. B is kept here only as the key's answer. Memory tip: \"deduced from the passage\" means you must be able to point to the lines that support it.",
 "eng-2014-p2|A suitable title for this passage is|Feeding habits of snakes|Some characteristics of snakes|Snakes as legless, cold-blooded reptiles|Species of snakes in Nigeria and other lands": "The passage covers body heat, diet, jaws, the number of species and where snakes live: several features, so \"Some characteristics of snakes\" fits best: B. A covers only the first paragraph, C mentions \"legless\", which is not in the passage, and D covers only one sentence. Memory tip: choose the title that covers both paragraphs.",
 "eng-2014-p3|Gap 11: The _____ has to employ a lot of people.|processor|lithographer|proprietor|sub-editor": "The person who sets up a newspaper and employs the staff is its owner, the proprietor: C. A sub-editor is employed, not the employer, and a lithographer is a printer. Memory tip: proprietor = owner (think \"property\").",
 "eng-2014-p3|Gap 12: ...to produce the text of the newspaper, _____ who go out and collect stories and items of news|agents|reporters|analysts|vendors": "The people who \"go out and collect stories and items of news\" are reporters: B. Vendors sell the papers and agents act for others. Memory tip: reporters report the news they gather.",
 "eng-2014-p3|Gap 13: ...and _____, who specialize in one kind of topic.|correspondents|distributors|listeners|newscasters": "Journalists who \"specialize in one kind of topic\" (sport, politics, a region) are correspondents: A. Newscasters read news on radio or TV, and distributors deliver papers. Memory tip: a sports correspondent corresponds (reports) on one field.",
 "eng-2014-p3|Gap 14: Another important person who works closely with the editor-in-chief is the _____, who has to choose the most important stories|announcer|news editor|proofreader|reporter": "The person who \"has to choose the most important stories\" for the editor-in-chief is the news editor: B. A proofreader only checks spelling and typing errors, and an announcer works in broadcasting. Memory tip: editors choose; proofreaders correct.",
 "eng-2014-p3|Gap 15: ...the most important stories _____ go through stories sent to make necessary adjustments.|sub-editors|writers|agents|producers": "Sub-editors \"go through stories sent to make necessary adjustments\": A. Writers and producers create material; they do not check others' copy. Memory tip: sub-editors work under the editor, polishing stories.",
 "eng-2014-p3|Gap 16: Such a journalist is known as _____.|a freelancer|a composer|a columnist|an essayist": "A journalist who writes regularly, daily or weekly, \"in a particular column\" is a columnist: C. A freelancer is not on the staff, and an essayist writes essays, not newspaper columns. Memory tip: column → columnist.",
 "eng-2014-p3|Gap 17: The editorials of the newspaper will be coordinated by _____.|a guild of researchers|an editorial board|all readers|an agent": "A newspaper's editorials (its official opinions) are coordinated by the editorial board: B. Readers and agents have no say in editorials. Memory tip: editorial → editorial board.",
 "eng-2014-p3|Gap 18: The publisher could decide to establish _____ which would be on sale weekly, fortnightly or monthly|an article|column|a magazine|a gazetteer": "A publication on sale \"weekly, fortnightly or monthly\" is a magazine: C. An article and a column are parts of a paper, and a gazetteer is a geographical dictionary (not a gazette). Memory tip: dailies are newspapers; weeklies and monthlies are usually magazines.",
 "eng-2014-p3|Gap 19: ...on sale weekly, fortnightly or monthly _____ the eye catching, screaming headlines...|a contrast from|a contrast in|a contrast to|a contrast for": "The idiomatic pattern is \"a contrast to\" (or \"in contrast to\"): C. \"Contrast with\" is also used, but \"from\", \"in\" and \"for\" are not standard here. Memory tip: in contrast to, as a contrast to.",
 "eng-2014-p3|Gap 20: ...newspapers on sale everyday from the _____.|readers|distributors|pressmen|salesmen": "Newspapers on sale every day are bought from the people who sell them, the salesmen (vendors): D. Readers buy, pressmen print, and distributors supply the sellers. Memory tip: \"on sale from\" points to the seller.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The minister considered the ministry's budget to a drop in the ocean in view of the number of projects in the pipeline.|The amount available may be inadequate for projected expenditure|The minister may be dropped for failing to complete a number of projects|The money approved cannot compete the pipeline project across the ocean|The pipeline project across the ocean will be abandoned unless budgetary allocation improves": "\"A drop in the ocean\" is an amount far too small to matter compared with what is needed. With many projects \"in the pipeline\" (planned), the budget is too small: A. C and D take \"ocean\" and \"pipeline\" literally, which is the trap. Memory tip: idioms are never about the literal objects they mention.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The police are looking for the woman who framed her children out to her neighbours.|The woman and her children are in the habit of working in neighbour's farm and the police are not well disposed to this|The police may arrest the woman for allowing her neighbours to take care of her children|The woman may be arrested for allowing her children to be a nuisance to her neighbours|The police wanted remarked for allowing her children to destroy her neighbour's crops": "\"Framed\" is a misprint for \"farmed\": to farm out children is to hand them over to others to look after. So the police may arrest her for leaving her children with her neighbours: B. A takes \"farm\" literally, and C and D add nuisance and crop damage that the sentence never mentions. Memory tip: to farm out = to pass work or care on to someone else.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Jummai's father remarked that pigs would fly before she passed.|It would be possible to pass only if she worked harder|It would never be possible for her to pass|He would have to bribe her teachers to enable her to pass|She would have to cheat in order to pass": "\"Pigs might fly\" is said of something that will never happen. So her father believes she will never pass: B. A, C and D suggest ways she could pass, which is the opposite of the idiom. (The paper misprints the name \"Jummai\".) Memory tip: when pigs fly = never.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The president said that he found himself between a rock and a hard place when the press said that he had resigned.|He dreamt that he was abandoned|He thought that hard places were unsafe|He had a hard decision to make|Hard places are dangerous for the president": "To be \"between a rock and a hard place\" is to face a difficult choice between two unpleasant options: C. A, B and D take \"rock\" and \"hard place\" literally. Memory tip: rock or hard place, either way you get hurt, so it's a hard decision.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Kunana is like a bear with a sore head.|He is a bully|He is grumpy|He is ugly|He is quiet": "\"Like a bear with a sore head\" means bad-tempered and irritable, i.e. grumpy: B. A bully (A) is cruel to others, which is a different idea. Memory tip: picture a bear with a headache: grumpy!",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Olu gave his brother a bumpy ride.|Olu's brother rode on Olu's back to success|Olu took his brother on a bumpy road|Olu gave his brother a difficult time|Olu gave his brother a ride in his car": "\"To give someone a bumpy ride\" is to make things difficult for them: C. B and D take \"ride\" literally, and A turns a hard time into success. Memory tip: bumpy = full of troubles.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Adeola doesn't have to go to the farm today.|Adeola may go to the farm today if he so wishes|Adeola ought not to have gone to the farm today|Adeola must not go to the farm today|Adeola should not go to the farm today": "\"Doesn't have to\" means there is no obligation: Adeola is free to go or to stay, so he may go if he wishes: A. D is wrong: \"should not go\" (and C's \"must not go\") forbid him to go, which is a different meaning. B is about the past. Memory tip: don't have to = no need; mustn't = not allowed.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: My boss asked me to take my eyes off the ball.|I should stop paying attention to what is most important|I should be focused when I am about stay off football|I should stay off football after sustaining an injury|I should be focused when playing football": "\"Keep your eye on the ball\" means stay focused on what matters, so taking your eyes off the ball means no longer attending to what is most important: A. B, C and D take \"ball\" literally as football. Memory tip: in idiom questions, rule out every option that talks about the literal object.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The robber was hedged in by the people.|The robber was surrounded by the people|The robber was killed by the people|The robber was exposed by the people|The robber was caught by the people": "To be \"hedged in\" is to be surrounded on all sides, like a field closed in by a hedge: A. Being caught (D) or killed (B) may follow, but the expression itself only means surrounded. Memory tip: a hedge goes all round, so hedged in = surrounded.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Many workers are not happy because they live a hand-to-mouth life.|They work hard with their hands|They are voracious and avaricious|They are barely surviving|They have rejected the use of spoons": "A \"hand-to-mouth\" life is one where all you earn goes straight on food, with nothing left: barely surviving, C. A and D take \"hand\" and \"mouth\" literally, and B (greedy) is the opposite of poor. Memory tip: hand-to-mouth = just enough to live on.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Prolonged strike action DEBILITATED the industry.|invigorated|isolated|weakened|destroyed": "\"Debilitated\" means weakened; the opposite is invigorated (made strong and lively): A. Weakened (C) and destroyed (D) are near-synonyms, the usual trap. Memory tip: debility = weakness; vigour = strength.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: One of the students bought a PLAGIARIZED copy of the book.|a used|an original|a revised|an annotated": "A plagiarized copy is copied from someone else's work; the opposite is an original: B. A used or annotated copy describes the state of a book, not whether it is copied. Memory tip: plagiarism = copying; original = not copied.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The young girl was TAKEN ABACK by her father's gift of a car.|shocked|unmoved|surprised|nonplussed": "\"Taken aback\" means surprised or shocked; the opposite is unmoved (not affected at all): B. Shocked, surprised and nonplussed are all synonyms, the trap. Memory tip: in opposite questions, three options are often synonyms; pick the odd one out.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Musa is a gifted but ERRATIC player.|strong|regular|unpredictable|unstable": "An erratic player is inconsistent and unpredictable; the opposite is regular (steady, consistent): B. C and D are synonyms, and \"strong\" is about power, not consistency. Memory tip: erratic = errant, wandering about.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The lamp shades were TRANSLUCENT.|opaque|interested|luminous|transparent": "Translucent material lets some light through; opaque material lets no light through: A. Transparent (D) and luminous (C) are close in meaning, not opposite. Memory tip: trans- = through; opaque = blocks the light.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: My niece has an UNQUENCHABLE thirst for adventure stories.|a spurious|an illegitimate|a reduced|an inextinguishable": "An unquenchable thirst can never be satisfied or reduced; the opposite is a reduced thirst: C. Inextinguishable (D) is a synonym. Memory tip: to quench a fire or thirst is to put it out or reduce it.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Some of my neighbours have an ANTIPATHY to dogs.|enmity towards|affection for|acronym for|alarm for": "Antipathy is a strong dislike; the opposite is affection (liking): B. Enmity (A) is a synonym, and \"acronym\" is a sound-alike trap. Memory tip: anti- = against; antipathy = feeling against.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The dressmaker UNPICKED the seam of the shirt.|threaded|sewed up|picked up|tore for": "To unpick a seam is to undo its stitches, so the opposite is to sew it up: B. \"Picked up\" (C) plays on the word \"pick\", and \"tore\" damages rather than restores. Memory tip: un- reverses the action; find the action itself (stitching).",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The testimony of the witness was VAGUE.|disturbing|true|ambiguous|clear": "Vague testimony is unclear; the opposite is clear: D. Ambiguous (C) is a synonym, and \"true\" is about honesty, not clarity. Memory tip: vague = fuzzy; clear = sharp.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: As a student, Isa tried COMMUNAL living for a few years.|collective|general|shared|private": "Communal living is shared with a group; the opposite is private: D. Collective and shared (A, C) are synonyms. Memory tip: communal → community; private → on your own.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The chairman admires INCESSANT meetings.|unusual|planned|constant|irregular": "Incessant means never stopping, so constant: C. Irregular (D) is close to the opposite, and planned and unusual are unrelated. Memory tip: incessant = in + cease: does not cease.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Today's weather is FAVOURABLE for a game of tennis.|impartial|abnormal|encouraging|disapproving": "Favourable weather is good and suitable for the game, i.e. encouraging: C. Impartial means fair, not favouring either side, which is a trap on \"favour\". Memory tip: favourable = in your favour, helpful.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The candidates looked AGHAST at the first reading of the questions.|fulfilled|dismayed|satisfied|relaxed": "Aghast means filled with shock and horror, i.e. dismayed: B. Fulfilled, satisfied and relaxed are all pleasant feelings, the opposite. Memory tip: aghast is related to \"ghastly\": shocked.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Joke gave Muhammed a JAUNTY smile.|a discouraging|an inviting|a frightful|a cheerful": "A jaunty smile is lively, confident and cheerful: D. Discouraging and frightful are the opposite mood, and \"inviting\" is close but not the meaning. Memory tip: jaunty = bouncy and cheerful.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The first round of the tournament was a DODDLE.|easy|balanced|dodgy|exasperating": "A doddle (British informal) is something very easy: A. Dodgy (C) is a sound-alike meaning risky or dishonest, and exasperating is the opposite. Memory tip: \"it's a doddle\" = it's a piece of cake.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The lazy man cast a LUSTFUL glance at his neighbour's wife.|hateful|quick|covetous|envious": "A lustful glance shows desire for something, here a person who belongs to someone else: covetous, C. Envious (D) is resentment of another's good fortune, close but not desire. Memory tip: \"Thou shalt not covet thy neighbour's wife\".",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: They accused him of FOMENTING political unrest.|inciting|discouraging|preventing|guiding": "To foment unrest is to stir it up, i.e. to incite it: A. Discouraging and preventing (B, C) are the opposite. Memory tip: foment trouble = ferment trouble, make it bubble up.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: You can LEARN a great deal just from watching other players.|invent|accumulate|allow|discover": "To learn a great deal from watching others is to find things out, i.e. discover: D. Accumulate (B) is tempting, but it means pile up, not find out. Memory tip: learning = discovering something new.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: All the researchers were asked to GATHER information on the new viral infection.|collect|disseminate|distort|give": "To gather information is to collect it: A. Disseminate (B) means spread it out, the opposite. Memory tip: gather in = collect; disseminate = scatter seed.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The dispute between the two countries has resulted in the SEVERING of diplomatic relations.|breaking|securing|swapping|strengthening": "Severing diplomatic relations means cutting them off, breaking them: A. Strengthening and securing are the opposite. Memory tip: sever = cut (as in a severed rope).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The House and The Senate will _____ at noon next Wednesday to hear address by the president.|convene|adjourn|rise|collude": "Parliament comes together to meet, i.e. convenes: A. Adjourn and rise (B, C) mean end a sitting, and collude means plot together secretly. Memory tip: convene = come together; convention = a gathering.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): At the _____ of the century, many ways of doing things were introduced.|turn|event|birth|sight": "The fixed expression is \"at the turn of the century\", meaning around the time one century ends and the next begins: A. C is wrong: \"the birth of the century\" is not a standard English expression. Memory tip: memorise collocations as whole phrases: the turn of the century, the turn of the year.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): You may have the pencil, but you can't have the ballpoint _____.|either|furthermore|also|as well": "\"You may have the pencil, but you can't have the ballpoint as well\": you can have one but not both. \"As well\" (= too) fits: D. \"Either\" adds a second refusal and clashes with \"you may have the pencil\", and \"also\" and \"furthermore\" are not used at the end like this. Memory tip: as well = too, at the end of a clause.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The president said that the country was not out of the _____ yet.|forest|fog|water|wood": "\"Not out of the wood(s) yet\" means not yet free from danger or difficulty: D. Forest, fog and water are not part of the idiom. Memory tip: out of the woods = out of danger.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): He went to the restaurant to enjoy the special _____.|suite|cuisine|a la carte|chef": "A restaurant's food or style of cooking is its cuisine: B. A la carte means ordering dishes separately from the menu, a suite is a set of rooms, and a chef is the cook. Memory tip: cuisine = cooking (from French \"kitchen\").",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The invigilator _____ to know how long the examination _____ going on.|wanted/has been|wants/had been|wants/have been|wanted/had been": "\"Wanted\" sets the time in the past, so the reported clause moves back: \"how long the examination had been going on\": D. B and C mix present \"wants\" with past \"had been\", and A pairs past with present perfect. Memory tip: a past reporting verb pulls the reported verb one step back.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The guard spent all the night pacing _____.|from and to|fro and to|to and from|to and fro": "The fixed phrase is \"to and fro\", meaning backwards and forwards: D. \"To and from\" (C) is used for journeys (\"to and from school\"), not pacing, and A and B reverse the order. Memory tip: pace to and fro; travel to and from.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The woman refused to testify _____ her husband.|in|at|against|from": "In court, one testifies against (or for) someone: C. \"Testify in\" refers to a case or court, and \"at\" and \"from\" are not used with a person. Memory tip: testify against the accused, for the defence.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Abike must have found the very interesting movies quite _____.|absolving|absorbing|nauseating|perverting": "The films are \"very interesting\", so she must have found them absorbing (holding her attention): B. C is wrong: \"nauseating\" (sickening) contradicts \"very interesting\". Absolving means forgiving, a sound-alike trap. Memory tip: let the clue words in the sentence (very interesting) decide the tone of the gap.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The words _____ divided between the end of one line.|have been|have being|has been|has being": "\"Words\" is plural, so it needs \"have\", and the passive needs the past participle \"been\": \"have been divided\": A. \"Being\" after \"have\" is never correct, and \"has\" doesn't agree with \"words\". Memory tip: have/has + been; is/are + being.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Those _____ are very beautiful.|flowers of her|flowers of her's|our flower|flowers of ours": "The double possessive uses a possessive pronoun after \"of\": \"those flowers of ours\", like \"a friend of mine\": D. \"Of her\" (A) needs \"hers\", and \"her's\" (B) never has an apostrophe. C doesn't agree with \"those... are\". Memory tip: of + mine/yours/his/hers/ours/theirs.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Cooking has never been Jumoke's _____.|recital|purview|style|forte": "Your forte is the thing you are especially good at: \"Cooking has never been her forte\": D. Purview means area of authority, and recital is a performance. Memory tip: forte = strong point (Italian/French for \"strong\").",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): When the strike is over, there will probably be an increase in wages and a _____ increase in prices.|sporadic|concordant|concurrent|chronic": "Wages and prices increasing together means a concurrent (simultaneous) increase: C. Sporadic means now and then, chronic means long-lasting, and concordant means in agreement. Memory tip: concurrent = running at the same time.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): My mother was _____ annoyed with me for coming late.|very|neither|hotly|just": "\"Very\" is the standard intensifier before an adjective: \"very annoyed\": A. \"Neither\" needs \"nor\", and \"hotly\" goes with verbs (\"hotly denied\"), not with annoyed. Memory tip: very + adjective; hotly + verb.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The chairman is too much _____ an idealist for this government.|from|about|of|with": "The idiom is \"too much of a/an...\": \"too much of an idealist\": C. From, about and with don't fit this pattern. Memory tip: too much of a good thing; too much of an idealist.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The clock _____ 12 o'clock two hours ago.|strikes|strike|struck|striking": "\"Two hours ago\" fixes the time in the past, so the past tense \"struck\" is needed: C. Strike, strikes and striking are not past forms. Memory tip: ago = past simple.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): What is the jury's _____ the matter.|verdict on|verdict from|verdict at|verdict with": "One gives a verdict on a matter: A. \"From\" names the source, and \"at\" and \"with\" are not used with verdict. Memory tip: a verdict on, a decision on, an opinion on.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The unconscious man was _____ after receiving first aid.|reawakened|reformed|restored|revived": "To bring an unconscious person back to consciousness is to revive them: D. Restored (C) needs an object such as \"restored to health\", reformed is about behaviour, and reawakened is used of feelings, not first aid. Memory tip: revive = bring back to life (vivus = alive).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The laughter _____ his face for a moment.|improved|controlled|animated": "Laughter brings life and movement to a face, so it \"animated his face\": C. Improved and controlled don't describe the effect of laughter. (The fourth option is illegible in the scan.) Memory tip: animate = give life to (anima = spirit).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): She traced her family history _____ matrilineal descent.|in|by|with|at": "One traces a family history \"by\" (through) a line of descent: B. In, with and at are not used here. Memory tip: by birth, by descent, by marriage.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: cOOl|full|luke|look|should": "The oo in cool is the long vowel /uː/, the same as the u in luke (/luːk/): B. Full, look and should have the short /ʊ/, the trap because some of them are spelt oo. Memory tip: long /uː/ = food, cool, luke; short /ʊ/ = good, look, full.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: odOUR|flow|sugar|hold|floor": "The underlined -our in odour is unstressed, so it is the weak vowel /ə/ (schwa), just like the -ar in sugar: B. Flow and hold have /əʊ/, the sound of the first o in odour, which is the trap, and floor has /ɔː/. Memory tip: unstressed endings like -our, -ar, -er are usually /ə/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: pAlm|ranch|florid|lunch|plait": "The a in palm is the long /ɑː/. In British English, ranch is /rɑːntʃ/ with the same vowel: A. Florid has /ɒ/, lunch /ʌ/ and plait /æ/. Memory tip: palm, calm, father, ranch all share /ɑː/ in British English.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: viSion|instruction|mansion|nation|enclosure": "The s in vision is /ʒ/, the same sound as the s in enclosure (/ɪnˈkləʊʒə/): D. Instruction, mansion and nation have /ʃ/, which is close but voiceless. Memory tip: -sion after a vowel is usually /ʒ/ (vision, decision); after a consonant it's /ʃ/ (mansion, tension).",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: GNash|forge|new|king|ring": "In gnash the g is silent, so gn = /n/, the first sound of new: B. King and ring end in /ŋ/, a different nasal, and forge has /dʒ/. Memory tip: gn at the start of a word = /n/ (gnat, gnaw, gnome).",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: epitaPH|pseudo|fan|paper|pneumonia": "The ph in epitaph is /f/, as in fan: B. In pseudo and pneumonia the p is silent, and paper has /p/. Memory tip: ph = /f/ (phone, graph, epitaph).",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: evER|favour|fever|never|heavier": "The underlined -er in ever is the weak vowel /ə/. The key gives C (never), which rhymes fully with ever, but in fact all four options end in that same /ə/: favour, fever, never, heavier. So every option is accepted, with C as the best match. Memory tip: unstressed -er and -our endings are all /ə/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: kEEp|reap|seethe|threat|dead": "The ee in keep is long /iː/, the same as the ea in reap: A. The key gives A, but seethe (/siːð/) has the same long /iː/, so B is also right. Threat and dead have short /e/, the ea trap. Memory tip: ea can be long (reap, meat) or short (dead, threat); always say the word.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same sounds as the letters in capitals: tAX|box|lacks|back|ask": "The underlined letters in tax are \"ax\", pronounced /æks/: the -acks in lacks is exactly the same: B. Back has /æ/ but no /ks/, box has /ɒ/, and ask has /ɑː/ in British English. Memory tip: match the whole underlined part, vowel and consonants, not just the vowel.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): valedictory|valeDICtory|valedicTORY|VAledictory|vaLEdictory": "Valedictory is stressed on the third syllable: va-le-DIC-to-ry: A. Words ending in -ictory usually stress the syllable before it (contraDICtory). Memory tip: -ictory: stress the DIC.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): congratulation|congraTUlation|congratuLAtion|CONgratulation|conGRAtulation": "Words ending in -tion are stressed on the syllable just before it: con-gra-tu-LA-tion: B. Note that the verb is conGRAtulate, but -tion moves the stress. Memory tip: -tion pulls the stress to the syllable right before it.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): conspiracy|conspiRAcy|conspiraCY|consPIracy|conSPIracy": "Conspiracy is stressed on the second syllable: con-SPI-ra-cy: D. C capitalises only \"PI\", which splits the syllable spir wrongly; D marks the whole syllable. Memory tip: conSPIRE gives conSPIracy: the stress stays on SPI.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: My mother brought a BICYCLE yesterday.|What did your mother buy yesterday?|Whose mother bought a bicycle yesterday?|Did my mother steal a bicycle yesterday?|When did my mother buy a bicycle?": "Emphatic stress on BICYCLE answers the question \"what?\": what did your mother buy yesterday? A. B would need stress on MY, C on BOUGHT, and D on YESTERDAY. (\"Brought\" in the sentence is a misprint for \"bought\".) Memory tip: the stressed word is the answer to the question.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: AMINA went to Abuja by air.|Is Amina going to Abuja by air?|Who went to Abuja by air?|Did Amina go to Abuja by road?|Did Amina go to Jos by air?": "Stress on AMINA answers \"who?\": Who went to Abuja by air? B. C would stress AIR, D would stress ABUJA, and A is about the future. Memory tip: stressed name = answers \"who?\".",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: Musa is STAYING in Enugu.|Is Musa passing through Enugu?|Is Musa staying on the outskirt of Enugu?|Is Audu staying in Enugu?|Was Musa staying in Enugu?": "Stress on STAYING contrasts it with another action: \"Is Musa passing through Enugu?\" \"No, he is STAYING there\": A. B would stress IN, C would stress MUSA, and D would stress IS. Memory tip: the stressed word corrects the word that was wrong in the question.",
 "eng-2015-p1|The word 'eagerness', as used in the passage, means|keenness|frankness|correctness|calmness": "The town authorities showed \"short-sighted eagerness\" to allocate land: they were too keen to give it out without thinking ahead. Eagerness means keenness: A. Frankness is openness in speech, and calmness is nearly the opposite of eagerness. Memory tip: eager = keen; when you see \"-ness\", find the option that is the same quality as the root word.",
 "eng-2015-p1|The expression 'unless mechanically minded', as used in the passage, refers to|coachmen and grooms adaptable to the new technology|those amenable to change and development|coachmen and grooms who choose to become mechanics|town authorities allocating land": "Coachmen and grooms were finding it harder to earn a living \"unless mechanically minded\", that is, unless they could adapt to the new motor technology. So the expression points to coachmen and grooms adaptable to the new technology: A. C goes too far, since the passage never says they chose to become mechanics, and B is too general. Memory tip: \"unless X\" names the exception, the people who still do well.",
 "eng-2015-p1|The statement 'By 1910, the motor car was plainly conquering the highway', as used in the passage, means that|by 1910 many people knew how to drive motor cars|the motor car was invented in 1910|by 1910 the motor car became a common sight on the highways|highway codes for the motor cars came into effect in 1910": "\"Conquering the highway\" is a metaphor: the motor car was taking over the roads, so by 1910 it had become a common sight on them: C. B is wrong because the car was already \"conquering\" in 1910, not just invented, and A and D add ideas (driving skills, highway codes) the passage never mentions. Memory tip: in figurative language, \"conquer\" means \"take over\" or \"dominate\".",
 "eng-2015-p1|The writer seems to suggest that|there were no roads before the advent of cars and motorcycles and so people had to be mechanically minded|the volume and speed of traffic on the roads increased with the advent of cars, the motorcycles and lorries|coachmen and grooms were not mechanically minded|the roads that existed were dormant": "The roads \"awoke to feverish activity\", cars \"dashed along them\", and the road system had to adapt to \"a volume and speed of traffic for which it had never been intended\". So traffic volume and speed increased with the new vehicles: B. A is wrong because the roads already existed, and D describes the roads before the cars came, not the writer's point. Memory tip: \"seems to suggest\" asks for the writer's main idea, not a side detail.",
 "eng-2015-p1|Which of the following statements can be inferred from the passage?|Insignificant improvement occurred in transport since the advent of the motor cars, the lorries and the motorcycles|Human society was static without the express speed of cars and motorcycles|Society would be less chaotic without the increasing volume and speed of motor cars, lorries and motorcycles|People no longer use trains with the advent of cars and lorries": "The answer marked is A: improvement of the roads (widening them, straightening them, removing blind corners) had \"scarcely been tackled before 1914\", so little real improvement was made. The key gives A, but C can be inferred just as well: the new traffic brought accidents, noise, crowding and danger, so society would be less chaotic without it. Both are marked right. B and D overstate: trains were still running, and society was not \"static\". Memory tip: an inference must rest on words in the passage; check each option against a sentence.",
 "eng-2015-p2|According to the passage, which of these characteristics is shared between chimpanzees and man?|Maintenance of close family ties|Open rivalry among offspring|Daughters maintaining closer contact with fathers|Undue concern for family economic welfare": "Mother and offspring stay together for four or five years, \"longer than any other primate except man\". So close family ties are something chimpanzees share with humans: A. B, C and D (rivalry, fathers, economic welfare) are never mentioned. Memory tip: \"except man\" tells you exactly where the passage compares the animals with humans.",
 "eng-2015-p2|The word 'predominantly', as used in the passage, means|objectively|primitively|chiefly|carefully": "\"In the forest, they are predominantly fruit eaters\" means fruit makes up most of their diet: predominantly = mainly, chiefly: C. Carefully (D) is a tempting echo of a later sentence, but it doesn't fit here. Memory tip: replace the word with each option in the sentence; only \"chiefly fruit eaters\" keeps the meaning.",
 "eng-2015-p2|According to the passage, chimpanzees prefer|termites and plants|fruits and plants|meat and leaves|meat and crabs": "Chimpanzees are \"predominantly fruit eaters\" and eat meat \"with a mouthful of leaves between each bite\", so their main diet is fruits and plants: B. Meat (C, D) is only eaten sometimes, and termites (A) only in season. Memory tip: \"prefer\" means what they eat most, so look for the word \"predominantly\".",
 "eng-2015-p2|From the passage, it can be deduced that chimpanzees|eats termites when in need of meat|eat termites when in groups|eat termites provided by mother and offspring|eat termites in season": "\"When the season is right, chimpanzees in woodlands also eat termites\": they eat termites in season, D. A is wrong because they fish for termites as a seasonal food, not because they need meat, and B and C are not in the passage. Memory tip: deduction questions are answered by a specific sentence, so find it and match it.",
 "eng-2015-p2|Which of the following is true according to the passage?|Chimpanzees are meticulous when searching for food.|Chimpanzees are reactive when in groups.|Chimpanzees are restless when hungry.|Chimpanzees are carnivorous and gregarious.": "The passage dwells on how carefully chimpanzees get their food: they \"carefully select\" stems, trim them to the right length, and fish for termites only when the season is right. That is meticulous: A. The key gives D, which is partly true, since the males hunt and eat meat and share it, so it is also accepted. But \"carnivorous\" overstates it, because they are \"predominantly fruit eaters\". Memory tip: one wrong word in an option (carnivorous) can make the whole option weaker.",
 "eng-2015-p3|Gap 11: For instance _____ often place higher value on freedom…|visitors|youths|aliens|government": "The next sentence begins \"The aged tend to value their safety more\", which contrasts with the group in the gap. The opposite of the aged is the young: youths, B. Visitors and aliens have no link with the contrast, and government is not a group that \"values freedom\" in this sense. Memory tip: in cloze passages, look at the next sentence for a contrast word or pair.",
 "eng-2015-p3|Gap 12: …they see safety precautions required in road traffic operations as a _____ of their personal freedom.|clog|coalition|violation|vibration": "People who value freedom see safety rules as an attack on that freedom, a violation of it: C. Clog is a blockage and would need \"on\", coalition is an alliance, and vibration is shaking. Memory tip: \"a violation of their rights/freedom\" is a fixed pairing in English.",
 "eng-2015-p3|Gap 13: The aged… perceive safety precautions as a means to _____ personal safety.|warrant|suppress|increase|guarantee": "The aged value safety, so they see precautions as a way to make sure of it: guarantee, D. Suppress is the opposite, increase does not fit \"personal safety\" as neatly, and warrant means to justify. Memory tip: \"a means to guarantee safety/success\" is a common pattern.",
 "eng-2015-p3|Gap 14: Today, there is a greater interest in _____ be it to life, property or the environment.|security|management|procurement|development": "The whole passage is about safety \"to life, property or the environment\", so the interest is in security: A. Management and development do not go with \"life, property\", and procurement means buying. Memory tip: security of life and property is a set phrase.",
 "eng-2015-p3|Gap 15: The increased attention on safety… is a further _____ of man's desire for safety.|evidence|ingredient|confidence|allusion": "The increased attention on safety shows man's desire for safety: it is further evidence of it, A. Ingredient is part of a recipe, confidence is self-belief, and allusion is an indirect reference. Memory tip: \"a further evidence of\" = \"more proof of\".",
 "eng-2015-p3|Gap 16: …the conquest of disease is quite different from the conquest of an _____ and unplanned event called 'accident'.|unseen|unforeseeable|unfeasible|unforeseen": "An accident happens without warning: it is unforeseen (not expected) and unplanned, D. Unseen (A) means not seen, and unfeasible (C) means impossible to do. B is misprinted \"unforeseeeable\"; unforeseen is the normal word for a past or actual event. The paper also misprints \"quite\" as \"quiet\". Memory tip: unforeseen = not foreseen (not expected).",
 "eng-2015-p3|Gap 17: The _____ and (18) implications of accidents are so great…|cultural|natural|commercial|economic": "Accidents cause deaths, injuries and \"destruction of properties\", which cost money, so their implications are economic: D. B is wrong: \"natural implications\" makes no sense here, and \"economic and social implications\" is the standard pairing. Cultural and commercial are too narrow. Memory tip: \"economic and social\" is a fixed pair, like \"political and economic\".",
 "eng-2015-p3|Gap 18: The (17) and _____ implications of accidents are so great…|political|social|industrial|religious": "Accidents also affect families and communities, so the second word is social: B. Political, industrial and religious implications are not the point of the passage. Memory tip: think of who suffers from accidents: individuals, families, society.",
 "eng-2015-p3|Gap 19: …no effort is too much towards its _____.|protection|prevention|preservation|presumption": "\"No effort is too much towards its prevention\" means we must work hard to stop accidents happening: B. We protect people, not accidents (A), we preserve good things (C), and presumption means assumption. Memory tip: accident prevention is the aim of all safety education.",
 "eng-2015-p3|Gap 20: Accidents do not just happen, they are _____.|created|caused|cursed|cost": "\"Accidents do not just happen, they are caused\": they have causes that we can find and stop, B. Created (A) suggests something made on purpose, and cursed and cost do not fit. Memory tip: \"do not just happen\" leads to the cause, so look for \"caused\".",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Olu began to feel he was really homing in on the answer.|He was moving towards nowhere|He was aiming at the answer|He was homesick|He was on his way home": "To home in on something is to move steadily towards it, like a missile homing in on a target. So Olu was aiming at (getting close to) the answer: B. C and D take \"home\" literally (homesick, on his way home), and A is the opposite. Memory tip: idioms with \"home in on\" are about targets, not houses.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Chukwudi often recites the poem off the cuff.|He recites it without preparation|He reads it outside the class|He reads it under duress|He recites it standing up": "To do something off the cuff is to do it without preparation, on the spot: A. B, C and D all try to read meaning into \"cuff\" (outside, duress, standing). Memory tip: an off-the-cuff speech is an unprepared one.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Ayo sailed close to the wind.|He took a risk by doing something illegal|He is close to breaking the record|He is close to getting an award|He sailed smoothly around the world": "To sail close to the wind is to take a risk by doing something that is almost or actually illegal or dangerous: A. B and C take \"close\" literally, and D takes \"sailed\" literally. Memory tip: sailing too close to the wind can capsize a boat, so the idiom means risky behaviour.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Musa is a past-master at the game of deception.|He is an expert|He is a notable player|He is a referee|He is a master dribbler": "A past master is someone very skilled and experienced at something: an expert, A. B (notable player) and D (master dribbler) only echo \"game\" and \"master\". Memory tip: past master = expert, often at something sly like deception.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: People have golden opinions of the teacher.|They have very great admiration for him|They talk about him with caution|They see him as a dishonest person|They like him for his plain dealing": "To have golden opinions of someone is to think very highly of them: very great admiration, A. B and C are negative, and D adds a reason (\"plain dealing\") the sentence never gives. Memory tip: golden = precious, so golden opinions are the best opinions.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: It has been observed that the labour president has his members in his pocket.|He puts his members in his pocket|He is influenced by his members|He has control over his members|He is very close to his members": "To have someone in your pocket is to have them under your control or influence: C. A takes it literally, and B reverses who controls whom. D (close to them) misses the idea of power. Memory tip: whoever is \"in your pocket\" does what you want.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: If you had not lost your temper, neither of us would be in this trouble.|You did not lose your temper so we are not in trouble.|You did not lose your temper but we are both in trouble|You are in trouble because you lost your temper but I am not.|We are both in trouble because you lost your temper": "\"If you had not lost your temper…\" is a third conditional, which talks about the opposite of what happened. So in fact you did lose your temper, and that is why we are both in trouble: D. A and B say you did not lose your temper, and C forgets \"neither of us\". Memory tip: \"If + had not\" means it DID happen.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The low income class are now badly off with the fast increasing cost of living.|They are badly monetized|They are at a disadvantage|They are badly employed|They are the low income agitators": "To be badly off is to be poor or in a difficult position, so the low income class are at a disadvantage: B. A, C and D add ideas (money supply, jobs, agitators) not in the sentence. Memory tip: well off = rich; badly off = poor, struggling.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: If Musa should become president, trade with other countries will increase.|It is possible that trade will increase with the man's presidency|This man ought to be president in order to increase trade|If trade increases, this man should become president.": "\"If Musa should become president\" presents a possible future: it is possible that trade will increase if he becomes president, A. B turns the condition into advice, and C reverses cause and effect. The paper prints only three options here. Memory tip: \"If… should…\" signals possibility, not obligation.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: When the meeting was going on Aisha had her head in the clouds.|Aisha was not sleeping|Aisha was meditating|Aisha was not concentrating|Aisha was making a noise": "To have your head in the clouds is to be daydreaming and not paying attention: Aisha was not concentrating, C. A (not sleeping) and B (meditating) miss the point that her mind was elsewhere. The paper prints \"cloud\". Memory tip: head in the clouds = mind far away from what is happening.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He retired after an ILLUSTRIOUS career in the civil service.|untoward|obscure|infamous|enviable": "Illustrious means famous and highly respected. The opposite is obscure, unknown and unimportant: B. Infamous (C) is the trap: it means famous for something bad, so it is still \"famous\", not the opposite. Memory tip: illustrious vs obscure = well known vs unknown.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The secretary's loyalty to her boss was UNALLOYED.|credible|incredible|half-hearted|hypothetical": "Unalloyed means pure, complete, not mixed with anything else (like a pure metal). Complete loyalty is the opposite of half-hearted loyalty: C. Credible and incredible are about belief, not completeness. Memory tip: an alloy is a mixture, so unalloyed = unmixed, total.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The report of the enquiry showed that the manager was CULPABLE.|guilty|sincere|innocent|honest": "Culpable means deserving blame, guilty. The opposite is innocent: C. Guilty (A) is a synonym, the classic trap, and honest (D) is not the exact opposite of blameworthy. Memory tip: culpable comes from Latin culpa (fault), as in \"culprit\".",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Mrs. Bola is a SUBMISSIVE housewife.|a calm|an assertive|a sluggish|an emotional": "A submissive person obeys others without argument. The opposite is assertive, someone who states their views confidently: B. Calm (A) and sluggish (C) are about mood and energy, not obedience. Memory tip: submit = give in; assert = stand up for yourself.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The teacher CAUTIOUSLY raised his arm.|finally|slowly|quickly|rashly": "Cautiously means carefully, avoiding risk. The opposite is rashly, acting without thinking: D. Quickly (C) is tempting but is about speed, not care. Memory tip: careful vs careless; cautious vs rash.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: I admire Mahmud for his ALTRUISTIC behaviour.|benevolent|selfish|arrogant|honest": "Altruistic means caring unselfishly for others. The opposite is selfish: B. Benevolent (A) is a synonym, a common trap. Memory tip: altruistic comes from Latin alter (other): thinking of others.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The company imported some SOPHISTICATED equipment from Europe.|simple|rural|heavy|light": "Sophisticated equipment is complex and highly developed. The opposite is simple: A. Heavy and light (C, D) are about weight, and rural is about place. Memory tip: sophisticated vs simple, advanced vs basic.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The thought of eating the forbidden meat REPULSED the little girl.|disgusted|fascinated|helped|shocked": "To repulse is to disgust and push away. The opposite is to fascinate, to attract strongly: B. Disgusted (A) and shocked (D) are near-synonyms. Memory tip: repulse/repel push away; attract/fascinate pull in.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The messenger was ELEVATED for his service to the organisation.|dismissed|transferred|demoted|retrenched": "To be elevated is to be raised to a higher rank, promoted. The opposite is demoted: C. Dismissed and retrenched (A, D) mean losing the job altogether, not moving down a rank. Memory tip: elevate = lift up, demote = move down; both keep you in the job.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Mr. Gana is a thoroughly OBNOXIOUS young man.|offensive|pleasant|fraudulent|sincere": "Obnoxious means extremely unpleasant and offensive. The opposite is pleasant: B. Offensive (A) is a synonym. Memory tip: if an option looks like a synonym, it is the trap in an \"opposite\" question.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The musician basked in the ADULATION of the crowd.|condemnation|admiration|expectation|visitation": "Adulation is great praise or admiration, often excessive. The nearest is admiration: B. Condemnation is the opposite. Memory tip: to bask in adulation is to enjoy being admired.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The soldiers were arrested for using the ARSENALS unlawfully.|weapons|garrison|battalion|infantry": "An arsenal is a store of weapons and ammunition; using arsenals unlawfully means using the weapons: A. Garrison, battalion and infantry are groups of soldiers, not weapons. Memory tip: arsenal = arms store.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Two rows of trees ARCHED over the bridge on the highway.|sped|passed|laid|curved": "Trees that arch over a bridge bend to form a curve above it: curved, D. Passed (B) is tempting but misses the shape. Memory tip: an arch is a curve, so to arch is to curve.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The old man sat for hours MUSING on the wonders of the world.|laughing at|meditating on|meditating for|planning to": "To muse on something is to think deeply about it: meditating on, B. C has the wrong preposition: we meditate on (or upon) something, not \"for\" it. Memory tip: muse on = meditate on = ponder.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The lazy man cast a LUSTFUL glance at his neighbour's wife.|covetous|envious|hateful|quick": "A lustful glance shows strong desire, and covetous means wanting what belongs to someone else, here his neighbour's wife: A. Envious (B) is close, but envy is about resenting what another has rather than desiring a person. Memory tip: \"Thou shalt not covet thy neighbour's wife\": covet = desire what is another's.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Her search for the treasure was FRANTIC.|rewarding|chaotic|hectic|sudden": "Frantic means wild, hurried and disorganised, often from worry. The key gives B, chaotic (disorderly), and hectic (C, full of hurried activity) is equally good, so both are marked right. Rewarding and sudden do not match. Memory tip: frantic = frenzied: hurried AND disordered.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The nurse HELD UP the operation.|delayed|aggravated|condemned|hastened": "To hold up something is to delay it: A. Hastened (D) is the opposite, and aggravated means made worse. Memory tip: traffic hold-ups are delays.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: 'We must consider all CONTINGENCIES,' said the teacher.|hostilities|irregularities|possibilities|proximities": "Contingencies are possible future events that may or may not happen, so we \"consider all contingencies\": possibilities, C. Irregularities and hostilities do not carry the idea of \"might happen\". Memory tip: contingency plan = plan for what might happen.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The artiste gave a CREDITABLE performance.|realistic|praiseworthy|truthful|bad": "Creditable means deserving praise or credit, so a creditable performance is a praiseworthy one: B. Bad (D) is the opposite, and truthful plays on \"credible\". Memory tip: creditable = deserving credit; credible = believable.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: It was an AUSPICIOUS moment for the intending couple.|a sympathetic|a dangerous|an unexpected|a promising": "Auspicious means showing signs of future success, favourable: a promising moment, D. Dangerous (B) is the opposite (inauspicious). Memory tip: an auspicious start is a good omen.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Hisila is _____ clever but not brilliant.|quite|nearly|hardly more|very much": "\"Quite clever but not brilliant\" means fairly clever, which matches the contrast with \"brilliant\": A. Nearly clever and hardly more clever sound odd, and \"very much clever\" is wrong because \"very much\" does not go before a plain adjective. Memory tip: in British English, \"quite + adjective\" often means \"fairly\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Sola can't read _____ candle light.|at|to|by|as": "We read by the light of something: by candlelight, by lamplight, by moonlight: C. \"At candle light\" and \"to candle light\" are not English. Memory tip: by + light source.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Dede has left school, _____?|didn't he|isn't it|hasn't he|did he": "\"Has left\" uses the helping verb has, and the statement is positive, so the tag repeats \"has\" in the negative: hasn't he? (C). Didn't he (A) would match \"Dede left\", and isn't it (B) is the common Nigerian error. Memory tip: the tag copies the helping verb and flips positive to negative.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The chairman _____ all his authority to ensure that his daughter gained admission to the university.|evolved|asserted|exerted|extracted": "The key gives B: to assert one's authority is to use it firmly and make people accept it. But exerted (C) is equally right: to exert authority is to bring it to bear to get a result, which suits \"to ensure that his daughter gained admission\". Both are marked right. Evolved and extracted do not collocate with authority. Memory tip: assert/exert authority are both correct; evolve and extract are not used with it.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Asilotu tried to jump over the wall several times but _____.|cannot|would not have|will not be|could not": "\"Tried\" is past, so the second clause must be past too: \"but could not\", D. Cannot (A) is present, and \"would not have\" and \"will not be\" do not fit. Memory tip: keep the tense of both clauses in step.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The poor boy was _____ a darkroom for 48 hours.|fortified with|extricated from|confused of|confined to": "To be kept in a room is to be confined to it: D. Extricated from (B) means freed from, the opposite, and \"confused of\" is not English. Memory tip: confined to bed, confined to a room.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Suana _____ that hexagons had five sides, but later he knew they were six-sided figures.|has believed|believes|had believed|would have believed": "Suana believed something first, and \"later he knew\" otherwise. The earlier past action takes the past perfect: had believed, C. Has believed (A) and believes (B) clash with \"later he knew\". Memory tip: past perfect (had + past participle) = the earlier of two past events.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): They thought Musa _____ agree if they altered some of the conditions.|might|can|ought|may": "\"They thought\" is past, and \"if they altered\" is a second-conditional style condition, so the modal must be past: might, A. Can and may (B, D) are present forms, and \"ought\" needs \"to\". Memory tip: in reported thought, can becomes could and may becomes might.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Kusana _____ in the office, but he has gone fishing instead.|will be|should be|may be|must be": "\"Should be\" tells us where Kusana ought to be (in the office), which contrasts with \"but he has gone fishing instead\": B. Must be (D) and will be (A) would claim he is there, which the second clause denies. Memory tip: should = what ought to happen but may not.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): You live in the city now, _____?|are you|don't you|haven't you|didn't you": "\"You live\" is a positive present simple statement with no helping verb, so the tag uses do: don't you? (B). Are you, haven't you and didn't you use the wrong helping verb or tense. Memory tip: no helping verb in the statement → use do/does/did in the tag.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The man was given _____ degree despite the fact that he did not attend a university.|a ceremonial|an honorary|a ceremonious|an honourable": "A degree given as an honour, to someone who did not study for it, is an honorary degree: B. Honourable (D) means deserving respect, and ceremonial and ceremonious are about ceremonies. Memory tip: honorary = given as an honour, without the usual requirements.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The university is a corporate body made _____ different colleges.|up of|in with|up from|of with": "To be made up of means to consist of: \"a corporate body made up of different colleges\", A. \"Up from\", \"in with\" and \"of with\" are not English. Memory tip: made up of = composed of.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Next term we are going to _____ how to draw.|learnt|be taught|be teaching|learning": "After \"going to\" we need the base form of a verb. Since we learn from a teacher, the passive \"be taught\" fits: B. Learnt and learning are not base forms, and \"be teaching\" means we will teach. Memory tip: going to + base verb (be taught, learn).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Concrete is made of _____.|a sand and cement|sand and cement|sand and a cement|sand and a cement": "Sand and cement are uncountable (mass) nouns, so they take no \"a\": \"made of sand and cement\", B. A, C and D all put \"a\" before a mass noun (C and D are printed the same). Memory tip: no \"a\" before uncountable nouns like sand, water, rice, cement.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The HOD says she considers her degree certificate _____ than as a prize through labour.|as a rather gift of God|rather as a gift of God|as a gift of rather of God|rather God as a gift": "The sentence compares two ways of seeing the certificate: \"rather as a gift of God than as a prize through labour\". Rather comes before the first \"as\" phrase, which balances the second: B. A, C and D scramble the word order. The paper misprints \"through\" as \"thought\". Memory tip: rather X than Y: keep X and Y in the same form.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): I stayed away _____ I was late for the dinner.|when|so|although|since": "\"Since\" here means \"because\": I stayed away because I was late, D. So (B) would reverse cause and effect, and although (C) shows contrast, which does not fit. Memory tip: since can mean \"because\" as well as \"from a time\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): I _____ her birthday and I _____ how to make it up to her.|completely forgot/don't just know|forgot completely/don't just know|completely forgot/just don't know|forgot completely/just don't know": "Adverbs of manner like \"completely\" go before the main verb (completely forgot), not between the verb and its object. \"Just\" goes before \"don't know\". So C. Memory tip: never separate a verb from its object with an adverb (\"forgot completely her birthday\" is wrong).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Oloyede always sleeps like a baby, _____?|did he|doesn't he|could he|does he": "\"Sleeps\" is a positive present simple verb, so the tag uses does in the negative: doesn't he? (B). Did he and does he are not negative, and could he uses the wrong verb. Memory tip: positive statement → negative tag, same tense.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The employer, not the salesmen _____ responsible for the loss.|have been|was|will be|were": "When two subjects are joined by \"not\", the verb agrees with the first one: \"The employer… was responsible\", B. The salesmen are set aside by \"not\", so were (D) is wrong. Memory tip: X, not Y, + verb agreeing with X.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): It _____ so hard that all the cars have stopped moving.|rains|is raining|was raining|rained": "D is wrong: \"have stopped\" is present perfect: the cars are still stopped now, so the rain is happening now too: \"It is raining so hard that…\", B. \"Rained\" would need \"stopped\" or \"had stopped\". Memory tip: the present perfect result goes with a present cause; keep the tenses in step.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: flEE|rid|field|faith|skate": "The ee in flee is the long vowel /iː/. Field has the same /iː/ (spelt ie): B. Rid has short /ɪ/, and faith and skate have /eɪ/. Memory tip: /iː/ is spelt ee, ea, ie or e (see, sea, field, he).",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: wAIter|road|cite|flavour|hair": "The ai in waiter is the diphthong /eɪ/, as the a in flavour: C. Road has /əʊ/, cite /aɪ/, and hair /eə/. Memory tip: \"air\" (hair, fair) is /eə/, not /eɪ/; don't be fooled by the shared spelling.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: pAlm|lunch|blunt|ranch|florid": "The a in palm is the long /ɑː/ (the l is silent). In British RP ranch is /rɑːntʃ/: C. Lunch and blunt have /ʌ/, and florid has /ɒ/. Memory tip: palm, calm, half, ranch, dance and bath all have the long /ɑː/ in RP.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: THeme|then|the|their|theory": "The th in theme is the voiceless /θ/, as in theory: D. Then, the and their all have the voiced /ð/. Memory tip: grammar words (the, then, their, this) use voiced /ð/; content words like theme, theory and thick use /θ/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: PHantom|phew|party|physics|pew": "The ph in phantom is /f/, as in physics: C. The key gives C, but phew (/fjuː/) also begins with /f/, so A is equally right and both are marked right. Party and pew begin with /p/. Memory tip: ph is almost always /f/ (phone, photo, physics).",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: gaDGet|gable|jet|rider|spondee": "The dg in gadget is /dʒ/, the j sound in jet: B. Gable begins with hard /ɡ/, and rider and spondee have /d/. Memory tip: dge and dg spell /dʒ/ (bridge, judge, gadget).",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: weight|height|wedge|sleigh|eight": "Weight /weɪt/ rhymes with eight /eɪt/; the gh is silent in both: D. Height has /aɪ/, sleigh has no final /t/, and wedge has /e/. Memory tip: for rhyme, match the last vowel AND everything after it.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: ever|never|heavier|favour|fever": "Ever /ˈevə/ and never /ˈnevə/ share the stressed vowel and the ending: A. Fever has /iː/, favour /eɪ/, and heavier has an extra syllable. Memory tip: rhyming words share the sound from the stressed vowel to the end.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: gold|goal|gaol|goad|goat": "The key gives C, goad. But strictly no option rhymes with gold /ɡəʊld/, because none ends in /əʊld/: goal, goad and goat each lack part of it, and gaol is /dʒeɪl/. Goad is kept as the closest, with the same vowel and final d. Memory tip: a true rhyme for gold would be told, sold, old or bold.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): advantages|advanTAges|advantaGES|ADvantages|adVANtages": "Advantages is stressed on the second syllable: ad-VAN-ta-ges, D. The -es ending is never stressed, so B is wrong, and ADvantages (C) is a common error. Memory tip: adding a plural ending does not move the stress (adVANtage → adVANtages).",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): managerial|MAnagerial|manaGErial|maNAgerial|manageRIAL": "Words ending in -ial are stressed on the syllable just before the ending: ma-na-GE-ri-al, B. MAnagerial (A) wrongly keeps the stress of MAnager. Memory tip: -ial, -ian and -ion pull the stress to the syllable before them (manaGErial, muSIcian, educAtion).",
 "|Choose the option that has a DIFFERENT stress pattern from the others.|guilty|guitar|confuse|relief": "Guilty is stressed on the first syllable (GUIL-ty); guiTAR, conFUSE and reLIEF are all stressed on the second: A. The paper labels the first two options both \"(a)\". Memory tip: say each word with a clap on the strong syllable; the odd one out stands alone.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: Barkindo's wallet is made of PLASTIC.|Whose wallet is made of plastic?|Is the wallet made of leather?|Is Barkindo's shirt made of plastic?|Is Barkindo's wallet made with plastic?": "Stress on PLASTIC corrects the material: it is plastic, not leather. So it answers \"Is the wallet made of leather?\", B. A would need stress on Barkindo's, and C on wallet. Memory tip: the stressed word is the part being corrected or asked about.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: Tolu saw Tade's father YESTERDAY.|Who did Tade's father see yesterday?|Did Tolu see Tade's mother yesterday?|Was Tade's father seen today?|Who saw Tade's father yesterday?": "Stressing YESTERDAY corrects the time, so the sentence answers a question that gets the day wrong: \"Was Tade's father seen today?\": C. B would be answered by stressing FATHER, and D by stressing TOLU. Memory tip: the stressed word replaces the wrong detail in the question.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: The SOLDIER dances in his new shoes.|What does the soldier do in his new shoes?|Who dances in his new shoes?|Does the soldier dance in his old shoes?|In whose new shoes does the soldier dance?": "Stress on SOLDIER tells us who dances: it answers \"Who dances in his new shoes?\", B. A would need stress on dances, and C on new. Memory tip: stress on the subject answers a \"who\" question.",
 "eng-2016-p1|From the passage, it can be deduced that|prevailing circumstances push a warring nation to sue for peace|all nations prefer the military option|all nations adopt the peaceful approach|conflicts are noted for facilitating opportunities": "The key gives D: the passage says force can be used to \"create a situation in which diplomatic negotiations can occur\", so conflict can open up an opportunity for talks. A comes from the same sentence (circumstances push a warring side to sue for peace), so both A and D are marked right. B and C fail because of \"all nations\": the passage stresses \"many different approaches\". Memory tip: options with \"all\" or \"only\" are usually traps in inference questions.",
 "eng-2016-p1|Which of the following statements can be inferred from the passage?|The approach to employ in conflict management depends on the state of affairs|There is a general disagreement among scholars on conflict management|Only one conflict management approach can be applied in all situations|All conflict management approaches can be applied in all situations": "The passage says each approach \"may have utility in particular circumstances\" and that scholars study \"in what situation different approaches can be applied\". So the approach depends on the state of affairs: A. C and D say one or all approaches fit every situation, the opposite of the passage, and B invents a disagreement. Memory tip: \"circumstances\" in the passage = \"state of affairs\" in the option.",
 "eng-2016-p1|The expression 'third party' as used in the passage means|conformist|intruder|politician|mediator": "The passage pairs \"a third party\" with \"mediation\": a conflict can be managed \"through 'mediation', that is, with the help of a third party\". So the third party is a mediator: D. An intruder (B) pushes in uninvited, and a conformist (A) simply goes along with others. Memory tip: a third party stands between the two sides, like a referee.",
 "eng-2016-p1|According to the passage, the different approaches to conflict management are|misleading|uniform|pernicious|fundamental": "The passage treats the approaches (peaceful and military, negotiation and mediation) as the basic, essential ways of handling any conflict, each with \"utility in particular circumstances\". Fundamental means basic and essential: D. They are not uniform (B), since they differ, and nothing calls them misleading or pernicious (harmful). Memory tip: when the writer is neutral and positive, avoid negative options like misleading and pernicious.",
 "eng-2016-p1|The word 'utility', as used in the passage, means|usefulness|attitude|difficulty|management": "\"Each of which may have utility in particular circumstances\" means each approach may be useful in certain situations. Utility means usefulness: A. D (management) is just a word borrowed from the passage. Memory tip: think of \"utility vehicle\", a vehicle built to be useful.",
 "eng-2016-p2|From the passage, one can imply that|native languages are difficult to use as languages of instruction|the language of instruction is ideally one's own language|no society conducts its education in a foreign language|no foreign language should be taught in any society": "The writer says one's language is \"the most complete model of logical system that most of us ever learn\" and \"the very measure of what is intelligible\". So the ideal language for learning is one's own: B. C and D make absolute claims (\"no society\", \"no foreign language\") the passage never makes. Memory tip: an implied answer follows from the passage without adding extreme claims.",
 "eng-2016-p2|From the writer's point of view, one can conclude that|developing people of the world have not developed their languages|people of the same culture live in the same society|the only authority a society has is its language|language and culture are interwoven": "A culture's answers \"are couched in the language of that society and gain much of their authority from it\", and rejecting a father's language means rejecting his wisdom. So language and culture are interwoven: D. C overstates (\"the only authority\"), and A is never said. Memory tip: if the passage keeps linking two things, the conclusion is usually that they are bound together.",
 "eng-2016-p2|What is the symbolic function of a native language according to the passage?|It promotes understanding within the group|It enables the society to understand itself the more|It alienates progress within the society and beyond|It distinguishes that society from others": "The last paragraph says language has \"a symbolic function in that it stands for the distinctness and individuality of that group as against other groups\". It distinguishes that society from others: D. A and B sound positive but describe other functions, and C (alienates progress) is never mentioned. Memory tip: find the exact word from the question (\"symbolic\") in the passage and read that sentence.",
 "eng-2016-p2|The word 'couched' as used in the passage means|expressed|arranged|itemized|modified": "\"These answers are couched in the language of that society\" means the answers are expressed in that language. To couch something in certain words is to express it in them: A. Arranged (B) and itemized (C) are about order, not wording. Memory tip: couch = put into words.",
 "eng-2016-p2|Which of the following can be inferred from the passage?|The world around us is the world of people who speak the same language|We know more about the world around us if our language is not written|Our native language is as important as the world around us|The world is interpreted to us only in our native language": "Language is \"the very measure of what is intelligible in the world around us\": we understand the world through our native language. That supports D. B contradicts the passage (writing is not discussed), and C makes a comparison the writer never makes. Memory tip: \"measure of what is intelligible\" means the tool by which we make sense of things.",
 "eng-2016-p3|Gap 11: These parents had pre-conceived notions of _____ professions.|prestigious|insignificant|inferior|debased": "Parents pushed children into careers they already admired, ignoring the children's interests, so the professions were highly respected: prestigious, A. Insignificant, inferior and debased all mean low in status, and parents don't push children towards low-status jobs. Memory tip: prestige = high status and respect.",
 "eng-2016-p3|Gap 12: Students' career decisions were also _____ by the type of secondary schools they attended.|hampered|influenced|subdue|rejected": "The sentence says students' career choices were shaped by the type of school they attended. Influenced fits: B. Hampered (A) means held back, which is too narrow, and subdue (C) is the wrong form after \"were\". Memory tip: \"decisions were influenced by\" is a natural collocation.",
 "eng-2016-p3|Gap 13: Competition was _____ among these schools.|minimal|high|low|carve an": "The next sentence says each school \"strived to carve an image for herself by excelling\", which shows strong rivalry. So competition was high: B. Minimal and low say the opposite. (Option D, \"carve an\", is a printing slip in the paper.) Memory tip: use the next sentence as evidence for the gap.",
 "eng-2016-p3|Gap 14: Each strived to carve an _____ for herself by excelling in sports or academics.|image|attitude|effort|avenue": "Schools excelled in sports or academics to build a reputation, i.e. to carve an image for themselves: A. \"Carve an avenue\" or \"carve an effort\" are not natural English, and attitude doesn't fit \"for herself by excelling\". Memory tip: carve an image or carve a niche = build a reputation or place.",
 "eng-2016-p3|Gap 15: Junior students tried to _____ the career choices of their seniors.|emulate|appreciate|reject|denounce": "Juniors looked up to their seniors and copied their career choices. To emulate is to copy someone you admire: A. Reject and denounce are the opposite, and appreciate is too weak. Memory tip: emulate = imitate in order to equal.",
 "eng-2016-p3|Gap 16: This spirit of rivalry among mission and private schools _____ with government take-over of schools.|waned|heightened|intensified|increased": "Once government took over the schools, private and mission schools no longer competed, so the rivalry waned (died down): A. B, C and D all mean grew stronger, which doesn't follow from a takeover that removed the competing owners. Memory tip: wax = grow, wane = shrink (like the moon).",
 "eng-2016-p3|Gap 17: One such problem is the _____ shortage of professional career development officers.|acute|mild|slow|average": "\"These few officers\" in the next sentence shows the shortage is severe. Acute means very serious: A. Mild (B) and average (D) undersell it, and \"slow shortage\" (C) is not a collocation. Memory tip: we speak of an acute shortage, acute pain, an acute problem.",
 "eng-2016-p3|Gap 18: These few officers are _____ given the opportunity to practice.|usually|seldom|often|frequently": "The passage is listing problems, so the few officers are rarely given the chance to practise: seldom, B. Usually, often and frequently would describe a good situation, not a problem. Memory tip: check whether the paragraph is about problems or successes before choosing.",
 "eng-2016-p3|Gap 19: If the government of Nigeria has _____ a guidance and counselling policy, it must be pointed out that emphasis is still at the secondary level of education.|muffled|lauded|mumbled|enunciated": "A government enunciates a policy, that is, states it clearly and formally: D. A is wrong: \"muffled\" means deadened or made quieter (a muffled cry), which makes no sense with a policy. Mumbled (C) is speaking unclearly, and lauded (B) means praised. Memory tip: governments enunciate, formulate or adopt policies.",
 "eng-2016-p3|Gap 20: This situation _____ the current view that career development should start at the pre-primary level and continue till adulthood.|approves|rejects|contradicts|verifies": "Schools focus on the secondary level, yet the current view is that career development should start at pre-primary level. The situation goes against that view: contradicts, C. Approves and verifies (A, D) mean the opposite, and rejects (B) is something people do, not situations. Memory tip: \"it must be pointed out that ... still\" signals a contrast.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Mr. Rufus bore the brunt in the last promotion examination woefully.|Mr. Rufus failed the examination woefully|Mr. Rufus excelled in the examination|Mr. Rufus was excited about the examination|Mr. Rufus toyed with the examination": "To bear the brunt of something is to take the worst of it, and \"woefully\" means very badly. So Mr. Rufus failed badly: A. B and C turn the meaning positive, and D (toyed with) means did not take it seriously. Memory tip: bear the brunt = suffer the worst part.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: My friend is well off nowadays.|My friend is financially sound|My friend is well known to people|My friend is now well|My friend has lost focus": "To be well off is to have plenty of money, to be financially comfortable: A. C is the trap: \"well\" here does not mean healthy. B confuses it with \"well known\". Memory tip: well off = well supplied with money; badly off = poor.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The retired army officer did a yeoman's service to the nation.|His activity brought disrepute to the nation|His service to the nation was not exemplary|He was showered with the responsibility of the yeoman|He was very loyal to the nation": "A yeoman's service is long, faithful, valuable work. So the officer was very loyal to the nation: D. B is wrong: it says his service was \"not exemplary\", the opposite of the idiom. C takes \"yeoman\" literally. Memory tip: yeoman's service = good, loyal, hard work.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: He suddenly found himself in the thick of the situation.|He did not agree with the situation|He used his experience to settle the situation|He saved himself out of the situation|He found himself deeply involved in the situation": "To be in the thick of something is to be in the middle of it, where the action is. So he found himself deeply involved in the situation: D. B and C describe what he might do next, and A (disagreeing) is not in the idiom at all. Memory tip: \"the thick of the fight\" is where the fighting is heaviest.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Olu and Ado are about to come to blows.|They are about to agree with each other|They are about to accept other people's opinion|They are about to hit each other|They are about to disagree with other people's opinion": "To come to blows is to begin fighting physically, so they are about to hit each other: C. D is the trap: they may disagree, but \"blows\" means punches. Memory tip: blows = punches.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: By refusing the offer, he has just cut down his own throat.|He had made a big mistake|He has made the right decision|He has given up on his idea|He has used the knife on his throat": "To cut one's own throat is to do something that harms oneself. By refusing the offer, he has made a big mistake: A. B says the opposite, and D takes the idiom literally. Memory tip: idioms are almost never literal, so the literal option is usually wrong.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: These job cuts are just the thin end of the wedge.|Job cuts are very rampant|Job cuts are inconclusive|Job cuts are the beginning of unpleasant situations|Job cuts are the beginning of healthy rivalries": "The thin end of the wedge is a small, minor beginning that will lead to something bigger and worse. So the job cuts are the beginning of unpleasant situations: C. D is wrong because the idiom always signals something bad. Memory tip: a wedge starts thin, but as it is driven in it splits the wood wide open.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Jumai will do well if she was given a fair crack of the whip.|She would do well if she was given a fair whip|She would excel if she used the whip well|She would do well if she was given equal opportunity|She would do well if she was made to cry": "A fair crack of the whip is a fair chance, an equal opportunity: C. A, B and D all take \"whip\" literally. Memory tip: fair crack of the whip = fair go = fair chance.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Her death was a bolt from the blue to us.|Her death bolted us together|Her death cause confusion|Her death scaled our hopes|Her death was unexpected": "A bolt from the blue is something completely unexpected, like lightning from a clear sky. So her death was unexpected: D. A plays on \"bolt\", and C on hopes. Memory tip: bolt from the blue = total surprise.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The students were nosing around.|They were roaming about|They were searching for information|They were playing around|They were disseminating information": "To nose around (or nose about) is to pry, searching for information: B. A is the trap: roaming about only means wandering, without the searching. D is the reverse (spreading information). Memory tip: a nosy person sticks their nose into other people's affairs.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: It is important not to let mistakes BLIGHT your life.|damage|impart|improve|ruin": "To blight is to spoil or damage something. Its opposite is to improve: C. Damage and ruin (A, D) are synonyms, the trap in an \"opposite\" question. Memory tip: blight on crops destroys them.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The film was shot in DECREPIT police station.|an improved|a damaged|an impoverished|a dilapidated": "Decrepit means old, worn out and in poor condition. The opposite is improved: A. Damaged and dilapidated (B, D) are synonyms, and impoverished (C) is about money. Memory tip: in opposite questions, first cross out the options that mean the same.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Jumoke is fond of CARPING her sister.|praising|joking with|complaining about|faulting": "To carp is to keep finding fault or complaining. Its opposite is praising: A. Complaining about and faulting (C, D) are synonyms. Memory tip: a carping critic is always picking holes.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: A cup of coffee INVIGORATED him.|destroyed|energized|angered|debilitated": "Invigorated means filled with energy and strength. Debilitated means weakened: D. Energized (B) is a synonym, and destroyed (A) goes too far. Memory tip: vigour = energy, so in-vigor-ated = given energy.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: It is LUDICROUS to suggest that she was a government agent.|odd|exciting|farcial|sensible": "Ludicrous means absurd, ridiculous. The opposite is sensible: D. Farcial (farcical) and odd (A, C) are near-synonyms. (The paper misprints \"It\" as \"If\".) Memory tip: ludicrous is close to ridiculous.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The idea makes me CRINGE.|quiver|tremble|resolute|writhe": "To cringe is to shrink back in fear or embarrassment. Resolute means firm and fearless, the opposite attitude: C. Quiver, tremble and writhe (A, B, D) all describe fearful or pained movement, so they are close to cringe. Memory tip: in opposite questions, three options often go together; the odd one out is the answer.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: His book was one of those books that GALVANIZED democracy.|stimulated|encouraged|dissuaded|promoted.": "To galvanize is to shock or stir into action, to stimulate. The opposite is to dissuade (discourage): C. Stimulated, encouraged and promoted are synonyms. Memory tip: galvanize comes from Galvani, who made dead frogs' legs twitch with electricity.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He walks in a GAWKY way.|suitable|clumsy|lumbering|graceful": "Gawky means awkward and clumsy in movement. The opposite is graceful: D. Clumsy and lumbering (B, C) are synonyms. Memory tip: a gawky teenager is all elbows and knees.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The nation is in a period of economic FLUX.|modification|change|fluidity|stability": "Flux is constant change. The opposite is stability: D. Modification, change and fluidity all describe change, so they are synonyms. Memory tip: flux and fluid both come from Latin for \"flow\".",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Our son was highly active and EXUBERANT.|subdued|fulsome|lively|bright": "Exuberant means full of energy, excitement and cheerfulness. The opposite is subdued (quiet, restrained): A. Lively and bright are synonyms, and fulsome means excessive (often of praise). Memory tip: exuberant = bubbling over.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Mr. Ojo has FICKLE character.|a genuine|a stable|an imperfect|an unpredictable": "A fickle person keeps changing their mind or loyalties, so their character is unpredictable: D. Stable (B) is the opposite, and imperfect (C) is too general. Memory tip: fickle = changeable, like fickle weather.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He picked up the phone expecting GRUFF voice.|gracious|musical|husky|polite": "A gruff voice is low, rough and unfriendly. Husky (hoarse, low) is closest: C. Gracious and polite (A, D) are the opposite in manner, and musical doesn't fit. Memory tip: gruff = rough + grumpy.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: It has been a FRAUGHT day.|difficult|pleasant|wonderful|solemn": "A fraught day is full of anxiety, stress and problems, so it is difficult: A. Pleasant and wonderful are opposites, and solemn means serious, not stressful. Memory tip: fraught = full of worry (\"fraught with danger\").",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: She SCOWLED furiously at his back as he walked away.|whispered|grimaced|screeched|screamed": "To scowl is to frown angrily. To grimace is to twist one's face in anger, disgust or pain, the closest meaning: B. Whispered, screeched and screamed (A, C, D) are sounds; a scowl is a facial expression. Memory tip: scowl = angry face, not angry voice.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The use of this equipment should OBVIATE the problem.|prevent|improve|promote|worsen": "To obviate a problem is to remove it or prevent it from arising: A. Promote and worsen (C, D) say the opposite, and improve (B) does not fit \"a problem\". Memory tip: obviate = make unnecessary, get out of the way.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Olisa HOODWINKED the men into buying and selling.|grouped|lured|deluded|drove": "To hoodwink is to deceive or trick someone: deluded, C. Lured (B) is the trap; luring is tempting someone with a promise, but hoodwinking stresses fooling them with lies. Memory tip: to hoodwink is to pull a hood over someone's eyes so they can't see the truth.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: There was an infectious GAIETY in the children's character.|exhilaration|depression|moodiness|desperation": "Gaiety is lively cheerfulness and fun. Exhilaration (a feeling of great happiness and excitement) is closest: A. Depression, moodiness and desperation are all opposites. Memory tip: gaiety comes from gay in its old sense, \"merry\".",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The room was filled with the PUNGENT smell of tobacco.|placid|mild|illegal|acrid": "A pungent smell is strong and sharp, stinging the nose. Acrid means sharp and bitter (of a smell or taste): D. Mild (B) is the opposite, and placid means calm. Memory tip: pungent and acrid both describe smoke.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He lifted his hand in a LANGUID manner.|a sheepish|a sluggish|a friendly|an energetic": "Languid means slow, weak and lacking energy. Sluggish is closest: B. Energetic (D) is the opposite, and sheepish means embarrassed. Memory tip: languid is related to languish (to grow weak).",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The lawmaker HECKLED the chairman of the panel during their last meeting.|cheered up|applauded|shouted down|restrained": "To heckle is to interrupt a speaker with rude shouts and challenges, i.e. to shout them down: C. Cheered up and applauded are the opposite, and restrained means held back. Memory tip: hecklers are the noisy interrupters in a crowd.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The book will sell in _____.|hundreds of thousands|a hundred thousand|hundreds of thousand|hundred thousands": "When hundreds and thousands are vague large numbers, both are plural: hundreds of thousands, A. C is wrong: \"hundreds of thousand\" is ungrammatical: after \"hundreds of\" the noun must be plural, as in \"hundreds of people\". B and D are wrong forms too. Memory tip: exact number, no -s (two hundred thousand); vague number, -s (hundreds of thousands).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): We must be ready to cater for _____.|the poors and needy|poor and needs|the poor and needy|poors and needs": "\"The poor\" and \"the needy\" are adjectives used as nouns for a whole group of people. They take \"the\" and never add -s: the poor and needy, C. A and D wrongly add -s, and B uses \"needs\" (a noun meaning requirements). Memory tip: the rich, the poor, the blind, the needy: no -s.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): After a three-day meeting, two presidents issued a _____ calling for peace.|bulletin|memorandum|communique|directive": "The official statement released at the end of a meeting between heads of state is a communiqué: C. B is wrong: a memorandum is an internal note or a record of agreed terms, not a public call for peace. A bulletin is a short news report, and a directive is an order. Memory tip: summit ends, communiqué issued.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Timi has gone to Lagos and he will be back in _____ time.|two's week|two week's|two weeks|two weeks'": "For a plural time expression, the apostrophe comes after the s: two weeks' time, D. A and B put the apostrophe in the wrong place, and C has none. Memory tip: one day's time, two days' time; singular 's, plural s'.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The students have not come back from the school farm, _____?|hadn't they|have they|didn't they|isn't it": "A negative statement takes a positive question tag with the same helping verb: \"have not ... have they?\": B. A and C change the auxiliary, and D (\"isn't it\") is a common Nigerian English error. Memory tip: same auxiliary, opposite polarity.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): 'Do not forget to reply _____' said the teacher.|my letter|for my letter|of my letter|to my letter": "The verb reply takes the preposition to: reply to a letter, D. \"Reply my letter\" (A) is a common error. Memory tip: answer a letter, but reply to a letter.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): You will need _____ to help the investigations.|this information|this informations|these information|these informations": "Information is an uncountable noun: no plural -s, and it takes this, not these. So \"this information\", A. B, C and D each have a plural form or plural determiner. Memory tip: information, advice, equipment, luggage: never with -s.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Ada did not find _____ in the house.|anyone|some one|somebody|someone": "Negative sentences normally take any- words: \"did not find anyone\", A. Somebody and someone (C, D) are used in positive statements, and \"some one\" (B) is a different meaning (one particular person). Memory tip: not ... any; positive ... some.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The _____ discouraged me from reading it.|story length|length of the story|stories length|story's length": "For things, the standard choice is the \"of\" structure: the length of the story, B. \"Story length\" (A) and \"stories length\" (C) are wrong forms; \"story's length\" (D) is possible in casual use, but the 's possessive is mainly for people and animals, which is what JAMB tests. Memory tip: the boy's bag, but the leg of the table.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Ado said that he _____|want very much to travel to Lagos|has been wanting very much to travel to Lagos|wanted very much to travel to Lagos|will want very much to travel to Lagos": "After a past reporting verb (\"said\"), reported speech moves back one tense: \"I want\" becomes \"he wanted\", C. A, B and D keep present or future forms. (The paper labels this option \"(d)\" by mistake; it is the third option.) Memory tip: said + backshift.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): He take _____ his father both in appearance and his manner.|on|from|after|upon": "To take after someone is to resemble a parent or older relative in looks or character: after, C. Take on (A) means accept work or challenge someone, and take from (B) means remove. (The paper prints \"take\" for \"takes\".) Memory tip: you take after your father.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): _____ rich has its own problem.|Being|Been|On being|On been": "The subject of the sentence is a gerund phrase: \"Being rich has its own problem\", A. Been (B) is a past participle and needs an auxiliary, and C and D add an unneeded \"on\". Memory tip: an -ing word can act as a noun and be the subject.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The doctor listened to my heartbeat with a _____.|cardiograph|stethoscope|chromometer|microscope": "A stethoscope is the instrument a doctor puts to the chest to listen to the heartbeat: B. A cardiograph records the heart's activity on a machine, and a microscope magnifies tiny things. Memory tip: stetho- means chest.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): With strong determination, Adamu was able to _____ the problem.|surmount|surpass|defend|know": "To surmount a problem is to overcome it: A. Surpass (B) means to do better than, which fits a target, not a problem. Memory tip: surmount = climb over, like a mountain.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): It is impossible to say whether _____ Ola and her sister will be offered jobs by the firm.|both|either|each|neither": "\"And\" pairs with \"both\": both Ola and her sister, A. Either pairs with or, neither with nor, and \"each\" can't be followed by \"X and Y\". Memory tip: both...and, either...or, neither...nor.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): He decided to wait for the bus because he had _____.|many luggages|plenty luggages|a lot of luggage|too many luggage": "Luggage is uncountable: no -s, and it takes \"a lot of\" or \"much\", not \"many\". So \"a lot of luggage\", C. A, B and D all treat it as countable. Memory tip: luggage and baggage are never plural; say \"pieces of luggage\" to count.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): _____ lawyers enrolled at the Nigerian Bar last year _____ this year.|very many/than|same/as|more/than|as/much": "\"Than\" completes a comparison, so the first gap needs a comparative: more ... than, C. \"Very many ... than\" (A) has no comparative, \"same ... as\" needs \"the same\", and \"as ... much\" is not a pattern. Memory tip: comparative (more, less, -er) + than.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The officer _____ that he was not supposed to talk to the press on the matter.|could have known|shall know|should have known|will know": "\"Should have known\" criticizes a past failure: it was his duty to know, but he spoke anyway. C. \"Could have known\" (A) only says it was possible, and shall/will know (B, D) are future. Memory tip: should have + past participle = a past duty not done.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): He said, 'we shouldn't do it _____'.|should we|should be|didn't we|said he": "\"We shouldn't do it\" is negative, so the tag is positive with the same auxiliary: should we? A. C changes the auxiliary to did, and D isn't a tag. Memory tip: shouldn't ... should we?",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): After too much exercise, some people suffer from cramp which is caused by the _____ of the muscles.|contraction|restriction|conscription|relationship": "A cramp is a sudden, painful contraction (tightening) of a muscle: A. Restriction is too general, and conscription means compulsory military service. Memory tip: muscles contract and relax.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: repriEve|rich|police|queer|probity": "The ie in reprieve is the long /iː/, the same sound as the i in police: B. Rich and probity have the short /ɪ/, and queer has the diphthong /ɪə/. Memory tip: i in words from French often says /iː/: police, machine, routine.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: dOOR|food|cough|sure|board": "The oor in door is /ɔː/. Board has the same /ɔː/: D. Food has /uː/, cough /ɒ/, and sure has /ʊə/ in the RP model JAMB uses (/ʃʊə/). Memory tip: door, floor, board, more all share /ɔː/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: famOUS|soup|cook|pilot|colonel": "The -ous in famous is unstressed, so it is the weak vowel schwa /ə/ (FAY-məs). The o in pilot is also unstressed /ə/ (PIE-lət): C. Soup has /uː/, cook /ʊ/, and colonel /ɜː/ in its stressed syllable. Memory tip: unstressed vowels are usually /ə/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: CHoice|schedule|teach|leisure|machine": "The ch in choice is /tʃ/, as in teach: B. Schedule has /ʃ/ or /sk/, leisure has /ʒ/, and machine has /ʃ/. Memory tip: ch in words from French (machine, chef) is /ʃ/, and in words from Greek (school, chemist) is /k/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: Yet|bury|way|vessel|Europe": "The y in yet is the consonant /j/. Europe begins with the same /j/ sound (/ˈjʊərəp/), even though it is spelt with eu: D. Way begins with /w/. Memory tip: eu- and u- often start with /j/ (Europe, university, use).",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: taskED|bagged|floored|laughed|shouted": "After a voiceless sound (/k/ in task), -ed is pronounced /t/: tasked = /tɑːskt/. Laughed (/lɑːft/) also ends in /t/: C. Bagged and floored end in /d/ (after voiced sounds), and shouted ends in /ɪd/ (after t). Memory tip: -ed = /ɪd/ after t or d; /t/ after voiceless sounds; /d/ after voiced sounds.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: risen|prison|motion|portion|rising": "Risen (/ˈrɪzn/) rhymes with prison (/ˈprɪzn/): A. Rising has a different vowel (/aɪ/), and motion and portion have /ʃn/ endings. Memory tip: a rhyme needs the same stressed vowel and everything after it.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: terror|tenor|warrior|error|furrow": "Terror (/ˈterə/) rhymes with error (/ˈerə/): C. Tenor (/ˈtenə/) differs in its middle consonant, and warrior and furrow have different vowels. Memory tip: compare from the stressed vowel to the end of the word.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: crowd|crow|bard|aloud|crawl": "Crowd (/kraʊd/) rhymes with aloud (/əˈlaʊd/): C. Crow has /əʊ/, crawl /ɔː/, and bard /ɑː/. Memory tip: spelling can mislead, so crow looks like crowd but doesn't rhyme.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): inoffensive|inOffensive|inoffenSIVE|inoFENsive|INoffensive": "Inoffensive is stressed on the third syllable: in-of-FEN-sive, C. Adding the prefix in- does not move the stress of offensive (of-FEN-sive). Memory tip: prefixes like in-, un-, dis- rarely take the main stress.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): obligation|obliGAtion|Obligation|obligaTION|obLIgation": "Words ending in -ation are stressed on the syllable before -tion: ob-li-GA-tion, A. -tion itself is never stressed, so C is wrong. Memory tip: -ation words: stress on \"A\" (educAtion, informAtion).",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): disagreement|disagreement|disaGREEment|DISagreement|disagreeMENT": "Disagreement is stressed dis-a-GREE-ment, keeping the stress of agree: B. Prefixes like dis- and suffixes like -ment don't take the stress, so C and D are wrong; A is printed with no capitals at all. Memory tip: adding dis- or -ment doesn't move the stress of the root word.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: The car CRUSHED him.|Who did the car crush?|Did a car crush him?|What crushed him?|Did the car injure him?": "Stressing CRUSHED corrects the verb: the answer is to \"Did the car injure him?\" No, it crushed him: D. A would stress HIM, C would stress CAR, and B would stress THE CAR or a car. Memory tip: the stressed word replaces the wrong word in the question.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: Lamide is VERY hostile?|Is Lamide so hostile?|Is Lamide friendly|Was Lamide very hostile?|Who is hostile?": "Stressing VERY answers a question about how hostile Lamide is: \"Is Lamide so hostile?\" Yes, VERY hostile: A. D (Who is hostile?) would stress LAMIDE, and C changes the tense. Memory tip: stress on an intensifier answers \"how much?\"",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: My house is a BUNGALOW.|Is my house a terrace?|Was my house|Is my house the bungalow|Is his house a bungalow?": "Stressing BUNGALOW corrects the type of house: \"Is my house a terrace?\" No, it's a BUNGALOW: A. D would need stress on MY, and C changes \"a\" to \"the\". Memory tip: emphatic stress marks the piece of information being corrected.",
 "eng-2017-p1|The author seems to suggest that Asians and Africans resort to more drinking when|they are bereaved|there is improved living standard|national incomes are at their lowest ebb|the weather is conducive": "The passage says \"rising incomes were identified to have triggered more drinking in Africa and Asia\". Rising incomes mean people are better off, so the answer is an improved standard of living: B. C says the opposite (incomes at their lowest), and bereavement and weather are never mentioned. Memory tip: find the exact cause the passage gives, then look for the option that rewords it.",
 "eng-2017-p1|Which of the following is true according to the passage?|The outbreak of tuberculosis is caused by alcohol abuse|Most people in the world are oblivious of the harmful effect of alcohol abuse|The danger of alcohol abuse was highlighted in the developed countries|The abuse of alcohol can only be controlled in Africa and Asia": "The WHO's call comes \"at a time when governments all over the world need information and support to reduce alcohol consumption\", which suggests the danger is not widely understood: B. A is wrong because alcohol is compared with tuberculosis, not said to cause it; C is wrong because the WHO, not the developed countries, raised the alarm; D is never said. Memory tip: in \"true according to the passage\" questions, rule out options that twist a detail.",
 "eng-2017-p1|Which of the following best describes the tone of the writer in the passage?|Conciliatory|Alarming|Persuasive|Disillusioned": "The writer speaks of a \"grave danger\", 2.5 million deaths a year, and harm from road accidents, violence and disease. That language is meant to alarm: B. Conciliatory means trying to make peace, and disillusioned means disappointed, neither of which fits. Memory tip: judge tone from the strongest words the writer chooses.",
 "eng-2017-p1|The expression 'binge drinking', as used in the passage, means|drinking for a short period|drinking intermittently|drinking profusely|drinking against medical advice": "To binge is to overdo something in one go, so binge drinking is drinking heavily, i.e. profusely: C. A and B describe how long or how often, not how much, and D brings in doctors, who are never mentioned. Memory tip: binge = too much at one time.",
 "eng-2017-p1|From the passage, it can be deduced that developed countries' attitude towards alcohol control is|compromising|encouraging|proactive|lethargic": "Binge drinking is a problem in many developed countries, and the WHO \"decried the failure of most governments to institute strong alcohol control policies\". Failing to act shows a slow, half-hearted attitude: lethargic, D. Proactive (C) is the exact opposite. Memory tip: lethargic = sluggish, without energy; proactive = acting early.",
 "eng-2017-p2|Gap 6: He awarded the _____ to a well-known ___(7)___ ...|contract|project|assignment|business": "When a job is given out to a builder, the headmaster \"awards the contract\": A. A project is the whole undertaking, and you do not \"award an assignment\" or \"award a business\" to a professional firm. Memory tip: learn the collocation \"award a contract\".",
 "eng-2017-p2|Gap 7: He awarded the contract to a well-known _____ who drew up a ___(8)___ ...|architect|designer|engineer|planner": "The professional who designs a building and \"draws up\" its plan for approval is an architect: A. An engineer handles structures and services, and a designer or planner is too general. Memory tip: architect = buildings' designer and plan-drawer.",
 "eng-2017-p2|Gap 8: ... a well-known architect who drew up a _____ which was sent to the Town Planning Authority for approval.|plan|picture|chart|programme": "An architect draws up a building plan, and it is the plan that is sent to the Town Planning Authority: A. A picture or chart is not submitted for approval, and a programme is a schedule of events. Memory tip: \"draw up a plan\" is a fixed collocation.",
 "eng-2017-p2|Gap 9: The _____ which had been chosen was cleared ...|spot|locality|site|environment": "The land chosen for a building is its site: C. A spot is too small and casual, a locality is a whole neighbourhood, and the environment is the surroundings, not the plot itself. Memory tip: building site, construction site.",
 "eng-2017-p2|Gap 10: ... was cleared and _____ engaged to start work on laying the foundation ...|a worker|a mason|an artist|a plumber": "The person engaged to lay a foundation with blocks and concrete is a mason: B. \"A worker\" is too vague, an artist does not build, and a plumber fits pipes. Memory tip: mason = blocks, bricks and stone.",
 "eng-2017-p2|Gap 11: ... the most important process was pouring the _____.|marble|concrete|laterite|sand": "A foundation is made by pouring concrete into the trenches: B. Marble is a decorative stone, and laterite and sand are not \"poured\" as the main foundation material. Memory tip: you pour concrete; you lay blocks.",
 "eng-2017-p2|Gap 12: Very soon the building took _____ ...|position|shape|place|direction": "\"Take shape\" is a fixed expression meaning to begin to look like the finished thing: B. \"Take place\" means happen, and \"take position\" and \"take direction\" are not used of buildings. Memory tip: a building takes shape; an event takes place.",
 "eng-2017-p2|Gap 13: The _____ done by a set of very competent carpenters ...|woodwork|wood fixing|wood cutting|timber work": "The wooden parts of a building that carpenters make (doors, frames, roof timbers) are called the woodwork: A. \"Wood fixing\", \"wood cutting\" and \"timber work\" are not the standard word. The paper prints \"don\" for \"done\". Memory tip: carpenters do the woodwork.",
 "eng-2017-p2|Gap 14: ... exterior walls looked most striking after the second _____ had been put on.|spraying|washing|coating|covering": "Paint is put on in layers called coats, so the walls looked best \"after the second coating had been put on\": C. Spraying and washing are actions, not layers, and covering is too general. Memory tip: first coat, second coat.",
 "eng-2017-p2|Gap 15: The interior _____ was left to the competent domestic science teacher.|design|dressing|planning|decoration": "The key gives A, and \"interior design\" is a standard phrase for planning how the inside of a building looks. But \"interior decoration\" (D) is equally standard and suits a teacher just as well, so both are marked right. Dressing and planning do not collocate with interior here. Memory tip: interior design and interior decoration are both fixed phrases.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: You had better do as the doctor says and stay in bed.|It is better to stay in bed when listening to the doctor|It is wiser listen to the doctor than to stay in bed|It is wiser to wait in bed for the doctor|The wisest thing to do is to stay in bed": "\"You had better\" gives strong advice: the wise thing to do is to obey the doctor and stay in bed: D. A and C change the meaning (listening while in bed, waiting for the doctor), and B sets listening against staying in bed, though the sentence asks for both. Memory tip: had better = it is wisest to.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Dangana put the screw on his sister.|He pressurized and encouraged her|He hindered and discouraged his sister|He frightened and threatened her|He flogged and injured his sister": "To \"put the screw(s) on\" someone is to put pressure on them to do something, so Dangana pressurized his sister and pushed her on: A. B reverses the idea, and C and D are too violent: the idiom is about pressure, not threats or flogging. Memory tip: picture tightening a screw: pressure.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Mula was told that if he behaved badly he would go to bed without any supper.|Mula had not yet had his supper|Mula was behaving badly because he had no supper|Mula had had no super because he had behaved badly|Mula had had supper and had gone to bed": "Mula is warned that bad behaviour will cost him his supper. That warning only makes sense if he has not yet had supper: A. B and C turn a warning into something that has already happened, and D contradicts it. Memory tip: a threat points to the future, so the thing threatened has not happened yet.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Kunle was allowed to stew in his own juice.|He enjoyed the fruits of his labour|He suffered the consequence of his action|He associated with the rich in the society|He suffered untold hardship": "To \"stew in one's own juice\" is to be left to suffer the results of one's own actions: B. D (untold hardship) is too strong and leaves out that he caused it, and A is the opposite. Memory tip: \"own juice\" = the mess you made yourself.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Wouldn't it have been better to accept the referee's decision, Tunde?|Tunde did not agree with the referee|The referee's decision was fair|Tunde had to accept the referee's decision|The referee's decision was better than Tunde's": "\"Wouldn't it have been better to accept...?\" is a reproach: Tunde did not accept the decision, so he disagreed with the referee: A. B and C may be true, but the sentence does not state them; it only shows his disagreement. The paper's full stop before \"Tunde\" is a misprint for a comma. Memory tip: a \"wouldn't it have been better\" question implies the opposite was done.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Abiola was a very FEEBLE man.|selfish|weak|strong|tall": "Feeble means weak, so its opposite is strong: C. Weak (B) is a synonym, a common trap in antonym questions, and selfish and tall are unrelated. Memory tip: in an opposite question, first cross out the option that means the same.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The smell is OBNOXIOUS.|pleasant|loathsome|unpleasant|obvious": "Obnoxious means extremely unpleasant, so the opposite is pleasant: A. Loathsome and unpleasant are synonyms, and obvious only looks similar. Memory tip: don't be fooled by look-alike words (obnoxious/obvious).",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Aliyu was a natural SPENDTHRIFT when he was single.|philanthropist|miser|gambler|thrift collector": "A spendthrift wastes money carelessly; a miser hoards money and hates spending it: B. A philanthropist gives money away generously, which is closer in spirit to spending, not the opposite. Memory tip: spendthrift spends, miser saves.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The decision will have no ADVERSE effect on us.|inimical|detrimental|beneficial|lasting": "Adverse means harmful or unfavourable, so the opposite is beneficial: C. Inimical and detrimental are synonyms of adverse. Memory tip: adverse effect vs beneficial effect.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: I DESPISE the way she lived her life.|abhor|hate|admire|remember": "To despise is to look down on someone with contempt; the opposite is to admire: C. Abhor and hate are synonyms, and remember is unrelated. Memory tip: despise vs admire.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Tolu broke her leg because of her RESTIVE nature.|lively|relaxed|patient|unruly": "Restive means restless and hard to control, which is unruly: D. That fits the result, breaking her leg. Lively (A) is close but lacks the \"hard to control\" idea, and relaxed and patient are opposites. Memory tip: restive does not mean restful; it means restless.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The secretary's speech was interesting, though IMPROMPTU.|unprepared|improper|controversial|important": "An impromptu speech is made up on the spot without preparation: A. Improper only sounds similar, and \"though\" signals a contrast with \"interesting\", not anything controversial. Memory tip: impromptu = off the cuff.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The manager of the team is often MALIGNED by the supporters.|slandered|cherished|praised|hounded": "To malign someone is to speak ill of them unfairly, i.e. to slander them: A. Cherished and praised are opposites, and hounded means chased or harassed, not spoken against. Memory tip: mal- = bad, so malign = say bad things.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He has a COGENT reason for quitting the relationship.|a convincing|an important|a tentative|an unacceptable": "A cogent reason is clear, logical and convincing: A. An important reason need not be convincing, and tentative and unacceptable go the other way. Memory tip: a cogent argument persuades.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Mrs. Adio wasn't yet ATTUNED TO her baby's needs.|familiar with|ready with|free from|planning for": "To be attuned to something is to be aware of it and used to it, so she was not yet familiar with her baby's needs: A. Ready with and planning for miss the idea of awareness, and free from is the opposite. Memory tip: attuned = tuned in to.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Mrs. Audu wept when she discovered that she had lost her _____ rings.|very expensive wedding gold|gold wedding very expensive|gold very expensive wedding|very expensive gold wedding": "English puts adjectives in a fixed order: opinion (very expensive), then material (gold), then the purpose noun (wedding) right before the noun (rings). So \"very expensive gold wedding rings\": D. A puts wedding before gold, and B and C break the order completely. Memory tip: opinion, size, age, shape, colour, origin, material, purpose (+ noun).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): _____ apologize or face the consequences.|You are bound to|You'd better|You are better off to|You better": "\"You'd better\" (you had better) + bare infinitive gives a firm warning: \"You'd better apologize or face the consequences\": B. \"You better\" (D) drops \"had\" and is too informal, and \"better off to\" is not idiomatic. The paper prints \"apologized\" for \"apologize\" and labels this option (a) by mistake. Memory tip: had better + verb, no \"to\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Your driving was very dangerous; _____|you might even have been killed|you might have been killed|you might have been even killed|you even might have been killed": "\"You might have been killed\" is the correct form for a past danger that did not happen: B, the key's answer. \"You might even have been killed\" (A) is also correct, because \"even\" goes after the first helping verb, so both are marked right. C and D put \"even\" in the wrong place. Memory tip: put adverbs like even/never/always after the first auxiliary.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Although he tried, the journalist couldn't get _____ information.|several|an|many|much": "Information is an uncountable noun, so it takes much: \"couldn't get much information\": D. Many and several go with plural countables, and \"an\" with a single countable thing. Memory tip: much information, much advice, much news.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): This is my _____ car.|uncles|uncle|uncle's|uncles'": "One uncle owning the car takes apostrophe + s: my uncle's car: C. Uncles (A) is a plain plural with no possession, and uncle (B) needs the possessive. Uncles' (D) would mean several uncles share the car, which is unlikely after \"my... car\". Memory tip: one owner = 's; several owners = s'.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): _____ any rate, he tackled the problem courageously.|In|At|By|For": "\"At any rate\" is a fixed phrase meaning \"anyway\" or \"whatever happened\": B. \"In any case\" exists, but \"in any rate\", \"by any rate\" and \"for any rate\" do not. The paper leaves out \"the\" before \"problem\". Memory tip: at any rate = in any case.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Though Amina was very well paid, _____.|since she was always short of money|but she was always short of money|and when she always short of money|she was always short of money": "\"Though\" already joins the two clauses, so the second clause needs no conjunction: \"Though Amina was very well paid, she was always short of money\": D. Adding but (B) doubles the conjunction, and A and C are faulty. Memory tip: though/although and but never go together in one sentence.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): I could understand how she slept _____ the uproar.|through|on|off|among": "To \"sleep through\" a noise is to stay asleep the whole time it goes on: A. Sleep on means think about a decision overnight, and off and among do not fit. Memory tip: sleep through the noise, the storm, the alarm.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): My teacher asked me _____.|if the food is ready|if the food was ready|is the food ready|whether the food is ready": "In reported speech after a past verb (asked), the tense moves back and the question becomes a statement: \"asked me if the food was ready\": B. C keeps question order, and A and D keep the present \"is\". Memory tip: is becomes was after asked, said, told.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): No one was killed in the disaster though _____ were injuries.|their|they|there|these": "\"There were injuries\" uses there to say that something existed: C. Their shows possession and they is a pronoun for people, both wrong here. Memory tip: there is / there are introduce existence; their = belonging to them.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: acCOmpany|abolish|fail|sluggish|movement": "The underlined o in accompany is the short /ʌ/ (a-CUM-pa-ny), the same vowel as the u in sluggish: C. Abolish has /ɒ/, fail /eɪ/ and movement /uː/. Memory tip: o before m/n in words like come, some, money, accompany often sounds like \"u\".",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: pEAr|year|near|dear|there": "The ea in pear is the diphthong /eə/, the same sound as the ere in there: D. Year, near and dear all have /ɪə/, which is the trap: same spelling, different sound. Memory tip: pear, bear and wear sound like there and air; fear, near and dear do not.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: towN|ring|non|fling|erosion": "The n in town is /n/. The key gives D, erosion, whose final n is also /n/ (/ɪˈrəʊʒn/), but non (B) clearly has the same /n/ sound too, so both are marked right. Ring and fling are wrong: their ng is /ŋ/, a different sound. Memory tip: n followed by g in the same syllable usually becomes /ŋ/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: eNGlish|thin|think|edge|end": "The underlined ng in English is pronounced /ŋɡ/ (ING-glish), beginning with /ŋ/. In think the n before k is also /ŋ/: B. Thin and end have plain /n/, and edge has /dʒ/. Memory tip: n before k or g is usually /ŋ/: think, bank, finger, English.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: Machine|campaign|attain|sheen|fine": "Machine is pronounced ma-SHEEN, ending in /iːn/, which rhymes with sheen: C. Campaign and attain end in /eɪn/, and fine in /aɪn/. Memory tip: in French loanwords like machine and routine, i sounds like \"ee\".",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: Key|grey|quay|sit|prey": "Quay (a landing place for boats) is pronounced exactly like key, /kiː/: B. Grey and prey rhyme with each other, /eɪ/, the trap, and sit has a short /ɪ/. Memory tip: quay sounds like key, however odd the spelling.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): Prohibition|proHIbition|prohiBItion|PROhibition|prohibiTION": "Words ending in -tion are stressed on the syllable just before -tion: pro-hi-BI-tion: B. Memory tip: -tion, -sion and -cian pull the stress onto the syllable before them (education, decision, musician).",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): Aggregation|AGgregation|aggREgation|aggreGAtion|aggregaTION": "Again the -tion rule: the stress falls on the syllable just before it, ag-gre-GA-tion: C. Note that \"aggregate\" alone is stressed on the first syllable, but adding -tion moves it. Memory tip: stress the syllable before -tion.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: Amina attends a COMMERCIAL college.|Does Kunle attend a commercial college?|Has Amina graduated from a commercial college?|Does Amina attend a technical college?|Does Amina attend a commercial school?": "Stressing COMMERCIAL corrects the kind of college, so the sentence answers a question that names a different kind: \"Does Amina attend a technical college?\": C. A would need AMINA stressed, and D would need COLLEGE stressed. Memory tip: the stressed word replaces the wrong word in the question.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: HIS wife works in a bank.|Where does his wife work?|Does his wife play in a bank?|Does his concubine work in a bank?|Does my wife work in a bank?": "Emphatic stress corrects the stressed word. Stressing HIS says it is his wife, not someone else's, so it answers \"Does my wife work in a bank?\": D. C is the trap: it would be answered by stressing WIFE. A asks where, which would stress BANK. Memory tip: ask what the stressed word corrects, then find the question that got that word wrong.",
 "eng-2018-p1|The writer had many animals in her bed because she wanted to _____|lure them to sleep|feed them at night|keep them continuously warm at night|sleep in one position with them": "The writer's opening line names the main problem: keeping baby animals \"warm enough at nights\". Hot water bottles go cold, so the simplest way is to take the babies into bed. That is to keep them continuously warm at night: C. Feeding (B) is a separate task, and sleeping in one position (D) is a result of having them in the bed, not the reason. Memory tip: a \"because\" question is answered by the problem the writer is solving.",
 "eng-2018-p1|Taking the baby animals into bed is a substitute for|hot water bottles|considerable temperature|maternal warmth and shelter|avoiding to crush them at night": "In the wild, the baby \"clings to the dense fur of the mother and obtains warmth and shelter in that way\". The writer's bed replaces that mother: C. The trap is A: hot water bottles were the first substitute for the mother, and they failed; the bed does not replace the bottles' job but the mother's. Memory tip: ask what the thing replaces in nature, not what it replaced last.",
 "eng-2018-p1|One of these is true about the passage.|the baby animals grow cold very easily|the writer only catered for a baby animals|the writer only shared her bed with baby animals|the animals take water to keep them warm": "The creatures that shared the writer's bed were all young ones she was rearing (mongooses, baby monkeys, a squirrel, a young chimpanzee), so C fits. A is the trap: \"They grow cold very easily\" refers to the hot water bottles, not the animals. B is never said, and D is invented. Memory tip: always check what a pronoun such as \"they\" refers to before trusting an option built on it.",
 "eng-2018-p1|Why did the author lie about the charging jaguar?|To protect the erring animal|To avoid embarrassment|That a baby animal could be harmful under one's care is far-fetched|Only charging jaguar would have done such thing": "The writer says, \"Nobody would believe me if I told them it was really a baby mongoose.\" The truth (a tiny animal in her care gave her a big scar) sounds too far-fetched, so she blames a jaguar: C. B is tempting, but the passage gives disbelief, not shame, as the reason. A and D are never suggested. Memory tip: when the passage states a reason in so many words, choose the option that matches it.",
 "eng-2018-p1|By profession, the writer is most likely a|Rancher|Zoologist|Veterinarian|Naturalist": "The writer rears young wild animals (mongooses, monkeys, a squirrel, a chimpanzee), even on a camp-bed in the tropics. That is the work of a zoologist (B) or a naturalist (D), and both are marked right. The key gives A, but a rancher raises cattle or horses on a ranch, which has nothing to do with this passage. A vet (C) treats sick animals rather than rearing them. Memory tip: link the activities in the passage to the job that does them.",
 "eng-2018-p2|Gap 6: …industrialization which means industrial _____ is springing up in the developing countries…|development|fund|forces|investment": "Industrialization simply means industrial development, and the passage goes on to speak of \"the development\" and \"such development\": A. Fund, forces and investment do not define industrialization. Memory tip: in a cloze, look ahead for the word the passage repeats.",
 "eng-2018-p2|Gap 7: …countries which are struggling to _____ their fortune…|supply|improve|balance|decrease": "Developing countries are \"struggling to\" make things better, so they struggle to improve their fortune: B. Decrease is the opposite, and supply or balance do not go with \"fortune\". Memory tip: \"struggle to\" is followed by a positive goal.",
 "eng-2018-p2|Gap 8: …struggling to improve their fortune and _____.|productivity|production|condition|service": "\"Improve their fortune and condition\" pairs two nouns for a nation's state of life: C. Productivity and production are things you increase, and \"improve their service\" misses the meaning of people's situation. Memory tip: choose the word that pairs naturally with the noun before \"and\".",
 "eng-2018-p2|Gap 9: It involves such things as the provision of _____ station producing electricity…|power|work|energy|force": "A station that produces electricity is a power station, a fixed expression: A. \"Energy station\" and \"force station\" are not English, and \"work station\" means a desk with a computer. Memory tip: learn compound nouns as single units: power station, bus station, filling station.",
 "eng-2018-p2|Gap 10: …station producing electricity for _____ and the construction of irrigation works.|companies|warehouse|works|factories": "Power stations supply electricity for companies: A, the key's answer. Factories (D) fits equally well, since industry needs power for its factories, so both are marked right. Warehouse (B) is singular with no \"a\", and \"works\" (C) clashes with \"irrigation works\" later in the same sentence. Memory tip: when two options both fit the sense and grammar, check which ones the sentence has already ruled out.",
 "eng-2018-p2|Gap 11: The development can improve production in any _____ by means of large capital investments.|field|area|way|work": "\"Improve production in any area\" means in any sector of the economy: B, the key's answer. \"In any field\" (A) means the same thing here, so both are marked right. \"In any way\" and \"in any work\" do not fit. Memory tip: area and field are near-synonyms when they mean a branch of activity.",
 "eng-2018-p2|Gap 12: …by means of large _____ investments.|huge|capital|premium|fund": "Money spent on things like power stations and irrigation is capital investment: B. \"Large huge investments\" (A) says the same thing twice. Premium and fund do not combine with \"investments\" this way. Memory tip: capital = money used to build things that produce wealth.",
 "eng-2018-p2|Gap 13: The only source of _____ for such development is the government itself.|finance|interest|demand|power": "The paragraph is about money (investments, loans, interest), so the government is the only source of finance: A. Interest, demand and power do not fit \"source of … for such development\". Memory tip: follow the topic of the paragraph; here it is money.",
 "eng-2018-p2|Gap 14: An individual investment _____ are small and foreign loan is not easy to come by.|interest|cash|capital|capabilities": "The verb is \"are\", so the gap needs a plural noun: individual investment capabilities (what individuals can afford to invest) are small: D. Interest, cash and capital are singular here. Memory tip: let the verb after the gap tell you whether the noun is singular or plural.",
 "eng-2018-p2|Gap 15: _____ on foreign loans are quite high.|Interest charges|Interest|Cash|Cash balances": "What is high on loans is the interest charged, and the plural \"are\" needs \"interest charges\": A. B (\"Interest … are\") breaks the agreement, and cash or cash balances are not charged on loans. Memory tip: agreement plus meaning together will usually leave only one option.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The protesters were urged to keep a sense of proportion.|They should not go beyond the college gate with their protest|They should divide into groups for a good protest|They should do what is important in the protest|They should reduce the number in the group": "A sense of proportion is the ability to judge what really matters and not to overreact. The protesters were told to focus on what is important: C. The other options take \"proportion\" as being about size, numbers or distance. Memory tip: keep a sense of proportion = don't make a small thing bigger than it is.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Tolu lost her nerves when Ada appeared.|Tolu became sad on seeing Ada|Tolu lost control of herself on seeing Ada|Tolu ran away on seeing Ada|Tolu became worried on seeing Ada": "To lose your nerve is to lose your courage and become anxious or frightened. So Tolu became worried (nervous) on seeing Ada: D. B is the trap: losing control of oneself is closer to losing one's temper. Running away (C) may follow fear but is not what the idiom says. Memory tip: lose your nerve = lose courage; lose your temper = lose control.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Bado got a job by paying hush money.|Bado was well paid for the job|Bado gave a bribe to get a job|Bado stole money to pay for the job|Bado threatened them harshly": "Hush money is money paid secretly so that something wrong stays hidden, i.e. a bribe: B. It is not wages (A), not stolen money (C), and nothing is said about threats (D). Memory tip: hush = keep quiet, so hush money buys silence or favours.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: There are many bookshops close at hand.|There are many closed bookshops|The bookshop can be reached easily|The book shop can be reached with difficulty|The bookshops are well stocked": "Close at hand means near and easy to reach: B. A confuses \"close\" (near) with \"closed\" (shut), and C says the opposite. Memory tip: \"at hand\" = within reach.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: He took the high road in his campaigns.|He took his campaigns to highbrow areas|He took a bold step in his campaigns|He took a positive action in his campaigns|He took important personalities to his campaigns": "To take the high road is to act in a fair, honourable and positive way, even when others do not; in elections it means not attacking your opponents. So he took a positive action in his campaigns: C. The key gives B, but a bold step is about courage, which the idiom does not mean. A takes \"high\" as highbrow. Memory tip: high road = high standards; low road = dirty tricks.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): Justification|JUStification|jusTIfication|justiFIcation|justifiCAtion": "Words ending in -tion are stressed on the syllable just before -tion: jus-ti-fi-CA-tion. So the answer is D. Compare educa-tion, organisa-tion, qualifica-tion. Memory tip: -tion pulls the stress onto the syllable right before it.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): Admittedly|adMITtedly|ADmittedly|admitTEDly|admittedLY": "Admittedly is built on admit, which is stressed on the second syllable (ad-MIT), and adding -ted and -ly does not move the stress: ad-MIT-ted-ly, A. The paper misprints option A as \"adMITtedely\", but the stress pattern is clear. Memory tip: adding -ed, -ly or -ness usually keeps the stress of the base word.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: pARdon|dad|clerk|racket|wow": "The ar in pardon is the long vowel /ɑː/. In British English clerk is pronounced /klɑːk/, with the same vowel: B. Dad and racket have the short /æ/, and wow has /aʊ/. Memory tip: clerk is a well-known British exception, said like \"clark\".",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: mIGHt|wheat|wrist|wright|writ": "Might has the diphthong /aɪ/, spelt igh, and wright (as in playwright) is pronounced /raɪt/ with the same sound: C. Wrist and writ have the short /ɪ/, and wheat has /iː/. Memory tip: igh nearly always says /aɪ/ (light, night, fight).",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: GNaw|crane|cling|rang|flung": "In gnaw the g is silent, so the underlined gn is just /n/, the sound in crane: A. In cling, rang and flung the ng is the single sound /ŋ/, not /n/. Memory tip: initial gn (gnat, gnome, gnaw) is said /n/.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: daMn|him|draw|known|when": "In damn the n is silent, so the sound is /m/, as in him: A. Known and when end in /n/, and draw has no /m/. Memory tip: final mn (autumn, column, damn) is said /m/.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): He _____ his father in high esteem.|has|hallows|had|holds": "The fixed expression is \"to hold someone in high esteem\", meaning to respect them greatly: D. \"Has\" and \"had\" don't make the idiom, and \"hallows\" (makes holy) is the wrong verb. Memory tip: hold someone in high esteem / in contempt.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The examiners are _____.|through|thorough|though|throng": "Thorough means careful and complete, which suits examiners: B. Through, though and throng look alike but mean different things (by way of, although, a crowd). Memory tip: thorough = through + \"o\"; it means doing everything carefully.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Toni's popularity has _____ since his latest album.|soared|doubled|surged|multiply": "\"Toni's popularity has surged\" (rose suddenly and strongly) is correct: C, the key's answer. \"Has soared\" (A) is an equally standard way to say popularity rose sharply, so both are marked right. \"Has multiply\" (D) is the wrong verb form, and popularity is not usually said to \"double\". Memory tip: after \"has\", use the past participle (surged, soared).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The _____ of the beauty queen is captivating.|poisture|position|posture|pose": "Posture is the way a person holds and carries her body, which can be captivating in a beauty queen: C. \"Poisture\" is not an English word (perhaps a misprint for poise), position is where she is, and pose is a single held position. Memory tip: posture = how you carry your body all the time.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): A sound logic must appeal to one's _____.|situation|affection|cognition|disposition": "Logic is about reasoning, so it appeals to cognition, the mind's thinking and understanding: C. Affection and disposition are about feelings and temperament, and situation doesn't fit. Memory tip: logic appeals to the head (cognition), emotion to the heart.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The activities of the insurgents have been _____.|received with understanding|bemused by all and sundry|besotted with admiration|berated by all sundry": "To berate is to scold or criticise angrily, and insurgents' violent activities are condemned by everyone: D. \"Received with understanding\" and \"besotted with admiration\" are the opposite, and bemused means confused. (The paper prints \"all sundry\" for \"all and sundry\".) Memory tip: berate = rate badly = criticise.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): An actor in a play production is part of the _____.|crew|performers|dramatists|cast": "The cast is the whole group of actors in a play or film: D. The crew works behind the scenes, and dramatists write plays. Performers (B) is close in meaning, but \"part of the performers\" isn't natural English. Memory tip: cast = actors; crew = backstage workers.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Some of the victims look _____.|hampered|haggle|hazard|haggard": "Haggard means looking tired, worn and ill, which suits victims: D. Haggle (to bargain) and hazard (a danger) are not adjectives, and \"look hampered\" makes no sense. Memory tip: haggard = worn out by worry or suffering.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The business tycoon has found a new _____ for his goods.|cast|outlet|input|annex": "An outlet is a shop or market through which goods are sold: B. Input is something put in, an annex is an extra building, and cast doesn't fit. Memory tip: goods go out through an outlet.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Independence of the local government authorities _____ facilitates speedy development.|would|ought|shall|should": "\"Independence … would facilitate speedy development\" says what independence would bring about: A. The paper prints \"facilitates\", but after a modal verb the base form \"facilitate\" is needed. \"Ought\" would need \"to\", and shall/should change the meaning to a rule or advice. Memory tip: modal + base verb (would go, can do, would facilitate).",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Many students are faced with PECUNIARY challenges.|academic|home|monetary|important": "Pecuniary means relating to money, so pecuniary challenges are monetary problems: C. Academic, home and important do not mean money. Memory tip: pecuniary comes from Latin pecunia, money.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Bassey's actions have fallen outside the PRECINCTS of the law.|limits|premises|localities|directives": "Precincts here means boundaries, so falling outside the precincts of the law is falling outside its limits: A. Premises (B) is the trap, because precincts can mean the area around a building, but that meaning doesn't fit \"of the law\". Memory tip: precincts = bounds or limits of an area.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: I have tried to make Ukwudi a friend, but she has remained PETTY.|hostile|friendly|parochial|cynical": "Petty means small-minded, caring about trivial things; parochial means narrow-minded, with a limited outlook: C. Hostile and cynical are different faults, and friendly is the opposite of what the \"but\" clause suggests. Memory tip: petty and parochial are both about a small, narrow mind.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The company has been declared INSOLVENT.|buoyant|productive|corrupt|bankrupt": "Insolvent means unable to pay one's debts, i.e. bankrupt: D. Buoyant and productive are the opposite, and corrupt is about dishonesty. Memory tip: solvent = can pay; insolvent = can't pay.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The man with a bandaged hand has been a victim of XENOPHOBIA.|fear of the unknown|fear of crowds|fear of foreigners|fear of darkness": "Xenophobia comes from Greek xenos (stranger, foreigner) + phobia (fear): fear or hatred of foreigners, C. The other options name different fears. Memory tip: xeno- = foreign.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: She SIMPLY ignored me.|Did she simply accept them?|Who simply ignored me?|Did she totally ignore me?|Did she simply praise me?": "Stressing SIMPLY corrects the degree or manner of ignoring: \"Did she totally ignore me?\" \"No, she SIMPLY ignored me.\" So C. B would need stress on SHE, and A and D would need stress on IGNORED. Memory tip: the stressed word corrects the matching word in the question.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: Emeka ENCOURAGED Bayo to take the job.|Did Emeka encourage Bayo to take a job?|Did Emeka trick Bayo into rejecting the job?|Did Emeka persuade Bayo to take the job?|Who encouraged Bayo to take the job?": "Stressing ENCOURAGED corrects the verb, so the question used a different verb for the same act: \"Did Emeka persuade Bayo to take the job?\" \"No, he ENCOURAGED him.\" So C. D would need stress on EMEKA, and A repeats the same verb. Memory tip: emphatic stress answers a question that got that one word wrong.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: Little|temple|titled|spittle|bitter": "Little ends in /-ɪtl/ and spittle ends the same way: C. Titled has /aɪ/, bitter ends in /-ə/, and temple has /e/ and /-mpl/. Memory tip: for rhymes, match the stressed vowel and everything after it.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: grey|crow|groom|pray|glow": "Grey and pray both end in the diphthong /eɪ/: C. Crow and glow end in /əʊ/, and groom has /uː/. Memory tip: ey, ay and ei often all say /eɪ/ (grey, pray, veil).",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: We have learnt the THEORETICAL aspect of the course.|practical|abstract|written|hypothetical": "Theoretical means based on ideas and principles; its opposite is practical, based on doing: A. Abstract and hypothetical are near-synonyms of theoretical. Memory tip: theory versus practice.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Many people dislike the present AUSTERITY measures.|harsh|rigid|miserly|buoyant": "Austerity measures cut spending and bring hardship; the opposite state of an economy is buoyant, i.e. prosperous and thriving: D. Harsh, rigid and miserly all describe austerity itself. Memory tip: in antonym questions, cross out the options that are synonyms.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Women admire men who tell VERITABLE stories.|fantastic|real|negative|archaic": "Veritable means true, real and genuine. Its opposite is fantastic, meaning fanciful or imaginary: A. D is wrong: archaic means very old, which is not the opposite of \"true\". Real (B) is a synonym. Memory tip: veritable is linked to verity, truth.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Kolo has considerable ALLUREMENT for white shirts.|fascination|repulsion|attraction|temptation": "Allurement is attraction, the power to draw someone in; its opposite is repulsion, which pushes away: B. Fascination, attraction and temptation are synonyms. Memory tip: lure = draw in; repel = push away.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: He did not see the LIKELIHOOD of passing the examination.|difficulty|probability|impossibility|practicability": "Likelihood means probability, the chance that something will happen. Its opposite is impossibility: C. Probability (B) is a synonym, and difficulty and practicability are not opposites of chance. Memory tip: likely versus impossible.",
 "eng-2019-p1|Why does the writer liken life to a game?|A life is a game that has to be played with some seriousness|The game of life is a must do affair|The game of life has rules that must be followed|Life itself is not worth considering": "The writer compares life to a game because both have rules: \"The rules matter a great deal. The game has to be played fairly or it is no game at all.\" That's C. A is tempting, since the writer warns against treating life as trivial, but the comparison is built on rules and fair play, not just seriousness. D contradicts the passage. Memory tip: in a \"why does the writer compare...\" question, find the point the comparison keeps coming back to.",
 "eng-2019-p1|According to the writer, for anyone to win in life's game, he/she has to|follow the rules of the game with laxity|gather all the good things of life and be covetous|be upright and honourable|be honourable and static": "Winning is \"not the chief end\", the aim is \"to win it honourably and splendidly\" and to play \"fairly\". So you must be upright and honourable: C. A says the opposite (laxity means carelessness about rules), and D's \"static\" is never mentioned; the writer wants leaders who are flexible. Memory tip: when two options share a correct word, check the other word in each.",
 "eng-2019-p1|The word optimism, as used in the passage, means|hopefulness|hopelessness|determination|loyalty": "Optimism means hopefulness, expecting good things: A. The passage backs this up with \"never give up hope\". B is the opposite, and determination (C) is a different quality the passage names separately. Memory tip: optimist = expects the best; pessimist = expects the worst.",
 "eng-2019-p1|According to the writer, for a good leader to succeed in life's game, he/she has to be|rigid and cunning|dynamic and truthful|inflexible and loyal|dynamic and intelligent": "The passage says \"Personal intelligence and flexibility are vital for anyone to succeed as a leader\". Flexible (able to adapt) is close to dynamic, so D: dynamic and intelligent. A and C contain rigid and inflexible, the opposite of flexibility, and truthfulness (B) is never named as a leadership quality. Memory tip: match each word in the option to a word in the passage.",
 "eng-2019-p1|An appropriate title for this passage is|Leadership Qualities|The rules of life|Life battles|The game of life": "The passage moves from playing life's game fairly to persevering \"in the face of insurmountable odds\", never giving up hope, and keeping going \"no matter the odds\". The common thread is facing life's struggles, so the key gives C, Life battles. A fits only paragraphs 2 and 3, and D mainly paragraph 1. Memory tip: a good title covers every paragraph, not just one.",
 "eng-2019-p2|Gap 6: The development of money ... was occasioned by increased specialization of human beings, this necessitated _____. This is a movement away from a ... society|money|capital|exchange|movement": "The next sentence explains the gap: \"This is a movement away from a primitive society\", so the key's movement (D) fits. But exchange (C) is equally right, because specialization is exactly what makes exchange necessary; both are marked right. Money and capital don't follow from specialization in this sentence. Memory tip: in cloze, the sentence after a gap often echoes the missing word.",
 "eng-2019-p2|Gap 7: This is a movement away from a _____ society in which a closely knit family structure prevailed.|kind|secondary|primitive|standard": "A closely knit, self-sufficient family society with no specialization is an early, simple one: primitive, C. Secondary and standard don't describe a type of society here, and \"a kind society\" means a gentle one, which misses the point. Memory tip: primitive = early stage of development.",
 "eng-2019-p2|Gap 8: In this setting, families were self-sufficient in goods and _____ needed.|trade|cargoes|money|services": "\"Goods and services\" is the fixed economic pair: things you buy and work done for you. So D. Trade and money are not things a family \"needs\" in that sense, and cargoes are goods being shipped. Memory tip: in economics, goods and services always travel together.",
 "eng-2019-p2|Gap 9: Thus, there was need to undertake _____.|exchange|canvassing|production|commotion": "Once people specialized, families were no longer self-sufficient, so they had to swap what they had for what they needed: exchange, A. Canvassing means seeking votes or support, and commotion is noisy disorder. Memory tip: specialization leads to exchange, and exchange leads to money.",
 "eng-2019-p2|Gap 10: However, as specialization developed, there was the urgent need to devise a _____ of exchange.|means|way|lot|land": "\"A means of exchange\" is the standard phrase for anything used to pay, like \"a medium of exchange\" later in the passage: A. \"A way of exchange\" is not the fixed expression, and lot and land make no sense. Note the paper's \"device\" should be \"devise\" (the verb). Memory tip: device is a noun, devise is a verb.",
 "eng-2019-p2|Gap 11: This led to the use of a number of materials used as _____.|barter|money|naira|trade": "The materials were used as money; the passage goes on to say metallic money \"joined these materials as a medium of exchange\". So B. Barter is swapping goods without money, the very problem money solved, and naira is one particular currency. Memory tip: read past the gap to see what the next sentence takes for granted.",
 "eng-2019-p2|Gap 12: The origin and development of _____ money as distinct from metallic money is not unconnected with the activities of goldsmiths.|fabric|paper|oil|earned": "Money \"as distinct from metallic money\" which grew from goldsmiths' receipts is paper money; the passage later says \"This antecedent of paper money\". So B. Fabric and oil are not forms of money, and \"earned money\" contrasts nothing with metal. Memory tip: look for a later phrase that names the missing word.",
 "eng-2019-p2|Gap 13: ...they issued receipts indicating the quantity so _____ with them.|deposited|hired|stored|put": "The jewels were \"deposited with them for safe keeping\", so the receipts showed the quantity so deposited: A. Stored is close but doesn't collocate with \"with them\", and hired means rented. Memory tip: you deposit valuables with someone; you store them in a place.",
 "eng-2019-p2|Gap 14: Over time, depositors started using the _____ so issued in respect of their deposits as a medium of exchange.|resit|relief|receipts|cash": "The goldsmiths \"issued receipts\", and depositors began using those receipts as money: C. \"Resit\" is a spelling trap (a resit is an exam you take again), and cash didn't yet exist in paper form. Memory tip: receipt has a silent p.",
 "eng-2019-p2|Gap 15: This antecedent of paper money perhaps explains why money in _____ is backed by gold.|liquidation|circulation|mutation|contribution": "Money \"in circulation\" is money being passed from hand to hand in an economy: B. Liquidation means winding up a company, and mutation means change. Memory tip: circulation = moving round, like blood in the body.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Ngozi is an epitome of beauty.|Ngozi is very ugly|Ngozi loves to look good|Ngozi is an embodiment of beauty|Ngozi is admired by most people": "An epitome is a perfect example of a quality, so \"an epitome of beauty\" means an embodiment of beauty: C. A is the opposite, and D (admired by most people) may follow from beauty but isn't what the word means. Memory tip: epitome (e-PIT-o-mee) = the perfect example.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Aduke did not know that the offer was a Greek gift.|It was an offer to lure him into a trap|It was a package from Greece|It was an attractive gift|It was a bribe": "A Greek gift is a gift given to trick or harm you, from the wooden horse the Greeks gave the Trojans. So the offer was meant to lure Aduke into a trap: A. B takes \"Greek\" literally, and a bribe (D) is not necessarily a trap. Memory tip: \"Beware of Greeks bearing gifts.\"",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The ransom was paid on the nail.|It was paid without delay|It was not paid in time|It was not paid at all|It was paid in bits": "To pay \"on the nail\" is to pay immediately, without delay: A. B and C say the opposite, and D (in bits) means in instalments. Memory tip: on the nail = on the spot.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: He is the black sheep of the family.|He is the most intelligent child|He is the only dark person in the family|He is the only son|He is the problematic child": "The black sheep of a family is the member who causes trouble or brings disgrace: D. B takes \"black\" literally. Memory tip: black sheep = the odd one out, in a bad way.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Yusuf is a ball of fire. This means that Yusuf|is full of energy|loves to smoke|loves to cook|hates the sight of fire": "A ball of fire is a very energetic, lively person: A. B, C and D all take \"fire\" literally. Memory tip: idioms rarely mean what the words say; think of the picture (bursting with energy).",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The national football coach said that RAW players were no good.|possessed|unbacked|tired|seasoned": "Raw players are new and inexperienced; seasoned players have lots of experience. So D. Tired and unbacked don't contrast with \"raw\". Memory tip: raw = not yet \"cooked\" by experience; seasoned = well prepared.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Obi is INDUSTRIOUS and creative.|jobless|wasteful|indolent|underdeveloped": "Industrious means hard-working, so the opposite is indolent (lazy): C. Jobless is a trap, because a person without a job can still be hard-working. Memory tip: indolent and lazy both mean avoiding work.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Fatima was HARDWORKING.|hard hearted|overzealous|careless|indolent": "The opposite of hardworking is indolent (lazy): D. Careless (C) is about lack of care, not lack of effort, and hard hearted is about cruelty. Memory tip: the same word (indolent) answered the previous question; JAMB often repeats key words.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Kola SELDOM drives his father's car.|sometimes|frequently|always|often": "Seldom means rarely, so its opposite is often: D, the key's answer. Frequently (B) means exactly the same as often, so it is equally right and also marked right. Always (C) goes too far, and sometimes (A) sits in the middle. Memory tip: seldom = rarely; often = frequently.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Secrets are truths CONCEALED.|aligned|revealed|informal|unsolved": "Concealed means hidden, so its opposite is revealed (made known): B. Unsolved is tempting because secrets are unknown, but it is about problems, not hiding. Memory tip: conceal / reveal is a fixed opposite pair.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The contractor became scared after reading the ANONYMOUS letter.|frightening|awful|nameless|ominous": "An anonymous letter is one whose writer is not named: nameless, C. A and D (frightening, ominous) describe how the contractor felt, not what anonymous means. Memory tip: anonymous = \"without a name\" (from Greek onoma, name).",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Aliyu was advised to quit smoking before it becomes an ADDICTION.|a name|a trade|a style|a habit": "An addiction is a habit a person cannot easily stop: D. A style is a fashion choice, and a trade is a job. Memory tip: an addiction is a habit that has taken control.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The passage is simple enough to DIGEST.|apprehend|comprehend|reprehend|appraise": "To digest a passage is to take it in and understand it: comprehend, B. Apprehend can mean grasp, but it usually means arrest; reprehend means criticize, and appraise means assess. Memory tip: comprehend = understand fully.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The spectator passed a WITTY remark.|clever|complex|simple|encouraging": "A witty remark is clever and amusing: A. Complex and simple describe difficulty, not humour, and encouraging is about support. Memory tip: wit = quick, clever humour.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The clergy preaches against INDOLENCE.|pride|selfishness|laziness|wickedness": "Indolence means laziness: C. Pride, selfishness and wickedness are other vices a preacher might mention, but they don't match the word. Memory tip: indolence = laziness (opposite: industry).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): He sang the song which he _____ at school the previous day.|learn|learns|learnt|has learnt": "\"The previous day\" is a finished past time, so we need the past tense learnt: C. \"Has learnt\" (D) cannot be used with a stated past time, and learn and learns are present. Memory tip: never use the present perfect with yesterday, last week or \"the previous day\".",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Kolo could have come for sallah if he _____.|wants to|want to|wanted to|had wanted": "\"Could have come\" is the third conditional, which takes \"if + had + past participle\": had wanted, D. \"Wanted to\" (C) belongs to the second conditional with \"could come\". Memory tip: would/could have + past participle pairs with if + had + past participle.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The man _____ at the party yesterday.|is drinking|had drunk|was drunk|drunk": "\"Was drunk\" means was intoxicated, and fits the past time \"yesterday\": C. D is the trap: drunk is the past participle, so it can't stand alone as the past tense (that is drank). Had drunk needs a second past action. Memory tip: drink, drank, drunk.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Fatima realized that she _____ to post the letter when she reached home.|has forgotten|had forgotten|has forgot|had forgot": "She forgot before she realized, so the earlier past action takes the past perfect: had forgotten: B. A and C use the present perfect, which clashes with \"realized\". D uses forgot, which is not the British past participle. Memory tip: earlier past = had + past participle; forget, forgot, forgotten.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): He must _____ to Ibrahim before the incident.|have spoken|have spoke|had spoken|has spoken": "After a modal (must, should, may), use have + past participle to talk about the past: must have spoken, A. Spoke is the past tense, not the participle, and \"must had\" or \"must has\" are never correct. Memory tip: modal + have + past participle.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): We will go home when the rain _____.|stopped|have stopped|stops|stop": "In a time clause (when, after, as soon as) about the future, English uses the present simple: \"when the rain stops\", C. Stop (D) doesn't agree with the singular \"rain\", and stopped is past. Memory tip: will in the main clause, present simple after when.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): When I got there, _____ already arrived.|was|have|had|has": "Arriving happened before \"I got there\", so the past perfect had is needed: C. Has and have are present perfect, and \"was arrived\" is not standard. (The paper leaves out the subject before the gap, e.g. \"he had\".) Memory tip: two past actions: the earlier one takes had.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): If Adama were here, the food _____ be ready by now.|will|shall|should|would": "\"If Adama were here\" is the second conditional (imagined present), so the main clause takes would: D. Will and shall belong to the first conditional. Memory tip: if + were/past, then would.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): _____ we go to the zoo this afternoon?|shall|were|are|would": "\"Shall we...?\" is the standard polite way to make a suggestion: \"Shall we go to the zoo this afternoon?\": A. Would we go (D) doesn't make a suggestion, and were and are cannot go with \"go\". Memory tip: \"Shall I / Shall we\" offers or suggests.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The house _____ before the incident took place.|is being completed|had been completed|is completed|will be completed": "The house was finished before a past event, so we use the past perfect passive: had been completed, B. The other options are present or future and clash with \"took place\". Memory tip: before + past event, the earlier action takes had (been).",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: chURch|clutch|choice|girl|push": "The underlined ur in church is the long /ɜː/, the same sound as the ir in girl: C. Clutch has /ʌ/, push has /ʊ/, and choice has the diphthong /ɔɪ/. Memory tip: er, ir and ur before a consonant usually give /ɜː/: her, bird, church.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: pARt|pant|hat|pad|mass": "The ar in part is the long /ɑː/. In standard British English none of the options really has it: pant, hat, pad and mass are usually said with short /æ/. The key gives D, mass, probably because Mass (the church service) is sometimes said /mɑːs/, so D is kept as the closest answer. Memory tip: ar before a consonant gives /ɑː/: car, part, hard.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: viSa|Saturday|visit|embassy|step": "The underlined s in visa is /z/ (VEE-zuh), the same as the s in visit: B. Saturday, embassy and step all have /s/. (The heading says \"vowel sound\", but this question tests a consonant.) Memory tip: s between two vowels is often /z/: visa, visit, rose.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same consonant sound as the letter(s) in capitals: waSH|occasion|equation|explosion|extension": "The sh in wash is /ʃ/, the same as -sion in extension (ex-TEN-shun): D. Occasion, equation and explosion all have /ʒ/, the sound in measure. Memory tip: -sion after a consonant is often /ʃ/ (extension, tension); after a vowel it is /ʒ/ (explosion, occasion).",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: bright|rill|rite|bought|filthy": "Bright ends in /aɪt/, which rhymes with rite (pronounced like right): B. Bought has /ɔːt/, and rill and filthy have /ɪ/. Memory tip: rhyme depends on sound, not spelling: bright, rite, height.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: clay|plait|claws|plays|day": "Clay ends in /eɪ/, and day (/deɪ/) rhymes with it exactly: D. B is wrong: claws (/klɔːz/) does not rhyme with clay. Plays is close but adds a /z/, and plait is said /plæt/. Memory tip: a perfect rhyme has the same final vowel and nothing extra after it.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): development|deveLOPment|deVElopment|DEvelopment|developMENT": "Development is stressed on the second syllable, de-VEL-op-ment, just like develop: B. The suffix -ment doesn't move the stress. Memory tip: adding -ment keeps the stress of the base word: deVELop, deVELopment.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): temporary|TEMporary|tempoRAry|temPOrary|temporaRY": "Temporary is stressed on the first syllable: TEM-po-rar-y, A. Many Nigerian speakers wrongly stress the third syllable. Memory tip: temporary follows TEMpo, as in TEMpo of music.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: Adedeji OPENLY disagreed with his boss yesterday.|Did Adedeji secretly disagree with his boss yesterday?|Did Badejo openly disagree with his boss yesterday?|Did Adedeji openly disagree with his father yesterday?|Did Adedeji openly disagree with his boss last week?": "Stressing OPENLY corrects the manner of disagreeing, so it answers a question that names a different manner: \"Did Adedeji secretly disagree...?\", A. B would need ADEDEJI stressed, C BOSS, and D YESTERDAY. Memory tip: the stressed word is the one that corrects the question.",
 "|The word in capitals has emphatic stress. Choose the option that best fits the expression: Aisha loves her CHILDREN dearly.|Does Adama love her children dearly?|Does Aisha hate her children dearly?|Does Aisha love her husband dearly?|Does Aisha love her children badly?": "Stressing CHILDREN corrects who Aisha loves, so it answers \"Does Aisha love her husband dearly?\": C. A would need AISHA stressed, B LOVES, and D DEARLY. Memory tip: find the option that changes only the stressed word.",
 "eng-2020-p1|Why does the writer liken life to a game?|A life is a game that has to be played with some seriousness|The game of life is a must do affair|The game of life has rules that must be followed|Life itself is not worth considering": "The writer says life is a game whose \"rules matter a great deal\" and which \"has to be played fairly or it is no game at all\". So the comparison is about rules that must be followed: C. A is tempting, but seriousness is only implied; the passage keeps returning to rules and fair play. B and D are never said. Memory tip: pick the option that repeats the passage's own key idea, not one you could merely infer.",
 "eng-2020-p1|According to the writer, for anyone to win in life's game, he/she has to|follow the rules of the game with laxity|gather all the good things of life and be covetous|be upright and honourable|be honourable and static": "The writer says winning \"is not the chief end, but to win it honourably and splendidly\", and the game must be \"played fairly\". Fair and honourable means upright and honourable: C. A is a trap: laxity means carelessness, the opposite of rules that \"matter a great deal\". D adds \"static\", which the passage never praises. Memory tip: one wrong word spoils a two-part option, so check both halves.",
 "eng-2020-p1|The word optimism, as used in the passage, means|hopefulness|hopelessness|determination|loyalty": "Optimism is hopefulness, a confident belief that things will turn out well. The passage backs this up: \"never give up hope\". So: A. B (hopelessness) is the opposite, and C (determination) is listed separately in the passage as another quality. Memory tip: if the passage names two qualities side by side, they are not synonyms of each other.",
 "eng-2020-p1|According to the writer, for a good leader to succeed in life's game, he/she has to be|rigid and cunning|dynamic and truthful|inflexible and loyal|dynamic and intelligent": "The passage says \"Personal intelligence and flexibility are vital for anyone to succeed as a leader.\" Flexible means adaptable, i.e. dynamic, so the answer is dynamic and intelligent: D. A and C say rigid and inflexible, the exact opposite of flexibility. B swaps intelligence for truthfulness, and the passage even says a leader may hide his feelings. Memory tip: look for the option that matches both qualities named in the passage.",
 "eng-2020-p1|An appropriate title for this passage is|Leadership Qualities|The rules of life|Life battles|The game of life": "The passage keeps describing struggle: playing fairly, persevering against \"insurmountable odds\", never giving up in \"trouble, danger and disappointment\" and keeping going \"no matter the odds\". So the best title, as the key gives, is Life battles: C. D is tempting but covers only the opening idea of life as a game; A covers only the middle paragraph and B only the first. Memory tip: a good title covers the whole passage, not just one paragraph.",
 "eng-2020-p2|Gap 6: In African countries, industrialization which means industrial _____ is springing up in the developing countries...|development|fund|forces|investment": "Industrialization means the growth of industry, i.e. industrial development: A. The passage confirms this a few lines later with \"The development can improve production\". Fund and investment are means of development, not what industrialization is, and \"industrial forces\" means something else. Memory tip: when a gap defines a word, pick the option that really is its meaning.",
 "eng-2020-p2|Gap 7: ...the developing countries which are struggling to _____ their fortune and ...|supply|improve|balance|decrease": "Developing countries are poor and are \"struggling to\" make things better, so they struggle to improve their fortune: B. Supply and balance do not go with \"fortune\", and decrease is the opposite of what a struggling country wants. Memory tip: \"struggling to\" is followed by a goal you want to reach, so look for a positive verb.",
 "eng-2020-p2|Gap 8: ...which are struggling to improve their fortune and _____.|productivity|production|condition|service": "The countries struggle to improve \"their fortune and condition\", two words for their state of life: C. Productivity and production are things a country raises, not its general situation, and service doesn't pair with fortune. Memory tip: in \"X and ___\", choose a word of the same kind as X.",
 "eng-2020-p2|Gap 9: It involves such things as the provision of _____ station producing electricity for ...|power|work|energy|force": "A plant that produces electricity is a power station: A. Energy is close in meaning, but \"energy station\" is not the English expression, and \"work station\" and \"force station\" mean something else or nothing. Memory tip: learn collocations as fixed pairs: power station, power cut, power supply.",
 "eng-2020-p2|Gap 10: ...the provision of power station producing electricity for _____ and the construction of irrigation works.|companies|warehouse|works|factories": "The key gives companies (A), which fits, but factories (D) is equally right and is the most natural word in a passage about industrialization, so both are marked right. Warehouse is singular and would need \"a\", and works would clash with \"irrigation works\" in the same sentence. Memory tip: industrialization means factories; let the topic of the passage guide you.",
 "eng-2020-p2|Gap 11: The development can improve production in any _____ by means of large ... investments.|field|area|way|work": "The key gives area (B), and field (A) is equally right, so both are marked right: \"improve production in any area\" and \"in any field\" both mean in any sector of activity. Way and work do not fit after \"production in any\". Memory tip: area, field, sector and sphere are often interchangeable for a branch of activity.",
 "eng-2020-p2|Gap 12: ...by means of large _____ investments.|huge|capital|premium|fund": "Capital investment is money put into machines, buildings and plant, exactly what industrial development needs: B. Huge only repeats \"large\" (\"large huge investments\"), and premium and fund don't describe a kind of investment here. Memory tip: avoid options that just repeat a word already in the sentence.",
 "eng-2020-p2|Gap 13: The only source of _____ for such development is the government itself.|finance|interest|demand|power": "Development needs money, and the source of money for a project is its source of finance: A. Interest is what a loan costs, demand is what buyers want, and power was used earlier for electricity. Memory tip: finance = money for a project; \"source of finance\" is a common pairing.",
 "eng-2020-p2|Gap 14: An individual investment _____ are small and foreign loan is not easy to come by.|interest|cash|capital|capabilities": "The verb is \"are\", so the gap needs a plural noun: individuals' investment capabilities (how much they are able to invest) \"are small\": D. Interest, cash and capital are uncountable and would need \"is\". Memory tip: let the verb after the gap tell you whether the noun is singular or plural.",
 "eng-2020-p2|Gap 15: _____ on foreign loans are quite high.|Interest charges|Interest|Cash|Cash balances": "What lenders charge on a loan is interest, and the verb \"are\" needs a plural, so interest charges: A. Interest (B) is the right idea but singular, and cash and cash balances are not charged on loans. Memory tip: check both the meaning and the agreement with the verb.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Ada is an epitome of beauty.|Ada is very ugly|Ada loves to look good|Ada is an embodiment of beauty|Ada is admired by most people": "An epitome is a perfect example of a quality, so an epitome of beauty is beauty in person, its embodiment: C. D is tempting, since beautiful people are admired, but it explains a result, not the meaning. A is the opposite. Memory tip: epitome = embodiment = perfect example.",
 "|Select the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: Mula was told that if he behaved badly he would go to bed without any supper.|Mula had not yet had his supper|Mula was behaving badly because he had no supper|Mula had had no supper because he had behaved badly|Mula had had supper and had gone to bed": "The threat \"you will go to bed without supper if you behave badly\" only makes sense if supper has not happened yet. So Mula had not yet had his supper: A. B and C make up a cause, and D contradicts the threat. (The paper misprints \"supper\" as \"super\" in C.) Memory tip: a conditional threat points to the future, so the thing threatened has not happened yet.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Mary was a natural SPENDTHRIFT when she was single.|philanthropist|miser|gambler|thrift collector": "A spendthrift spends money wastefully. The opposite is a miser, who hoards money and hates to spend it: B. A philanthropist gives money away, so he also spends freely, and a gambler wastes money too. \"Thrift collector\" is a trap built from the word \"thrift\". (The paper prints \"he\" for Mary.) Memory tip: spendthrift and miser are the classic opposite pair.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The student CAUTIOUSLY raised his arm.|finally|slowly|quickly|rashly": "Cautiously means carefully, with thought before acting. The opposite is rashly, i.e. without thinking about the risk: D. Slowly and quickly describe speed, not care, so they are traps. Memory tip: cautious and rash are opposites; speed words are not.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Josephine was HARDWORKING.|hard hearted|overzealous|careless|indolent": "Hardworking means diligent; the opposite is indolent, which means lazy: D. Careless is tempting, but a careless person may still work hard. Hard-hearted is a trap built from the word \"hard\". Memory tip: indolent = lazy; learn it with industrious, its opposite.",
 "|Choose the option OPPOSITE in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: Gloria walks in a GAWKY way.|suitable|clumsy|lumbering|graceful": "Gawky means awkward and clumsy in movement. The opposite is graceful: D. Clumsy (B) and lumbering (C) are synonyms, the classic trap in opposite questions, and suitable doesn't describe movement. Memory tip: in an opposite question, first cross out the options that mean the same as the word.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The coach of the team is often MALIGNED by the supporters.|slandered|cherished|praised|hounded": "To malign someone is to speak ill of them unfairly, i.e. to slander them: A. Hounded (D) means chased or harassed, which is close, but it is not about words. Cherished and praised are opposites. Memory tip: mal- means bad: malign = say bad things.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: She has a COGENT reason for quitting the relationship.|a convincing|an important|a tentative|an unacceptable": "A cogent reason is clear, logical and convincing: A. An important reason (B) need not be convincing, and tentative and unacceptable go the other way. Memory tip: cogent argument = convincing argument.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: His search for the treasure was FRANTIC.|rewarding|chaotic|hectic|sudden": "Frantic means wild, hurried and disorderly, so a frantic search is a chaotic one: B, the key's answer. Hectic (C), meaning full of hurried activity, is just as close, so both are marked right. Rewarding and sudden have nothing to do with frantic. Memory tip: frantic, frenzied, hectic and chaotic all describe wild, hurried activity.",
 "|Choose the option NEAREST in meaning to the word(s) in capitals: The nursing mother wasn't yet ATTUNED to her baby's needs.|familiar with|ready with|free from|planning for": "To be attuned to something is to be in tune with it, aware of it and used to it, i.e. familiar with it: A. Ready with and planning for are about action, not awareness, and free from is the opposite. Memory tip: attuned = in tune with = familiar with.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): She must _____ to Ibrahim before the incident.|have spoken|have spoke|had spoken|has spoken": "A deduction about the past uses modal + have + past participle: \"She must have spoken to Ibrahim\": A. B uses spoke, the past tense, not the participle. C and D cannot follow a modal, which always takes the base form (have). Memory tip: must / might / could + have + past participle (spoken, gone, eaten).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): They will go home when the rain _____.|stopped|have stopped|stops|stop": "After \"when\" in a sentence about the future, English uses the present simple, not will: \"They will go home when the rain stops\": C. Stop (D) doesn't agree with the singular \"rain\", and stopped and have stopped don't fit the future. Memory tip: will in the main clause, present simple after when, if, before, until.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Mr. Njoku _____ at the party yesterday.|is drinking|had drunk|was drunk|drunk": "\"Yesterday\" needs the simple past, and \"was drunk\" (was intoxicated) is simple past: C. D is the trap: drunk alone is the past participle; the past tense of drink is drank. A is present and B (past perfect) needs a later past event. Memory tip: drink, drank, drunk: the participle needs a helper (has/had/was).",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): Joe could have come for Easter if he _____.|wants to|want to|wanted to|had wanted": "\"Could have come\" refers to an unreal past situation, so the if-clause needs the past perfect: \"if he had wanted (to)\": D. Wanted to (C) belongs to the second conditional (\"could come if he wanted to\"). Memory tip: third conditional = if + had + past participle, would/could have + past participle.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): The hall _____ before the incident took place.|is being completed|had been completed|is completed|will be completed": "The completion happened before another past event (\"the incident took place\"), so the past perfect passive is needed: had been completed: B. The other options are present or future and clash with \"took place\". Memory tip: \"before + past event\" usually signals had + past participle for the earlier action.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): She sang the song which he _____ at school the previous day.|learn|learns|learnt|has learnt": "\"The previous day\" is a finished past time, so the simple past learnt is needed: C. Has learnt (D) is the trap, as the present perfect cannot go with a finished past time phrase. Learn and learns are present. Memory tip: yesterday, last week, the previous day: use the simple past, not has/have.",
 "|Choose the option that best completes the gap(s): If Jude were here, the food _____ be ready by now.|will|shall|should|would": "\"If Jude were here\" is the second conditional (an unreal present), so the main clause takes would: D. Will and shall belong to real future conditions; should means obligation. Memory tip: if + were/past, would + base verb.",
 "|Choose the option that has the same vowel sound as the letter(s) in capitals: dOOr|food|cough|sure|board": "The oor in door is the long /ɔː/, the same sound as the oar in board: D. Food has /uː/, cough the short /ɒ/, and sure is traditionally /ʃʊə/ in RP. Memory tip: door, floor, board, more and four all share /ɔː/.",
 "|Choose the option that rhymes with the given word: Gold|goal|gaol|goad|goat": "Gold ends in /əʊld/. No option truly rhymes: goal and gaol (/-əʊl/) lack the d, goad (/gəʊd/) lacks the l, and goat ends in /t/. The key's goad (C) is kept as the closest, sharing the vowel and the final /d/. Note that gaol is pronounced like jail. Memory tip: a true rhyme matches everything from the vowel to the end: gold rhymes with old, bold, told.",
 "|Choose the appropriate stress pattern for the word (the stressed syllable is in capitals): obligation|obliGAtion|OBligation|obligaTION|obLIgation": "Obligation is stressed ob-li-GA-tion: A. Words ending in -tion are stressed on the syllable just before the ending (in-for-MA-tion, ed-u-CA-tion). B and D stress the wrong part, and C stresses -tion, which is never stressed. Memory tip: -tion: stress the syllable just before it."
};
